          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Python Syntax Rules",
            "explanation": "First, let's understand **Python's syntax rules**. **Syntax** refers to the set of rules that define how Python code should be written. If these rules are not followed correctly, Python will display an error and the program will not run.\n\n **Indentation Matters**\n**Indentation** means adding spaces at the beginning of a line. Python uses indentation to identify blocks of code and determine which statements belong together.\n\n **Python is Case-Sensitive**\nPython treats uppercase and lowercase letters as different characters. For example, `name`, `Name`, and `NAME` are considered different identifiers.\n**Example:** `name = 'John'` and `Name = 'Python'` are two different variables.\n\n **Strings are Written Inside Quotes**\nA **string** is a collection of characters or text. In Python, text values must be enclosed within single quotes or double quotes.\n**Example:** `message = 'Hello World'`\n\n **Statements are Usually Written on Separate Lines**\nIn Python, each statement is typically written on a separate line to make the code easier to read and understand.\n**Example:** `age = 25` on one line and `print(age)` on the next line.\n\n **Key Takeaway**\nFollowing Python's syntax rules helps you write clean, readable, and error-free programs.",
            "example": "# Indentation inside an if block\nif age > 18:\n    print('Adult')\n\n# Python is case-sensitive\nName = 'Python'\nprint(name)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Python Syntax Rules",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into the blank.",
