        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Fundamental Data Types",
            "explanation": "In the previous lesson, you learned that variables are used to store values. However, not all values are the same. Sometimes we store numbers, sometimes text, and sometimes values that represent a condition such as yes/no or true/false. To handle these different kinds of information, Python uses **data types**.\nA **data type** tells Python what kind of value is stored in a variable. Knowing the data type helps Python decide what operations can be performed on that value. For example, numbers can be added together, while text can be combined or searched.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Numeric Types",
                    "body": "Python provides numeric data types for mathematical calculations.\n\n• int → Stores whole numbers without decimal points\n\n      Example: age = 25\n\n• float → Stores numbers with decimal points\n\n      Example:percentage = 99.5\n\nCommon Uses:\nAge, marks, prices, height, percentages."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Text Type (str)",
                    "body": "Used for storing text, words, sentences, or any sequence of characters.The str data type stores text enclosed in single or double quotes.\n\n      Examples:name = 'John' \n\nCommon Uses:\nNames, addresses, emails, messages, usernames."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Boolean Type (bool)",
                    "body": "Used when a value can have only one of two possible states.\n\nValues:\nTrue\nFalse\n\n      Examples is_student = True\n\nCommon Uses:\nLogin status, payment status, eligibility checks, feature flags."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Sequence Types",
                    "body": "Sequence types store multiple values.\n\n• list → Ordered and changeable\n\n      Examples: marks = [10, 20, 30]\n\n• tuple → Ordered and immutable\n\n      Examples: coordinates=(10, 20, 30)\n\n• range → Sequence of numbers\n\n      Example: number = range(1, 6)"
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Mapping Type (dict)",
                    "body": "A dictionary stores information as key-value pairs.\n\n      Example: student = {'name':'John', 'age':21}\n\nCommon Uses:\nStudent records, employee information, JSON data, API responses."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Set Types",
                    "body": "Set types store only unique values.\n\n• set → Mutable collection\n\n      Example: unique_ids = {1,2,3}\n\n• frozenset → Immutable set\n\nDuplicate values are automatically removed."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Binary Types",
                    "body": "Binary types work with raw binary data.\n\n• bytes\n• bytearray\n• memoryview\n\nCommon Uses:\nFiles, images, videos, networking, and binary data processing."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "age = 21                      # int\npercentage = 89.5            # float\nstudent_name = 'John'        # str\nfees_paid = True             # bool\n\nmarks = [85, 90, 95]         # list\ncoordinates = (10, 20)       # tuple\nnumbers = range(1, 5)        # range\n\nstudent = {'name': 'John', 'age': 21}  # dict\n\nunique_ids = {101, 102, 103} # set\n\n# Type Casting\nuser_input = '50'\nscore = int(user_input)\nprint(score + 10)"
          }
        },
