        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "Variable Scope In Python",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "What",
                "text": "Variable scope refers to the region of a program where a variable is recognized and can be accessed."
              },
              {
                "label": "Why",
                "text": "It helps prevent bugs by keeping variables isolated. You don't want a variable in one function accidentally changing a value in another!"
              },
              {
                "label": "Theory",
                "text": "Python follows the LEGB rule: Local -> Enclosing -> Global -> Built-in. The interpreter looks for a name in this specific order."
              },
              {
                "label": "Example",
                "code": "x = 'Global'\n\ndef my_func():\n    x = 'Local'\n    print(x) # Prints 'Local'\n\nmy_func()\nprint(x) # Prints 'Global'"
              },
              {
                "label": "How",
                "text": "Define variables inside a function for 'Local' scope. Define them at the top level for 'Global' scope. Use the 'global' keyword if you MUST modify a global variable inside a function."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
