        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "List Comprehensions & Iterators",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "The Pythonic Way",
                "text": "List comprehensions provide a concise way to create lists. Instead of writing a 4-line 'for loop', you can transform data in a single line."
              },
              {
                "label": "Syntax",
                "code": "new_list = [expression for item in iterable if condition]"
              },
              {
                "label": "Example",
                "code": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nsquares = [x**2 for x in nums if x > 2]\n# Result: [9, 16]"
              },
              {
                "label": "Iterators",
                "text": "When data is too large for memory (like a 50GB image dataset), we use Iterators. An iterator yields exactly one item at a time instead of loading the entire list into memory. You trigger it using the next() function."
              },
              {
                "label": "Example",
                "code": "nums = iter([10, 20, 30])\nprint(next(nums))  # 10\nprint(next(nums))  # 20"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
