            "title": "Working with Configuration Files",
            "explanation": "\nHardcoding variables (like API keys, database passwords, or server ports) directly into your Python scripts is a massive security and maintenance risk.\nModern applications use **Configuration Files** to separate code from environment settings.\n**Common Formats:**\n• **.env**: Used for secrets (API Keys, Passwords). Read using the `python-dotenv` library.\n• **.json` / `.yaml**: Used for complex settings (e.g., specifying which AI model to load, batch sizes, timeout limits).\n\nBy keeping settings in a config file, you can change how your program behaves without ever touching the Python code itself.",
            "example": "# Example of a .env file:\n# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-12345abcdef\n# PORT=8080\n\nimport os\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\n\nload_dotenv() # Loads the .env file into the environment\n\napi_key = os.getenv('OPENAI_API_KEY')\nprint(\"Securely loaded API key!\")",
            "warning": "Never commit your `.env` files to GitHub! Always add `.env` to your `.gitignore` file so your passwords don't get leaked to the internet."
          }
