    {
      "id": "M001-pandas-introduction",
      "title": "Introduction to Pandas",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Pandas?",
            "explanation": "In the previous lessons, you learned Python programming. Now it's time to work with real-world data.\n\n**Pandas** is one of the most popular Python libraries used for working with structured data. It helps us load, organize, clean, analyze, and manipulate data efficiently.\n\nImagine a company has thousands of customer records stored in an Excel or CSV file. Reading and processing this data manually would take a lot of time. With Pandas, we can load the entire dataset and perform operations such as filtering, sorting, updating, and analyzing the data using just a few lines of code.\n\n### Why Do We Use Pandas?\n\n• Read data from CSV, Excel, JSON, SQL databases, and more.\n• Clean missing or incorrect data.\n• Filter and sort records.\n• Perform calculations and statistical analysis.\n• Prepare data for Machine Learning and AI models.\n• Save processed data back into files.\n\n### Real-World Applications\n\n• Data Analysis\n• Machine Learning\n• Business Intelligence\n• Financial Analysis\n• Healthcare Analytics\n• Sales Reporting\n• Data Preprocessing for AI",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI,sans-serif;background:#0f172a;padding:24px;border-radius:12px;color:white;\"><h2 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;\">Why Pandas?</h2><div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;margin-top:30px;\"><div style=\"flex:1;background:#1e293b;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\"><h3>📄 CSV / Excel</h3><p>Raw Data</p></div><div style=\"font-size:32px;color:#38bdf8;\">→</div><div style=\"flex:1;background:#1e293b;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\"><h3>🐼 Pandas</h3><p>Clean & Analyze</p></div><div style=\"font-size:32px;color:#38bdf8;\">→</div><div style=\"flex:1;background:#1e293b;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\"><h3>📊 Insights</h3><p>Charts, Reports & AI</p></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Pandas Data Structures",
            "explanation": "Now that you know how Pandas works with data, let's learn how it stores that data.\n\nPandas mainly provides **two data structures**: **Series** and **DataFrame**.\n\n### 1. Series\nA **Series** is a one-dimensional collection of data. You can think of it as a **single column** in a table.\n\n<div style=\"background:#0f172a;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;\"><div style=\"max-width:220px;margin:auto;background:#1e293b;border-radius:8px;padding:12px;\"><h4 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;margin-top:0;\">Series</h4><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:white;text-align:center;\"><tr style=\"background:#334155;\"><th>Age</th></tr><tr><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>29</td></tr></table></div></div>\n\n### 2. DataFrame\nA **DataFrame** is a two-dimensional table made up of **rows and columns**. It is similar to an Excel spreadsheet or a SQL table.\n\n<div style=\"background:#0f172a;padding:18px;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0;\"><div style=\"background:#1e293b;border-radius:8px;padding:12px;\"><h4 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;margin-top:0;\">DataFrame</h4><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;color:white;text-align:center;\"><tr style=\"background:#334155;\"><th>Name</th><th>Age</th><th>City</th></tr><tr><td>Emma</td><td>24</td><td>London</td></tr><tr><td>Liam</td><td>31</td><td>New York</td></tr><tr><td>Olivia</td><td>27</td><td>Sydney</td></tr></table></div></div>\n\nA **Series** stores data in a single column, while a **DataFrame** stores data in multiple rows and columns. In real-world projects, you'll mostly work with **DataFrames** because datasets usually contain many columns such as names, ages, salaries, and departments."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Creating Series and DataFrames",
            "explanation": "Now that you know the two data structures, let's see how to create them.Before working with Pandas, you first need to install it using `pip install pandas`.\n\nA **Series** is created from a single list.\n\nA **DataFrame** is created using a dictionary where each key becomes a column.\n\nIn real-world projects, you usually don't create DataFrames manually. Instead, you load them from files such as CSV, Excel, or databases.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create Series\nages = pd.Series([25,30,22,28])\nprint(ages)\n\n# Create DataFrame\nstudents = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Name':['John','Sara','Tom'],\n    'Age':[25,30,22],\n    'City':['Delhi','Mumbai','Pune']\n})\n\nprint(students)",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#111;padding:10px 15px;border-top:1px solid #333;color:#4ec9b0;font-weight:bold;'>Terminal</div><pre style='margin:0;padding:15px;font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;white-space:pre-wrap;color:#dcdcdc;'>0    25\n1    30\n2    22\n3    28\ndtype: int64\n\n   Name  Age    City\n0  John   25   Delhi\n1  Sara   30  Mumbai\n2   Tom   22    Pune</pre></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create a Pandas Series named ages containing the values 24, 31, 27, and 29. Then create a DataFrame named students with the columns Name, Age, and City using the following data:\n\nEmma - 24 - London\nLiam - 31 - New York\nOlivia - 27 - Sydney\n\nFinally, print both the Series and the DataFrame.",
            "starterCode": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create the Series\n\n\n# Create the DataFrame\n\n\n# Print both\n",
            "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create the Series\nages = pd.Series([24, 31, 27, 29])\n\n# Create the DataFrame\nstudents = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Name': ['Emma', 'Liam', 'Olivia'],\n    'Age': [24, 31, 27],\n    'City': ['London', 'New York', 'Sydney']\n})\n\n# Print both\nprint(ages)\nprint(students)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use pd.Series() for a single column and pd.DataFrame() with a dictionary for multiple columns."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Common Pandas Functions",
            "explanation": "Pandas provides many built-in functions to quickly understand your dataset.\n\n### Frequently Used Functions\n\n**head()** → Shows the first 5 rows.\n\n**tail()** → Shows the last 5 rows.\n\n**shape** → Returns the number of rows and columns.\n\n**columns** → Displays all column names.\n\n**dtypes** → Shows the data type of each column.\n\n**info()** → Provides a summary of the dataset.\n\n**describe()** → Displays statistical information for numeric columns.\n\nThese functions are usually the first ones developers use after loading a dataset.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('students.csv')\n\nprint(df.head())\nprint(df.shape)\nprint(df.info())\nprint(df.describe())"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Loading Data into Pandas",
            "explanation": "In real-world projects, data is usually stored in files or databases rather than being created manually.\n\nPandas can load data from many different sources.\n\n### Common Data Sources\n\n• CSV Files (.csv)\n• Excel Files (.xlsx)\n• JSON Files (.json)\n• SQL Databases\n\nEach source stores data in a different format, but Pandas converts all of them into a DataFrame, making them easy to analyze.\n\nIn the next lesson, we'll learn how these file formats look and how to read them using Pandas.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Load CSV file\ncsv_df = pd.read_csv('students.csv')\n\n# Load Excel file\nexcel_df = pd.read_excel('students.xlsx')\n\n# Load JSON file\njson_df = pd.read_json('students.json')\n\n# Load data from a SQL database\nconn = sqlite3.connect('students.db')\nsql_df = pd.read_sql('SELECT * FROM students', conn)\nconn.close()"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Assume you have a CSV file named **students.csv** that contains the following data:\n\nName,Age,City,Marks\nJohn,25,Delhi,85\nSara,30,Mumbai,92\nTom,22,Pune,78\nAlice,28,Bangalore,88\nEmma,24,Chennai,95\n\nWrite a Python program to:\n1. Load the CSV file into a Pandas DataFrame.\n2. Display the first 5 rows.\n3. Display the last 5 rows.\n4. Display the shape of the dataset.\n5. Display the column names.\n6. Display the statistical summary of the dataset.",
            "starterCode": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Load the CSV file\n\n\n# Display the first 5 rows\n\n\n# Display the last 5 rows\n\n\n# Display the shape\n\n\n# Display the column names\n\n\n# Display the statistical summary\n",
            "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Load the CSV file\nstudents = pd.read_csv('students.csv')\n\n# Display the first 5 rows\nprint(students.head())\n\n# Display the last 5 rows\nprint(students.tail())\n\n# Display the shape\nprint(students.shape)\n\n# Display the column names\nprint(students.columns)\n\n# Display the statistical summary\nprint(students.describe())",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use pd.read_csv() to load the CSV file. Then use head(), tail(), shape, columns, and describe() to explore the dataset."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Data Preprocessing?",
            "explanation": "Real-world datasets are rarely perfect. They often contain missing values, duplicate records, incorrect data types, or unnecessary columns.\n\n**Data Preprocessing** is the process of cleaning and preparing data before analysis or Machine Learning.\n\nCommon preprocessing tasks include:\n\n• Removing missing values.\n• Filling missing values.\n• Removing duplicate records.\n• Renaming columns.\n• Changing data types.\n• Removing unwanted columns.\n• Standardizing text values.\n\nClean data produces more accurate results and better Machine Learning models."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Common Data Cleaning Functions",
            "explanation": "Pandas provides many built-in functions for cleaning datasets.\n\n**isnull()** → Finds missing values.\n\n**dropna()** → Removes rows containing missing values.\n\n**fillna()** → Replaces missing values.\n\n**drop_duplicates()** → Removes duplicate rows.\n\n**rename()** → Renames columns.\n\n**astype()** → Changes the data type of a column.\n\nThese functions are commonly used before performing analysis or training Machine Learning models.",
            "example": "df.isnull()\ndf.dropna()\ndf.fillna(0)\ndf.drop_duplicates()\ndf.rename(columns={'Age':'Student_Age'})\ndf['Age']=df['Age'].astype(int)",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:Segoe UI;background:#0f172a;color:white;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;\"><h2 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;\">Data Cleaning Process</h2><div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;margin-top:20px;\"><div style=\"background:#1e293b;padding:15px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\">Raw Data<br>❌ Missing Values</div><div style=\"font-size:30px;color:#38bdf8;\">→</div><div style=\"background:#1e293b;padding:15px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\">Pandas Cleaning<br>dropna()<br>fillna()</div><div style=\"font-size:30px;color:#38bdf8;\">→</div><div style=\"background:#2563eb;padding:15px;border-radius:10px;text-align:center;\">Clean Dataset ✅</div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Assume you have a CSV file named **employees.csv** with the following data:\n\nName,Age,Department,Salary\nJohn,25,Sales,50000\nSara,,HR,60000\nTom,22,Sales,55000\nJohn,25,Sales,50000\nAlice,28,IT,\n\nWrite a Python program to:\n1. Load the CSV file into a DataFrame.\n2. Display all missing values.\n3. Replace missing Salary values with 0.\n4. Remove duplicate rows.\n5. Rename the column 'Age' to 'Employee_Age'.\n6. Display the cleaned DataFrame.",
            "starterCode": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Load the CSV file\n\n\n# Display missing values\n\n\n# Fill missing Salary values\n\n\n# Remove duplicate rows\n\n\n# Rename the Age column\n\n\n# Display the cleaned DataFrame\n",
            "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Load the CSV file\ndf = pd.read_csv('employees.csv')\n\n# Display missing values\nprint(df.isnull())\n\n# Fill missing Salary values\ndf['Salary'] = df['Salary'].fillna(0)\n\n# Remove duplicate rows\ndf = df.drop_duplicates()\n\n# Rename the Age column\ndf = df.rename(columns={'Age':'Employee_Age'})\n\n# Display the cleaned DataFrame\nprint(df)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use isnull(), fillna(), drop_duplicates(), and rename() to clean the dataset."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Selecting and Filtering Data",
            "explanation": "After loading and cleaning the data, the next step is selecting the information you need.\n\nPandas provides several ways to access rows and columns.\n\n**df['Column']** → Select a single column.\n\n**df[['Col1','Col2']]** → Select multiple columns.\n\n**loc[]** → Select rows and columns using labels.\n\n**iloc[]** → Select rows and columns using index positions.\n\nYou can also filter data using conditions.\n\nFor example, to find students older than 25 years:\n\n**df[df['Age'] > 25]**\n\nSelecting only the required data makes analysis faster and easier.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create a sample DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Name': ['John', 'Sara', 'Tom', 'Emma'],\n    'Age': [25, 30, 22, 28],\n    'City': ['Delhi', 'Mumbai', 'Pune', 'Bangalore']\n})\n\n# Select a single column\nprint(df['Name'])\n\n# Select multiple columns\nprint(df[['Name', 'Age']])\n\n# Select the first row using loc\nprint(df.loc[0])\n\n# Select the first row using iloc\nprint(df.iloc[0])\n\n# Filter rows where Age is greater than 25\nprint(df[df['Age'] > 25])",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background:#1e1e1e;color:#d4d4d4;font-family:Consolas,monospace;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;line-height:1.6;\"><div style=\"color:#9cdcfe;\"#OUTPUT\n>>>> print(df['Name'])</div><pre style=\"margin:6px 0 18px;color:#d4d4d4;\">0    John\n1    Sara\n2     Tom\nName: Name, dtype: object</pre><div style=\"color:#9cdcfe;\">>>> print(df[['Name','Age']])</div><pre style=\"margin:6px 0 18px;color:#d4d4d4;\">   Name  Age\n0  John   25\n1  Sara   30\n2   Tom   22</pre><div style=\"color:#9cdcfe;\">>>> print(df.loc[0])</div><pre style=\"margin:6px 0 18px;color:#d4d4d4;\">Name     John\nAge        25\nCity    Delhi\nName: 0, dtype: object</pre><div style=\"color:#9cdcfe;\">>>> print(df.iloc[0])</div><pre style=\"margin:6px 0 18px;color:#d4d4d4;\">Name     John\nAge        25\nCity    Delhi\nName: 0, dtype: object</pre><div style=\"color:#9cdcfe;\">>>> print(df[df['Age'] > 25])</div><pre style=\"margin:6px 0;color:#d4d4d4;\">   Name  Age    City\n1  Sara   30  Mumbai</pre></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Grouping and Aggregating Data",
            "explanation": "When working with large datasets, we often want to analyze data by categories instead of individual rows. **groupby()** groups similar records together, and aggregation functions such as **mean(), sum(), count(), min(), max(), and agg()** perform calculations on each group.\n\nFor example, if you have employee data, you can group employees by department and calculate the average salary, total salary, minimum salary, maximum salary, or employee count for each department.\n\nGrouping and aggregation are widely used in data analysis, business intelligence, reporting, and machine learning to summarize large datasets into meaningful insights.",
            "example": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create sample employee data\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Employee': ['John', 'Sara', 'Tom', 'Emma', 'David', 'Lisa', 'Mike', 'Anna'],\n    'Department': ['Sales', 'Sales', 'Sales', 'Sales', 'HR', 'HR', 'IT', 'IT'],\n    'Salary': [45000, 50000, 60000, 65000, 40000, 50000, 70000, 80000]\n})\n\n# Create groups\ngroup = df.groupby('Department')\n\n# Group data\nprint(group)\n\n# Average salary\nprint(group['Salary'].mean())\n\n# Total salary\nprint(group['Salary'].sum())\n\n# Employee count\nprint(group['Salary'].count())\n\n# Minimum salary\nprint(group['Salary'].min())\n\n# Maximum salary\nprint(group['Salary'].max())\n\n# Multiple aggregations\nprint(group['Salary'].agg(['min', 'max', 'mean']))",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:'Segoe UI',sans-serif;background:#0f172a;color:white;padding:20px;border-radius:12px;\"><h2 style=\"text-align:center;color:#60a5fa;\">Common Aggregation Functions</h2><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:20px;font-size:14px;\"><thead><tr style=\"background:#2563eb;\"><th style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Function</th><th style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Use</th><th style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Example</th><th style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\"><b>groupby()</b></td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Groups similar records.</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">df.groupby('Department')</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;color:#4ade80;\">Groups created by Department</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\"><b>mean()</b></td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Average value.</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">group['Salary'].mean()</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;color:#4ade80;\">Sales → 55000</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\"><b>sum()</b></td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Total value.</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">group['Salary'].sum()</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;color:#4ade80;\">Sales → 220000</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\"><b>count()</b></td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Counts records.</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">group['Salary'].count()</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;color:#4ade80;\">Sales → 4</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\"><b>min()</b></td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Lowest value.</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">group['Salary'].min()</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;color:#4ade80;\">Sales → 45000</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\"><b>max()</b></td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Highest value.</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">group['Salary'].max()</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;color:#4ade80;\">Sales → 65000</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\"><b>agg()</b></td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">Multiple calculations.</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;\">group['Salary'].agg(['min','max','mean'])</td><td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #334155;color:#4ade80;\">Shows Min, Max & Mean</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Pandas Advanced Function",
            "explanation": "In real-world projects, data is often stored in multiple tables or files. Pandas provides three powerful functions to combine these datasets: **merge()**, **concat()**, and **join()**.\n\n### 1. merge()\nThe **merge()** function combines two DataFrames using a common column, just like an SQL JOIN. It is useful when both DataFrames share the same key, such as **Employee ID**, **Customer ID**, or **Order ID**.\n\n**Common Join Types in merge()**\n\n• **Inner Join** → Returns only matching rows from both DataFrames.\n• **Left Join** → Returns all rows from the left DataFrame and matching rows from the right.\n• **Right Join** → Returns all rows from the right DataFrame and matching rows from the left.\n• **Outer Join** → Returns all rows from both DataFrames and fills missing values with NaN.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\nemployees = pd.DataFrame({\n    'EmpID':[1,2,3],\n    'Name':['John','Sara','Tom']\n})\n\ndepartments = pd.DataFrame({\n    'EmpID':[1,2,3],\n    'Department':['Sales','HR','IT']\n})\n\nresult = pd.merge(employees, departments, on='EmpID', how='inner')\nprint(result)\n```\n\n### 2. concat()\nThe **concat()** function combines DataFrames by stacking them vertically (rows) or horizontally (columns). Unlike **merge()**, it does not require a common column.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\njan = pd.DataFrame({'Sales':[100,120]})\nfeb = pd.DataFrame({'Sales':[130,150]})\n\nresult = pd.concat([jan, feb])\nprint(result)\n```\n\n### 3. join()\nThe **join()** function combines DataFrames using their index. It is useful when both DataFrames already have matching indexes.\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\nleft = pd.DataFrame({'Name':['John','Sara']})\nright = pd.DataFrame({'City':['Delhi','Mumbai']})\n\nresult = left.join(right)\nprint(result)\n```\n\nAlthough all three functions combine data, they are used in different situations:\n\n• **merge()** → Combine using a common column.\n• **concat()** → Stack DataFrames together.\n• **join()** → Combine using the index.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/python/pa1.png]\n"
          }
        },
        {
