          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create two DataFrames using the following data.\n\nEmployee Data:\nEmpID | Name | Department | Salary\n1 | John | Sales | 50000\n2 | Sara | HR | 60000\n3 | Tom | Sales | 55000\n4 | Alice | IT | 70000\n\nManager Data:\nDepartment | Manager\nSales | David\nHR | Emma\nIT | Chris\n\nNow perform the following tasks:\n1. Display only the Name and Salary columns.\n2. Display employees whose Salary is greater than 55000.\n3. Find the average Salary for each Department.\n4. Merge both DataFrames using the Department column.\n5. Display the final merged DataFrame.",
            "starterCode": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create Employee DataFrame\n\n\n# Create Manager DataFrame\n\n\n# Display Name and Salary\n\n\n# Filter Salary > 55000\n\n\n# Average Salary by Department\n\n\n# Merge both DataFrames\n\n\n# Display the merged DataFrame\n",
            "solution": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Create Employee DataFrame\nemployees = pd.DataFrame({\n    'EmpID':[1,2,3,4],\n    'Name':['John','Sara','Tom','Alice'],\n    'Department':['Sales','HR','Sales','IT'],\n    'Salary':[50000,60000,55000,70000]\n})\n\n# Create Manager DataFrame\nmanagers = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Department':['Sales','HR','IT'],\n    'Manager':['David','Emma','Chris']\n})\n\n# Display Name and Salary\nprint(employees[['Name','Salary']])\n\n# Filter Salary > 55000\nprint(employees[employees['Salary'] > 55000])\n\n# Average Salary by Department\nprint(employees.groupby('Department')['Salary'].mean())\n\n# Merge both DataFrames\nresult = pd.merge(employees, managers, on='Department')\n\n# Display the merged DataFrame\nprint(result)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use column selection, filtering, groupby().mean(), and pd.merge() to complete the challenge."
          }
