      "id": "M001-env-async",
      "title": "Python Environments & Async Programming",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Virtual Environments: The Dependency Sandbox",
            "explanation": "As you continue learning Python, you'll create different programs for different purposes. Imagine you are working on two separate Python projects. One project needs certain tools or settings, while the other project needs different ones.\n`For Example:` Imagine you're building a calculator app and gaming app. Each project should have its own workspace so changes in one don't accidentally affect the other.\n\nIf both projects use the same Python setup, changes made for one project could accidentally affect the other. This can lead to unexpected errors and make projects harder to manage.\nTo solve this problem, Python provides **Virtual Environments**.\nA **Virtual Environment** is an isolated workspace for a Python project. It has its own packages and settings, separate from other projects on your computer.\nUsing virtual environments helps keep projects organized and prevents package conflicts.\n\n**Creating a Virtual Environment:**\n• `python -m venv my_env`\nThis command creates a new virtual environment named `my_env`.\n\n**Activating on Mac/Linux:**• `source my_env/bin/activate`\n**Activating on Windows:**• `my_env\\Scripts\\activate`\nOnce activated, any packages you install will be available only inside that virtual environment.\nVirtual environments are considered a best practice in Python development because they help keep projects clean, organized, and independent from each other.",
            "example": "# Notice the prefix in your terminal once activated:\n(my_env) user@laptop:~/AI_Project python script.py",
            "tip": "Never commit your virtual environment folder to GitHub! It contains thousands of files. Always add your venv folder name (e.g., `venv`, `env`, `.venv`) to your `.gitignore`."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Virtual Environment Review",
            "question": "Fill in the blanks using the correct virtual environment concepts.",
            "template": [
              "A virtual environment provides an isolated ____ for a Python project.",
              "Virtual environments help prevent package ____ between projects.",
              "The command `python -m ____ my_env` is used to create a virtual environment.",
