      "id": "M001-final-project",
      "title": "Practice Project: Student Information System",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Overview",
            "explanation": "Congratulations! You have completed the core Python concepts. Now it's time to combine everything you have learned into a small project.\n\nIn this project, you will build a simple Student Information System. The program will allow users to enter student details, display information, and interact with the program through a menu.\n\nThis project helps you practice variables, input/output, conditions, loops, functions, and basic data structures in a real-world scenario.\n\nBy the end of this project, you will understand how multiple Python concepts work together to build a complete application."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How the Project Works",
            "explanation": "The program will continuously display a menu and wait for the user's choice.\n\nThe user can:\n\n1. Add a student name.\n2. View all student names.\n3. Exit the application.\n\nThe program uses a list to store student names, a loop to keep the application running, and conditions to perform different actions based on the user's choice.\n\nThis is similar to how many real-world applications work by repeatedly accepting user input and performing actions."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Build a Student Information System that allows users to add student names, view all students, and exit the program.",
            "starterCode": "students = []\n\n# Your code here",
            "solution": "students = []\n\nwhile True:\n    print('\\n1. Add Student')\n    print('2. View Students')\n    print('3. Exit')\n\n    choice = input('Enter your choice: ')\n\n    if choice == '1':\n        name = input('Enter student name: ')\n        students.append(name)\n        print('Student added successfully!')\n\n    elif choice == '2':\n        print('\\nStudent List:')\n        for student in students:\n            print(student)\n\n    elif choice == '3':\n        print('Goodbye!')\n        break\n\n    else:\n        print('Invalid choice. Please try again.')",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use a list to store student names, a while loop to keep the program running, and if-elif-else statements to handle user choices."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M002-ml-comprehensive-lesson-1",
      "title": "Introduction to Machine Learning & Core Paradigms",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#0f766e",
        "accent": "#115e59"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Machine Learning?",
            "explanation": "Have you ever wondered how YouTube recommends videos, how Netflix suggests movies, or how Gmail detects spam emails? These applications are able to make smart decisions because they use **Machine Learning**.\nBefore understanding Machine Learning, let's look at how traditional programming works.\nSuppose we want to check whether a person is eligible to vote. We can write a rule like this:\n`if age >= 18: Allow Voting`\n\nHere, the programmer writes the rule, and the computer simply follows it. This is called **Traditional Programming**.\nHowever, some problems are much more difficult. For example, how do we write thousands of rules to identify spam emails, predict house prices, or recommend videos? Writing every rule manually would be almost impossible.\n\nThis is where **Machine Learning (ML)** helps.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in.png]\nInstead of giving the computer all the rules, we provide many examples and their correct answers. The computer studies these examples, finds patterns, and learns the rules by itself.\nThink about how a child learns to recognize cats. We don't explain every rule about ears, eyes, fur, and tail. Instead, we show many pictures and say, 'This is a cat.' After seeing enough examples, the child learns to recognize cats on their own.\n\nMachine Learning works in the same way. We give the computer data and correct answers, and it learns the rules automatically.\n**For example:**\nStudy Hours → Data and Pass/Fail → Answers\nAfter looking at many students' records, the computer learns the relationship between study hours and exam results. It can then predict whether a new student is likely to pass or fail.Refer the dig.",
            "example": "• Traditional Programming: Data + Rules ➔ Answers\n• Machine Learning: Data + Answers ➔ Rules"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Different Levels of AI",
            "explanation": "Artificial Intelligence is a broad field with several levels of technology. As you move from AI to Machine Learning and then to Deep Learning, the systems become more specialized in how they learn from data.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in1.png]\n\n**Artificial Intelligence (AI):**\nAI is the largest category. It includes any technology that enables computers to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as solving problems, making decisions, understanding language, or recognizing patterns.\n\n**Machine Learning (ML):**\nMachine Learning is a subset of AI. Instead of following only manually written rules, ML systems learn from data. By analyzing examples, they can make predictions or decisions without being explicitly programmed for every situation.\n\n**Deep Learning (DL):**\nDeep Learning is a subset of Machine Learning. It uses artificial neural networks with multiple layers to automatically learn complex patterns from large amounts of data. Deep Learning is widely used for image recognition, speech recognition, natural language processing, and generative AI."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Core Machine Learning Workflow",
            "explanation": "Building an operational machine learning solution requires an iterative pipeline process. The model calculation phase is actually a minor fraction of the engineering workload:\n\n1. **Data Collection:** Gathering underlying telemetry, database records, logs, or raw assets.\n2. **Data Preprocessing:** Scrubbing corrupt entries, interpolating missing matrices, and normalising distributions. (Takes up ~80% of an engineer's time).\n3. **Feature Engineering:** Filtering and refining which data columns hold structural weight. (e.g., omitting a patient's ID string when trying to predict high blood pressure risk).\n4. **Model Training:** Routing your refined inputs through a mathematical engine to optimize weight profiles.\n5. **Evaluation:** Verifying predictions against a hidden sandbox partition of data to measure real-world reliability.\n6. **Deployment:** Packing the final model parameter array into a responsive interface like a FastAPI web node.",
            "warning": "Garbage In, Garbage Out (GIGO). A rudimentary linear math algorithm working over hyper-clean, immaculate datasets will systematically out-perform a state-of-the-art transformer architecture trained on noisy data."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Supervised vs. Unsupervised Learning",
            "explanation": "There are three main types of Machine Learning: Supervised Learning, Unsupervised Learning, and Reinforcement Learning. The difference between them is how they learn from data. Let's explore each one below.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in2.png]\n`**Supervised Learning (Guided Training)**`\nThe algorithm acts like a student working with an answer key. It is given a table of features alongside the true desired target values.\n• *Goal:* Map structural input rows directly to discrete categories (Classification) or numerical continuums (Regression).\n• *Example:* Processing 50,000 real estate data lines containing home dimensions and past sale prices to predict pricing scales.\n\n`**Unsupervised Learning (Exploratory Mining)**`\nThe machine receives raw feature dimensions with no target outcome values provided. It must autonomously identify hidden groups, distributions, or anomalies.\n• *Goal:* Uncover implicit organization or clustering mechanics within hidden spaces.\n• *Example:* Partitioning user account transaction frequencies to automatically discover hidden segments of customer tiers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "reorder",
          "challenge": {
            "instruction": "Map each business scenario to its correct foundational ML paradigm category:",
            "correctOrder": [
              "Predicting expected electricity grid failure times using historical hardware degradation data profiles.",
              "Labeling incoming user profile photos as containing appropriate or inappropriate structural content based on past mod actions.",
              "Sifting across a company's database of receipts to bundle related internal expense patterns together without any preset categorizations.",
              "Isolating aberrant, anomalous sensor vibrations on a jet propulsion engine during operational stress testing loops."
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Reinforcement Learning (RL)",
            "explanation": "Have you ever trained a pet dog? When the dog performs the correct action, you give it a treat. When it does something wrong, you do not reward it. Over time, the dog learns which actions lead to rewards and starts repeating those actions.\n**Reinforcement Learning** works in a very similar way.\nIn Reinforcement Learning, we have an **Agent** and an **Environment**.\n• **Agent:** The learner or decision-maker.\n• **Environment:** The world in which the agent operates.\nThe agent starts by observing its environment and then takes an action.\nAfter taking the action, the environment responds and provides feedback.\nThis feedback can be:\n• **Reward (+):** Given when the agent makes a good decision.\n• **Penalty (-):** Given when the agent makes a bad decision.\n\nThe goal of the agent is simple: earn as many rewards as possible and avoid penalties.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/in3.png]\nLet's understand this with a simple example.\nImagine a robot is trying to find the exit of a maze.\n\n• The robot is the **Agent**.\n• The maze is the **Environment**.\n• Moving left, right, forward, or backward are **Actions**.\n• Reaching the exit gives a **Reward**.\n• Hitting a wall gives a **Penalty**.\n\nAt first, the robot does not know the correct path. It tries different actions, makes mistakes, receives feedback, and gradually learns the best route.\n\nAfter many attempts, it discovers the path that gives the highest reward.\nThis learning process is called **Reinforcement Learning**.\n\nReinforcement Learning is commonly used in:\n• Self-driving cars.\n• Game-playing AI.\n• Robots.\n• Recommendation systems.\n• Route optimization systems.\n\nThe key idea is that the agent learns by trying actions, receiving feedback, and improving its decisions over time."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "Architecting an ML Strategy",
          "description": "You are the newly hired AI Director for a multi-national logistics enterprise. The executive board hands you a raw archive containing 8 million unsorted shipping invoice records. They want a solution that isolates broken workflow patterns and flags unusual operational deviations, but there are no historical classifications or manual auditing logs available. How do you approach the problem?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Hire a temp data-entry workforce to spend 6 months reading all 8 million lines to create explicit binary classification labels from scratch.",
              "outcome": "Inefficient. This burns an immense budget and introduces severe human bias into raw data. You should let the algorithm discover structural groupings organically instead.",
              "score": 10
            },
            {
              "text": "Deploy a clean Unsupervised Anomaly Detection and Clustering framework directly over the structural raw invoice variables.",
              "outcome": "Brilliant Move! Since you lack targets, letting unsupervised learners detect cluster variances and outline mathematical deviations naturally isolates anomalies without manually labeled datasets.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "Build a simulation engine using Reinforcement Learning to reward a software agent every time it opens a correct invoice form.",
              "outcome": "Misapplied Engineering. Reinforcement structures track dynamic agents choosing path choices in active feedback loops. They are not meant for processing static, historical database spreadsheets.",
              "score": 0
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive_simulation",
          "title": "The Data Pipeline Architecture",
