    {
      "id": "M002-ml-lesson-3",
      "title": "Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#f59e0b",
        "accent": "#d97706"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Anatomy of Data: Features, Labels & Structure",
            "explanation": "Before an algorithm can learn, you must understand what you are feeding it.\n**Features (X):** The inputs or characteristics used to make a prediction (e.g., Square Footage, Number of Bedrooms, Zip Code).\n**Labels (Y):** The target output you are trying to predict (e.g., House Price). *Note: Unsupervised learning relies only on features and has no labels.*\n\n**Data Format Types:**\n• **Structured Data:** Highly organized tabular data that fits perfectly into a spreadsheet or SQL database (rows and columns).\n• **Unstructured Data:** Messy, raw data that doesn't fit into tables. This includes images, audio files, raw text documents, and videos. Deep Learning is usually required to process this.",
            "example": "In a dataset of patient medical records:\nFeatures = [Blood Pressure, Heart Rate, Cholesterol, Age]\nLabel = [Risk of Heart Disease (High/Low)]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Data Split: Training, Validation, and Test Data",
            "explanation": "A machine learning model learns from data, but simply training and testing it on the same dataset is not a good idea. The model may memorize the training examples instead of learning general patterns. This problem is called **Overfitting**. To measure how well a model performs on new, unseen data, the dataset is divided into three parts.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/dp1.png]\n**1. Training Data (≈70%)**\nThis is the largest portion of the dataset. The model repeatedly learns from this data by finding patterns and adjusting its internal parameters. Think of it as the model's textbook.\n\n**2. Validation Data (≈15%)**\nAfter learning from the training data, the model is evaluated on the validation data during training. This data is used to fine-tune hyperparameters, compare different models, and check whether the model is overfitting or improving. It acts like a practice test before the final exam.\n\n**3. Test Data (≈15%)**\nThe test data is kept completely separate and is never shown to the model during training. It is used only once after training is complete to measure the model's final performance. Since the model has never seen this data before, it provides an unbiased estimate of how well the model will perform in real-world situations."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Data Quality & AI Bias",
            "explanation": "Machine Learning models learn from data. If the data is incorrect, incomplete, or unfair, the model may also produce incorrect or unfair results.\nThis is why **Data Quality** is very important in Machine Learning.\n\nAnother important concept is **AI Bias**. Bias occurs when a model learns unfair patterns from the training data and produces unfair predictions.\n**Historical Bias:**\nImagine a company has mostly hired experienced employees in the past. If a model is trained only on those records, it may incorrectly assume that new candidates without experience are never suitable.\n**Sampling Bias:**\nImagine you want to understand students' opinions about a school, but you only ask students from one classroom. The results may not represent all students. Similarly, if a model is trained on limited or unbalanced data, it may perform poorly in real-world situations.\n\nTo build reliable AI systems, we must use high-quality data that is accurate, complete, and fairly represents different situations.\n\nAlways remember: A Machine Learning model can only be as good as the data used to train it.",
            "warning": "Never blindly trust your data. Always audit your datasets for demographic imbalances before training begins."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Data Cleaning & Missing Values",
            "explanation": "Real-world data is disastrously messy. Sensors break, humans leave forms blank, and databases crash.\nWhen a row is missing data (a 'NaN' or Null value), your algorithm will instantly crash. You have two choices:\n**1. Dropping (Deletion):**\n•If only 1% of your rows have missing values, simply delete those rows. \n•*Risk:* If you delete too much, you lose valuable training data.\n\n**2. Imputation (Guessing):**\nFill in the missing blanks using statistical estimates.\n• *Mean Imputation:* Replace missing salaries with the average salary.\n• *Mode Imputation:* Replace a missing 'City' with the most common city in the dataset.",
            "example": "# Python (pandas) example of filling missing ages with the median age:\ndf['Age'] = df['Age'].fillna(df['Age'].median())"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "A pandas DataFrame contains a column named 'Salary' with some missing (NaN) values. Write code to replace all missing salaries with the average salary of the column.",
            "starterCode": "import pandas as pd\n\n# DataFrame already exists as df\n\n# Write your code below\n",
            "solution": "df['Salary'] = df['Salary'].fillna(df['Salary'].mean())",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use fillna() along with mean() on the 'Salary' column."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Encoding: Words to Numbers",
            "explanation": "Algorithms cannot do math on the word 'Red'. We must encode categorical text into numbers.\n**1. Label Encoding (Ordinal Data):**\nUsed when there is a natural order. \n*Example:* Size [Small, Medium, Large] becomes [1, 2, 3]. The math understands that 3 is bigger than 1.\n**2. One-Hot Encoding (Nominal Data):**\nUsed when there is no natural order (e.g., Colors). If we made Red=1, Blue=2, Green=3, the algorithm would incorrectly assume Green is 'greater than' Red. \n*Solution:* We create a new binary column for every color. 'Is_Red', 'Is_Blue', 'Is_Green'. If a car is Red, its vector is [1, 0, 0].",
            "example": "Practical Example (Python)\n\n# Label Encoding\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n\nsizes = ['Small', 'Medium', 'Large', 'Small']\n\nencoder = LabelEncoder()\nencoded_sizes = encoder.fit_transform(sizes)\n\nprint(encoded_sizes)\n\nOutput:\n[2 1 0 2]\n\n-----------------------------------\n\n# One-Hot Encoding\nimport pandas as pd\n\ncolors = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Color': ['Red', 'Blue', 'Green', 'Red']\n})\n\nencoded_colors = pd.get_dummies(colors['Color'])\n\nprint(encoded_colors)\n\nOutput:\n\n   Blue  Green  Red\n0     0      0    1\n1     1      0    0\n2     0      1    0\n3     0      0    1\n\nReal-World Usage:\n• Label Encoding → Education Level (High School < Bachelor < Master < PhD)\n• One-Hot Encoding → Colors, Country Names, Product Categories, Departments",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 20px; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;\"><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background: #ffffff; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.04);\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #6366f1, #8b5cf6); padding: 12px 15px; color: white; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; text-align: center;\">Label Encoding (Ordered)</div><div style=\"padding: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; align-items: center;\"><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; width: 100%; max-width: 180px; background: #f8fafc; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;\"><span style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #475569; font-weight: 500;\">Small</span><span style=\"color: #cbd5e1;\">➔</span><span style=\"font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #6366f1; font-family: monospace;\">1</span></div><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; width: 100%; max-width: 180px; background: #f8fafc; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;\"><span style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #475569; font-weight: 500;\">Medium</span><span style=\"color: #cbd5e1;\">➔</span><span style=\"font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #6366f1; font-family: monospace;\">2</span></div><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; width: 100%; max-width: 180px; background: #f8fafc; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;\"><span style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #475569; font-weight: 500;\">Large</span><span style=\"color: #cbd5e1;\">➔</span><span style=\"font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #6366f1; font-family: monospace;\">3</span></div><div style=\"margin-top: 10px; font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; text-align: center;\"><strong style=\"color: #4f46e5;\">Math Logic:</strong> 1 &lt; 2 &lt; 3 (Size naturally scales)</div></div></div><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 280px; background: #ffffff; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.04);\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #10b981, #059669); padding: 12px 15px; color: white; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; text-align: center;\">One-Hot Encoding (Unordered)</div><div style=\"padding: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 15px; align-items: center;\"><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px;\"><div style=\"background: #fee2e2; color: #b91c1c; border: 1px solid #fecaca; padding: 6px 12px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600;\">\"Red\"</div><span style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-size: 18px;\">➔</span></div><div style=\"display: flex; gap: 8px; text-align: center;\"><div style=\"background: #f0fdf4; border: 2px solid #10b981; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; width: 70px;\"><div style=\"font-size: 10px; color: #166534; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid #bbf7d0; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Is_Red</div><div style=\"font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; color: #15803d; font-family: monospace;\">1</div></div><div style=\"background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; width: 70px;\"><div style=\"font-size: 10px; color: #64748b; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Is_Blue</div><div style=\"font-size: 18px; font-weight: 600; color: #94a3b8; font-family: monospace;\">0</div></div><div style=\"background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 8px; padding: 8px; width: 70px;\"><div style=\"font-size: 10px; color: #64748b; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid #e2e8f0; padding-bottom: 4px;\">Is_Green</div><div style=\"font-size: 18px; font-weight: 600; color: #94a3b8; font-family: monospace;\">0</div></div></div><div style=\"margin-top: 5px; font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; text-align: center;\"><strong style=\"color: #059669;\">Math Logic:</strong> Vector <code style=\"background: #f1f5f9; padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: 4px; color: #334155;\">[1, 0, 0]</code>. (No color is greater)</div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "A dataset contains an ordinal feature called 'Size' with values Low, Medium, and High. Use Label Encoding to convert them into numerical values.",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n\nsizes = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\n\n# Write your code below\n",
            "solution": "from sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\n\nsizes = ['Low', 'Medium', 'High']\n\nencoder = LabelEncoder()\nencoded_sizes = encoder.fit_transform(sizes)\n\nprint(encoded_sizes)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Create a LabelEncoder object and use fit_transform()."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scaling: Leveling the Playing Field",
            "explanation": "Imagine predicting a house price using 'Number of Bedrooms' (range: 1-5) and 'Square Footage' (range: 500-5000).\nLarger Square Footage values can dominate the model, reducing the impact of Bedrooms.!\n**Scaling fixes this by squashing all numbers into the same range:**\n• **Normalization (Min-Max):** Squashes all values to exist strictly between 0.0 and 1.0.\n• **Standardization (Z-Score):** Centers the data around a mean of 0, measuring how many standard deviations a value is from the average. Handles outliers better.",
            "example": "After Min-Max scaling:\nBedrooms: 3 ➔ 0.6\nSq Footage: 2500 ➔ 0.5\nNow the algorithm can compare them equally!",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 24px; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; background: #ffffff; padding: 24px; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.04); max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto;\"><div style=\"border-bottom: 2px solid #f1f5f9; padding-bottom: 12px;\"><h3 style=\"margin: 0; font-size: 16px; font-weight: 700; color: #1e293b; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px;\">Feature Scaling: Line Visualization</h3><p style=\"margin: 4px 0 0 0; font-size: 12px; color: #64748b;\">Comparing how data points are compressed or shifted on a 1D coordinate axis.</p></div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;\"><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; color: #2563eb;\">Normalization (Min-Max)</span><span style=\"font-size: 11px; background: #eff6ff; color: #1e40af; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 12px; font-weight: 500;\">Bounded: [0.0, 1.0]</span></div><div style=\"position: relative; height: 50px; background: #f8fafc; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #f1f5f9; display: flex; align-items: center; padding: 0 20px;\"><div style=\"position: absolute; left: 20px; right: 20px; height: 3px; background: #cbd5e1; border-radius: 2px;\"></div><div style=\"position: absolute; left: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; transform: translateX(-50%);\"><div style=\"width: 10px; height: 10px; background: #2563eb; border-radius: 50%; box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(37,99,235,0.2);\"></div><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; color: #1e40af; margin-top: 4px; font-family: monospace;\">0.0</span></div><div style=\"position: absolute; left: 50%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; transform: translateX(-50%);\"><div style=\"width: 10px; height: 10px; background: #3b82f6; border-radius: 50%;\"></div><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600; color: #3b82f6; margin-top: 4px; font-family: monospace;\">0.5</span></div><div style=\"position: absolute; right: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; transform: translateX(50%);\"><div style=\"width: 10px; height: 10px; background: #2563eb; border-radius: 50%; box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(37,99,235,0.2);\"></div><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; color: #1e40af; margin-top: 4px; font-family: monospace;\">1.0</span></div></div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; color: #64748b; line-height: 1.4;\">All values are squished into the exact region between <strong>0.0</strong> and <strong>1.0</strong>. Shape of distribution is preserved, but outliers can compress inliers.</div></div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;\"><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center;\"><span style=\"font-size: 13px; font-weight: 600; color: #059669;\">Standardization (Z-Score)</span><span style=\"font-size: 11px; background: #ecfdf5; color: #065f46; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 12px; font-weight: 500;\">Unbounded: Mean = 0</span></div><div style=\"position: relative; height: 50px; background: #f8fafc; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #f1f5f9; display: flex; align-items: center; padding: 0 20px;\"><div style=\"position: absolute; left: 20px; right: 20px; height: 3px; background: #cbd5e1; border-radius: 2px;\"></div><div style=\"position: absolute; left: 25%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; transform: translateX(-50%);\"><div style=\"width: 10px; height: 10px; background: #10b981; border-radius: 50%;\"></div><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600; color: #047857; margin-top: 4px; font-family: monospace;\">-1.0</span></div><div style=\"position: absolute; left: 50%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; transform: translateX(-50%);\"><div style=\"width: 12px; height: 12px; background: #059669; border-radius: 50%; box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(5,150,105,0.2);\"></div><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; color: #065f46; margin-top: 4px; font-family: monospace;\">0.0 (Mean)</span></div><div style=\"position: absolute; left: 75%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; transform: translateX(-50%);\"><div style=\"width: 10px; height: 10px; background: #10b981; border-radius: 50%;\"></div><span style=\"font-size: 11px; font-weight: 600; color: #047857; margin-top: 4px; font-family: monospace;\">+1.0</span></div></div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; color: #64748b; line-height: 1.4;\">Centers the dataset average exactly at <strong>0.0</strong>. The values scale outwards by standard deviation units <code style=\"background: #f1f5f9; padding: 1px 3px; border-radius: 3px;\">&sigma;</code> (not bound to 0-1). Highly resilient to outliers!</div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Feature Engineering & Selection",
            "explanation": "**Feature Engineering**is the art of creating brand new, highly predictive columns out of existing data using human intuition.\n• *Example:* You have 'Date_of_Purchase'. The algorithm just sees a random string. You engineer a new column called 'Is_Weekend' (1 or 0). Suddenly, the model realizes sales spike on weekends!\n\n**Feature Selection** is the process of ruthlessly deleting useless columns to make your model faster and less confused.\n• *Example:* Dropping 'Customer_ID' or 'First_Name'. These have zero mathematical predictive power for whether someone will default on a loan. Keeping them adds 'noise'.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 20px; font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;\"><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background: #ffffff; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.04);\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #0ea5e9, #0284c7); padding: 12px 15px; color: white; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; text-align: center;\">Feature Engineering 🛠️</div><div style=\"padding: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; align-items: center;\"><div style=\"background: #f8fafc; border: 1px dashed #cbd5e1; padding: 10px 15px; border-radius: 8px; font-family: monospace; font-size: 13px; color: #64748b; width: 100%; max-width: 180px; text-align: center;\">Date: '2023-10-14'</div><div style=\"color: #0ea5e9; font-size: 20px; font-weight: bold;\">↓</div><div style=\"background: #f0f9ff; border: 2px solid #0ea5e9; padding: 10px 15px; border-radius: 8px; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; font-weight: 700; color: #0369a1; width: 100%; max-width: 180px; text-align: center; box-shadow: 0 2px 4px rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.1);\">Is_Weekend: <span style=\"background: #0ea5e9; color: white; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px;\">1</span></div><div style=\"margin-top: 5px; font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; text-align: center;\"><strong style=\"color: #0284c7;\">Creation:</strong> Extracting hidden, predictive signals.</div></div></div><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background: #ffffff; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(0,0,0,0.04);\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #f43f5e, #e11d48); padding: 12px 15px; color: white; font-weight: 600; font-size: 14px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.5px; text-align: center;\">Feature Selection ✂️</div><div style=\"padding: 20px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; align-items: center;\"><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; width: 100%; max-width: 200px; background: #f0fdf4; border: 1px solid #bbf7d0; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px;\"><span style=\"font-family: monospace; font-size: 13px; color: #166534; font-weight: 600;\">Income</span><span style=\"font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; color: #15803d;\">✓ KEEP</span></div><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; width: 100%; max-width: 200px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px dashed #fecdd3; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px; opacity: 0.8;\"><span style=\"font-family: monospace; font-size: 13px; color: #9f1239; text-decoration: line-through;\">Customer_ID</span><span style=\"font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; color: #e11d48;\">✗ DROP</span></div><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; width: 100%; max-width: 200px; background: #fff1f2; border: 1px dashed #fecdd3; padding: 8px 12px; border-radius: 6px; opacity: 0.8;\"><span style=\"font-family: monospace; font-size: 13px; color: #9f1239; text-decoration: line-through;\">First_Name</span><span style=\"font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; color: #e11d48;\">✗ DROP</span></div><div style=\"margin-top: 5px; font-size: 12px; color: #64748b; text-align: center;\"><strong style=\"color: #e11d48;\">Deletion:</strong> Removing noise to reduce model confusion.</div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Preprocessing Architect",
          "description": "You are preparing a dataset to predict employee turnover. You have a column called 'Employee_ID' (e.g., EMP-4092) and a column called 'Department' (Sales, HR, IT). How should you preprocess these?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Label Encode the IDs (1, 2, 3...) and Label Encode the Departments.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. IDs have no predictive value and should be dropped. Departments have no numerical ranking, so Label Encoding will confuse the model.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Keep the ID as a string, and One-Hot Encode the Departments.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. The algorithm will crash if you leave the ID as a string. Algorithms only accept numbers.",
              "score": 20
            },
            {
              "text": "Drop the Employee_ID column completely. One-Hot Encode the Department column.",
              "outcome": "Correct! You delete the noisy ID column, and safely convert the unordered Department categories into binary 1s and 0s.",
              "score": 100
            }
          ]
        },
        {
