    {
      "id": "M002-regression-algorithms",
      "title": "Regression Algorithms",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#2563eb",
        "accent": "#1d4ed8"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Regression?",
            "explanation": "Great job! Now that you understand the basic concepts of Machine Learning, it's time to learn our first Machine Learning algorithm: **Regression**.\n Lets Understand with simple examle imagine Rahul owns an electronics store. Every month, he records information such as the number of products sold, advertising expenses, and total revenue.\nNow Rahul wants to estimate next month's revenue based on previous sales data. He is not interested in categories such as 'High Sales' or 'Low Sales'. Instead, he wants an exact value such as ₹5,00,000 or ₹7,50,000.\nThis type of problem is called **Regression**.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/re1.png]\n**Regression** is a Machine Learning technique used to predict **continuous numerical values**. Continuous values can take many possible values, including decimals.\n\n`How It Works:`\n• Collect historical data containing input features and known outputs.\n• Find relationships between the inputs and the target value.\n• Learn a mathematical equation that best fits the data.\n• Use the learned equation to predict values for new unseen data.\n\n`Common Applications:` House Price Prediction, Sales Forecasting, Revenue Estimation, Demand Forecasting, and Delivery Time Prediction."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Regression Terminology Essentials",
            "explanation": "\nBefore building a Regression model, let's understand a few important terms that are commonly used in Regression. These terms will help you understand how the model makes predictions and measures its accuracy.\n`β₀ (Intercept)`The starting value of y when x = 0.\n`β₁ (Slope / Coefficient)`Shows how much y changes when x increases by 1 unit.\n• Formula: β₁ = Change in y / Change in x\n`Residual (Error)`Difference between the actual and predicted value.\n• Formula: Error = y - ŷ\n`Line of Best Fit`The straight line that minimizes the overall prediction error.\n• Formula: ŷ = β₀ + β₁x\n`Target Variable`The value the model tries to predict.\n• Ex.: y (e.g Hoise price,stock price,loan price)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Simple Linear Regression",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand the basic concepts and terminology of Regression, let's learn the most important Regression algorithm: **Simple Linear Regression**.\nImagine a real-estate company wants to estimate the price of a house based on its size. They have records of many houses, including their square footage and selling prices.\nWhen a customer asked, **How much would a 2,000 sq ft house cost?**, the real-estate company didn't want to rely on guesswork. By analyzing previous house sales, they learned that larger houses generally sold for higher prices. Using Simple Linear Regression, they drew a Line of Best Fit that captured this relationship and used it to predict the price of new houses.\n**Simple Linear Regression** learns the relationship between one input feature (**x**) and one target value (**y**) using a straight line called the **Line of Best Fit**.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/re2.png]\n\n**Formula:**\n**y = β₀ + β₁x**\nWhere:\n• `y` = Predicted Value,`x` = Input Feature,`β₀` = Intercept,`β₁` = Slope or Weight\n\nPredictions are not always perfect. The difference between the actual value and predicted value is called a **Residual (Error)**. During training, the model tries to minimize these errors.\n**Applications:** House Pricing, Revenue Forecasting, Demand Prediction, Fuel Consumption Estimation, and Delivery Time Prediction.",
            "example": "Training Data: 1000 sq ft → $200,000, 2000 sq ft → $300,000.\n\nThe model learns β₀ = 100,000 and β₁ = 100, giving the equation y = 100,000 + 100x.\n\nFor a 1500 sq ft house: y = 100,000 + (100 × 1500) = $250,000.\n\nActual Price = $260,000.\n\nResidual (Error) = Actual - Predicted = $260,000 - $250,000 = $10,000."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multiple Linear Regression",
            "explanation": "Let's think about this. If I show you two houses with the exact same size, can you confidently tell me they have the same price? Of course not! One might be in a luxury area, have extra bedrooms, a garage, and excellent schools nearby. The other might not. That's why, in the real world, house prices depend on many factors. To make better predictions, Machine Learning models often learn from multiple features rather than just one.\n`**Multiple Linear Regression**` extends Simple Linear Regression by using several input features to predict one target value.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/re3.png]\n**Formula:**\n **`y = β₀ + β₁x₁ + β₂x₂ + ... + βₙxₙ`**\nEach feature receives a weight based on its importance. Features that have a stronger impact on the outcome receive larger weights.\n`**Example Features:**`House Size,Bedrooms,Bathrooms,Property Age,Location\nDuring training, the model learns which factors matter most. For example, Location may have a greater impact on price than Wall Color.\n`**Applications:**` Loan Risk Analysis, Customer Spending Prediction, Healthcare Outcome Prediction, Revenue Forecasting, and Infrastructure Cost Estimation.",
            "example": "Predicting house prices using size, bedrooms, bathrooms, and location."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Simple Linear Regression Review",
            "question": "Fill in the blanks using the correct Regression concepts.",
            "template": [
              "Simple Linear Regression uses a straight line called the Line of Best ____.",
              "β₀ is called the ____.",
              "The difference between the actual value and predicted value is called a ____."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Fit",
              "Residual",
              "Weight",
              "Intercept",
              "Feature"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "Fit",
              "blank_2": "Intercept",
              "blank_3": "Residual"
            },
            "explanation": "Simple Linear Regression uses a Line of Best Fit. β₀ is the Intercept, and the difference between the actual and predicted value is called a Residual.",
            "hint": "Think about the key terms used in Simple Linear Regression."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "Model Building Steps & Python Libraries",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "1. Import Libraries",
                "code": "import pandas as pd\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\nfrom sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error"
              },
              {
                "label": "2. Prepare Data",
                "code": "X = df[['sq_ft', 'bedrooms']] # Features\ny = df['price'] # Target\n\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.2)"
              },
              {
                "label": "3. Instantiate & Train",
                "code": "model = LinearRegression() # Create the blueprint\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train) # The algorithm learns the math here"
              },
              {
                "label": "4. Predict & Evaluate",
                "code": "predictions = model.predict(X_test)\nerror = mean_squared_error(y_test, predictions)\nprint(f\"MSE: {error}\")"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Build your first Simple Linear Regression model to predict house prices based on house size.",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\n# House Size (sq ft)\nX = [[1000], [1500], [2000], [2500], [3000]]\n\n# House Price\ny = [200000, 300000, 400000, 500000, 600000]\n\n# Your code here\n",
            "solution": "from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression\n\n# House Size (sq ft)\nX = [[1000], [1500], [2000], [2500], [3000]]\n\n# House Price\ny = [200000, 300000, 400000, 500000, 600000]\n\nmodel = LinearRegression()\nmodel.fit(X, y)\n\npredicted_price = model.predict([[2200]])\n\nprint('Predicted House Price:', predicted_price[0])",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Create a LinearRegression model, train it using fit(), and predict a price using predict()."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "Real-World Use Cases",
          "description": "You are consulting for various enterprises. Match the business problem to the correct implementation of a Regression model.",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "An e-commerce site wants to know exactly how much a user will spend in their lifetime based on their first 3 purchases.",
              "outcome": "Correct. Customer Lifetime Value (CLV) is a classic regression problem predicting a continuous dollar amount.",
