    {
      "id": "M002-ml-lesson-6-classification",
      "title": "Classification Algorithms",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Classification?",
            "explanation": "Great job! So far, we have learned about Regression, which is used to predict numerical values such as house prices or sales revenue. Now in this lesson let's learn another important Machine Learning technique called **Classification**.\n\nImagine you work at a post office sorting letters. You have two buckets: one for 'Junk Mail' and another for 'Important Bills'. For each letter, you decide which bucket it belongs to.\nThis is exactly how **Classification** works.\n\n**Classification** is a Machine Learning technique used to predict categories or labels. Instead of predicting a number, the model predicts which group an item belongs to.\n\n**Types of Classification:**\n**Binary Classification:** Only 2 possible categories.\n•Examples: Spam or Not Spam, Pass or Fail, Sick or Healthy.\n **Multi-Class Classification:** More than 2 categories.\n• Examples: Dog, Cat, or Bird.\n\n**Key Idea:** If you need to place data into predefined groups, categories, or labels, then Classification is the right Machine Learning technique to use."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Logistic Regression?",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand Classification, let's learn one of the most popular classification algorithms: **Logistic Regression**.\nImagine a teacher wants to predict whether a student will pass or fail an exam based on their study habits and previous performance.\nInstead of directly saying 'Pass' or 'Fail', the model first calculates the probability of passing.For example:• Probability = 0.80 (80%) → Pass• Probability = 0.30 (30%) → Fail.\n\nThis is how **Logistic Regression** works.\nEven though the name contains the word 'Regression', Logistic Regression is actually used for **Classification** problems.\n**Sigmoid Function:**\nThe Sigmoid Function converts any input value into a probability between 0 and 1.This probability helps the model decide which class the data belongs to.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/cl1.png]\n**Sigmoid Formula:**`σ(x) = 1 / (1 + e^(-x))`\n**Use Cases:**Predicting if a customer will click an ad (Yes/No), determining whether an email is spam or not, or identifying if a bank transaction is fraudulent.\n**Pros:**\n• Fast and easy to train.\n• Easy to understand and interpret.\n• Works well for binary classification problems.\n**Cons:**\n• Struggles with complex relationships in data.\n• Performance depends on selecting good input features."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How Logistic Regression Works Mathematically",
            "explanation": "Logistic Regression predicts the probability that a data point belongs to a class.\nLets understand how it works mathematically.\n**How It Works:**\n• Collect training data with input features and known outcomes.\n• Calculate a score using the inputs: `z = β₀ + β₁x₁ + β₂x₂ + ...`\n• Pass the score through the Sigmoid Function to convert it into a probability between 0 and 1.\n• Example: If z = 2, then σ(2) ≈ 0.88, meaning an 88% probability of Class 1.\n• Compare the probability with a threshold (usually 0.5).\n• If probability ≥ 0.5 → Class 1, otherwise → Class 0.\n• During training, the model updates its weights to reduce errors and improve predictions.\nBut hey, don't worry! You don't need to do all these steps manually. You can use libraries that handle them for you. Check out the snippet below.",
            "example": "#Practical Implementation \nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\nmodel = LogisticRegression()\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\n\nprediction = model.predict([[5, 3]])"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
