    {
      "id": "M002-ml-lesson-7-clustering",
      "title": "Clustering & Unsupervised Learning",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#10b981",
        "accent": "#059669"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Clustering?",
            "explanation": "Great job! So far, we have learned algorithms that make predictions using known answers. Now let's learn about **Clustering**, one of the most popular Unsupervised Learning techniques.\n\nImagine a news website automatically grouping articles into categories such as Sports, Politics, Business, and Technology. Nobody manually labels the articles—the system finds similar articles and groups them together.\n\n**Clustering** works in a similar way. It automatically groups similar data points together without using predefined labels or categories.\n\n**Use Cases:** Customer Segmentation, Grouping Similar News Articles, and Discovering Shopping Behavior Patterns."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Important Clustering Terminologies",
            "explanation": "Before learning clustering algorithms such as K-Means, it is important to understand a few common terms.\n **Cluster:**  cluster is a group of similar data points. The goal of clustering is to place similar items into the same group.\n **Centroid:** The center point of a cluster. In K-Means, the centroid represents the average position of all data points in that cluster.\n **Distance Metric:** A mathematical method used to measure how similar or different two data points are.\n`Common distance metrics:`\n• Euclidean Distance – Straight-line distance between points.\n• Manhattan Distance – Distance measured by moving horizontally and vertically.\n• Cosine Similarity – Measures similarity based on direction rather than actual distance.\n **Inertia (Within-Cluster Sum of Squares):** A measure of how closely data points are grouped around their centroid. Lower inertia generally indicates tighter clusters. resul\n **Similarity:** A measure of how closely related two data points are. Higher similarity usually means the points should belong to the same cluster.\nUnderstanding these terms makes it easier to learn clustering algorithms and interpret their results."
          },
          "example": "Imagine a customer segmentation project:\n\n• Data Points → Individual customers\n• Features → Age, Income, Spending Score\n• Distance Metric → Measures customer similarity\n• Centroid → Center of each customer group\n• Cluster → Customers with similar behavior\n• Scaling → Ensures income does not dominate age when creating clusters",
          "tip": "Remember: Clustering groups similar data points together using distance measurements, centroids, and feature values."
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "K-Means Clustering",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand Clustering, let's learn the most popular clustering algorithm: **K-Means Clustering**.\nSuppose a pizza company wants to open 3 delivery centers in a city. They start by choosing locations randomly. Then, they look at where their customers live and move each center closer to the middle of the customers it serves.\nLets Discuss another real use case example imagine a shopping mall wants to group its customers based on their shopping behavior. Instead of manually creating groups, the mall uses K-Means to automatically find customers with similar behavior and place them into the same group.\n**K-Means** is the most popular clustering algorithm.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/clu1.png]\n**How it works:**\n• 1. You tell the algorithm how many groups you want (This is the 'K'). Let's say K=3.\n• 2. The AI drops 3 random 'Centroids' (center points) onto the data.\n• 3. Every data point looks around and assigns itself to the closest Centroid.\n• 4. The Centroid then moves to the exact geographical middle of its new group.\n• 5. It repeats this until the Centroids stop moving.\n**Drawback:** You have to guess the number 'K' before you start. If you choose K=2 but the data actually has 5 natural groups, the algorithm will perform poorly.",
            "example": "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n# Age, Spending Score\nX = [[22, 90],[25, 85]]\nmodel = KMeans(n_clusters=2, random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X)\n\nprint(model.labels_)\nprint(model.cluster_centers_)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "K-Means Basics",
            "question": "Fill in the blanks using the correct terms.",
            "template": [
              "K-Means is a __________ algorithm.",
              "It groups similar data points into __________.",
              "Each cluster has a center called a __________."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Clustering",
              "Clusters",
              "Centroid",
              "Classification",
              "Label"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank1": "Clustering",
              "blank2": "Clusters",
              "blank3": "Centroid"
            },
            "explanation": "K-Means is a clustering algorithm that groups similar data points into clusters. The center of each cluster is called a centroid.",
            "hint": "Think: Algorithm → Group → Center."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hierarchical Clustering (The Family Tree)",
            "explanation": "Great job! We have learned K-Means Clustering, where we must choose the number of groups (K) before starting. Now let's learn another clustering technique called **Hierarchical Clustering**\n Imagine 10 strangers in a room. The two people standing closest to each other shake hands and form a pair. Then, the next closest pair forms. Eventually, smaller groups merge into larger groups, until all 10 people are holding hands in one giant circle.\n**Hierarchical Clustering** builds a tree of data from the bottom up.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/clu2.png]\n**How it works:**\nUnlike K-Means, you do NOT need to guess 'K' in advance! The algorithm calculates the distance between every single data point and starts merging them into clusters.\nIt produces a beautiful diagram called a **Dendrogram** (which looks like a family tree or a sports tournament bracket). You can look at the Dendrogram and slice it wherever you want to create your groups.\n**Drawback:** It requires an immense amount of math to calculate the distance between every single point, making it very slow for massive datasets.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #ffffff; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; box-shadow: 0 10px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif; overflow: hidden; max-width: 750px; margin: 0 auto;\"><div style=\"background: linear-gradient(135deg, #8b5cf6, #6d28d9); padding: 16px; color: white; text-align: center; font-weight: bold; font-size: 16px; letter-spacing: 0.5px; text-transform: uppercase;\">Hierarchical Clustering: The Dendrogram 🌳</div><div style=\"padding: 20px; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 20px; align-items: stretch;\"><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 280px; display: flex; flex-direction: column;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 700; color: #475569; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 10px;\">1. Nested Grouping</div><div style=\"background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 12px; padding: 15px; flex-grow: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 200\" style=\"width: 100%; height: auto;\"><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"100\" rx=\"90\" ry=\"85\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"65\" cy=\"95\" rx=\"45\" ry=\"75\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-dasharray=\"4\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"60\" cy=\"135\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#eff6ff\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"70\" cy=\"55\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"20\" fill=\"#eff6ff\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"155\" cy=\"105\" rx=\"25\" ry=\"25\" fill=\"#f0fdf4\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"135\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A</text><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"135\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">B</text><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"55\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">C</text><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"55\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">D</text><circle cx=\"145\" cy=\"105\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">E</text><circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"105\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#1e293b\"/><text x=\"165\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\">F</text></svg></div></div><div style=\"flex: 1; min-width: 280px; display: flex; flex-direction: column;\"><div style=\"text-align: center; font-size: 13px; font-weight: 700; color: #475569; text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 10px;\">2. The Dendrogram Tree</div><div style=\"background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 12px; padding: 15px; flex-grow: 1; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 260 200\" style=\"width: 100%; height: auto;\"><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"9\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#64748b\" transform=\"rotate(-90 10 85)\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Distance (Height)</text><path d=\"M 30 160 L 30 130 L 70 130 L 70 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M 110 160 L 110 110 L 150 110 L 150 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b82f6\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M 50 130 L 50 60 L 130 60 L 130 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M 190 160 L 190 90 L 230 90 L 230 160\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M 90 60 L 90 20 L 210 20 L 210 90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ef4444\" text-anchor=\"end\">Slice here = 2 Clusters</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">A</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">B</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">C</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">D</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">E</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"175\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#1e293b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">F</text></svg></div></div></div><div style=\"background: #fff1f2; border-top: 1px solid #fecdd3; padding: 12px 20px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px;\"><div style=\"font-size: 20px;\">🐢</div><div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #9f1239; line-height: 1.4;\"><strong>Drawback: Computational Complexity.</strong> To build this tree, the algorithm must calculate the distance between <em>every single point</em> and <em>every other point</em> continuously. For 100,000 customers, it requires billions of calculations, making it painfully slow for massive datasets.</div></div></div>",
            "example": "from sklearn.cluster import AgglomerativeClustering\n\n# Customer Ages\nX = [[22], [24], [26], [50], [53]]\n\nmodel = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=2)\nclusters = model.fit_predict(X)\n\nprint(clusters)\n# Output: [0, 0, 0, 1, 1]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "Customer Segmentation in Python",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "1. Import",
                "code": "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nimport pandas as pd"
              },
              {
                "label": "2. The Business Problem",
                "text": "We have a list of customers with their 'Age' and 'Annual Income'. We want to find 4 distinct marketing groups, but we have no labels."
              },
