        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "A shopping mall wants to group customers based on their Age and Spending Score. Use K-Means Clustering to find 3 customer segments.",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n\n# Age, Spending Score\nX = [\n    [22, 90],\n    [25, 85],\n    [27, 88],\n    [45, 30],\n    [50, 25],\n    [48, 35],\n    [35, 60],\n    [38, 55],\n    [40, 58]\n]\n\n# Create K-Means model with 3 clusters\n# Train the model\n# Print customer clusters",
            "solution": "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n\n# Age, Spending Score\nX = [\n    [22, 90],\n    [25, 85],\n    [27, 88],\n    [45, 30],\n    [50, 25],\n    [48, 35],\n    [35, 60],\n    [38, 55],\n    [40, 58]\n]\n\nmodel = KMeans(n_clusters=3, random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X)\n\nprint('Customer Clusters:')\nprint(model.labels_)\n\nprint('\\nCluster Centers:')\nprint(model.cluster_centers_)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use KMeans(n_clusters=3) and fit() the dataset.",
            "explanation": "The mall does not know customer categories beforehand. K-Means automatically discovers groups by placing centroids, assigning customers to the nearest centroid, and updating centroids until they stabilize.\n\nExpected Groups:\n• Cluster 1 → Young High Spenders\n• Cluster 2 → Older Low Spenders\n• Cluster 3 → Medium Age Medium Spenders"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dimensionality Reduction & PCA (The Shadow)",
            "explanation": "Now let's learn about **PCA (Principal Component Analysis)**, one of the most popular Dimensionality Reduction techniques in Machine Learning. PCA helps simplify large datasets while preserving most of the useful information.\nImagine holding a complex 3D object, like a coffee mug. If you shine a flashlight on it, it casts a flat, 2D shadow on the wall. You lost the '3D depth', but the shadow still perfectly captures the *shape* and *essence* of the mug. \nIn Machine Learning, datasets often have 100 or even 1,000 features (columns). Processing 1,000 columns takes massive computing power, and humans cannot visualize anything past 3 dimensions.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/clu3.png]\n**PCA (Principal Component Analysis)**is an algorithm that performs **Dimensionality Reduction**.\nIt mathematically squashes a 100-column dataset down to just 2 or 3 columns, while keeping 95% of the original mathematical 'essence' intact. It throws away the noise and keeps the most important patterns.\n\n**Key Idea:** PCA is like writing a 1-page summary of a 500-page book. You lose the minor details, but you save massive amounts of time reading it.",
            "example": "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\n# Student Scores\nX = [[90,88,85,89,87],[75,72,80,70,73],[95,94,92,96,93]]\n\n# Reduce 5 features to 2\npca = PCA(n_components=2)\nX_reduced = pca.fit_transform(X)\n\nprint(X_reduced)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Reduce a dataset from 4 features to 2 features using PCA.",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = [[90,88,85,89],[75,72,80,70],[95,94,92,96]]\n\n# Create PCA model\n# Transform the data",
            "solution": "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n\nX = [[90,88,85,89],[75,72,80,70],[95,94,92,96]]\n\npca = PCA(n_components=2)\nX_reduced = pca.fit_transform(X)\nprint(X_reduced)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use n_components=2.",
            "explanation": "PCA compresses the original features into two principal components."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Evaluating Clustering Quality (WCSS & Silhouette Score)",
            "explanation": "Now that we know how clustering groups similar data together, an important question arises: how do we know whether the clusters created by the algorithm are actually good? To answer this, we use cluster evaluation metrics.\nUnlike Classification, Clustering has no ground-truth labels, so we need special metrics to judge cluster quality.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/ml/clu4.png]\n**1. WCSS (Within-Cluster Sum of Squares)**\nMeasures how close data points are to their cluster centroid.\n• Lower WCSS = Tighter clusters\n• Higher WCSS = More spread-out clusters\n• Commonly used with the Elbow Method to find the optimal value of K\n**2. Silhouette Score**\nMeasures both cluster compactness and separation.\n**Score Range:**\n• +1 → Excellent separation\n• 0 → Overlapping clusters\n• -1 → Poor clustering\n**Rule of Thumb:**• > 0.7 → Excellent• 0.5 - 0.7→ Good• 0.25 - 0.5 → Weak• < 0.25 → Poor\n**Key Difference:**\n• WCSS helps choose the best K.\n• Silhouette Score helps evaluate the quality of the final clusters.",
            "example": "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\nfrom sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score\n\n# Age, Spending Score\nX = [[22,90], [25,85], [27,88],[45,30],[50,25], [48,35],[35,60], [38,55], [40,58]]\nfor k in range(2, 5):\n    model = KMeans(n_clusters=k, random_state=42)\n    labels = model.fit_predict(X)\n\n    wcss = model.inertia_\n    silhouette = silhouette_score(X, labels)\n\n    print(f'K={k}')\n    print(f'WCSS={wcss:.2f}')\n    print(f'Silhouette={silhouette:.2f}')\n"
          }
        },
