        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Feature Maps",
            "explanation": "After the Kernel scans the image, what happens next?\nEach time the Kernel finds a useful pattern, it records that information in a new image called a **Feature Map**.\nYou can think of a Feature Map as a summary showing where specific patterns were found.One Feature Map might highlight edges.Another might highlight curves.Another might highlight color differences.\nCNNs create many Feature Maps simultaneously to learn different visual features.\n**The Feature Map**\nBy the time the kernel has finished sliding over the entire original image, it has created a completely new grid called a **Feature Map**.\\n\nA Deep Learning network will use dozens of different kernels simultaneously, creating dozens of different Feature Maps (one for horizontal edges, one for curves, one for color contrasts, etc.).",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"padding: 1rem; background-color: #1e293b; color: white; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; font-family: sans-serif;\"><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; gap: 20px;\"><div style=\"width: 80px; height: 80px; background-color: #3b82f6; border: 2px solid #93c5fd; border-radius: 8px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Input Image</div><div style=\"font-size: 24px; font-weight: bold;\">→</div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;\"><div style=\"width: 100px; height: 30px; background-color: #10b981; border: 1px solid #6ee7b7; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Edges Map</div><div style=\"width: 100px; height: 30px; background-color: #a855f7; border: 1px solid #d8b4fe; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Curves Map</div><div style=\"width: 100px; height: 30px; background-color: #f59e0b; border: 1px solid #fcd34d; border-radius: 4px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;\">Color Map</div></div></div><p style=\"margin-top: 15px; font-size: 14px; color: #cbd5e1;\">A single kernel creates one feature map. Multiple kernels create a stack of different feature maps.</p></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Image Augmentation",
            "explanation": "Even a good model can fail if it only sees perfect training images.\nImagine teaching a child to recognize cats using only one photo. The child may struggle to recognize the same cat when it is rotated, darker, or farther away.\nTo solve this problem, we use **Image Augmentation**.\n**Image Augmentation**\nTo make our model robust, we artificially alter our training data on the fly before feeding it to the network. We take an image of a cat and mathematically:\n• Rotate it 15 degrees.\n• Flip it horizontally.\n• Zoom in by 10%.\n• Shift the color brightness.\n\nThis forces the model to learn the fundamental *features* of a cat (ears, whiskers), rather than memorizing a specific pixel layout.",
            "example": "Code Implementation:\nfrom tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.image import ImageDataGenerator\ndatagen = ImageDataGenerator(\n    rotation_range=20,\n    horizontal_flip=True,\n    brightness_range=[0.8, 1.2]\n)\n`Result:` The network learns that a cat is a cat, even if it's looking left, tilted, or in shadows."
          }
        },
