    {
      "id": "M003-dl-lesson-6-cnn-core",
      "title": "CNN Foundations & Core Concepts",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#059669",
        "accent": "#047857"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why CNNs Were Invented",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! Now that we understand images are made of pixels and tensors, let's learn why Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) were created.\nTraditional Neural Networks work well for tabular data, but images are different. Before processing an image, a standard Neural Network converts it into one long list of numbers.\nWhen this happens, the image loses its spatial information. The network no longer knows which pixels are next to each other.\n\nCNNs solve this problem by keeping the image in its original 2D form and learning patterns such as edges, shapes, textures, and objects.\n\n**Key Idea:** CNNs were invented to understand images while preserving their structure and spatial relationships.",
            "example": "If you flatten a picture of a face, the left eye and right eye might be separated by thousands of numbers in the 1D list. \nA CNN keeps them in their correct 2D places so it can learn what a 'face' actually looks like."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Convolution Operation & Feature Extraction",
            "explanation": "Awesome! Now let's learn the most important operation inside a CNN: Convolution. we can say this is mathematical detective.\nThe detective uses a tiny magnifying glass called a **Kernel** or **Filter** (usually a 3x3 grid of numbers). The kernel holds a specific pattern—for example, it might be mathematically tuned to find vertical lines.\nNext step the **scan**,\nThe kernel slides over the image. At every stop, it multiplies its own numbers with the image's pixels. If the pattern in the image matches the pattern in the kernel, it outputs a high score!\nThen **Feature Extraction**,\nBy sliding dozens of different kernels over the image, the network creates a new stack of images called **Feature Maps**. It has effectively *extracted the features* (edges, corners, circles) and mapped out exactly where they are.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:500px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#f0fdf4; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #bbf7d0;\"><h3 style=\"color:#166534; margin-bottom:10px;\">🔍 Convolution Math Visualized</h3><p style=\"font-size:14px; color:#15803d; margin-bottom:15px;\">How a 3x3 Kernel multiplies with a 3x3 piece of an image.</p><div style=\"display:flex; justify-content:center; align-items:center; gap:15px; font-weight:bold; color:#166534;\"><div style=\"display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3, 35px); gap:2px; border:2px solid #22c55e; padding:2px;\"><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#dcfce7; padding:8px;\">1</div></div><div>X</div><div style=\"display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3, 35px); gap:2px; border:2px solid #ef4444; padding:2px;\"><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">-1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">-1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">1</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#fee2e2; padding:8px;\">-1</div></div><div>=</div><div style=\"font-size:24px; color:#15803d; background:#bbf7d0; padding:15px; border-radius:8px;\">0</div></div><p style=\"margin-top:15px; font-size:12px; color:#166534;\">(1x1) + (0x0) + (1x-1) ... etc. Sum them all up to get the final score!</p></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Convolution Review",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "A CNN uses a ____ to scan the image.",
              "Convolution helps perform Feature ____. ",
              "Kernels can detect edges, corners, and ____."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Kernel",
              "Extraction",
              "shapes",
              "epochs",
              "labels"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "Kernel",
              "blank_2": "Extraction",
              "blank_3": "shapes"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Stride",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We know how the Kernel scans an image, but how far should it move each time?\nThe number of pixels a Kernel moves at each step is called the **Stride**.\n**Stride = 1 (The Careful Scan)**\nBy default, the kernel moves 1 pixel at a time. This is highly detailed, but it produces a massive Feature Map and takes a lot of time.\n**Stride = 2 (The Skipping Scan)**\nIf we set the stride to 2, the kernel jumps over 2 pixels at a time. \n**The Effect of Stride:**\nIncreasing the stride shrinks the physical dimensions (Height and Width) of your resulting Feature Map. A stride of 2 roughly cuts the image size in half. This is useful for reducing the computational workload as we go deeper into the network.",
            "example": "Code Implementation:\nConv2D(filters=32, kernel_size=(3,3), strides=(2,2))\n\nIf the input image is 100x100, a stride of 2 will output a feature map that is roughly 50x50."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Padding (Same vs Valid)",
            "explanation": "Great progress! There is one small issue during Convolution.\nPixels near the edges of an image may not be fully analyzed because the Kernel cannot move outside the image boundary.\nBecause of this, standard convolution simply stops before it goes off the edge. This causes two problems:\n• 1. The edge pixels are barely analyzed compared to the center pixels.\n• 2. The image shrinks slightly after every Convolution layer.\n\nTo solve this problem, we use **Padding**.\n**Valid Padding (No Padding)**\nThe default setting. The image shrinks. A 5x5 image scanned by a 3x3 kernel becomes a 3x3 output.\n\n**Same Padding (Zero Padding)**\nTo solve the shrinking problem, we artificially wrap the image in a border of 0s (black pixels). Now, the kernel can scan the true edge pixels perfectly. The output Feature Map stays the exact *SAME* size as the input.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:400px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #cbd5e1;\"><h3 style=\"color:#334155; margin-bottom:15px;\">🖼️ Same Padding (Zero Padding)</h3><p style=\"font-size:13px; color:#475569; margin-bottom:10px;\">A 3x3 image padded with zeros to become 5x5.</p><div style=\"display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(5, 40px); gap:2px; justify-content:center;\"><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#3b82f6; color:white; padding:10px;\">P</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div><div style=\"background:#94a3b8; color:white; padding:10px;\">0</div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
