        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Historical Architectures: LeNet to VGG",
            "explanation": "Before we explore modern CNN architectures, it's important to understand that CNNs did not become powerful overnight. Over the years, researchers introduced new ideas and improvements that made CNNs deeper, faster, and more accurate. Let's look at some of the most important milestones in the evolution of CNNs.\n\n**1. LeNet-5 (1998)**\nThe grandfather of CNNs. It had only 2 convolutional layers and was used by banks to read handwritten numbers on checks. It proved CNNs worked, but computers were too slow to make it bigger.\n\n**2. AlexNet (2012)**\nThe breakthrough. AlexNet was much deeper (5 Conv layers) and, crucially, was trained on Graphics Processing Units (GPUs) rather than CPUs. It crushed the ImageNet competition and kicked off the modern AI boom.\n\n**3. VGG-16 (2014)**\nVGG simplified everything. Instead of mixing big and small kernels, VGG proved that simply stacking a massive number of tiny 3x3 kernels (13 Conv layers) extracted better features than a few large kernels."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Deep Network Problem: Vanishing Gradients",
            "explanation": "After VGG, researchers realized that deeper networks extracted better features. So, they tried building networks with 50, 100, and 200 layers. But a disaster happened: the networks stopped learning completely.And That problem we called as Vanishing Grdient Problem.\n\n**The Vanishing Gradient Problem**\nRemember the Chain Rule? Backpropagation requires multiplying gradients backward through the network. If you multiply a small number (like 0.5) by itself 100 times, it approaches **zero**.\n\nBy the time the gradient reached the earliest layers (the edge detectors), the feedback signal had completely vanished. The early layers were 'frozen' and couldn't update their weights. Because the foundation was broken, the whole 100-layer network performed worse than a shallow 10-layer network."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Solution: ResNet & Skip Connections",
            "explanation": "In 2015, Microsoft researchers solved the Vanishing Gradient problem with a brilliant, simple hack: The **Residual Network (ResNet)**.\n\n**The Skip Connection (Shortcut)**\nInstead of forcing data and gradients to flow strictly layer-by-layer, ResNet added a 'bypass' wire. It takes the original input of a block and adds it directly to the output of the block, skipping the layers in between.\n\n**Mathematical Magic:** Output = `Layer_Output(x) + Original(x)`\n\nWhy does this work? \nDuring Backpropagation, the gradient now has an unobstructed 'superhighway' (the shortcut) to flow straight back to the early layers without being multiplied into oblivion. ResNet allowed networks to grow to 152 layers deep, breaking all previous records.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:sans-serif;text-align:center;max-width:550px;margin:auto;padding:20px;background:#f0fdfa;border-radius:12px;border:1px solid #5eead4;box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);\"><h3 style=\"color:#0f766e;margin-bottom:5px;\">ResNet Skip Connection Visualizer</h3><p style=\"font-size:13px;color:#115e59;margin-bottom:18px;\">Compare gradient flow in a standard CNN versus a ResNet with skip connections.</p><div style=\"display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:15px;\"><div style=\"flex:1;background:#fff;padding:15px;border-radius:10px;border:2px solid #cbd5e1;\"><h4 style=\"margin:0;color:#334155;\">Standard VGG</h4><p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#64748b;margin:8px 0 12px;\">Gradient becomes smaller after every layer.</p><div id=\"vggGrad\" style=\"background:#ef4444;width:100%;height:20px;border-radius:10px;transition:width 1s;\"></div><button onclick=\"document.getElementById('vggGrad').style.width='5%';\" style=\"margin-top:12px;padding:6px 12px;border:none;border-radius:6px;background:#D8DFF0;color:white;cursor:pointer;\">Backpropagate</button></div><div style=\"flex:1;background:#fff;padding:15px;border-radius:10px;border:2px solid #5eead4;\"><h4 style=\"margin:0;color:#0f766e;\">ResNet Block</h4><p style=\"font-size:11px;color:#64748b;margin:8px 0 12px;\">Skip connection preserves gradient flow.</p><div style=\"position:relative;height:38px;\"><div style=\"position:absolute;top:0;left:0;width:100%;border-top:3px dashed #10b981;\"></div><div style=\"position:absolute;top:-14px;width:100%;font-size:10px;color:#059669;font-weight:bold;\">Skip Connection Highway</div><div id=\"resGrad\" style=\"position:absolute;bottom:0;background:#10b981;width:100%;height:20px;border-radius:10px;transition:width 1s;\"></div></div><button onclick=\"document.getElementById('resGrad').style.width='95%';\" style=\"margin-top:12px;padding:6px 12px;border:none;border-radius:6px;background:#D8DFF0;color:white;cursor:pointer;\">Backpropagate</button></div></div><button onclick=\"document.getElementById('vggGrad').style.width='100%';document.getElementById('resGrad').style.width='100%';\" style=\"margin-top:18px;padding:8px 16px;background:#64748b;color:white;border:none;border-radius:6px;cursor:pointer;\">Reset Gradients</button></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
