              "blank_2": "Skip",
              "blank_3": "easily"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "CNN Applications & Limitations",
            "explanation": "Now that we understand how CNNs work, let's see where they are used in the real world and what challenges they face.\nCNNs are extremely powerful for image-related tasks and are used in many modern applications.\n**Common Applications:**\n• Face Recognition\n• Medical Image Analysis\n• Self-Driving Cars\n• Object Detection\n• Security Cameras\n\nAlthough CNNs are very powerful, they have some limitations.\nA CNN looks at only a small part of the image at a time using a Kernel. Because of this, it may struggle to understand relationships between objects that are far apart in an image.\nFor example, if a dog's head appears in one corner and its tail appears in another corner, the early CNN layers may not immediately understand they belong to the same dog.\nCNNs can also struggle when objects appear in unusual positions or rotations unless they have seen similar examples during training.\n**Key Idea:** CNNs are excellent at finding local patterns, but they sometimes struggle to understand the complete picture."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Paradigm Shift: Vision Transformers (ViTs)",
            "explanation": "CNNs were the most popular computer vision models for many years. However, researchers wanted models that could understand the entire image more effectively.\n\nThis led to the development of **Vision Transformers (ViTs)**.In 2020, Google researchers introduced the **Vision Transformer (ViT)**.\nTransformers (the architecture behind ChatGPT) were designed for text. ViTs adapted them for images.\n**How ViTs Work:**\nInstead of sliding a tiny kernel over an image, a ViT chops the image into a grid of 'Patches' (like pieces of a puzzle). It treats each patch like a word in a sentence.\nIt then uses a mechanism called **Self-Attention**.\n\n**Why they are replacing CNNs:**\nUnlike a CNN kernel that only looks locally, Self-Attention allows *every patch to look at every other patch simultaneously*. It has global context from layer one. If the top-left patch is a head, it instantly connects mathematically with the tail in the bottom-right. For massive datasets and massive models, ViTs now outperform CNNs."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Implement the mathematical logic of a ResNet Skip Connection. You have the `original_input` and the output from the convolutional layers `conv_output`. Combine them correctly to bypass the vanishing gradient.",
            "starterCode": "import tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Add\n\ndef resnet_block(original_input, conv_output):\n    # TODO: Implement the skip connection logic using the Add() layer\n    final_output = None\n    return final_output",
            "solution": "import tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Add\n\ndef resnet_block(original_input, conv_output):\n    # TODO: Implement the skip connection logic using the Add() layer\n    final_output = Add()([original_input, conv_output])\n    return final_output",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "A skip connection is just element-wise addition. Use `Add()([tensor1, tensor2])` to add the original_input to the conv_output.",
            "explanation": "The brilliance of ResNet is exactly this simple. By literally adding the unmodified input back into the output, you create an alternative mathematical route that preserves the gradient during backpropagation."
          }
        },
        {
