      "id": "M003-dl-lesson-capstone-catdog",
      "title": "Capstone Project: Cat vs. Dog Image Classification",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#3b82f6",
        "accent": "#1d4ed8"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Automation Crisis",
            "explanation": "Welcome to 'Paws & Claws Rescue'! The shelter receives hundreds of pet photos a day for their new adoption app.\n\n**The Problem Statement:**\nCurrently, staff members are manually looking at every uploaded photo and sorting them into 'Cats' and 'Dogs' folders. It takes hours of tedious work.\n\nYour job as the new AI Engineer is to build a Deep Learning model—specifically a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)—that can automatically analyze the pixels of an image and classify whether it contains a cat or a dog. If successful, the app will auto-tag and sort the images instantly."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Workflow",
            "explanation": "`1.Problem Statement` → Automate binary image classification.\n`2.Data Collection` → Gather thousands of labeled cat and dog images.\n`3.Data Preprocessing` → Images are different sizes! We must resize them all to a fixed dimension (e.g., 150x150 pixels).\n`4.Normalization` → Scale pixel color values from 0-255 down to 0-1 so the neural network learns efficiently.\n`5.Model Architecture` → Build a CNN with Convolutional layers (to find features like whiskers) and Pooling layers (to compress the image).\n`6.Compilation` → Set the loss function (Binary Crossentropy) and optimizer.\n`7.Training` → Pass the images through the network over multiple epochs.\n`8.Evaluation` → Test the model on unseen images to prevent overfitting.\n\n**Key Idea:** Unlike standard tabular data, images require deep learning architectures that can understand spatial patterns and visual features."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dataset Overview: Cats vs Dogs Classification",
            "explanation": "Before building our Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), we first need a dataset. For this project, we'll use the **Cats vs Dogs Dataset** from Kaggle.\n\n**Dataset:** Cats vs Dogs Classification Dataset\n\n**Download the Dataset:**\nhttps://www.kaggle.com/datasets/karakaggle/kaggle-cat-vs-dog-dataset\n\nAfter downloading, extract the dataset into your project folder.\n\n**Dataset Overview:**\n• Total Images: Approximately 25,000\n• Classes: 2 (Cat and Dog)\n• Image Type: RGB Images\n• Classification Type: Binary Classification\n\n**Project Workflow:**\n1. Download and organize the dataset.\n2. Load the images using TensorFlow.\n3. Preprocess and normalize the images.\n4. Split the dataset into training and validation sets.\n5. Build the CNN model.\n6. Train the model.\n7. Evaluate the model.\n8. Predict whether a new image is a Cat or a Dog.\n\nIn industry, Data Engineers usually provide the dataset. However, while learning, you will download and prepare it yourself."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Step 1: Prepare the Dataset & Build the CNN",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the missing functions to load the dataset, preprocess the images, create the datasets, and build the CNN model.",
            "starterCode": "import tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras import layers, models\n\n# TODO: Load the dataset\ndef load_dataset():\n    pass\n\n# TODO: Preprocess the dataset\ndef preprocess_dataset(train_dataset, validation_dataset):\n    pass\n\n# TODO: Build the CNN model\ndef build_model():\n    pass\n\ntrain_dataset, validation_dataset = load_dataset()\ntrain_dataset, validation_dataset = preprocess_dataset(train_dataset, validation_dataset)\nmodel = build_model()",
            "solution": "import tensorflow as tf\nfrom tensorflow.keras import layers, models\n\n# Load the dataset\ndef load_dataset():\n    train_dataset = tf.keras.utils.image_dataset_from_directory(\n        'dataset/train',\n        image_size=(150,150),\n        batch_size=32\n    )\n\n    validation_dataset = tf.keras.utils.image_dataset_from_directory(\n        'dataset/validation',\n        image_size=(150,150),\n        batch_size=32\n    )\n\n    return train_dataset, validation_dataset\n\n# Normalize images\ndef preprocess_dataset(train_dataset, validation_dataset):\n    normalization = layers.Rescaling(1./255)\n\n    train_dataset = train_dataset.map(lambda x, y: (normalization(x), y))\n    validation_dataset = validation_dataset.map(lambda x, y: (normalization(x), y))\n\n    return train_dataset, validation_dataset\n\n# Build CNN\ndef build_model():\n    model = models.Sequential([\n        layers.Conv2D(32,(3,3),activation='relu',input_shape=(150,150,3)),\n        layers.MaxPooling2D((2,2)),\n        layers.Conv2D(64,(3,3),activation='relu'),\n        layers.MaxPooling2D((2,2)),\n        layers.Flatten(),\n        layers.Dense(64,activation='relu'),\n        layers.Dense(1,activation='sigmoid')\n    ])\n\n    return model\n\ntrain_dataset, validation_dataset = load_dataset()\ntrain_dataset, validation_dataset = preprocess_dataset(train_dataset, validation_dataset)\nmodel = build_model()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Create three functions: one for loading the dataset, one for preprocessing it, and one for building the CNN model."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Step 2: Train, Evaluate & Predict",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the functions to compile the CNN, train it, evaluate its performance, and predict whether a new image is a cat or a dog.",
            "starterCode": "# TODO: Compile the CNN model\ndef compile_model(model):\n    pass\n\n# TODO: Train the model\ndef train_model(model, train_dataset, validation_dataset):\n    pass\n\n# TODO: Evaluate the trained model\ndef evaluate_model(model, validation_dataset):\n    pass\n\n# TODO: Predict a new image\ndef predict_image(model, image_path):\n    pass\n\ncompile_model(model)\ntrain_model(model, train_dataset, validation_dataset)\nevaluate_model(model, validation_dataset)\npredict_image(model, 'test_images/sample.jpg')",
            "solution": "# Compile model\ndef compile_model(model):\n    model.compile(\n        optimizer='adam',\n        loss='binary_crossentropy',\n        metrics=['accuracy']\n    )\n\n# Train model\ndef train_model(model, train_dataset, validation_dataset):\n    return model.fit(\n        train_dataset,\n        validation_data=validation_dataset,\n        epochs=5\n    )\n\n# Evaluate model\ndef evaluate_model(model, validation_dataset):\n    loss, accuracy = model.evaluate(validation_dataset)\n\n    print('Validation Accuracy:', accuracy)\n    print('Validation Loss:', loss)\n\n# Predict new image\ndef predict_image(model, image_path):\n    image = tf.keras.utils.load_img(\n        image_path,\n        target_size=(150,150)\n    )\n\n    image = tf.keras.utils.img_to_array(image)\n    image = tf.expand_dims(image,0)/255.0\n\n    prediction = model.predict(image)\n\n    if prediction[0][0] > 0.5:\n        print('Prediction: Dog')\n    else:\n        print('Prediction: Cat')\n\ncompile_model(model)\ntrain_model(model, train_dataset, validation_dataset)\nevaluate_model(model, validation_dataset)\npredict_image(model,'test_images/sample.jpg')",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Separate the workflow into four functions: compile, train, evaluate, and predict."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What's Next? From Model to Production",
            "explanation": "Congratulations! You have successfully built, trained, evaluated, and tested your CNN model. If your model achieves good accuracy and correctly classifies new images, the next step is to make it available for real users.\n\n**Typical Industry Workflow:**\n• Save the trained model using `model.save('cat_dog_classifier.keras')`.\n• Load the saved model inside a web application or API.\n• Build a REST API using frameworks such as **FastAPI**, **Flask**, or **Django**.\n• Users upload an image through a web or mobile application.\n• The API preprocesses the image, runs the model, and returns the prediction (Cat or Dog).\n• Deploy the application to cloud platforms such as **AWS**, **Azure**, **Google Cloud**, or **Render** so users can access it from anywhere.\n\n**What's Coming Next:** In the upcoming deployment modules, you'll learn how to save trained models, build prediction APIs, containerize applications with Docker, and deploy AI models to production."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-1-fundamentals",
      "title": "Introduction to NLP & Text Processing Fundamentals",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#4f46e5",
        "accent": "#4338ca"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Language Barrier: What is NLP?",
            "explanation": "Welcome to the NLP module! Until now, we have worked with numbers and images. Now let's learn how computers understand human language.\nHumans communicate using text and speech, but computers only understand numbers. This creates a communication gap.\n\n**Natural Language Processing (NLP)** is a branch of Artificial Intelligence that helps computers read, understand, and work with human language.\n\nUsing NLP, computers can analyze emails, translate languages, understand customer reviews, answer questions, and even power AI systems like ChatGPT.\n**Key Idea:** NLP helps computers understand and process human language."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "NLP in the Wild: Applications",
            "explanation": "Great start! Now let's see where NLP is used in our daily lives.\nYou interact with NLP more often than you think. Here is how industries use it:\n\n`📱 Virtual Assistants` → Siri and Alexa converting your voice into text, extracting the intent, and generating a response.\n`📧 Email Filtering` → Gmail automatically classifying incoming emails as 'Primary', 'Promotions', or 'Spam'.\n`🌍 Machine Translation` → Google Translate mapping the complex grammar of English into the complex grammar of Japanese.\n`📊 Sentiment Analysis` → Companies analyzing thousands of Twitter posts to see if public opinion about their new product is positive or negative.\n`🤖 Generative AI` → Large Language Models (LLMs) like ChatGPT writing essays, coding, and summarizing documents."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Data Hierarchy",
            "explanation": "Before we process text, we must understand how NLP organizes data. Think of it as zooming in with a microscope.Lets discuss basic terms like corpus,Document,Sentense and Toeken.\n\n`📚 Corpus` → Corpus is the entire collection of text data. (e.g., All of Wikipedia, or a database of 10,000 movie reviews).\n`📄 Document` → Document is a single distinct piece of text within the corpus. (e.g., One specific Wikipedia article, or one specific movie review).\n`📝 Sentence` → Sentence is a sequence of words expressing a complete thought within a document.\n`🔤 Token` → Token is a smallest unit of meaning. This is usually a single word, but it can also be a punctuation mark or a number.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/in1.png]\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Text Processing Pipeline: Cleaning & Tokenization",
            "explanation": "Now that we understand what text data is, let's discuss the next step. If you feed raw, messy internet text directly into a machine learning model, it will produce poor results. That's why we first build a **Text Processing Pipeline**.\n\n### 1. Text Cleaning\nText cleaning removes unnecessary information from the text so the model focuses only on meaningful words.\n\nCommon cleaning steps include:Convert text to lowercase,Remove HTML tags,Remove URLs,Remove special characters and extra spaces.\n\n```python\nimport re\n\ntext = 'Visit https://abc.com <b>Hello!</b>'\nclean = re.sub(r'<.*?>|https?://\\S+|[^a-zA-Z ]', '', text)\nprint(clean.lower())\n# hello\n```\n\n### 2. Tokenization\nTokenization means breaking a sentence into smaller pieces called **tokens** (usually words). Machine Learning models cannot understand complete sentences directly, so we first split them into tokens.\nRaw Text:'NLP is fun!`\n```python\nfrom nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize\n\ntext = 'NLP is fun!'\nprint(word_tokenize(text))\n# ['NLP', 'is', 'fun', '!']\n```\n\n### 3. Stop Word Removal\nStop words are very common words such as **the, is, in, at, on, of**. These words help with grammar but usually don't add much meaning for Machine Learning tasks.\n\nBy removing them, the model can focus on the important keywords.\n\nExample:'I am learning Natural Language Processing'\n```python\nfrom nltk.corpus import stopwords\n\nwords = ['I','am','learning','Natural','Language','Processing']\nstop = set(stopwords.words('english'))\n\nresult = [w for w in words if w.lower() not in stop]\nprint(result)\n# ['learning', 'Natural', 'Language', 'Processing']\n```\n\nThese three preprocessing steps—**Text Cleaning**, **Tokenization**, and **Stop Word Removal**—are the foundation of almost every NLP project. They prepare raw text before it is converted into numerical features and used for Machine Learning or Deep Learning models."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Step 1: Build a basic tokenizer. Given a raw string, convert it to lowercase, split it into a list of words based on spaces, and remove any 'stop words' from your custom list.",
            "starterCode": "raw_text = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"\nstop_words = [\"the\", \"over\"]\n\n# TODO: Convert to lowercase\nlower_text = ____\n\n# TODO: Split into a list of tokens\ntokens = lower_text.____()\n\n# TODO: Keep only tokens that are NOT in the stop_words list\nclean_tokens = [word for word in tokens if ____ not in ____]\n\nprint(clean_tokens)",
            "solution": "raw_text = \"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog\"\nstop_words = [\"the\", \"over\"]\n\n# TODO: Convert to lowercase\nlower_text = raw_text.lower()\n\n# TODO: Split into a list of tokens\ntokens = lower_text.split()\n\n# TODO: Keep only tokens that are NOT in the stop_words list\nclean_tokens = [word for word in tokens if word not in stop_words]\n\nprint(clean_tokens)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `.lower()` for casing, `.split()` to turn a string into a list, and a simple `word not in stop_words` check."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Stemming vs. Lemmatization",
            "explanation": "Excellent! After cleaning and tokenizing text, we often find different forms of the same word.For example:Run, Running, Runs, Ran.To a computer, these look like completely different words. To solve this problem, we use **Word Normalization**.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/in2.png]\nThere are two popular techniques Stemming and Lemmatization lets discuss below.\n`🪓 Stemming (The Axe)`\n Stemming is a crude, rule-based approach that simply chops the end off a word.\n*Example:* 'Flying' → 'Fli', 'Studies' → 'Studi'.\n*Pros/Cons:* Extremely fast, but often creates fake words that aren't in the dictionary.\n\n`🧠 Lemmatization (The Scalpel)`\nLemmatization is a smart approach that uses a built-in dictionary and morphological analysis to find the true linguistic root (the Lemma).\n*Example:* 'Better' → 'Good', 'Studies' → 'Study'.\n*Pros/Cons:* Highly accurate and creates real words, but computationally slower."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Context is King: N-Grams & Word Frequency",
            "explanation": "Awesome! Now let's learn how computers understand the meaning and context of text.\nOne simple technique is **Word Frequency** tells us what a document is about. If 'goal', 'stadium', and 'referee' appear 50 times, it's a sports article.\n\nHowever, standard tokenization creates single words (Unigrams). This destroys context. \nImagine the review: *\"The movie was not good.\"*\nIf we look at Word Frequency of Unigrams, we see the word 'good'. The computer might think it's a positive review!\n\n**N-Grams to the Rescue**\nAn N-Gram is a continuous sequence of *N* words grouped together.\n* **Unigrams (N=1):** `['The', 'movie', 'was', 'not', 'good']`\n* **Bigrams (N=2):** `['The movie', 'movie was', 'was not', 'not good']`\n* **Trigrams (N=3):** `['The movie was', 'movie was not', 'was not good']`\n\nBy generating Bigrams, our model suddenly sees the token `'not good'`, preserving the negative sentiment!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Word Normalization & N-Grams Review",
            "question": "Fill in the blanks using the correct NLP concepts.",
            "template": [
              "____ removes word endings using simple rules.",
              "____ finds the true dictionary root of a word.",
              "Word ____ counts how often words appear in a document.",
              "A ____ contains two consecutive words."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Frequency",
              "Bigram",
              "Token",
              "Stemming",
              "Lemmatization",
              "Trigram",
              "Corpus"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "Stemming",
              "blank_2": "Lemmatization",
              "blank_3": "Frequency",
              "blank_4": "Bigram"
            },
            "explanation": "Stemming cuts words using simple rules, while Lemmatization finds the actual dictionary root. Word Frequency counts word occurrences, and a Bigram contains two consecutive words such as 'not good'.",
            "hint": "Think about word normalization techniques and N-Grams."
          }
        },
