    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-3-similarity",
      "title": "Similarity Metrics & Topic Modeling",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#0d9488",
        "accent": "#0f766e"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Similarity Matters",
            "explanation": "Welcome to the Similarity Metrics lesson! So far, we have learned how to convert text into vectors using embeddings.\nBut a very important question remains:\nHow do computers know whether two pieces of text are similar?\nThis is where **Similarity Metrics** come in.\n\nAlmost every NLP application depends on measuring similarity.\nExamples:\n• Search Engines → Find webpages similar to your query.\n• Recommendation Systems → Suggest similar articles, movies, or products.\n• Chatbots → Find responses related to your question.\n• Plagiarism Detection → Compare documents for similarity.\n\n**Key Idea:** Similarity Metrics help computers measure how closely two texts are related."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Tale of Two Distances: Euclidean & Manhattan",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! Before learning the most popular similarity metric, let's understand two basic ways of measuring distance.Imagine you are an AI detective trying to figure out if two documents are talking about the exact same subject. \nDocument A is a tiny 5-word tweet: *\"Apples are delicious red fruits.\"*\nDocument B is a massive 5,000-word Wikipedia article about *Apples*.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/sm1.png]\nIf we turn these texts into vectors and plot them on a graph, the traditional methods of measuring distance will completely fail us. Let's look at the two oldest ways to measure distance:\n`1. Euclidean Distance (The Crow's Flight)`\nThis is the straight-line distance between two points in space. It is the classic Pythagorean theorem you learned in geometry.\n\nDistance = √((x₂ - x₁)² + (y₂ - y₁)²)\n\n`2. Manhattan Distance (The Taxi Cab Grid)`\nImagine you are driving a taxi in Manhattan. You cannot drive a straight line through buildings; you must stick to the rigid grid of horizontal and vertical streets. It calculates the absolute sum of differences across all dimensions.\n\nDistance = |x₂ - x₁| + |y₂ - y₁|",
            "example": "#First install scipy library\n#pip install scipy\nfrom scipy.spatial.distance import euclidean, cityblock\n\npoint_a = [1, 2]\npoint_b = [4, 6]\n\nprint('Euclidean Distance:', euclidean(point_a, point_b))   #Euclidean Distance: 5.0\nprint('Manhattan Distance:', cityblock(point_a, point_b))   #Manhattan Distance: 7"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Gold Standard: Cosine Similarity",
            "explanation": "In NLP, we care more about meaning than physical distance.That's why we use **Cosine Similarity**.\nInstead of measuring the distance between vectors, Cosine Similarity measures the angle between them. focusing on direction rather than length.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/sm2.png]\nCosine Similarity Formula:\n**Cosine Similarity (cos(θ))** = `(A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)`\n\nWhere: A · B = Dot Product of vectors, ||A|| = Length of Vector A, ||B|| = Length of Vector B.\n`0° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = 1.0 (Perfectly Similar)\n`90° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = 0.0 (Unrelated)\n`180° Angle` → Cosine Similarity = -1.0 (Exact Opposites)`Example:`\nKing = [1,2], Queen = [2,3] → A·B = 8,\n ||A|| = 2.24, ||B|| = 3.61 \ncos(θ) = 8 / (2.24 × 3.61) ≈ 0.99 (very similar)\nUnlike Euclidean or Manhattan Distance, Cosine Similarity ignores vector size and focuses on semantic direction. This means a short tweet and a 500-page book about the same topic can still have a similarity score close to 1.0 if they point in the same semantic direction.\n The below The diagram demonstrates that words with similar meanings, such as 'King' and 'Qween', have vectors pointing in nearly the same direction, resulting in a high Cosine Similarity score.",
            "example": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nvector_a = [[1, 1, 1]]\nvector_b = [[1, 1, 0]]\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(vector_a, vector_b)\nprint('Cosine Similarity:', similarity[0][0])"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Using sklearn's cosine_similarity function, calculate the cosine similarity between the following vectors and print the result rounded to 2 decimal places.\n\nvec1 = [[1, 1, 1, 1, 0]]\nvec2 = [[1, 1, 1, 0, 1]]",
            "starterCode": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nvec1 = [[1, 1, 1, 1, 0]]\nvec2 = [[1, 1, 1, 0, 1]]\n\n# Write your code here\n",
            "solution": "from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nsimilarity = cosine_similarity(vec1, vec2)\nprint(round(similarity[0][0], 2))",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use sklearn.metrics.pairwise.cosine_similarity and access the result using similarity[0][0]."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Jaccard Similarity: The Set Theory Approach",
            "explanation": "Awesome! Sometimes we don't have embeddings. Instead, we only have lists of words, tags, or keywords.In these situations, we use **Jaccard Similarity**.\nJaccard is incredibly simple: **Intersection over Union**.\nHow many words do they share, divided by the total number of unique words across both?\n\n* **Set A:** `['React', 'NextJS', 'Tailwind']`\n* **Set B:** `['React', 'NodeJS']`\n* **Intersection (Shared):** `['React']` (1 word)\n* **Union (Total Unique):** `['React', 'NextJS', 'Tailwind', 'NodeJS']` (4 words)\n* **Jaccard Score:** 1 / 4 = **0.25 (25%)**\n\nIt is fantastic for comparing hashtags, keyword lists, or finding exactly duplicated sentences.",
            "example": "set_a = {'React', 'NextJS', 'Tailwind'}\nset_b = {'React', 'NodeJS'}\n\nintersection = len(set_a.intersection(set_b))\nunion = len(set_a.union(set_b))\n\njaccard_similarity = intersection / union\nprint('Jaccard Similarity:', jaccard_similarity)"
          }
        },
