        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Plagiarism Dilemma",
          "description": "You are building a plagiarism detection tool for a university. A student submits a 5-page essay on 'The Cold War'. You suspect they copied a single paragraph from a 500-page textbook. You convert both the student's essay and the entire textbook into TF-IDF vectors. Which metric should you use to find if they share semantic meaning?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Euclidean Distance",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. The 500-page textbook vector will be astronomically larger than the 5-page essay vector. The Euclidean distance will be massive, and the system will say they are completely unrelated.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Cosine Similarity",
              "outcome": "Correct! Cosine similarity only looks at the *angle* of the vectors. Because it ignores magnitude (document length), it will recognize that both texts point in the exact same 'Cold War' semantic direction.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "Jaccard Similarity",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. While Jaccard can find exact overlapping words, it struggles with synonyms (e.g., 'USSR' vs 'Soviet Union') which a dense embedding with Cosine Similarity would catch.",
