    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-4-rnn-lstm",
      "title": "Sequential Models for NLP: RNN & LSTM",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#0284c7",
        "accent": "#0369a1"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "A Story of Lost Context",
            "explanation": "Welcome! So far, we have learned models that work well with images and fixed data. But what happens when the order of information matters?\n\nImagine reading the sentence: 'I lived in France for ten years, so I speak fluent ____.' To guess the missing word 'French', you must remember something that appeared much earlier in the sentence.\n\nTraditional Neural Networks cannot remember previous information. They process each word independently and quickly forget what came before.\nThis creates a challenge called the **Lost Context Problem**.\n\n**Key Idea:** Some tasks require memory, and traditional neural networks do not have memory."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Sequential Data: The Power of Order",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Before learning RNNs, we first need to understand Sequential Data.Imagine someone gives you three words: 'Dog', 'Bites', and 'Man'. If arranged as 'Dog bites man', the meaning is normal. But if arranged as 'Man bites dog', the meaning changes completely even though the same words are used. This happens because the order matters.\nData where the sequence of information is important is called **Sequential Data**. We see it everywhere:\n\n• Language → Words appear in a specific order.\n• Stock Prices → Today's price depends on previous days.\n• Music & Audio → Notes and sounds arrive over time.\n• Weather Data → Tomorrow's weather depends on recent patterns.\n\nUnlike regular data, sequential data cannot be shuffled because changing the order changes the meaning. To understand such data, AI models need memory so they can remember what happened earlier and use that information to understand what comes next.",
            "example": "The sentence 'I love AI' and 'AI love I' contain the same words, but only one makes sense because the sequence is correct."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN)",
            "explanation": "Now let's learn the first neural network designed specifically for sequential data.Imagine reading a sentence one word at a time. When you read 'I', you remember it. Then you read 'love' and combine it with what you already remember. Finally, when you read 'AI', you understand the complete meaning because you remembered the previous words.\n\nA traditional neural network cannot remember previous inputs, but a Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) introduces a memory called the Hidden State. This hidden state carries information from one step to the next, allowing the model to understand sequences.\nThink of the Hidden State as a notebook that travels through the sentence and gets updated at every step.\n`Recurrent Neural Network (RNN)` is a type of neural network designed for sequential data that remembers previous information using a hidden state to make better predictions.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/seq1.png]\n`How RNN Works:`\n• **1️.Input (Xt):** Receive the current word.\n• **2️.Previous Memory (h(t−1)):** Recall information from the previous step.\n• **3️.Update Memory (ht):** Combine the current input and previous memory to create new memory. ht = f(Xt + h(t−1)).\n• **4️.Generate Output (Yt):** Use the new memory to make a prediction. Yt = g(ht).\n• **5️.Pass Memory Forward:** Send ht to the next step as future memory.\nExample:\n'I' → Memory = 'I'\n'love' → Memory = 'I love'\n'AI' → Memory = 'I love AI'\nBecause the RNN remembers previous words, it can understand context and sequence."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why RNNs Fail: The Vanishing Gradient Problem",
            "explanation": "Nice work! RNNs introduced memory, but they still have a major limitation.\nImagine trying to remember the first sentence of a book after reading 100 pages. Most people forget many details.\n\nRNNs face a similar problem.During training, neural networks learn by sending error information (gradients) backward to update their weights. In long sequences, these gradients can become smaller and smaller until they are almost zero. This is called the `Vanishing Gradient` Problem.\nWhen the gradient becomes too small, the RNN stops learning information from earlier parts of the sequence and only remembers recent inputs.\n\nFor example, in a long sentence, the model may remember the last few words but forget important words that appeared much earlier.\nBecause of this memory limitation, standard RNNs struggle with long documents, long conversations, and long-term dependencies.\n\nKey Idea:\n`Vanishing Gradient` → Earlier information fades away.\n`RNN Failure` → Cannot remember important long-term context."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Implementation of RNN (Sentiment Classifier)",
            "explanation": "To process sequential data like text or time-series, we use a **Recurrent Neural Network (RNN)**. Unlike a traditional neural network, an RNN has a **hidden state** that acts like memory. As it reads each word in a sequence, it remembers important information from the previous words and uses it to understand the current word.\n\nThe hidden state is updated using the following equation:\n\n**hₜ = tanh(Wₕₕhₜ₋₁ + Wₓₕxₜ + bₕ)**\n\nThis allows the model to capture the context of a sentence instead of treating every word independently. For example, an RNN can read a movie review word by word and finally predict whether the review is **Positive** or **Negative**.\n\nA simple RNN model for sentiment analysis looks like this:\n\n```python\nfrom tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Embedding, SimpleRNN, Dense\n\n# Define the RNN model\nmodel = Sequential([\n    Embedding(input_dim=10000, output_dim=32, input_length=100),\n    SimpleRNN(64, activation='tanh'),\n    Dense(1, activation='sigmoid')\n])\n\n# Predict the sentiment\nprediction = model.predict(padded_sequence)\n\nprint('Positive Review 👍' if prediction > 0.5 else 'Negative Review 👎')\n```\n\nIn this model:\n\n• **Embedding Layer** converts each word into a dense numerical vector.\n• **SimpleRNN Layer** processes one word at a time while remembering previous words.\n• **Dense Layer** produces the final prediction. A value close to **1** indicates a Positive Review, while a value close to **0** indicates a Negative Review.\n\nThe complete implementation with step-by-step explanation is shown below."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Build a SimpleRNN model to classify emails as Spam (1) or Not Spam (0). Use 16 RNN units and a sigmoid output layer.",
            "starterCode": "from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import SimpleRNN, Dense\n\n# Build the model\n",
            "solution": "from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import SimpleRNN, Dense\n\nmodel = Sequential([\n    SimpleRNN(16, input_shape=(50, 1)),\n    Dense(1, activation='sigmoid')\n])\n\nmodel.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='binary_crossentropy', metrics=['accuracy'])\nmodel.summary()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use SimpleRNN followed by Dense(1, activation='sigmoid')."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "RNN and Vanishing Gradient Review",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "RNN is designed for ___ data.",
              "RNN struggles with ___ sequences.",
              "The memory problem is called the ___ Gradient Problem.",
              "Earlier information gradually ___ away."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Image",
              "long",
              "Sequential",
              "Vanishing",
              "fades",
              "grows",
              "image"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "Sequential",
              "blank_2": "long",
              "blank_3": "Vanishing",
              "blank_4": "fades"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Solution: Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)",
            "explanation": "To solve the memory problem of RNNs, researchers created special types of RNN that is **LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory)**\nImagine you are reading a long book. You do not remember every single word, but you keep the important information and forget the unnecessary details. LSTM works in a similar way.\nInstead of relying on a single memory, LSTM introduces a Cell State, which acts like a long-term memory storage. This allows important information to travel through the network for a long time without being forgotten.\n`LSTM also uses special gates that decide:`\n• What information to keep.\n• What information to forget.\n• What new information to store.\nBecause of this controlled memory system, LSTMs can remember important information from much earlier in a sequence and handle long sentences much better than standard RNNs.\n`Example:`\nSentence: 'I grew up in France... many words later ... I speak fluent French.'\nA standard RNN may forget that the person grew up in France. An LSTM can remember this important information and use it later to better understand why the person speaks French.\nKey Idea:\nRNN → Short-term memory.\nLSTM → Short-term + Long-term memory."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Inside the LSTM: Understanding the Gates",
            "explanation": "Lets discuss how LSTM remember long term context.In LSTM manages its memory using a special structure called the Cell State (Ct). Think of the Cell State as a long-term memory highway that carries important information through the sequence.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/seq2.png]\n\nTo control this memory, LSTM uses three gates:\n•**1.Forget Gate (ft):** Decides what information should be removed from the Cell State.\nExample: If the topic changes from 'Alice' to 'Bob', the model can forget information related to Alice.\n•**2️.Input Gate (it):** Decides what new information is important enough to store in memory.\nExample: When reading a new important word, the model saves it in the Cell State.\n•**3.Output Gate (ot):** Decides what information from memory should be used to produce the current output.\nExample: The model reveals only the relevant information needed at the current step.\nCell State Update:\n`Ct = (ft × Ct-1) + (it × Candidate Information)`\nThis means:\n• Keep important old information.\n• Remove unnecessary information.\n• Add useful new information.\n\nBecause of these gates, LSTMs can remember important information for long periods and overcome the memory limitations of standard RNNs.•\n\n`Key Variables:` Xt = Current Input, Ct-1 = Previous Cell State, Ct = Updated Cell State, ft = Forget Gate, it = Input Gate, ot = Output Gate, ht = Hidden State (Output Memory)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How to Implement Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) architecture",
            "explanation": "Before writing code, let's understand what we are trying to build. An LSTM model is designed to process sequential data while remembering important information from earlier time steps.Now, let's see how to implement an LSTM architecture step by step using a deep learning framework.\n•`Step 1:` Import the required classes (Sequential, LSTM, and Dense) from TensorFlow/Keras.\n•`Step 2:` Create a Sequential model, which allows layers to be stacked one after another.\n•`Step 3:`Add an LSTM layer with 64 memory units and define the input shape as (100,50), where 100 is the sequence length and 50 is the number of features per step.\n•`Step 4:` Add a Dense(1) layer that takes the LSTM output and generates the final prediction.\n•`Step 5:` During training, the LSTM reads the sequence one step at a time, updates its memory (Cell State), and learns important patterns.\n•`Step 6:` When a new sequence such as 'I love learning AI' is passed to the model, the LSTM processes each word, uses its memory to understand the context, and the Dense layer produces the final prediction.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background:#111827;border:1px solid #374151;border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;font-family:Consolas,monospace;\"><div style=\"background:#1f2937;padding:10px 15px;border-bottom:1px solid #374151;color:#9ca3af;font-size:13px;\">lstm_example.py</div><pre style=\"margin:0;padding:20px;color:#e5e7eb;font-size:13px;line-height:1.7;overflow:auto;\"><span style='color:#60a5fa'># Sequence Processing Example</span>\nSentence = 'I love learning AI'\n\n<span style='color:#60a5fa'># Memory Flow</span>\nInput('I')        → Stored in memory\nInput('love')     → Combines previous context\nInput('learning') → Important information retained\nInput('AI')       → Complete meaning understood\n\n<span style='color:#60a5fa'>\n# TensorFlow / Keras</span>\nfrom tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import LSTM, Dense\n\nmodel = Sequential([\n    LSTM(64, input_shape=(100, 50)),\n    Dense(1)\n])\n\nmodel.summary()\n\n<span style='color:#60a5fa'># PyTorch</span>\nimport torch.nn as nn\n\nclass LSTMModel(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.lstm = nn.LSTM(\n            input_size=50,\n            hidden_size=64,\n            batch_first=True\n        )\n        self.fc = nn.Linear(64, 1)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        output, (hn, cn) = self.lstm(x)\n        return self.fc(output[:, -1, :])\n\n<span style='color:#60a5fa'># Parameters</span>\ninput_size  = Features per timestep\nhidden_size = Number of memory units\nhn          = Final Hidden State\ncn          = Final Cell State\nDense Layer = Final Prediction\n\n<span style='color:#22c55e'></pre></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit)",
            "explanation": "Researchers later asked an important question: Can we keep the memory power of LSTM while making it faster?\n\nThis led to the creation of the **GRU (Gated Recurrent Unit)**.In 2014, researchers realized LSTMs were highly accurate but computationally slow because of all those complex gates. They invented the **GRU**.\n\nThink of a GRU as an LSTM on a diet. It combines the Forget and Input gates into a single 'Update Gate', and it merges the Cell State and Hidden State together. \n\n* **RNN:** Fast, but terrible memory.\n* **LSTM:** Perfect memory, but slow and heavy.\n* **GRU:** The sweet spot. Excellent memory, but trains much faster than an LSTM."
          }
        },
        {
