    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-5-transformers",
      "title": "Attention Mechanism & Transformers",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Bottleneck of LSTMs",
            "explanation": "LSTMs solved many of the problems faced by traditional RNNs. However, they still have some limitations. In particular, they suffer from two major drawbacks.Let's discuss below.\n**1. The Information Bottleneck:**\nImagine reading a 1,000-word article. An LSTM processes it word-by-word, constantly trying to compress everything it reads into a single, fixed-size 'Hidden State' vector. By the time it reaches the end, the nuances of the first paragraph are almost entirely crushed and lost.\n\n**2. The Sequential Speed Limit:**\nBecause step `t` requires the output of step `t-1`, LSTMs are strictly sequential. You cannot process word 100 until you finish word 99. This means they **cannot be parallelized**. Thousands of GPU cores sit completely idle, waiting for the loop to finish. Training large models took months."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Birth of Attention",
            "explanation": "In 2014, a groundbreaking idea changed the way researchers thought about processing language. Until then, models like RNNs and LSTMs tried to compress an entire sentence or document into a small internal representation. This worked for short sentences, but as the text became longer, important information could be lost.\n\nResearchers asked a simple question: 'Why force the model to remember everything in a single memory?' Instead of compressing the entire input into one vector, they introduced the Attention Mechanism, which allows the model to keep access to all the words in the input sequence.\n\nThink of **Attention** like a student answering a question from a textbook. Instead of trying to memorize the entire book, the student quickly looks back at the relevant pages and focuses only on the important information. Similarly, when making a prediction, the model looks at all the words in the input, assigns an importance score (weight) to each word, and focuses more on the words that matter most. This ability to selectively focus on relevant information makes Attention highly effective for understanding long and complex text."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Self-Attention: Context is Everything",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! Now let's learn the most important idea behind modern AI models: Self-Attention.**Self-Attention** is the core idea behind Transformers. It allows every word in a sentence to look at all other words and decide which ones are important for understanding its meaning.\nWhy is this needed? Many words can have multiple meanings, and their actual meaning depends on the surrounding context.\nExample:\nSentence 1: 'I sat near the river bank.'\nSentence 2: 'I deposited money in the bank.'\nBoth sentences contain the word 'bank', but the meaning is different. In the first sentence, the word 'bank' pays more attention to 'river', so it understands that bank means the side of a river. In the second sentence, the word 'bank' pays more attention to 'money' and 'deposited', so it understands that bank means a financial institution.\n\n`How it works:`\n•Step 1: Every word creates Query, Key, and Value vectors.\n•Step 2: The current word compares its Query with the Keys of all other words.\n•Step 3: Important words receive higher attention scores.\n•Step 4: These scores are used to combine the Values of important words.\n•Step 5: The word updates its meaning using this contextual information.\n\nWithout Self-Attention:\n'bank' → Same meaning everywhere \nWith Self-Attention:\n'river' + 'bank' → River Side \n'money' + 'bank' → Financial Institution \n`Key Idea:` Self-Attention allows words to dynamically change their meaning based on the words around them, helping the model understand context much more accurately."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Query, Key, Value (QKV)",
            "explanation": "Great! But how does Self-Attention perform this calculation internally? To make this possible, every word creates three special vectors: Query (Q), Key (K),Value (V)\nImagine you are at a library:\n**Query (What I want):** You walk in and ask, *\"Do you have a book on Python?\"*\n**Key (What the library has):** You scan the spines of the books. The titles are the Keys. You compare your Query to the Keys to find a match.\n**Value (The actual content):** Once you find the matching Key, you pull out the book. The text inside is the Value.\n\nIn a neural network, every single word creates its own Q, K, and V vectors. To see how closely two words relate, the network takes the **Query** of Word A and multiplies it (dot product) with the **Key** of Word B. High scores mean high attention!\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/am1.png]\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How Attention Works?",
            "explanation": "The **Attention Mechanism** helps a model focus on the most important words in a sentence. Instead of treating every word equally, it calculates how much attention should be given to each word when understanding the current word.\n\nFor every input word, the model creates three vectors:\n\n• **Query (Q)** → What information am I looking for?\n• **Key (K)** → What information do I contain?\n• **Value (V)** → What information should I provide?\n\nThe Query is compared with every Key to calculate **Attention Scores**. These scores are then passed through the **Softmax** function to convert them into probabilities. Finally, these probabilities are multiplied with the Value vectors to create the **Context Vector**, which represents the meaning of the current word after considering the entire sentence.\n\nThe following example demonstrates how Attention works for the sentence **'I Love AI'**.\n\n```python\n# Attention Mechanism Workflow\n# Input Sentence → Embeddings → QKV Generation →\n# Attention Scores → Softmax → Context Vector\n\n# Practical Attention Example\nsentence = [\"I\", \"Love\", \"AI\"]\n\n# Step 1: Generate Embeddings\nI = Embedding(\"I\")\nLove = Embedding(\"Love\")\nAI = Embedding(\"AI\")\n\n# Step 2: Create Query, Key and Value\nQ_love = Query(Love)\n\nK_i = Key(I)\nK_love = Key(Love)\nK_ai = Key(AI)\n\nV_i = Value(I)\nV_love = Value(Love)\nV_ai = Value(AI)\n\n# Step 3: Calculate Attention Scores\nscore_i = Q_love @ K_i          # 20\nscore_love = Q_love @ K_love    # 30\nscore_ai = Q_love @ K_ai        # 50\n\nprint([score_i, score_love, score_ai])\n# [20, 30, 50]\n\n# Step 4: Apply Softmax\nweights = Softmax([20, 30, 50])\n\nprint(weights)\n# [0.2, 0.3, 0.5]\n\n# Step 5: Create Context Vector\ncontext = (\n    0.2 * V_i +\n    0.3 * V_love +\n    0.5 * V_ai\n)\n\nprint(context)\n# Context Vector\n\n# Output\nprint(\"Love pays highest attention to AI\")\n# Love pays highest attention to AI\n\nprint(\"Context-Aware Representation Generated\")\n# Context-Aware Representation Generated\n```\n\nIn this example, the word **'Love'** compares itself with every word in the sentence. Since **'AI'** receives the highest attention score, it contributes the most to the final **Context Vector**. This is how Transformers understand relationships between words, even when they are far apart in a sentence."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Head Attention",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! One attention mechanism works well.But researchers wondered:'What if multiple attention mechanisms worked together?'\nImagine five students reading the same sentence. One student focuses on grammar, another focuses on meaning, another looks for relationships between words, another tracks important entities, and another captures the overall context. Each student learns something different from the same sentence.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/nlp/am2.png]\n`Multi-Head Attention` works in a similar way. Instead of using a single attention mechanism, a Transformer uses multiple attention heads. Each head learns to focus on a different pattern or relationship in the text.\nFor example, one head may connect a pronoun to the correct noun, another may identify subject-verb relationships, while another may capture sentiment or topic information.\n\nAll heads process the sentence simultaneously and their outputs are combined together. This gives the model a richer and deeper understanding of the sentence than a single attention mechanism could provide."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Self Attention Review",
            "Question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "Q,K,V stands for ____.",
              "All heads work in ____.",
              "Self-Attention helps understand ____."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Question,Key,Value",
              "Meaning",
              "Sequential",
              "Query,Key,Value",
              "Parallel",
              "Context",
              "Query,Key,Vector"
