            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "case_study",
          "title": "Case Study: The Ambiguous Pronoun Resolution",
          "context": "Prior to 2017, standard translation models struggled heavily with pronouns in sentences like this: *\"The animal didn't cross the street because it was too tired.\"* When translating to languages with gendered nouns (like French or Spanish), the model didn't know if 'it' referred to the 'animal' or the 'street'.",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "How exactly does a Transformer solve this ambiguity without using an RNN loop?",
              "expectedAnswer": "Using Self-Attention. The model creates Query, Key, and Value vectors for the word 'it'. It computes the dot product of 'it' against every other word. Because it was trained on vast amounts of data, the attention score between 'it' and 'animal' (due to the context of 'tired') will be massive, while the score between 'it' and 'street' will be low."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "socratic",
          "topic": "The Cost of Attention",
          "seedQuestion": "If Transformers are so powerful because they look at every word simultaneously, what happens to the computational math when the document goes from 1,000 words to 100,000 words?",
          "guidingQuestions": [
            "If 10 words all look at 10 other words, how many connections is that?",
