    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-6-arch_transformers",
      "title": "Build Transformers From Scratch",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Lesson Overview: Building a Transformer from Scratch",
            "explanation": "In this lesson, we built the Transformer architecture from scratch using TensorFlow to understand how modern Large Language Models (LLMs) like GPT, Gemini, Claude, and Llama actually work internally.\n\nBefore Transformers, older models such as RNNs and LSTMs processed words one-by-one. This caused three major problems:\n\n• Slow Training → Words had to be processed sequentially.\n• Poor Long-Term Memory → Important information from earlier words was often forgotten.\n• Difficult Parallelization → GPUs could not efficiently process entire sentences at once.\n\nThe Transformer solved these problems using Self-Attention.\n\nThroughout this lesson we built:\n\n1. Positional Encoding → Helps the model understand word order.\n2. Multi-Head Attention → Allows the model to focus on multiple relationships simultaneously.\n3. Scaled Dot Product Attention → Calculates which words are important.\n4. Feed Forward Network → Learns deeper representations.\n5. Transformer Block → Combines Attention, Residual Connections, and Layer Normalization.\n6. Encoder → Understands the input sequence.\n7. Decoder → Generates the output sequence.\n8. Transformer Model → Connects Encoder and Decoder together.\n9. Forward Pass Testing → Verifies the model produces vocabulary predictions.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you have recreated the core architecture that powers modern Generative AI systems. While production models contain billions of parameters and thousands of GPUs, the fundamental building blocks remain exactly the same as the Transformer you implemented here.",
            "example": "Input Sentence:\n'The cat sat on the mat'\n\nTransformer Process:\nTokens → Embeddings → Positional Encoding → Multi-Head Attention → Feed Forward Network → Encoder → Decoder → Next Token Prediction\n\nOutput:\n'Mat' → predicts the next most likely word."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Importing Required Libraries",
            "explanation": "Welcome back! Before building a Transformer, we first need to import the required libraries.\n\nTensorFlow provides the deep learning layers used to build the Transformer architecture. NumPy helps us perform mathematical calculations for Positional Encoding. Keras layers such as Dense, Embedding, Dropout, and LayerNormalization will later be used inside the Attention and Encoder blocks.\n\nThink of this stage as gathering all the building materials before constructing a house. Without these libraries, we cannot create embeddings, attention layers, or neural networks.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Import Required Libraries</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>import</span> tensorflow <span style='color:#569CD6;'>as</span> tf<br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>from</span> tensorflow.keras.layers <span style='color:#569CD6;'>import</span> Dense, Input, Embedding, Dropout, LayerNormalization<br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>from</span> tensorflow.keras.models <span style='color:#569CD6;'>import</span> Model<br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>import</span> numpy <span style='color:#569CD6;'>as</span> np<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># TensorFlow → Deep Learning Framework</span><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># NumPy → Mathematical Operations</span><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Keras Layers → Transformer Components</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Positional Encoding",
            "explanation": "Before using Self-Attention, we add Positional Encoding to embeddings so the Transformer can understand the order of words. Let's implement the positional encoding function used in Transformer models.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Positional Encoding</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> positional_encoding(position, d_model):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;angle_rads = np.arange(position)[:, np.newaxis] / np.power(<br>&nbsp;10000,(2 * (np.arange(d_model) // 2)) / np.float32(d_model))<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;angle_rads[:, 0::2] = np.sin(angle_rads[:, 0::2])<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;angle_rads[:, 1::2] = np.cos(angle_rads[:, 1::2])<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>return</span> tf.cast(angle_rads[np.newaxis,...], dtype=tf.float32)<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Output → Position Aware Embeddings</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Multi-Head Attention",
            "explanation": "Now let's implement Multi-Head Attention, the core building block of Transformers. It creates Query (Q), Key (K), and Value (V) representations, splits them into multiple attention heads, processes them independently, and combines the results into a single output.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Multi Head Attention Class</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>class</span> MultiHeadAttention(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> __init__(self, d_model, num_heads):<br>&nbsp;super(MultiHeadAttention,self).__init__()<br>&nbsp;self.num_heads = num_heads<br>&nbsp;self.d_model = d_model<br><br>&nbsp;assert d_model % num_heads == 0<br><br>&nbsp;self.depth = d_model // num_heads<br><br>&nbsp;self.wq = Dense(d_model)<br>&nbsp;self.wk = Dense(d_model)<br>&nbsp;self.wv = Dense(d_model)<br><br>&nbsp;self.dense = Dense(d_model)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Query → Q</span><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Key → K</span><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Value → V</span><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Multiple Heads Learn Different Relationships</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Scaled Dot-Product Attention",
            "explanation": "Now let's implement Scaled Dot-Product Attention. This function computes attention scores using Query (Q) and Key (K), scales them by √dk, applies Softmax, and uses the resulting weights to generate a context-aware output from the Value (V) vectors.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Scaled Dot Product Attention</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> scaled_dot_product_attention(self, q, k, v, mask):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;matmul_qk = tf.matmul(q, k, transpose_b=True)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;dk = tf.cast(tf.shape(k)[-1], tf.float32)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;scaled_attention_logits = matmul_qk / tf.math.sqrt(dk)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>if</span> mask is not None:<br>&nbsp;scaled_attention_logits += (mask * -1e9)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;attention_weights = tf.nn.softmax(<br>&nbsp;scaled_attention_logits,<br>&nbsp;axis=-1<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;output = tf.matmul(attention_weights, v)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>return</span> output, attention_weights<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Q × Kᵀ → Scale → Softmax → V → Context Vector</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Feed Forward Network (FFN)",
            "explanation": "After the attention layer produces context-aware representations, each token is processed by a Feed Forward Network (FFN). The FFN applies two Dense layers with a ReLU activation in between to learn richer feature representations.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Position Wise Feed Forward Network</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>class</span> PositionwiseFeedForward(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> __init__(self, d_model, dff):<br>&nbsp;super(PositionwiseFeedForward,self).__init__()<br><br>&nbsp;self.dense1 = Dense(<br>dff,<br>activation='relu'<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;self.dense2 = Dense(d_model)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> call(self, x):<br>&nbsp;x = self.dense1(x)<br>&nbsp;x = self.dense2(x)<br>&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>return</span> x<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Token → ReLU Network → Rich Features</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Transformer Block",
            "explanation": "Now we'll combine Multi-Head Attention, Layer Normalization, Residual Connections, and the Feed Forward Network to build a complete Transformer Block—the core component used throughout Transformer models.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Transformer Block</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>class</span> TransformerBlock(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> __init__(self, d_model, num_heads, dff, dropout_rate=0.1):<br>&nbsp;super(TransformerBlock,self).__init__()<br><br>&nbsp;self.att = MultiHeadAttention(d_model, num_heads)<br>&nbsp;self.ffn = PositionwiseFeedForward(d_model, dff)<br><br>&nbsp;self.layernorm1 = LayerNormalization(epsilon=1e-6)<br>&nbsp;self.layernorm2 = LayerNormalization(epsilon=1e-6)<br><br>&nbsp;self.dropout1 = Dropout(dropout_rate)<br>&nbsp;self.dropout2 = Dropout(dropout_rate)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> call(self, x, training, mask):<br><br>&nbsp;attn_output = self.att(x, x, x, mask)<br>&nbsp;attn_output = self.dropout1(attn_output, training=training)<br><br>&nbsp;out1 = self.layernorm1(x + attn_output)<br><br>&nbsp;ffn_output = self.ffn(out1)<br>&nbsp;ffn_output = self.dropout2(ffn_output, training=training)<br><br>&nbsp;out2 = self.layernorm2(out1 + ffn_output)<br><br>&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>return</span> out2<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Attention → Add & Norm → FFN → Add & Norm</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Encoder",
            "explanation": "Now we'll build the Transformer Encoder. It combines token embeddings, positional encoding, and multiple Transformer Blocks to generate contextual representations for each token in the input sequence.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Encoder Layer</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>class</span> Encoder(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> __init__(self, num_layers, d_model, num_heads, dff,<br>&nbsp;input_vocab_size, maximum_position_encoding,<br>&nbsp;dropout_rate=0.1):<br><br>&nbsp;super(Encoder,self).__init__()<br><br>&nbsp;self.embedding = Embedding(<br>input_vocab_size,<br>d_model<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;self.pos_encoding = positional_encoding(<br>maximum_position_encoding,<br>d_model<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;self.enc_layers = [<br>TransformerBlock(<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;d_model,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;num_heads,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;dff<br>)<br>for _ in range(num_layers)<br>&nbsp;]<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> call(self, x, training, mask):<br>&nbsp;x = self.embedding(x)<br>&nbsp;x += self.pos_encoding<br><br>&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>for</span> layer <span style='color:#569CD6;'>in</span> self.enc_layers:<br>x = layer(x, training, mask)<br><br>&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>return</span> x<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Tokens → Embeddings → Encoder Blocks → Context Vectors</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Decoder",
            "explanation": "Now we'll build the Transformer Decoder. It combines embeddings, positional encoding, masked self-attention, encoder-decoder attention, and feed forward networks to generate output tokens sequentially.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Decoder Layer</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>class</span> Decoder(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> __init__(self, num_layers, d_model, num_heads, dff,<br>&nbsp;target_vocab_size, maximum_position_encoding,<br>&nbsp;dropout_rate=0.1):<br><br>&nbsp;super(Decoder,self).__init__()<br><br>&nbsp;self.embedding = Embedding(<br>target_vocab_size,<br>d_model<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;self.pos_encoding = positional_encoding(<br>maximum_position_encoding,<br>d_model<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;self.dec_layers = [<br>TransformerBlock(<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;d_model,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;num_heads,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;dff<br>)<br>for _ in range(num_layers)<br>&nbsp;]<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> call(self, x, enc_output, training,<br>&nbsp;look_ahead_mask, padding_mask):<br><br>&nbsp;x = self.embedding(x)<br>&nbsp;x += self.pos_encoding<br><br>&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>for</span> layer <span style='color:#569CD6;'>in</span> self.dec_layers:<br>x = layer(x, training, look_ahead_mask)<br><br>&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>return</span> x<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Previous Tokens + Encoder Output → Next Token Generation</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Complete Transformer Model",
            "explanation": "Let's build the complete Transformer architecture by combining the Encoder, Decoder, and a final output layer that predicts the next token.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Complete Transformer Model</span><br><span style='color:#569CD6;'>class</span> Transformer(tf.keras.Model):<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> __init__(self, num_layers, d_model, num_heads, dff,<br>&nbsp;input_vocab_size, target_vocab_size,<br>&nbsp;maximum_position_encoding):<br><br>&nbsp;super(Transformer,self).__init__()<br><br>&nbsp;self.encoder = Encoder(<br>num_layers,<br>d_model,<br>num_heads,<br>dff,<br>input_vocab_size,<br>maximum_position_encoding<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;self.decoder = Decoder(<br>num_layers,<br>d_model,<br>num_heads,<br>dff,<br>target_vocab_size,<br>maximum_position_encoding<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;self.final_layer = Dense(target_vocab_size)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>def</span> call(self, inputs, training=False):<br><br>&nbsp;inp, tar = inputs<br><br>&nbsp;enc_output = self.encoder(<br>inp,<br>training,<br>None<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;dec_output = self.decoder(<br>tar,<br>enc_output,<br>training,<br>None,<br>None<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;final_output = self.final_layer(dec_output)<br><br>&nbsp;<span style='color:#569CD6;'>return</span> final_output<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Encoder → Decoder → Dense → Next Token Probability</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Training and Testing the Transformer",
            "explanation": "Now we'll initialize the Transformer model, create sample input and target sequences, perform a forward pass, and inspect the output predictions generated by the model.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Training & Testing Transformer</span><br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Model Hyperparameters</span><br>num_layers = 4<br>d_model = 128<br>num_heads = 8<br>dff = 512<br><br>input_vocab_size = 8500<br>target_vocab_size = 8000<br>maximum_position_encoding = 10000<br>dropout_rate = 0.1<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Create Transformer Model</span><br>transformer = Transformer(<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;num_layers,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;d_model,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;num_heads,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;dff,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;input_vocab_size,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;target_vocab_size,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;maximum_position_encoding<br>)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Sample Input Batch</span><br>inputs = tf.random.uniform(<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;(64, 50),<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;minval=0,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;maxval=input_vocab_size,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;dtype=tf.int64<br>)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Sample Target Batch</span><br>targets = tf.random.uniform(<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;(64, 50),<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;minval=0,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;maxval=target_vocab_size,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;dtype=tf.int64<br>)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Forward Pass</span><br>output = transformer(<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;(inputs, targets),<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;training=True<br>)<br><br>print(output.shape)<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Output Shape:</span><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>(64, 50, 8000)</span><br><br><span style='color:#808080;'>64 = Batch Size</span><br><span style='color:#808080;'>50 = Sequence Length</span><br><span style='color:#808080;'>8000 = Vocabulary Predictions</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Assembling the Complete Transformer",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We have already built all major Transformer components in previous lessons.\nNow we simply connect all these building blocks together to create the complete Transformer architecture.\nNotice that we are not rewriting those classes again. We reuse the classes created in the previous slides and combine them into one final model.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Previous Components (Already Created)</span><br>def positional_encoding(position,d_model):<br># Use the Positional Encoding implementation from the previous slide<br><br>class MultiHeadAttention(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br># Use the Multi-Head Attention implementation from the previous slide<br><br>class PositionwiseFeedforward(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br># Use the Feed Forward Network implementation from the previous slide<br><br>class TransformerBlock(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br># Use the Transformer Block implementation from the previous slide<br><br>class Encoder(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br># Use the Encoder implementation from the previous slide<br><br>class Decoder(tf.keras.layers.Layer):<br># Use the Decoder implementation from the previous slide<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Final Transformer Model</span><br>class Transformer(Model):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def__init__(self,num_layers,d_model,num_heads,dff,input_vocab_size,target_vocab_size,maximum_position_encoding,dropout_rate=0.1):<br>&nbsp;super().__init__()<br><br>&nbsp;self.encoder = Encoder(num_layers,d_model,num_heads,dff,input_vocab_size,maximum_position_encoding,dropout_rate)<br>&nbsp;self.decoder = Decoder(num_layers,d_model,num_heads,dff,target_vocab_size,maximum_position_encoding,dropout_rate)<br>&nbsp;self.final_layer = Dense(target_vocab_size)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def call(self,inputs,training=False,look_ahead_mask=None,padding_mask=None):<br>&nbsp;inp,tar = inputs<br><br>&nbsp;enc_output = self.encoder(inp,training=training,mask=padding_mask)<br><br>&nbsp;dec_output,_ = self.decoder(tar,enc_output,training=training,look_ahead_mask=look_ahead_mask,padding_mask=padding_mask)<br><br>&nbsp;final_output = self.final_layer(dec_output)<br><br>&nbsp;return final_output<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>All Previously Built Components Combined Into One Transformer ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Initializing and Testing the Transformer",
            "explanation": "The final step is creating the Transformer object and testing it with sample input data.\n\nHere we define:\n• Number of Encoder/Decoder Layers\n• Embedding Size (d_model)\n• Number of Attention Heads\n• Feed Forward Network Size\n• Vocabulary Sizes\n• Maximum Sequence Length\n\nAfter initialization, we generate sample token sequences and perform a forward pass through the Transformer.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Hyperparameters</span><br>num_layers=2<br>d_model=128<br>num_heads=8<br>dff=512<br>input_vocab_size=8500<br>target_vocab_size=8000<br>maximum_position_encoding=10000<br>dropout_rate=0.1<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Build Transformer</span><br>transformer=Transformer(num_layers,d_model,num_heads,dff,input_vocab_size,target_vocab_size,maximum_position_encoding,dropout_rate)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Sample Input</span><br>inputs=tf.random.uniform((64,50),minval=0,maxval=input_vocab_size,dtype=tf.int64)<br>targets=tf.random.uniform((64,50),minval=0,maxval=target_vocab_size,dtype=tf.int64)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Forward Pass</span><br>output=transformer((inputs,targets),training=True)<br>print(output.shape)<br><br><span style='color:#808080;'>Output Shape → (64,50,8000)</span><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Transformer Successfully Built & Tested ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Create a Positional Encoding function that generates positional vectors for a sequence length of 50 and embedding size of 128. Print the resulting tensor shape.",
            "starterCode": "import tensorflow as tf\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Complete positional_encoding function\n",
            "solution": "import tensorflow as tf\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef positional_encoding(position, d_model):\n    angle_rads = np.arange(position)[:, np.newaxis] / np.power(\n        10000,\n        (2 * (np.arange(d_model) // 2)) / np.float32(d_model)\n    )\n\n    angle_rads[:, 0::2] = np.sin(angle_rads[:, 0::2])\n    angle_rads[:, 1::2] = np.cos(angle_rads[:, 1::2])\n\n    pos_encoding = angle_rads[np.newaxis, ...]\n    return tf.cast(pos_encoding, dtype=tf.float32)\n\nencoding = positional_encoding(50, 128)\nprint(encoding.shape)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use sine for even positions and cosine for odd positions.",
            "explanation": "Positional Encoding gives Transformers information about word order because self-attention alone does not understand sequence positions."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-7-metrics",
      "title": "NLP Evaluation Metrics & End-to-End Evaluation",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#e11d48",
        "accent": "#be123c"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Grading Problem: Why Accuracy is a Liar",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! So far, we have learned how NLP models classify text, translate languages, generate content, and summarize documents.But now an important question arises. how do you actually know if it is 'good'? \n\nMost beginners just look at Accuracy. But in the real world, Accuracy will lie to you. Imagine you are building an AI to detect a rare disease that only 1 in 100 people have. Your AI is incredibly lazy and just predicts 'Healthy' for every single person. \n\nBecause 99 out of 100 people are healthy, your AI is technically 99% Accurate! But as a medical tool, it is completely useless because it missed the 1 sick person. To truly evaluate NLP classification models (like spam detectors or sentiment analyzers), we have to look deeper.",
            "example": "If your Gmail Spam Filter just let every single email into your inbox without checking them, \nit would still be 90% 'accurate' because most emails aren't spam. But you would hate it.\n We need metrics that measure specific types of mistakes."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Confusion Matrix: Sorting the Mistakes",
            "explanation": "Once researchers realized Accuracy wasn't enough, they needed a better way to understand model mistakes.\nInstead of looking at one final score, they decided to categorize every prediction.This led to the creation of the **Confusion Matrix**.\n\n* `True Positives (TP)`: The AI predicted SPAM, and it WAS spam. (Good job!)\n* `True Negatives (TN)`: The AI predicted NOT SPAM, and it was a regular email. (Good job!)\n* `False Positives (FP)`: The AI predicted SPAM, but it was an email from your boss! (False Alarm / Type I Error).\n* `False Negatives (FN)`: The AI predicted NOT SPAM, but a hacker's email got into your inbox! (A Miss / Type II Error).",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:500px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#fff1f2; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #fecdd3; box-shadow:0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);\"><h4 style=\"color:#881337; margin-top:0; margin-bottom:15px;\">The Confusion Matrix</h4><div style=\"display:grid; grid-template-columns: 80px 1fr 1fr; grid-template-rows: 40px 1fr 1fr; gap:5px;\"><div style=\"grid-column:2/4; font-weight:bold; color:#4c0519; display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center;\">AI Prediction</div><div style=\"grid-row:2/4; font-weight:bold; color:#4c0519; writing-mode:vertical-rl; transform:rotate(180deg); display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center;\">Actual Truth</div><div style=\"background:#bbf7d0; padding:15px; border-radius:6px; border:2px solid #22c55e; display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center;\"><strong style=\"color:#166534;\">True Positive (TP)</strong><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#14532d;\">Correctly caught spam</span></div><div style=\"background:#fecaca; padding:15px; border-radius:6px; border:2px solid #ef4444; display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center;\"><strong style=\"color:#991b1b;\">False Positive (FP)</strong><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#7f1d1d;\">Flagged a normal email</span></div><div style=\"background:#fecaca; padding:15px; border-radius:6px; border:2px solid #ef4444; display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center;\"><strong style=\"color:#991b1b;\">False Negative (FN)</strong><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#7f1d1d;\">Missed a spam email</span></div><div style=\"background:#bbf7d0; padding:15px; border-radius:6px; border:2px solid #22c55e; display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center;\"><strong style=\"color:#166534;\">True Negative (TN)</strong><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#14532d;\">Correctly ignored normal email</span></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Precision, Recall, and the F1 Score",
            "explanation": "Great! We can now identify different types of mistakes using the Confusion Matrix.But another question appears.Are all mistakes equally important?\nThe answer is no.For example, in spam detection, we do not want important emails to be wrongly blocked.Thats why we learned some other metrics. \n\n**1. Precision:** Out of all the emails the AI *claimed* were spam, how many actually were? \n`Precision = TP / (TP + FP)`\n\n**2. Recall:** Out of all the *actual* spam emails hidden in the inbox, how many did the AI successfully find?\n`Recall = TP / (TP + FN)`\n\n**3. F1 Score:** Precision and Recall are a tug-of-war. If you make the AI super aggressive to catch ALL spam (High Recall), it will accidentally block real emails (Low Precision). The F1 Score is the harmonic mean of both, giving you one balanced final grade.\n`F1 Score = 2 * (Precision * Recall) / (Precision + Recall)`"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Evaluating Language Models: Perplexity",
            "explanation": "What if your AI isn't sorting spam, but is actually writing text (like ChatGPT)? We can't use Precision or Recall for text generation. Instead, we use a metric called **Perplexity**.\n\nPerplexity measures how 'surprised' or 'confused' the AI is by real human text. A lower score is better.\n\n`Perplexity = 2 ^ (Cross Entropy Loss)`\n\nWhen a Language Model reads a human sentence, it constantly tries to guess the next word. If the human sentence is 'I want to eat a...', the AI gives high probability to 'apple' or 'sandwich'. When the actual next word is 'sandwich', the AI is not surprised (Low Perplexity). If the next word was 'bulldozer', the AI would be highly confused (High Perplexity).```bash\n# Install Required Libraries\npip install transformers evaluate torch\n\n```python\n# Import Library\nimport evaluate\n\n# Load Perplexity Metric\nperplexity = evaluate.load('perplexity',module_type='metric')\n\n# Sample Text\npredictions = ['The cat sat on the mat.']\n# Calculate Perplexity\nresults = perplexity.compute(model_id='gpt2',predictions=predictions)\nprint('Perplexity:', results['mean_perplexity'])\n# Perplexity: 12.84\n#Lower Perplexity → Better Language Model Predictions"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Evaluating Translation: BLEU Score",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We have learned how to evaluate classification models and language models.\nBut what if our AI is translating one language into another?Imagine translating a French sentence into English.The challenge is that there can be many correct translations for the same sentence.so how do we measure translation quality?\nThis is where the **BLEU Score (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy)** comes in.\n\nThe **BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy)** score solves this. It relies heavily on Precision. It takes the AI's translated sentence and compares it against multiple human reference translations. \n\nIt counts how many n-grams (1-word, 2-word, 3-word chunks) in the AI's sentence actually appear anywhere in the human reference sentences. \n\n`BLEU Score ranges from 0 to 1.` (1 means perfect exact word overlap).\nFor clear understanding check the below example.",
            "example": "Reference 1: 'The cat is on the mat'\nReference 2: 'There is a cat on the mat'\n\nAI Output: 'The cat sits on the mat'\n\nMatched Words:'The' ✓,'cat' ✓,'sits' ✗,'on' ✓,'the' ✓,'mat' ✓\nBLEU Calculation (Simplified Unigram Precision):\nMatched Words = 5\nTotal AI Words = 6\n\nBLEU Score = 5 / 6 = 0.833 = 83.3%\n\nResult: The BLEU score is high because most of the words in the AI-generated sentence also appear in the reference sentence.\nIn real-world applications, you don't calculate the BLEU score manually. Instead, Python provides a built-in function called `sentence_bleu()` from the NLTK library.\n\nfrom nltk.translate.bleu_score import sentence_bleu, SmoothingFunction\n\nreferences = [[\"the\", \"cat\", \"sat\", \"on\", \"the\", \"mat\"]]\ncandidate = [\"the\", \"cat\", \"sat\", \"on\", \"mat\"]\n\n# Smoothing helps avoid zero BLEU scores for short sentences\nsmooth = SmoothingFunction().method1\n\nscore = sentence_bleu(references, candidate, smoothing_function=smooth)\nprint(f\"BLEU Score: {score:.4f}\")"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Evaluating Summarization: ROUGE Score",
            "explanation": "Let's discuss another important evaluation metric.\n\nWhile BLEU is commonly used for machine translation and focuses on Precision, ROUGE focuses on Recall and is widely used for evaluating text summarization.\n\nThink of it this way: BLEU asks, 'How much of the AI-generated text matches the human reference?' In contrast, ROUGE asks, 'How much of the important information from the human reference was successfully captured by the AI?'\n\nFor example, if a human writes a summary of an article, ROUGE measures how much of that summary's key content appears in the AI-generated summary. A higher ROUGE score indicates that the AI was able to recall more of the important information from the reference summary.",
            "example": "```bash\n# Install the library\npip install rouge-score\n```\n\n```python\n# Import ROUGE scorer\nfrom rouge_score import rouge_scorer\n\n# Initialize the scorer\nscorer = rouge_scorer.RougeScorer(\n    ['rouge1', 'rouge2', 'rougeL'],\n    use_stemmer=True\n)\n\n# Reference Summary\nreference_text = 'the cat sat on the mat'\n\n# AI Generated Summary\ncandidate_text = 'the cat sat on mat'\n\n# Calculate ROUGE scores\nscores = scorer.score(\n    reference_text,\n    candidate_text\n)\n\n# Display the scores\nfor metric, result in scores.items():\n    print(f'{metric.upper()}:')\n    print(f'Precision : {result.precision:.3f}')\n    print(f'Recall    : {result.recall:.3f}')\n    print(f'F1-Score  : {result.fmeasure:.3f}\\n')\n```\n\n**Example Output**\n\n```text\nROUGE1:\nPrecision : 0.800\nRecall    : 1.000\nF1-Score  : 0.889\n\nROUGE2:\nPrecision : 0.667\nRecall    : 1.000\nF1-Score  : 0.800\n\nROUGEL:\nPrecision : 0.800\nRecall    : 1.000\nF1-Score  : 0.889\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Flaw of Exact Matching: METEOR & Semantic Similarity",
            "explanation": "Wonderful! We now know how BLEU evaluates translations and how ROUGE evaluates summaries.\nBut both methods have an important limitation.\n\nIf the human reference says 'The dog is enormous', and the AI translates it as 'The hound is gigantic', BLEU and ROUGE will give a score of 0.0! They are too stupid to realize 'dog/hound' and 'enormous/gigantic' mean the same thing.\n\n**METEOR** fixes this slightly by including an internal dictionary to reward synonyms. \n\nHowever, the modern standard is **Semantic Similarity Evaluation** (like BERTScore). Instead of matching raw words, it converts both the human reference and the AI output into dense Word Embeddings. It then calculates the Cosine Similarity between the two vectors. It grades the AI purely on meaning, entirely ignoring the specific words used!",
            "example": "AI: 'The restaurant was terrible.'\nHuman: 'The food was awful.'\nBLEU Score: 0% (No exact overlapping words).\nBERTScore (Semantic): 92% (The vector mathematics prove the underlying meaning is nearly identical)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Self-Driving Car Dilemma",
          "description": "You are leading the NLP and Computer Vision team for a self-driving car company. Your model detects Stop Signs and alerts the brakes. During testing, you look at the Confusion Matrix. You must choose how to tune the model's threshold.",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Tune the model to maximize Precision. We don't want the car to brake randomly for red billboards (False Positives).",
              "outcome": "Dangerous choice! If you maximize Precision, the AI becomes hesitant. It will ignore a few real stop signs (False Negatives) just to be safe. Ignoring a real stop sign causes a crash.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Tune the model to maximize Recall. We absolutely must catch every single Stop Sign, even if it means the AI occasionally triggers the brakes for a red billboard.",
              "outcome": "Correct! In life-or-death scenarios, a False Negative (missing a stop sign) is fatal. A False Positive (braking for a billboard) is annoying but safe. You must maximize Recall!",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "Tune the model for Perplexity.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Perplexity is for text generation models, not binary classification tasks like Stop Sign detection.",
              "score": 10
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "NLP Evaluation Metrics",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct evaluation metric into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "____ is commonly used to evaluate Machine Translation.",
              "____ is mainly used to evaluate Text Summarization.",
              "____ measures how well a Language Model predicts the next word.",
              "____ combines Precision and Recall for Binary Classification tasks."
            ],
            "options": [
              "Perplexity",
              "F1 Score",
              "BLEU Score",
              "ROUGE Score",
              "Accuracy",
              "Mean Squared Error"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "BLEU Score",
              "blank_2": "ROUGE Score",
              "blank_3": "Perplexity",
              "blank_4": "F1 Score"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "case_study",
          "title": "Case Study: The Resume Filtering AI",
          "context": "A tech company built an NLP agent to automatically read resumes and filter out bad candidates. The company receives 10,000 resumes a day. Only 100 of them are actually qualified. \n\nThe AI was deployed, and it instantly rejected all 10,000 candidates. The engineering team looked at the dashboard, saw a '99% Accuracy' rating, and went home to celebrate.",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Why was the 99% Accuracy rating a complete illusion?",
              "expectedAnswer": "Because the dataset was massively imbalanced. 9,900 people were unqualified. By simply guessing 'Reject' for everyone, the AI was technically correct 99% of the time, but it completely failed to find the 100 qualified candidates (Zero True Positives)."
            },
            {
              "question": "If the company wants to ensure they do not miss any hidden superstar candidates, which metric should they instruct the engineering team to optimize?",
              "expectedAnswer": "Recall. Optimizing for Recall ensures the AI catches the maximum number of True Positives (good candidates), even if it accidentally lets a few unqualified people through to the interview stage (False Positives)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "speed_quiz",
          "timePerQuestion": 12,
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Which of the following is considered a 'Type I Error' (A False Alarm)?",
              "options": [
                "False Positive",
                "False Negative",
                "True Positive",
                "True Negative"
              ],
              "answer": 0
            },
            {
              "question": "If an AI language model is highly 'surprised' by a sequence of text because it defies the grammatical probabilities it learned, what happens to its Perplexity score?",
              "options": [
                "It drops to zero.",
                "It stays exactly the same.",
                "It gets very high."
              ],
              "answer": 2
            },
            {
              "question": "Which translation metric attempts to fix the 'exact match' flaw of BLEU by using an internal dictionary to reward synonyms?",
              "options": [
                "ROUGE",
                "F1 Score",
                "METEOR"
              ],
              "answer": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M004-nlp-lesson-8-capstone",
      "title": "Capstone Project: End-to-End Sentiment Analysis",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#f59e0b",
        "accent": "#d97706"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Mission: The PR Crisis",
            "explanation": "Welcome to your final project. You have just been hired as the Lead AI Engineer for 'CineStream', a massive movie streaming platform.\n\nOver the weekend, CineStream released a highly anticipated, multi-million dollar original movie. Overnight, 50,000 user reviews flooded the platform. The CEO bursts into your office: *\"Are people loving it or hating it? I need a report in 10 minutes!\"*\n\nYou cannot physically read 50,000 reviews in 10 minutes. You need to build a Machine Learning pipeline that can read the raw text of a review and automatically classify it as Positive (1) or Negative (0). This is called Sentiment Analysis."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: The NLP Pipeline Recap",
            "explanation": "To solve this, we cannot just throw raw English text at a neural network. We have to build an end-to-end pipeline using everything we have learned in this course.\n\nOur pipeline will look like this:\n**1. Data Cleaning:** Strip out HTML tags, punctuation, and lowercase everything.\n**2. Tokenization:** Break the paragraphs into lists of individual words.\n**3. Word Embeddings:** Convert those words into dense mathematical vectors so the computer understands their meaning.\n**4. Sequence Modeling:** Feed those vectors into an LSTM or Transformer so the AI understands the *order* and *context* of the words.\n**5. Prediction:** Output a final score between 0 and 1.",
            "example": "Raw Text: 'I LOVED this movie!!! <br>' -> Cleaned: 'i loved this movie' -> Tokens: ['i', 'loved', 'this', 'movie'] -> Model -> 98% Positive."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Step 2: Data Preprocessing. You have loaded the 50,000 reviews into a Pandas DataFrame. Use Keras to Tokenize the text and pad the sequences so every review is exactly 100 words long (neural networks require uniform input sizes!).",
            "starterCode": "from tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.text import Tokenizer\nfrom tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.sequence import pad_sequences\n\nreviews = [\"This movie was amazing\", \"I absolutely hated the plot\", \"It was okay\"]\n\n# 1. Initialize the Tokenizer to keep the top 10,000 most common words\ntokenizer = Tokenizer(num_words=____)\n\n# 2. Fit the tokenizer on our text\ntokenizer.____(reviews)\n\n# 3. Convert the English text into sequences of numbers\nsequences = tokenizer.texts_to_sequences(reviews)\n\n# 4. Pad the sequences so they are all exactly 100 numbers long\npadded_data = ____(sequences, maxlen=100)\n\nprint(padded_data[0])",
            "solution": "from tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.text import Tokenizer\nfrom tensorflow.keras.preprocessing.sequence import pad_sequences\n\nreviews = [\"This movie was amazing\", \"I absolutely hated the plot\", \"It was okay\"]\n\n# 1. Initialize the Tokenizer to keep the top 10,000 most common words\ntokenizer = Tokenizer(num_words=10000)\n\n# 2. Fit the tokenizer on our text\ntokenizer.fit_on_texts(reviews)\n\n# 3. Convert the English text into sequences of numbers\nsequences = tokenizer.texts_to_sequences(reviews)\n\n# 4. Pad the sequences so they are all exactly 100 numbers long\npadded_data = pad_sequences(sequences, maxlen=100)\n\nprint(padded_data[0])",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `Tokenizer(num_words=10000)`, `.fit_on_texts()`, and finally `pad_sequences()` to ensure uniform length."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 3: Choosing the Brain (LSTM)",
            "explanation": "Now that our text is converted into numbers, we need to build the Neural Network.\n\nWhy not use a standard, basic Dense Neural Network or Bag of Words? Because sequence matters in reviews! Consider the sentence: *\"The movie was not good, it was terrible.\"* If we ignore the sequence, the AI just sees the word 'good' and might guess Positive. \n\nWe will use an **LSTM (Long Short-Term Memory)** network. As the LSTM reads the review word-by-word, its Cell State will remember the word 'not' and apply that negative context to the word 'good'.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family:sans-serif; text-align:center; max-width:600px; margin:auto; padding:20px; background:#f0fdfa; border-radius:12px; border:1px solid #5eead4;\"><h4 style=\"color:#0f766e; margin-top:0; margin-bottom:15px;\">The Keras Architecture</h4><div style=\"display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:10px; align-items:center;\"><div style=\"background:#ffffff; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #94a3b8; width:80%; font-weight:bold; color:#334155;\">1. Input Layer (100 Tokens)</div><div style=\"font-size:20px; color:#0f766e;\">↓</div><div style=\"background:#dbeafe; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #3b82f6; width:80%;\"><strong style=\"color:#1e40af;\">2. Embedding Layer</strong><br><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#1e3a8a;\">Converts the 100 raw numbers into rich, dense Semantic Vectors (Word2Vec)</span></div><div style=\"font-size:20px; color:#0f766e;\">↓</div><div style=\"background:#fef08a; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #eab308; width:80%;\"><strong style=\"color:#854d0e;\">3. LSTM Layer</strong><br><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#713f12;\">Reads the vectors in order, maintaining context and tracking negation</span></div><div style=\"font-size:20px; color:#0f766e;\">↓</div><div style=\"background:#fecaca; padding:15px; border-radius:8px; border:2px solid #ef4444; width:80%;\"><strong style=\"color:#991b1b;\">4. Dense Output (Sigmoid)</strong><br><span style=\"font-size:12px; color:#7f1d1d;\">Squeezes the final LSTM thought into a probability between 0.0 (Hate) and 1.0 (Love)</span></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Step 4: Build the Model. Translate the architecture diagram above into actual Keras code. Build a Sequential model with an Embedding layer, an LSTM layer, and a final Dense output layer.",
            "starterCode": "from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Embedding, LSTM, Dense\n\n# Vocabulary size = 10,000. Sequence length = 100.\nmodel = Sequential()\n\n# 1. Add an Embedding layer (input_dim=10000, output_dim=64, input_length=100)\nmodel.add(____)\n\n# 2. Add an LSTM layer with 128 hidden units\nmodel.add(____)\n\n# 3. Add a Dense output layer with 1 neuron and a 'sigmoid' activation function\nmodel.add(____)\n\n# Compile the model for binary classification\nmodel.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='binary_crossentropy', metrics=['accuracy'])\nmodel.summary()",
            "solution": "from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential\nfrom tensorflow.keras.layers import Embedding, LSTM, Dense\n\n# Vocabulary size = 10,000. Sequence length = 100.\nmodel = Sequential()\n\n# 1. Add an Embedding layer (input_dim=10000, output_dim=64, input_length=100)\nmodel.add(Embedding(input_dim=10000, output_dim=64, input_length=100))\n\n# 2. Add an LSTM layer with 128 hidden units\nmodel.add(LSTM(128))\n\n# 3. Add a Dense output layer with 1 neuron and a 'sigmoid' activation function\nmodel.add(Dense(1, activation='sigmoid'))\n\n# Compile the model for binary classification\nmodel.compile(optimizer='adam', loss='binary_crossentropy', metrics=['accuracy'])\nmodel.summary()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use `Embedding(...)`, `LSTM(128)`, and `Dense(1, activation='sigmoid')` since we only need to output a single probability for Positive/Negative."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Sarcasm Dilemma",
          "description": "Your LSTM model finishes training. You test it by typing a custom review into the console: *\"Oh, brilliant. Another superhero movie where the sky shoots a blue laser. Just what I always wanted. A true cinematic masterpiece.\"*\n\nThe model outputs a score of 0.98 (98% Positive). The CEO sees this and is furious. What went wrong?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "The model suffered from the Vanishing Gradient problem.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. LSTMs are specifically designed to prevent the Vanishing Gradient. The problem here is semantic, not mathematical degradation.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "The model failed to understand Sarcasm. It saw strong positive word embeddings ('brilliant', 'masterpiece') and lacked the deep world-context to realize they were being used ironically.",
              "outcome": "Correct! Sarcasm is the final boss of Sentiment Analysis. Standard LSTMs struggle with it because the literal words are positive. Fixing this usually requires upgrading to massive Transformer models (like BERT) that have read the whole internet and understand subtle sarcastic context.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "The learning rate was too high during backpropagation.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. While a high learning rate can cause instability, it doesn't cause a model to specifically fail at sarcasm. This is a data context issue.",
              "score": 10
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 5: The Final Deployment",
            "explanation": "Despite the sarcasm bug, your model achieves an F1 Score of 91% on the testing vault! It is ready for the real world.\n\nYou save the model as an `.h5` file and hand it to the Backend Engineering team. They wrap your AI in a Python Flask API. \n\nNow, every time a user clicks 'Submit Review' on the website, the text is instantly sent to your API. Your AI cleans the text, tokenizes it, runs it through the LSTM, and returns a score in milliseconds. You run all 50,000 weekend reviews through the model. You generate a beautiful pie chart showing the CEO that 78% of the reviews are overwhelmingly positive. You just saved the company thousands of hours of manual reading.",
            "example": "This exact pipeline is how Amazon sorts product reviews, how Twitter flags toxic hate speech, and how banks automatically route angry customer service emails to priority managers."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M005_llm_01",
      "title": "Getting Started with LLMs",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is a Large Language Model (LLM)?",
            "explanation": "Excellent! In the previous lessons, we learned about Transformers and Attention Mechanisms. Now let's see what happens when we train a Transformer on an enormous amount of text.This creates a Large Language Model, commonly called an LLM.\n\nA **Large Language Model (LLM)** is a type of AI model trained to understand and generate human language. It learns by reading a massive amount of text such as books, websites, articles, and code.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/llm/in1.png]\nThink of it like a smart autocomplete system. When you type a sentence, the model looks at the words you have already written and predicts what word (or token) should come next.\nFor example:\nInput: 'The sky is'\nPossible predictions:\n• blue (80%)\n• cloudy (15%)\n• green (5%)\n\nThe model selects one of these predictions and then repeats the process again for the next word. By predicting one token at a time, it can generate complete sentences, paragraphs, stories, code, and answers.\nLLMs become powerful because they are trained on huge amounts of text and contain billions of parameters that help them learn patterns, grammar, facts, and relationships between words.\n**Key Ideas:**\n• LLMs learn from large amounts of text.\n• They generate text one token at a time.\n• They predict the most likely next token based on previous tokens.\n• They can write, summarize, translate, answer questions, and generate code.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; text-align: center; max-width: 500px; margin: auto; padding: 25px; background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1e1b4b, #311042); border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid rgba(236, 72, 153, 0.3); box-shadow: 0 8px 32px rgba(236, 72, 153, 0.15); color: #f8fafc;&quot;>\n    <h3 style=&quot;color: #f472b6; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 15px; font-weight: 600;&quot;>Token Probability Simulator</h3>\n    <p style=&quot;font-size: 14px; color: #cbd5e1; margin-bottom: 20px;&quot;>Enter a seed phrase and simulate next-token selection:</p>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;display: flex; gap: 8px; justify-content: center; margin-bottom: 15px;&quot;>\n        <select id=&quot;l1-seed&quot; style=&quot;padding: 10px; background: #0f172a; border: 1px solid #f472b6; color: white; border-radius: 8px;&quot;>\n            <option value=&quot;Artificial intelligence is&quot;>Artificial intelligence is</option>\n            <option value=&quot;Deep learning works by&quot;>Deep learning works by</option>\n            <option value=&quot;Large language models can&quot;>Large language models can</option>\n        </select>\n        <button id=&quot;l1-predict-btn&quot; style=&quot;padding: 10px 15px; background: #ec4899; border: none; color: white; font-weight: bold; border-radius: 8px; cursor: pointer; transition: 0.2s;&quot; onmouseover=&quot;this.style.transform='scale(1.05)'&quot; onmouseout=&quot;this.style.transform='scale(1)'&quot;>Predict</button>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div id=&quot;l1-sentence&quot; style=&quot;font-size: 16px; background: rgba(15, 23, 42, 0.6); padding: 12px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 15px; min-height: 44px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; border: 1px dashed rgba(244, 114, 182, 0.4);&quot;>\n        Artificial intelligence is...\n    </div>\n    \n    <div id=&quot;l1-distribution&quot; style=&quot;text-align: left; display: none;&quot;>\n        <h4 style=&quot;font-size: 13px; color: #f472b6; margin-bottom: 8px;&quot;>Next Token Probabilities:</h4>\n        <div id=&quot;l1-bars&quot; style=&quot;display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;&quot;></div>\n    </div>\n\n    <script>\n    (function() {\n        const predictions = {\n            &quot;Artificial intelligence is&quot;: [\n                { token: &quot;transforming&quot;, prob: 45 },\n                { token: &quot;evolving&quot;, prob: 25 },\n                { token: &quot;learning&quot;, prob: 18 },\n                { token: &quot;hyped&quot;, prob: 12 }\n            ],\n            &quot;Deep learning works by&quot;: [\n                { token: &quot;adjusting&quot;, prob: 40 },\n                { token: &quot;mimicking&quot;, prob: 30 },\n                { token: &quot;processing&quot;, prob: 20 },\n                { token: &quot;optimizing&quot;, prob: 10 }\n            ],\n            &quot;Large language models can&quot;: [\n                { token: &quot;generate&quot;, prob: 50 },\n                { token: &quot;reason&quot;, prob: 20 },\n                { token: &quot;write&quot;, prob: 18 },\n                { token: &quot;hallucinate&quot;, prob: 12 }\n            ]\n        };\n\n        const btn = document.getElementById('l1-predict-btn');\n        const seedSelect = document.getElementById('l1-seed');\n        const sentenceDiv = document.getElementById('l1-sentence');\n        const distDiv = document.getElementById('l1-distribution');\n        const barsDiv = document.getElementById('l1-bars');\n\n        btn.onclick = function() {\n            const seed = seedSelect.value;\n            const data = predictions[seed];\n            sentenceDiv.innerHTML = '<strong>' + seed + '</strong> <span style=&quot;color: #ec4899; animation: pulse 1s infinite;&quot;>[generating...]</span>';\n            distDiv.style.display = 'block';\n            barsDiv.innerHTML = '';\n            \n            setTimeout(() => {\n                const winner = data[0].token;\n                sentenceDiv.innerHTML = '<strong>' + seed + '</strong> <span style=&quot;background: rgba(236,72,153,0.3); padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px; color: #f472b6;&quot;>' + winner + '</span>';\n                \n                data.forEach((item, idx) => {\n                    const row = document.createElement('div');\n                    row.style.margin = '4px 0';\n                    row.innerHTML = `\n                        <div style=&quot;display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 2px;&quot;>\n                            <span>${item.token}</span>\n                            <span>${item.prob}%</span>\n                        </div>\n                        <div style=&quot;background: #0f172a; height: 8px; border-radius: 4px; overflow: hidden; border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);&quot;>\n                            <div style=&quot;background: ${idx === 0 ? '#ec4899' : '#a21caf'}; width: ${item.prob}%; height: 100%; transition: width 0.8s;&quot;></div>\n                        </div>\n                    `;\n                    barsDiv.appendChild(row);\n                });\n            }, 600);\n        };\n    })();\n    </script>\n</div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Traditional NLP Models vs Modern LLMs",
            "explanation": "Before Large Language Models, most Natural Language Processing systems were designed for one specific task. Modern LLMs, however, are foundation models that can perform many language tasks using the same model through prompting.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "vsComparison",
                "content": {
                  "left": {
                    "title": "Traditional NLP Models",
                    "points": [
                      "Designed for one specific task (Spam Detection, Sentiment Analysis, Translation).",
                      "Requires a separate model for every new NLP task.",
                      "Relies heavily on manually labeled training datasets.",
                      "Limited context understanding and short memory.",
                      "Often uses rule-based systems, RNNs, LSTMs, or classical ML models.",
                      "Changing tasks usually requires retraining or rebuilding the model."
                    ]
                  },
                  "right": {
                    "title": "Modern Large Language Models (LLMs)",
                    "points": [
                      "A single foundation model performs many NLP tasks.",
                      "Uses prompts instead of training a new model for every task.",
                      "Pretrained on massive amounts of unlabeled internet-scale text.",
                      "Understands long-range context using the Transformer architecture.",
                      "Supports Zero-shot, One-shot, and Few-shot learning.",
                      "Can be adapted using Prompt Engineering, Fine-tuning, or LoRA."
                    ]
                  }
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Foundation Models & Popular Providers",
            "explanation": "Wonderful! If one model can perform many different tasks, what do we call such a model?We call it a Foundation Model.\nA Foundation Model is trained on massive amounts of data and can later be adapted for many different applications.Today, many companies build and provide these models lets discuss below:\n\n**Closed API Providers:**\n• **OpenAI:** Pioneers of GPT-3, GPT-4, and GPT-4o. Famous for high intelligence, reasoning, and developers APIs.\n• **Anthropic:** Creators of the Claude series (Claude 3.5 Sonnet). Noted for safety, long context, and superb coding skills.\n• **Google:** Developers of Gemini models. Integrates native multimodality (image/video/audio) and extremely large context windows (up to 2M tokens).\n• **Groq:** A hardware-focused provider using **LPUs (Language Processing Units)**. Instead of designing models, they host open-source models at hyper-fast speeds (hundreds of tokens/second).\n\n**Open Source / Open Weights:**\n• Developed by Meta (Llama), Mistral AI, Qwen, DeepSeek, and Google (Gemma). Users download these model weights to run them privately on their own servers.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; max-width: 600px; margin: auto; padding: 25px; background: #18181b; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #ec4899; color: #f4f4f5; text-align: left;&quot;>\n    <h3 style=&quot;color: #ec4899; text-align: center; margin-top:0;&quot;>Model Selection Matrix</h3>\n    <p style=&quot;font-size:13px; color:#a1a1aa; text-align:center; margin-bottom: 20px;&quot;>Click on a provider to analyze its engineering trade-offs:</p>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;display: flex; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: center;&quot;>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('openai', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#ec4899; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>OpenAI</button>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('anthropic', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#27272a; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Anthropic</button>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('google', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#27272a; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Google</button>\n        <button onclick=&quot;selectProvider('groq', this)&quot; style=&quot;padding:8px 12px; background:#27272a; border:none; color:white; border-radius:8px; cursor:pointer; font-weight:bold;&quot;>Groq</button>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div id=&quot;provider-details&quot; style=&quot;background: #242427; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; border: 1px solid #3f3f46;&quot;>\n        <h4 id=&quot;provider-name&quot; style=&quot;margin-top:0; color:#f472b6; font-size:18px;&quot;>OpenAI</h4>\n        <p id=&quot;provider-desc&quot; style=&quot;font-size:14px; color:#d4d4d8; line-height: 1.5;&quot;>Leading general intelligence and agentic workflows. Powers GPT-4o. Great for structured output, tooling, and general coding.</p>\n        <div style=&quot;display:grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap:10px; font-size:12px; margin-top:15px; border-top:1px solid #3f3f46; padding-top:10px;&quot;>\n            <div><strong>Latency:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-lat&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>Medium-Low</span></div>\n            <div><strong>Context Size:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-ctx&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>128k tokens</span></div>\n            <div><strong>Best Use:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-use&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>Complex reasoning, structured JSON</span></div>\n            <div><strong>State:</strong> <span id=&quot;provider-type&quot; style=&quot;color:#f472b6;&quot;>Proprietary APIs</span></div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n\n    <script>\n    const data = {\n        openai: {\n            name: &quot;OpenAI (GPT-4o / GPT-4o-mini)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;The gold standard for general API capabilities. Excels at reasoning tasks, tool calls (function calling), and maintaining structured outputs. Good balance of cost, performance, and developer tooling.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Medium-Low (30-50 tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;128,000 tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Structured outputs, multi-agent systems&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Closed API&quot;\n        },\n        anthropic: {\n            name: &quot;Anthropic (Claude 3.5 Sonnet)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;Highly praised by developers for coding, advanced logic, and tone. Claude 3.5 Sonnet displays excellent software engineering capabilities and provides a large context window with precise retrieval.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Medium (40-60 tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;200,000 tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Advanced programming, document analysis, academic research&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Closed API&quot;\n        },\n        google: {\n            name: &quot;Google (Gemini 1.5 Pro / Flash)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;Built natively multimodal from the ground up, Gemini can ingest and reason across images, audio, and videos directly. Offers a massive context window that easily handles long books or massive code repositories.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Low-Medium (50-70 tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;2,000,000 tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Long repository digestion, video analysis&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Closed API&quot;\n        },\n        groq: {\n            name: &quot;Groq (LPU Inference Engine)&quot;,\n            desc: &quot;Custom silicon LPU technology. Instead of running its own models, it hosts open weight models like Llama 3 and Mixtral. Perfect for building ultra-low latency real-time voice and chat assistants.&quot;,\n            lat: &quot;Very Low (300+ tokens/s)&quot;,\n            ctx: &quot;8k - 32k tokens&quot;,\n            use: &quot;Real-time chat, instant agents, fast pipelines&quot;,\n            type: &quot;Hosting Provider (Open Weights)&quot;\n        }\n    };\n\n    window.selectProvider = function(key, btnEl) {\n        document.getElementById('provider-name').innerText = data[key].name;\n        document.getElementById('provider-desc').innerText = data[key].desc;\n        document.getElementById('provider-lat').innerText = data[key].lat;\n        document.getElementById('provider-ctx').innerText = data[key].ctx;\n        document.getElementById('provider-use').innerText = data[key].use;\n        document.getElementById('provider-type').innerText = data[key].type;\n\n        const buttons = btnEl.parentNode.querySelectorAll('button');\n        buttons.forEach(b => {\n            b.style.background = '#27272a';\n        });\n        btnEl.style.background = '#ec4899';\n    }\n    </script>\n</div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Capabilities of Modern LLMs",
            "explanation": "Now let's see why LLMs became so popular.Because Foundation Models learn the fundamental structure of logic and language, they possess a massive array of capabilities out-of-the-box:\n\n• **Content Generation:** Drafting emails, writing essays, or generating creative storytelling.\n• **Summarization & Extraction:** Reading a 50-page PDF and extracting a structured JSON array of key data points.\n• **Code Generation:** LLMs can write complete, production-ready frontend components using modern frameworks like React, Next.js, and Tailwind CSS.\n• **Semantic Reasoning:** Solving logic puzzles, explaining complex topics simply, or identifying the tonal sentiment of a customer review.\n• **Translation:** Seamlessly translating between dozens of human languages, or even translating between programming languages (e.g., Python to Rust)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Limitations & Risks of LLMs",
            "explanation": "So far, we've seen how LLMs can understand and generate language. But no model is perfect. To use LLMs effectively, we must also understand their limitations, risks, and common failure cases.Let's discuss below.\n• **Hallucinations:** Because they predict the next token probabilistically, they will confidently invent fake facts, fake citations, or fake code libraries if they don't know the answer.\n• **Context Windows:** Models have a strict limit on how much text they can 'hold in their head' at once (ranging from 8k tokens to 2 Million tokens). If you exceed it, they crash or forget early information.\n• **Knowledge Cutoffs:** Their internal knowledge freezes on the day their training data was collected. They do not natively know what happened yesterday.\n• **Stochastic Parrots:** They do not possess true human reasoning or a 'world model'; they are executing highly advanced pattern matching."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Real-World LLM Applications",
            "explanation": "Outstanding! Now let's see how companies actually use LLMs in the real world.\nDevelopers use LLMs to build applications like.:\n\n• **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG):** Customer support bots that search a company's internal knowledge base and use the LLM to format the answers, bypassing the 'Knowledge Cutoff' limitation.\n• **Autonomous Agents:** AI that doesn't just chat, but takes action. For instance, an Agentic AI Resume Editor can be built to automatically score a user's resume, compare it to a job description, and iteratively rewrite the bullet points to optimize for ATS systems.\n• **Code Assistants:** Tools like GitHub Copilot that live in the IDE, predicting the next block of code and explaining legacy infrastructure.\n• **Data Pipelines:** Automatically cleaning messy, unstructured data logs into clean, queryable SQL databases."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the primary training objective of autoregressive Large Language Models?",
              "options": [
                "Translating input text into mathematical graphs",
                "Predicting the next token given preceding text context",
                "Comparing document embeddings using Cosine Similarity",
                "Searching indexed web links for matching phrases"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Modern LLMs (like GPT, Claude, Llama) are autoregressive models trained primarily to predict the next token in a sequence of text."
            },
