    {
      "id": "M005-llm-lesson-2-how-they-learn",
      "title": "How LLMs Learn: From Tokens to Training",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tokenization & Vocabulary",
            "explanation": "Excellent! Now that we understand what LLMs are, let's learn how they read human language.\nComputers cannot directly understand words like humans do. Before an LLM can learn from books, websites, or articles, the text must be broken into smaller pieces called Tokens.\nThis process is known as **Tokenization**.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/llm/in2.png]\nInstead of storing every possible word in the world, LLMs create a fixed Vocabulary containing thousands of common tokens.These tokens act like Lego blocks. By combining them, the model can understand and generate millions of different words.\n**For example,** the word 'unbelievable' may be split into 'un', 'believ', and 'able'. Each token is then converted into a unique ID number.\n\n**Key Idea:** LLMs do not read words directly. They read tokens and token IDs.",
            "example": "#Example - \nThe word 'unbelievable' might be tokenized into three pieces: ['un', 'believ', 'able']. \nThe AI assigns a unique ID number to each piece: [45, 1029, 312]. The AI only ever sees the numbers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Embeddings & The Context Window",
            "explanation": "Great! After text is converted into token IDs, the model still cannot understand their meaning.\nTo solve this, token IDs are transformed into **Embeddings.**\nEmbeddings are mathematical vectors that capture meaning. Similar words such as 'dog' and 'puppy' receive similar embeddings.\nThese embeddings are then placed inside the **Context Window.**\nThink of the context window is the model's short-term memory. It dictates exactly how many tokens the AI can 'look at' simultaneously before making a prediction. Older models had a context window of 2,000 tokens (a few pages of a book). Modern models have context windows of 1 Million+ tokens (several entire books).",
            "example": "An LLM with a 4,000-token context window cannot process a 50-page document at once.\nEarlier content may be forgotten, which is why RAG retrieves only the most relevant information."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Core Engine: Next Token Prediction",
            "explanation": "Now let's look at the core intelligence behind every LLM.\nAt its heart, an LLM is simply a Next Token Prediction machine.It reads the current text, predicts the most likely next token, adds it to the sentence, and then repeats the process.\n**For example,** if the input is 'I would like a cup of ____', the model may assign probabilities such as coffee (85%), tea (10%), and water (5%). Since 'coffee' has the highest probability, it is selected as the next token and added to the sentence.\n\nThe model then repeats this process using the updated text as context. By continuously predicting one token at a time, it can generate complete sentences, paragraphs, code, summaries, and conversations. At its core, every LLM operates through this repeated next-token prediction process.\nFor a better understanding of how next-token prediction works, let's look at the diagram below."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Measuring Mistakes with Loss",
            "explanation": "Excellent! The model can now predict the next token. But how does it know whether its prediction was correct or wrong?\nTo answer this, we use something called a Loss Function.\nImagine a teacher checking a student's exam paper. The teacher compares the student's answer with the correct answer and calculates how many mistakes were made.A Loss Function works in the same way.\nIf the correct next token is 'blue' but the model predicts 'green', the Loss Function calculates an error score.\n• High Loss → The prediction was very wrong.\n• Low Loss → The prediction was close to correct.\n• Zero Loss → The prediction was perfect.\nThe goal of training is to reduce this loss as much as possible.\n**Key Idea:** The Loss Function tells the model how wrong its prediction is."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Learning from Mistakes: Backpropagation",
            "explanation": "Great! The model now knows how wrong its prediction was.The next step is to improve itself.To do this, the model uses a process called **Backpropagation**.\nThink of a student solving a math problem incorrectly. After seeing the correct answer, the student reviews their mistakes and adjusts their understanding.Backpropagation works in a similar way.\nThe error is sent backwards through the network, and millions of weights are adjusted slightly.\n• Helpful weights are strengthened.\n• Incorrect weights are adjusted.\n• Future predictions become more accurate.\nThis process repeats billions of times during training.\n**Key Idea:** Backpropagation helps the model learn by adjusting its weights after every mistake."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Practice Makes Perfect: Training Epochs",
            "explanation": "Wonderful! The model can now predict, measure mistakes, and improve itself. But learning does not happen in a single pass.\nImagine reading a textbook only once. You might remember a few concepts, but not everything.If you read it multiple times, your understanding becomes much stronger.\nIn Machine Learning, one complete pass through the entire training dataset is called an Epoch.\nDuring each epoch, the model sees the training data, makes predictions, calculates loss, and updates its weights.\n\nAs more epochs are completed, the model gradually becomes better at predicting the next token.\n**Key Idea:** Training over multiple epochs helps the model learn patterns more effectively."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Epoch Review",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "One complete pass through the dataset is called an ____.",
              "During each epoch the model updates its ____.",
              "More epochs usually improve the model's ____.",
              "Backpropagation sends the ____ backward through the network."
            ],
            "options": [
              "epoch",
              "error",
              "weights",
              "learning",
              "token",
              "kernel"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "epoch",
              "blank_2": "weights",
              "blank_3": "learning",
              "blank_4": "error"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Pretraining: Scaling Laws & Billions of Tokens",
            "explanation": "Amazing! Now that we know how an LLM predicts tokens, let's see how it learns.During Pretraining, the model reads massive amounts of text and repeatedly performs the same cycle:Tokenize → Predict → Calculate Error → Improve Weights.\nThis process runs trillions of times across huge datasets.This phase is called as **pretraining**,and it costs millions of dollars in GPU compute and electricity.\nResearchers discovered **Scaling Laws**: LLMs predictably get smarter as you increase three things simultaneously:\n• 1. Model Size (Number of parameters/weights)\n• 2. Dataset Size (Number of tokens read)\n• 3. Compute (Number of GPUs used)\n\nHowever, a paper by DeepMind (The 'Chinchilla' paper) proved that models were often built too big and not given enough data. To train an optimal model, for every 1 parameter you have, you need to feed it about 20 tokens of high-quality data.",
            "example": "#Example\nIf you build a model with 70 Billion parameters (like Llama 3 70B), to train it optimally according to scaling laws,\nyou need to feed it at least 1.4 Trillion tokens of text during Pretraining!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How an LLM Learns: End-to-End Training Code Flow",
            "explanation": "Let's see how an LLM learns in practice by following the complete training pipeline from raw text to weight updates.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Load Training Text",
                    "body": "Code:\n```text = 'India won the Cricket World Cup in 2011'```\n\nThis is a single training example taken from a massive dataset containing books, websites, code, and articles."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Tokenization",
                    "body": "Code:\n```tokens = ['India', 'won', 'the', 'Cricket', 'World', 'Cup', 'in', '2011']```\n\nThe sentence is broken into smaller pieces called tokens."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Convert Tokens to IDs",
                    "body": "Code:\n```token_ids = [1254, 892, 45, 6721, 310, 1988, 78, 450]```\n\nComputers cannot understand words directly, so every token is converted into a unique numerical ID."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Create Embeddings",
                    "body": "Code:```\nembeddings = EmbeddingLayer(token_ids)```\n\nEach token ID is converted into a dense vector representation that captures semantic meaning."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Forward Pass & Next Token Prediction",
                    "body": "Code:```\nhidden_states = transformer(embeddings)\npredictions = output_layer(hidden_states)```\n\nInput:\n'India won the'\n\nOutput Probabilities:\nCricket = 80%\nFootball = 10%\nHockey = 5%\nTrophy = 5%\n\nThe model predicts the most likely next token."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Calculate Loss & Backpropagation",
                    "body": "Code:```\ntarget = 'Cricket'\nloss = CrossEntropyLoss(predictions, target)\nloss.backward()```\n\nThe prediction is compared with the correct answer. The error is sent backward through the network to determine which weights caused the mistake."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Update Weights & Repeat",
                    "body": "Code:\n\n```optimizer.step()\noptimizer.zero_grad()\n\nfor epoch in range(num_epochs):\n\n    for input_tokens, target_token in dataset:\n\n        embeddings = EmbeddingLayer(input_tokens)\n\n        outputs = transformer(embeddings)\n\n        predictions = output_layer(outputs)\n\n        loss = CrossEntropyLoss(predictions, target_token)\n\n        loss.backward()\n\n        optimizer.step()\n\n        optimizer.zero_grad()\n\n```The model repeats this process billions of times until it becomes highly accurate at predicting the next token."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Use Python and the `tiktoken` library (OpenAI's official tokenizer) to see how a sentence is chopped into Token IDs.",
            "starterCode": "import tiktoken\n\n# Initialize the tokenizer used by GPT-4\nencoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")\n\ntext = \"Tokenization is totally unbelievable!\"\n\n# TODO: Use the encoding object to encode the text into token IDs\ntoken_ids = encoding.____(____)\n\nprint(f\"Raw Text: {text}\")\nprint(f\"Token IDs: {token_ids}\")\nprint(f\"Number of tokens: {len(token_ids)}\")",
            "solution": "import tiktoken\n\n# Initialize the tokenizer used by GPT-4\nencoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")\n\ntext = \"Tokenization is totally unbelievable!\"\n\n# TODO: Use the encoding object to encode the text into token IDs\ntoken_ids = encoding.encode(text)\n\nprint(f\"Raw Text: {text}\")\nprint(f\"Token IDs: {token_ids}\")\nprint(f\"Number of tokens: {len(token_ids)}\")",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use the `.encode()` method on the encoding object and pass in the raw text string.",
            "explanation": "Libraries like `tiktoken` allow developers to accurately count how many tokens a prompt will consume before sending it to an API, which is crucial for managing Context Window limits and API costs."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Legal App Dilemma",
          "description": "You are building an AI app for a law firm. Lawyers will upload entire 300-page case files (roughly 150,000 words) and ask the AI to summarize the key arguments. Which model limitation is going to be your primary engineering hurdle?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "The Tokenizer will fail to understand legal jargon.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Sub-word tokenizers are highly robust. Even if a word is extremely rare Latin legal jargon, the tokenizer will just break it down into smaller, recognizable character chunks.",
              "score": 10
            },
            {
              "text": "The Context Window limit.",
              "outcome": "Correct! 150,000 words is roughly 200,000 tokens. If you use a model with a 128k token context window, the AI will literally forget the first half of the case file. You must either choose a model with a massive context window (like Gemini 1.5) or build a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "The Loss Function will crash due to the length of the document.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. The Loss Function is used during the Pretraining phase by the researchers, not during the Inference phase when you are using the application.",
              "score": 0
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Why do modern LLMs use tokenization instead of storing every possible word in a vocabulary?",
              "options": [
                "To make text colorful",
                "Because new words are constantly created and a fixed word list would be too large",
                "To increase internet speed",
                "To reduce GPU temperature"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "LLMs split words into smaller sub-word tokens so they can represent both common and newly invented words using a manageable vocabulary."
            },
            {
