    {
      "id": "M005-llm-lesson-3-huggingfae",
      "title": "Hugging Face Transformers",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Introduction to Hugging Face",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! So far, we have learned how NLP models and LLMs work internally. But building models from scratch is extremely difficult.Imagine you want to build a chatbot. Training a model yourself would require huge datasets, powerful GPUs, and weeks of training time.Instead of doing all that work, developers use Hugging Face.\n**Hugging Face:**\nHugging Face is one of the most popular open-source AI platforms for working with Natural Language Processing (NLP), Large Language Models (LLMs), Computer Vision, and Speech AI.\n**Why Hugging Face Matters:**\n• Provides thousands of pre-trained models.\n• Makes AI development easier with simple APIs.\n• Supports PyTorch, TensorFlow, and JAX.\n• Used by developers, researchers, and companies worldwide.\n\n**Popular Use Cases:**\n• Chatbots,Text Summarization,Translation,Question Answering,Sentiment Analysis,Image Generation\n\nToday, Hugging Face is often called the '**GitHub of AI Models**' because it allows developers to share and use models easily.",
            "example": "Instead of training a chatbot from scratch,\n you can load a pre-trained GPT or BERT model from Hugging Face in just a few lines of code.",
            "tip": "Think of Hugging Face as an App Store for AI models."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building Your First Hugging Face Pipeline",
            "explanation": "Now that we know what Hugging Face is, let's learn the easiest way to use a model.\n A **Pipeline** is the simplest way to run AI models using Hugging Face. It automatically handles tokenization, model loading, inference, and output formatting.\n\n**Why Use Pipelines?**\n• Beginner-friendly\n• Minimal code required\n• Automatically loads tokenizer and model\n• Supports many NLP tasks\n\n**Installation:** `pip install transformers torch`\n**Import:** `from transformers import pipeline`\n**Common Pipeline Tasks:** `text-generation`, `sentiment-analysis`, `question-answering`, `summarization`, `translation`, `text-classification`\n\n**Workflow:** Import Pipeline → Select Task → Load Model → Pass Input → Get Output\n\n**Important Parameters:**\n• `model` → Pretrained model name\n• `max_length` → Maximum output length\n• `temperature` → Controls creativity\n• `top_p` → Controls randomness\n• `do_sample` → Enables sampling\n• `num_return_sequences` → Number of responses\n\n**Internal Process:** User Input → Tokenizer → Model → Prediction → Output",
            "example": "from transformers import pipeline\n\ngenerator = pipeline(\n    task='text-generation',\n    model='gpt2'\n)\n\nresult = generator(\n    'Artificial Intelligence is',\n    max_length=50,\n    temperature=0.7,\n    top_p=0.9,\n    do_sample=True\n)\n\nprint(result[0]['generated_text'])\n\nSample Output:\nArtificial Intelligence is a field of computer science focused on building systems that can learn and make decisions.",
            "tip": "Start with pipelines for learning. Later use AutoTokenizer and AutoModel for advanced control."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Transformer Pipelines",
            "explanation": "Awesome! Pipelines can perform many different AI tasks.Instead of writing separate code for every task, Hugging Face provides ready-made pipelines.\n\n**Popular Pipelines:**\n• Text Generation\n• Summarization\n• Translation\n• Question Answering\n• Sentiment Analysis\n• Text Classification\n\nWith a pipeline, you can start using AI models without understanding every technical detail.",
            "example": "A text-generation pipeline can generate paragraphs of text from a simple prompt.",
            "tip": "Pipelines are the fastest way for beginners to experiment with AI models."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Use a Hugging Face pipeline to perform sentiment analysis.",
            "starterCode": "from transformers import pipeline\n\n# Create sentiment pipeline\n\n# Analyze text\n",
            "solution": "from transformers import pipeline\nclassifier = pipeline('sentiment-analysis')\nresult = classifier('I love learning Hugging Face!')\nprint(result)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use pipeline('sentiment-analysis').",
            "explanation": "The pipeline automatically loads a pretrained sentiment analysis model."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Loading Pretrained Models",
            "explanation": "Great progress! So far, Hugging Face has automatically selected models for us.But what if we want a specific model?\nTraining large models requires huge datasets and expensive hardware. Instead of training from scratch, developers usually download pretrained models from Hugging Face Hub.\n\n**Benefits:**\n• Saves time\n• Saves computing resources\n• Achieves strong performance immediately\nInstead of spending weeks training a model, we simply download one that has already been trained.\n**Popular Models:** `gpt2`, `bert-base-uncased`, `t5-small`, `distilbert-base-uncased`",
            "example": "from transformers import AutoTokenizer, AutoModelForCausalLM\n\nmodel_name = 'gpt2'\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_name)\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(model_name)\n\nprint('Model Loaded Successfully!')",
            "tip": "Use AutoTokenizer and AutoModel classes because Hugging Face automatically loads the correct architecture."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Understanding Tokenizers",
            "explanation": "Language models cannot understand raw text directly. Tokenizers convert text into tokens and token IDs that models can process.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n• Split text into tokens\n• Convert tokens into IDs\n• Prepare input for models\n\n**Example Flow:**\nText → Tokens → Token IDs → Model Input",
            "example": "from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained('gpt2')\n\ntext = 'Hello World'\n\ntokens = tokenizer.tokenize(text)\nids = tokenizer.encode(text)\n\nprint('Tokens:', tokens)\nprint('Token IDs:', ids)\n\nOutput:\nTokens: ['Hello', 'ĠWorld']\nToken IDs: [15496, 2159]",
            "tip": "Always use the tokenizer that matches your model. GPT, BERT, and T5 use different tokenizers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tokenization Process",
            "diagram": [
              "Raw Text",
              "Tokens",
              "Token IDs",
              "Model Input"
            ],
            "explanation": "Tokenizers act as translators between human language and machine-readable numbers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Saving and Loading Models",
            "explanation": "After fine-tuning a model, developers often save it for later use.\n\n**Why Save Models?**\n• Avoid retraining.\n• Share models with others.\n• Deploy models into applications.\n\nModels can be stored locally or uploaded to the Hugging Face Hub.",
            "example": "After fine-tuning BERT on customer reviews, you can save the model and reload it whenever needed.",
            "tip": "Saving models allows reproducible and scalable AI development."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Running Your First LLM",
            "explanation": "Running an LLM involves loading a model, preparing a prompt, generating predictions, and displaying results.\n\n**Typical Workflow:**\n• Load Model\n• Load Tokenizer\n• Provide Prompt\n• Generate Output\n• Display Response\n\nThis workflow is used in chatbots, AI assistants, and content-generation tools.",
            "example": "Prompt: 'Explain Artificial Intelligence in one sentence.'\n\nResponse: 'Artificial Intelligence enables machines to learn, reason, and solve problems like humans.'",
            "tip": "Once you can load a model and generate text, you have completed your first LLM application."
