    {
      "id": "M005-llm-lesson-4-working-with-apis",
      "title": "Working with LLM APIs & Model Providers",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "API-Based Models vs Local Models",
            "explanation": "Great! Now that we understand how LLMs are trained, a practical question arises:'How do we actually use these models in real applications?'\nThere are two main ways:\n**1.API-Based Models:**\nCompanies like OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google train massive AI models and allow developers to access them through APIs.\nInstead of downloading the model, you simply send a request over the internet and receive a response.\n• **Pros:** Zero setup cost, access to state-of-the-art models (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5), high scalability, managed infrastructure.\n• **Cons:** Variable usage pricing, internet connection required, latency depends on API queues, zero control over data storage privacy.\n\n**2.Local Models:**\nInstead of calling an external service, you download and run the model on your own hardware.\n• **Pros:** 100% private, offline capabilities, zero token subscription costs (after hardware capital expenditure), fully customizable (weights fine-tuning).\n• **Cons:** Requires highly expensive enterprise GPU hardware (like Nvidia A100/H100), complex backend orchestration, smaller reasoning capacity compared to massive frontier API systems.",
            "tip": "Start with API-based models to quickly validate your product, then transition high-volume, standard tasks to local models to minimize token bills.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; max-width: 550px; margin: auto; padding: 25px; background: #0c0a09; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #10b981; color: #f5f5f4;&quot;>\n    <h3 style=&quot;color: #10b981; text-align: center; margin-top:0; font-weight:600;&quot;>Trade-Off Dashboard</h3>\n    <p style=&quot;font-size:13px; color:#a8a29e; text-align:center; margin-bottom:15px;&quot;>Adjust parameters to evaluate deployment costs vs hardware specs:</p>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;margin-bottom:15px;&quot;>\n        <label style=&quot;font-size:12px; color:#a8a29e;&quot;>Estimated Monthly Queries: <span id=&quot;l4-queries-val&quot; style=&quot;color:#10b981; font-weight:bold;&quot;>100,000</span></label>\n        <input type=&quot;range&quot; id=&quot;l4-queries&quot; min=&quot;10000&quot; max=&quot;1000000&quot; step=&quot;10000&quot; value=&quot;100000&quot; style=&quot;width:100%; accent-color:#10b981;&quot;>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;display:grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap:12px; margin-bottom:15px;&quot;>\n        <div style=&quot;background:#1c1917; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; border:1px solid #292524; text-align:center;&quot;>\n            <div style=&quot;font-size:12px; color:#a8a29e;&quot;>API Cost / Month</div>\n            <div id=&quot;l4-api-cost&quot; style=&quot;font-size:18px; color:#10b981; font-weight:bold;&quot;>$250</div>\n        </div>\n        <div style=&quot;background:#1c1917; padding:12px; border-radius:8px; border:1px solid #292524; text-align:center;&quot;>\n            <div style=&quot;font-size:12px; color:#a8a29e;&quot;>Local GPU Required</div>\n            <div id=&quot;l4-gpu-req&quot; style=&quot;font-size:16px; color:#10b981; font-weight:bold;&quot;>RTX 4090 (24GB)</div>\n        </div>\n    </div>\n    \n    <script>\n    (function() {\n        const slider = document.getElementById('l4-queries');\n        const qVal = document.getElementById('l4-queries-val');\n        const apiCost = document.getElementById('l4-api-cost');\n        const gpuReq = document.getElementById('l4-gpu-req');\n        \n        slider.oninput = function() {\n            const val = parseInt(slider.value);\n            qVal.innerText = val.toLocaleString();\n            \n            const cost = Math.round(val * 0.0025);\n            apiCost.innerText = '$' + cost.toLocaleString();\n            \n            if (val > 500000) {\n                gpuReq.innerText = &quot;8x H100 GPU Cluster&quot;;\n            } else if (val > 100000) {\n                gpuReq.innerText = &quot;2x A100 GPU (80GB)&quot;;\n            } else {\n                gpuReq.innerText = &quot;RTX 4090 (24GB)&quot;;\n            }\n        }\n    })();\n </script>\n<div style=&quot;margin-top:10px;font-size:12px;color:#a8a29e;text-align:center;&quot;><b>Note:</b> Values are approximate and for demonstration only.</div>\n</div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Comparing Closed Source Models: GPT, Claude, Gemini",
            "explanation": "Once you decide to use APIs, the next question is:'Which model should I choose?' Different models have different strengths.Selecting the right model SDK depends heavily on context length, developer pricing, and reasoning needs:\n\n• **GPT-4o:** The benchmark for speed and structure. Great for JSON mode, function calling, and structured outputs.\n• **Claude 3.5 Sonnet:** The premier developer model. Outperforms in coding tasks, complex multi-file logic, and document summaries.\n• **Gemini 1.5 Pro:** The long-context leader. Its 2M context allows you to upload entire codebases or reference dozens of PDF manuals at once.\n\n**Latency and Throughput:**\nLatency is the time it takes to get the first token back (**Time to First Token (TTFT)**). Throughput is the number of tokens generated per second. Closed models offer high intelligence but fluctuate in speed; hosted endpoints like Groq offer lower intelligence but blazing speeds.",
            "tip": "Use GPT-4o-mini or Claude 3.5 Haiku for fast, low-cost routing, and call the larger flagship models only for complex reasoning tasks."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "OpenAI API Basics",
            "explanation": "Now let's start coding! To talk to an LLM, we use an API. The OpenAI API provides access to GPT models using simple Python code.\n**Installation:** `pip install openai`\n**Import:** `from openai import OpenAI`\n**Steps:**\n• 1. Create API key\n• 2. Initialize client\n• 3. Send prompt\n• 4. Read response",
            "example": "from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(api_key='YOUR_API_KEY')\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[{'role':'user','content':'What is AI?'}]\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "tip": "Store API keys in environment variables, never hardcode them."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Sending Prompts",
            "explanation": "Awesome! Now that we can connect to the model, we need to tell it what to do.This instruction is called a Prompt. Better prompts usually produce better results.\n**Prompt Formula:**\nRole + Task + Context + Output Format\n**Example:** '''You are a Machine Learning Teacher.Explain Neural Networks.Use simple language.Give the answer in bullet points.'''\n**Types:**\n• Question Prompt\n• Instruction Prompt\n• Role Prompt\n• Few-Shot Prompt",
            "example": "response = client.chat.completions.create(\n model='gpt-4o-mini',\n messages=[{\n 'role':'user',\n 'content':'Explain Machine Learning in simple terms.'\n }]\n)",
            "tip": "Be specific and provide context whenever possible."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Understanding Responses",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! After sending a prompt, the model sends back a response.The API returns a JSON response containing generated text and metadata.\n**Important Fields:**\n• id\n• model\n• choices\n• usage\n• message\n\nMost developers extract only the generated content.",
            "example": "response = client.chat.completions.create(...)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\n\nOutput:\nArtificial Intelligence is a branch of computer science.",
            "tip": "The generated answer is usually inside choices[0].message.content."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Temperature & Max Tokens",
            "explanation": "We can now control how the model behaves.Parameters control how the model generates responses.\n**Temperature:** Controls creativity\n• 0.1 = More factual\n• 0.5 = Balanced\n• 1.0 = Creative\n**Max Tokens:**  Controls maximum response length.",
            "example": "response = client.chat.completions.create(\n model='gpt-4o-mini',\n messages=[{'role':'user','content':'Write a poem'}],\n temperature=0.9,\n max_tokens=100\n)",
            "tip": "Use low temperature for business applications and higher values for creative writing."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Complete the Python code to initialize the OpenAI client and generate a basic text completion.",
            "starterCode": "import os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\n# Instantiate the OpenAI client. It automatically reads the environment variable OPENAI_API_KEY.\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# TODO: Call chat.completions.create with a system instruction and user prompt using model 'gpt-4o-mini'\nresponse = client.____.____.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a helpful software tutor.\"},\n        {\"role\": \"____\", \"content\": \"What is an API key?\"}\n    ]\n)\n\n# Print the response\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "solution": "import os\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\n# Instantiate the OpenAI client. It automatically reads the environment variable OPENAI_API_KEY.\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# TODO: Call chat.completions.create with a system instruction and user prompt using model 'gpt-4o-mini'\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a helpful software tutor.\"},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is an API key?\"}\n    ]\n)\n\n# Print the response\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Access 'chat.completions' and set the second role to 'user'.",
            "explanation": "The OpenAI SDK uses `client.chat.completions.create` to call the completion API, passing the model and a list of messages."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive_simulation",
          "title": "Adjust API Parameters",
