    {
      "id": "M005_llm_05",
      "title": "LLM Inference & Generation Controls",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#ec4899",
        "accent": "#be185d"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LLM Inference & Generation Controls",
            "explanation": "Excellent! We now understand how an LLM predicts the next token. But an important question remains:'If the model knows many possible next words, how does it decide which one to choose?'This is controlled by Generation Controls.\nWhen an LLM predicts the next token, it doesn't produce just one option. It calculates probabilities for thousands of possible tokens.We can influence this selection process using several controls:\n\n• **Temperature:** Controls the 'creativity' or random dispersion. A temperature of `0.0` makes the model completely deterministic, always picking the top 1 token (greedy decoding). High temperature (e.g. `1.2`) flattens probabilities, making rare words more likely.\n• **Top-P (Nucleus Sampling):** Filters candidate tokens based on their cumulative probability. For example, `top_p=0.9` means the model only considers the smallest pool of tokens whose individual probabilities sum up to 90%. This filters out low-probability tail words.\n• **Top-K:** Limits candidate words to the top K most likely options.\n• **Stop Sequences:** Characters (like `\"\\n\"` or `\"User:\"`) that tell the model to instantly stop generating.\n• **Streaming:** Sending tokens back one by one as they are generated rather than waiting for the entire paragraph to finish.",
            "tip": "For tasks like coding, data extraction, or factual Q&A, use Temperature = 0.0. For creative brainstorming, poetry, or writing assistants, set Temperature to 0.7 or higher.",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:600px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;padding:0;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;min-height:100vh;background:transparent;}</style><div style=&quot;font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; max-width: 550px; margin: auto; padding: 25px; background: #0b0f19; border-radius: 16px; border: 1px solid #ec4899; color: #f3f4f6; text-align: left;&quot;>\n    <h3 style=&quot;color: #ec4899; text-align: center; margin-top:0;&quot;>Generation Controls Simulator</h3>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;margin-bottom:15px;&quot;>\n        <label style=&quot;font-size:12px; color:#9ca3af; display:block; margin-bottom:5px;&quot;>Temperature: <span id=&quot;t-val&quot; style=&quot;color:#ec4899; font-weight:bold;&quot;>0.7</span></label>\n        <input type=&quot;range&quot; id=&quot;t-slider&quot; min=&quot;0&quot; max=&quot;2&quot; step=&quot;0.1&quot; value=&quot;0.7&quot; style=&quot;width:100%; accent-color:#ec4899;&quot;>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div style=&quot;margin-bottom:20px;&quot;>\n        <label style=&quot;font-size:12px; color:#9ca3af; display:block; margin-bottom:5px;&quot;>Top-P (Nucleus): <span id=&quot;p-val&quot; style=&quot;color:#ec4899; font-weight:bold;&quot;>0.9</span></label>\n        <input type=&quot;range&quot; id=&quot;p-slider&quot; min=&quot;0.1&quot; max=&quot;1.0&quot; step=&quot;0.05&quot; value=&quot;0.9&quot; style=&quot;width:100%; accent-color:#ec4899;&quot;>\n    </div>\n    \n    <div id=&quot;token-probs&quot; style=&quot;background: rgba(255,255,255,0.05); padding:15px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);&quot;>\n        <h4 style=&quot;margin-top:0; font-size:13px; color:#ec4899; margin-bottom:10px;&quot;>Token Sampling Space:</h4>\n        <div id=&quot;token-list&quot; style=&quot;display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:8px;&quot;></div>\n    </div>\n    \n    <script>\n    (function() {\n        const tSlider = document.getElementById('t-slider');\n        const pSlider = document.getElementById('p-slider');\n        const tVal = document.getElementById('t-val');\n        const pVal = document.getElementById('p-val');\n        const tokenList = document.getElementById('token-list');\n        \n        const baseProbs = {\n            &quot;model&quot;: 0.50,\n            &quot;system&quot;: 0.25,\n            &quot;intelligence&quot;: 0.15,\n            &quot;coffee&quot;: 0.08,\n            &quot;spaceship&quot;: 0.02\n        };\n        \n        function updateProbs() {\n            const temp = parseFloat(tSlider.value);\n            const topP = parseFloat(pSlider.value);\n            \n            tVal.innerText = temp.toFixed(1);\n            pVal.innerText = topP.toFixed(2);\n            \n            tokenList.innerHTML = '';\n            \n            let rawWeights = {};\n            let sum = 0;\n            \n            Object.entries(baseProbs).forEach(([token, prob]) => {\n                let w;\n                if (temp === 0) {\n                    w = (token === &quot;model&quot;) ? 1e6 : 0;\n                } else {\n                    w = Math.exp(Math.log(prob) / temp);\n                }\n                rawWeights[token] = w;\n                sum += w;\n            });\n            \n            let normProbs = [];\n            Object.entries(rawWeights).forEach(([token, w]) => {\n                normProbs.push({ token: token, prob: w / sum });\n            });\n            \n            normProbs.sort((a,b) => b.prob - a.prob);\n            \n            let cumSum = 0;\n            normProbs.forEach((item) => {\n                const percentage = (item.prob * 100).toFixed(1);\n                const isFiltered = (cumSum >= topP) &amp;&amp; (temp > 0);\n                cumSum += item.prob;\n                \n                const row = document.createElement('div');\n                row.style.margin = '4px 0';\n                row.innerHTML = `\n                    <div style=&quot;display: flex; justify-content: space-between; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 2px; color: ${isFiltered ? '#6b7280' : '#f3f4f6'}; text-decoration: ${isFiltered ? 'line-through' : 'none'}&quot;>\n                        <span>&quot;${item.token}&quot; ${isFiltered ? '(Filtered out by Top-P)' : ''}</span>\n                        <span>${percentage}%</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <div style=&quot;background: #020617; height: 6px; border-radius: 3px; overflow: hidden;&quot;>\n                        <div style=&quot;background: ${isFiltered ? '#374151' : '#ec4899'}; width: ${percentage}%; height: 100%;&quot;></div>\n                    </div>\n                `;\n                tokenList.appendChild(row);\n            });\n        }\n        \n        tSlider.oninput = updateProbs;\n        pSlider.oninput = updateProbs;\n        updateProbs();\n    })();\n    </script>\n</div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Decoding Strategies: Greedy vs Beam Search",
            "explanation": "Great! Now we know how probabilities are controlled.The next question is:'How does the model actually choose the next token?'This process is called Decoding.\nLet's Discuss some decoding statergies,\n• **Greedy Decoding:** Simple and fast. The model picks the single most probable token at each step. While highly efficient, it can cause models to get stuck in repetitive loops or miss more natural, human-sounding sentences.\n• **Beam Search:** A more global, strategic search. The model keeps track of the 'B' (beam width) most likely sub-paths at each step, branching them out, and selecting the path with the highest overall probability once the sentence is completed. Excellent for precise translation or summarization, but computationally expensive.\n• **Stochastic Sampling:** Using the Temperature and Top-P parameters to sample tokens randomly based on their weights, generating varied and creative text.",
            "tip": "If your chatbot output sounds repetitive or robotic, raise the Temperature or enable Top-P sampling rather than using greedy decoding."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Repetition Trap: Frequency & Presence Penalties",
            "explanation": "After generating long responses, another problem appears.Sometimes the model starts repeating words again and again.\n**Example : **'The beautiful dragon flew through the beautiful sky with beautiful wings...' in this beautiful words are reapeated. \nTo solve this, APIs provide penalties.\n• **Frequency Penalty:** Penalizes a token based on *how many times* it has already appeared in the generated text. It actively discourages the model from repeating the exact same words over and over. Use this to reduce redundant vocabulary.\n• **Presence Penalty:** Penalizes a token based on whether it has appeared *at all* in the text so far. It encourages the model to shift to entirely new topics and ideas. Use this for creative brainstorming to force the model to explore new concepts.",
            "example": "response = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"List programming languages\"}],\n     presence_penalty=2\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\nHigher presence penalty encourages exploring new topics."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Ultimate Veto: Logit Bias",
            "explanation": "Sometimes you want absolute, unbreakable control over the model's vocabulary. What if you are building an AI for a children's application and want to mathematically guarantee it never uses a specific curse word? Or what if you want to force it to ONLY output the words 'Yes' or 'No'?\n\nYou use **Logit Bias**.\n\nBefore the model turns its predictions into percentages (using Softmax), it generates raw mathematical scores called 'logits'. Logit Bias allows you to artificially inflate or nuke those scores. Setting a token's bias to `-100` completely bans it from ever being generated. Setting it to `+100` forces the model to use it.",
            "tip": "Logit Bias operates on Token IDs, not words. You must look up the exact Token ID for the word you want to ban using a library like `tiktoken` before passing it to the API."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Under the Hood: The KV Cache",
            "explanation": "Why does generating long text take so much memory and time? \n\nTo predict word number 100, the model must read and calculate the mathematical context of all 99 previous words. To predict word 101, it must read all 100 previous words. Re-calculating the entire prompt from scratch every single time would be disastrously slow.\n\nTo solve this, LLMs use a **KV Cache** (Key-Value Cache). During inference, the GPU saves the mathematical matrices (the Keys and Values from the Attention mechanism) of all previous tokens into its RAM. This makes generating the next token lightning-fast! However, this means that long conversations physically eat up massive amounts of expensive GPU memory, which is why API providers charge money based on input length.",
            "example": "When building an AI app, if you send a chat history with 100,000 tokens to the API,\n the provider's servers must allocate gigabytes of VRAM \njust to store your KV Cache for that single request."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Generate a creative story using Temperature and Top-P sampling.",
            "starterCode": "from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# Complete the API call\n",
            "solution": "from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[{'role':'user','content':'Write a sci-fi story'}],\n    temperature=1.0,\n    top_p=0.9\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use temperature and top_p parameters.",
            "explanation": "Temperature controls creativity while Top-P controls the candidate token pool used during sampling."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the result of setting the Temperature parameter to exactly 0.0?",
              "options": [
                "The model selects random tokens, maximizing creative styles.",
                "The model outputs only numbers and no human strings.",
                "The model becomes completely deterministic, behaving like Greedy Decoding.",
                "The model automatically increases context window limit size."
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "explanation": "Setting Temperature to 0.0 collapses the sampling space to always pick the highest probability token, making outputs predictable and reproducible."
            },
            {
              "question": "Which decoding strategy tracks multiple parallel paths of text generation to find the highest total probability for the entire sequence?",
              "options": [
                "Greedy Decoding",
                "Beam Search",
                "Stop Sequences",
                "Streaming API"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Beam Search evaluates multiple branches (beams) simultaneously to optimize the overall sequence probability instead of just picking the immediate next token."
            },
            {
              "question": "If your AI assistant keeps repeating the exact same adjective throughout a long essay, which parameter should you increase to mathematically discourage it from reusing words?",
              "options": [
                "Top-K",
                "Presence Penalty",
                "Frequency Penalty",
                "Temperature"
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "explanation": "The Frequency Penalty mathematically reduces a token's probability based on how many times it has already appeared in the text."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Customer Support Bot",
          "description": "You are building an AI customer support bot for a bank. It is hooked up to a database of customer records. It must extract the customer's balance and output it as a strict JSON object: `{\"balance\": 500}`. During testing, the bot occasionally tries to be conversational and outputs `Here is the JSON you asked for: {\"balance\": 500}`. This breaks your application. How do you configure the API to fix this?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Increase the Temperature to 1.5 to make it more creative.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Increasing temperature makes the model more random and less likely to adhere to a strict structure.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Set the Temperature to 0.0, and optionally use Logit Bias or JSON mode.",
              "outcome": "Correct! Setting Temperature to 0.0 makes the model highly deterministic and focused. For strict programmatic data extraction, you want zero creativity.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "Increase the Presence Penalty.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Increasing the Presence penalty will encourage the model to talk about new topics, which is the exact opposite of what you want for a strict data extraction task.",
              "score": 10
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Complete the OpenAI API call to enable Streaming. Loop over the response chunk items to print them in real-time.",
            "starterCode": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# Call chat completion with streaming enabled\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a short poem.\"}],\n    # TODO: Enable streaming\n    stream=____\n)\n\nfor chunk in response:\n    # Extract text from delta chunk\n    content = chunk.choices[0].delta.content\n    if content:\n        print(content, end=\"\", flush=True)",
            "solution": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# Call chat completion with streaming enabled\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Write a short poem.\"}],\n    # TODO: Enable streaming\n    stream=True\n)\n\nfor chunk in response:\n    # Extract text from delta chunk\n    content = chunk.choices[0].delta.content\n    if content:\n        print(content, end=\"\", flush=True)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Set 'stream' to the boolean True.",
            "explanation": "Setting stream=True returns a generator that yields chunk objects representing intermediate token updates. This is how modern apps achieve the 'typewriter' effect, keeping UX latency extremely low."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Project: AI Story Generator with Controls. Write a script that configures Temperature, Top-P, Frequency Penalty, and Max Tokens as parameters to customize generated output.",
            "starterCode": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef generate_story(prompt, temp=0.7, top_p=0.9, freq_pen=0.5, max_tokens=150):\n    # TODO: Execute completions call passing generation control parameters\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        temperature=____,\n        top_p=____,\n        frequency_penalty=____,\n        max_tokens=____\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nstory = generate_story(\"Once upon a time in a cyberpunk Tokyo\", temp=0.9, top_p=0.95, freq_pen=1.0, max_tokens=100)\nprint(story)",
            "solution": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef generate_story(prompt, temp=0.7, top_p=0.9, freq_pen=0.5, max_tokens=150):\n    # TODO: Execute completions call passing generation control parameters\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        temperature=temp,\n        top_p=top_p,\n        frequency_penalty=freq_pen,\n        max_tokens=max_tokens\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nstory = generate_story(\"Once upon a time in a cyberpunk Tokyo\", temp=0.9, top_p=0.95, freq_pen=1.0, max_tokens=100)\nprint(story)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Pass temp, top_p, freq_pen, and max_tokens parameters directly to the chat completions call.",
            "explanation": "Passing these parameters to the API allows full, custom control over LLM output creativity, diversity, repetition, and length."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M005-llm-lesson-6-adaptation",
      "title": "Transfer Learning & Fine-Tuning",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#d946ef",
        "accent": "#c026d3"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Foundation: Transfer Learning",
            "explanation": "Great! So far, we have learned how LLMs are pretrained on trillions of tokens from books, websites, and code repositories.But imagine training a brand-new model from scratch every time you need a chatbot, coding assistant, or medical AI. That would cost millions of dollars and months of GPU time.\nTo solve this problem, we use Transfer Learning.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/llm/tl1.png]\n**Transfer Learning** is the strategy where a model is first trained on a massive, general task (Pretraining) and then its knowledge is 'transferred' to a specific task. Think of it like a human: first, you go to school to learn general language and logic (Pretraining). Then, you take that 'transferred' knowledge to medical school to become a surgeon. You don't learn how to read and speak all over again; you only learn the specialized medicine part. \n\nFor LLMs, the 'School' is the Pretraining on trillions of tokens from the internet. The result is a **Base Model**.",
            "example": "#For exmaple\nThink of a chatbot that understands general conversations.\nWe fine-tune it using customer support data so it can answer product-related questions accurately.."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Adaptation vs. Fine-Tuning",
            "explanation": "These terms are often used interchangeably, but they have subtle technical differences in a developer's workflow:\n\n• **Adaptation:** This is the high-level goal. It refers to the process of making a general model work for a specific domain (e.g., making Llama 3 talk like a Pirate or a Lawyer). Adaptation can be achieved through Fine-Tuning, but also through RAG (Retrieval) or Prompt Engineering.\n• **Fine-Tuning:** This is the specific technical *method* of adaptation where you actually update the mathematical weights (parameters) of the neural network using a specialized dataset.\n\nFine-Tuning is 'Brain Surgery'—you are changing how the model thinks. Prompt Engineering is 'Coaching'—you are just telling it how to behave using its existing knowledge.",
            "example": "Adapting a model to be\na medical assistant involves fine-tuning\nit on thousands of verified\ndoctor-patient transcripts so it learns\nthe specific professional medical tone\nand terminology required for safety."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Instruction Tuning & SFT",
            "explanation": "Wonderful! Let's look at the most common type of Fine-Tuning. A Base Model is trained to predict the next token, but it does not automatically know how to follow instructions. If you ask it a question, it may continue the text instead of giving a useful answer.\n\n**Instruction Tuning** is a form of Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) where the model is trained on thousands of Instruction-Response examples. By learning from these examples, the model understands that when a user asks a question or gives a command, it should generate a helpful response.\n\nOver time, the model learns the 'Assistant Pattern'—answering questions, following instructions, summarizing content, translating text, and completing tasks as expected. This process transforms a raw Base Model into an Instruct Model that is much more useful for real-world applications.",
            "example": "Instruction: 'Translate Hello to French.'\nResponse: 'Bonjour'\n\nAfter training on many examples like this, the model learns to follow instructions and provide the correct response instead of simply continuing the text."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Preparing for SFT Training",
            "explanation": "Before we can fine-tune a model, we need three things: a Base Model, a Dataset, and Training Libraries. The Base Model is the AI model we want to improve. We can download it from Hugging Face. Next, we need a dataset containing instruction-response examples that will teach the model how to behave. Finally, we install the required Python libraries that provide model loading, dataset handling, and training capabilities.\n\nFirst, install the required packages using:\n`pip install transformers datasets trl torch accelerate`\n\nEach library has a specific purpose:\n`transformers` loads pre-trained models and tokenizers.\n`datasets` downloads and manages training datasets.\n`trl` provides the `SFTTrainer` used for SFT.`from trl import SFTTrainer`\n`torch` is the deep learning framework that performs training.\n`accelerate` helps optimize training on different hardware.\n\nOnce the libraries are installed, we can download a Base Model, load a dataset, configure training settings, and start fine-tuning using SFTTrainer.",
            "example": "Step 1: Install libraries using `pip install transformers datasets trl torch accelerate`\nStep 2: Download a Base Model from Hugging Face\nStep 3: Load an Instruction Dataset\nStep 4: Configure Training Arguments\nStep 5: Run `trainer.train()` to start fine-tuning"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Build an SFT Training Pipeline. Use the `trl` library to set up a trainer that will fine-tune a model on an instruction dataset. This is the 'real-world' implementation for domain specialization.",
            "starterCode": "# Write your code below:\n",
            "solution": "from transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, TrainingArguments\nfrom trl import SFTTrainer\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\n\n# 1. Load a Base Model and Tokenizer\nmodel_id = \"facebook/opt-350m\"\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_id)\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\n\n# 2. Load a sample instruction dataset\ndataset = load_dataset(\"timdettmers/openassistant-guanaco\", split=\"train[:100]\")\n\n# 3. Define Training Arguments\ntraining_args = TrainingArguments(\n    output_dir=\"./sft_output\",\n    per_device_train_batch_size=4,\n    learning_rate=2e-4,\n    num_train_epochs=1,\n    logging_steps=10\n)\n\n# TODO: Initialize SFTTrainer with model, dataset, text field 'text', and args\ntrainer = ____(\n    model=model_id,\n    train_dataset=____,\n    dataset_text_field=\"____\",\n    args=____\n)\n\n# Start training\ntrainer.train()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use the class `SFTTrainer` and pass `dataset`, `\"text\"`, and `training_args` to the respective parameters.",
            "explanation": "SFTTrainer is a high-level wrapper that automates the training loop for fine-tuning LLMs on formatted text data."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "PEFT & LoRA: Fine-Tuning for Everyone",
            "explanation": "Fine-tuning 100% of an LLM's weights is impossible for most developers. A 70B model would require 140GB of VRAM just to load, and double that to train. \n\n**PEFT (Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning)** solves this. The most popular method is **LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation)**. Instead of updating the massive original weight matrix (W), we freeze it. We then add two tiny, skinny matrices (A and B) next to it. \n\nDuring training, we only update the tiny A and B matrices. This reduces the number of trainable parameters by 99.9%. It’s like leave the heavy library shelves alone and only writing new notes in the margins of the books.",
            "example": "Instead of training 7 billion\nparameters, LoRA allows you to\nonly train 4 million parameters,\nmeaning you can fine-tune a\nmassive model on a single\nconsumer gaming GPU very easily."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Real-World LoRA Fine-Tuning Example",
            "explanation": "Suppose we want to turn a general-purpose language model into a Customer Support Assistant. Instead of retraining billions of parameters, we use LoRA to efficiently teach the model how to answer customer questions.\n First, install the required libraries using `pip install transformers peft trl datasets torch accelerate`. Next, load a pre-trained model from Hugging Face. Then, load a customer-support dataset containing question-answer pairs.\n Create a LoRA configuration and attach it to the model using PEFT. Finally, train only the LoRA adapters using SFTTrainer and save the adapter. This approach requires far less memory while achieving strong task-specific performance.",
            "example": "Use Case: Fine-tune a chatbot for Customer Support.\n\n# Install:\n# pip install transformers peft trl datasets torch accelerate\n\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer, TrainingArguments\nfrom datasets import load_dataset\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, TaskType\nfrom trl import SFTTrainer\n\nmodel_id = 'facebook/opt-350m'\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(model_id)\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(model_id)\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\n\ndataset = load_dataset('timdettmers/openassistant-guanaco', split='train[:100]')\n\nlora_config = LoraConfig(r=16, lora_alpha=32, target_modules=['q_proj','v_proj'], lora_dropout=0.05, bias='none', task_type=TaskType.CAUSAL_LM)\n\nmodel = get_peft_model(model, lora_config)\nmodel.print_trainable_parameters()\n\ntraining_args = TrainingArguments(output_dir='./lora_output', per_device_train_batch_size=4, learning_rate=2e-4, num_train_epochs=1, logging_steps=10)\n\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(model=model, train_dataset=dataset, dataset_text_field='text', args=training_args)\n\ntrainer.train()\nmodel.save_pretrained('./customer_support_lora')\n\nprint('LoRA fine-tuning completed!')"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Implement LoRA. Use the `peft` library to wrap a base model in a LoRA configuration. This allows for 'Adapter-based' fine-tuning.",
            "starterCode": "from peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, TaskType\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\n\n# Load a small base model\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"facebook/opt-350m\")\n\n# 1. Define LoRA Config\n# r = rank (lower is smaller/faster), alpha = scaling\nlora_config = LoraConfig(\n    r=16,\n    lora_alpha=32,\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"v_proj\"],\n    lora_dropout=0.05,\n    bias=\"none\",\n    task_type=TaskType.CAUSAL_LM\n)\n\n# TODO: Wrap the base model with the LoRA config using get_peft_model\npeft_model = ____(____, ____)\n\n# Inspect trainable vs frozen parameters\npeft_model.print_trainable_parameters()",
            "solution": "from peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model, TaskType\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\n\n# Load a small base model\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"facebook/opt-350m\")\n\n# 1. Define LoRA Config\n# r = rank (lower is smaller/faster), alpha = scaling\nlora_config = LoraConfig(\n    r=16,\n    lora_alpha=32,\n    target_modules=[\"q_proj\", \"v_proj\"],\n    lora_dropout=0.05,\n    bias=\"none\",\n    task_type=TaskType.CAUSAL_LM\n)\n\n# TODO: Wrap the base model with the LoRA config using get_peft_model\npeft_model = get_peft_model(model, lora_config)\n\n# Inspect trainable vs frozen parameters\npeft_model.print_trainable_parameters()",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "The function `get_peft_model` takes the model and the lora_config as arguments.",
            "explanation": "By wrapping the model, PEFT freezes the original layers and injects the low-rank adapters, making the training memory-efficient."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "QLoRA: Fine-Tuning Large Models at Home",
            "explanation": "Even with LoRA, you still need to load the model into VRAM. A 7B model takes 14GB of VRAM in 16-bit. \n\n**QLoRA (Quantized LoRA)** fixes this by loading the base model in **4-bit precision**. It uses a technique called 'Double Quantization' and 'NF4' (Normal Float 4) to crush the weights without losing much intelligence. \n\nYou load the 4-bit model (frozen) and train 16-bit LoRA adapters on top. This is the 'Gold Standard' for Generative AI App Developers today because it allows fine-tuning 7B models on 8GB-12GB VRAM GPUs (like an RTX 3060).",
            "example": "A 7 billion parameter model\nnormally requiring 14GB of memory\nnow fits into just 4.5GB\nof memory using QLoRA, allowing\ndevelopers to train on laptops\nwithout needing expensive cloud clusters."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Implement QLoRA Loading. Configure `BitsAndBytesConfig` to load a model in 4-bit Normal Float (NF4) for maximum efficiency.",
            "starterCode": "import torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, BitsAndBytesConfig\n\n# TODO: Define the 4-bit quantization config\nbnb_config = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=____,\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True,\n    bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"____\",\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16\n)\n\n# Load model with quantization config\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    \"mistralai/Mistral-7B-v0.1\",\n    quantization_config=____,\n    device_map=\"auto\"\n)",
            "solution": "import torch\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, BitsAndBytesConfig\n\n# TODO: Define the 4-bit quantization config\nbnb_config = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True,\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True,\n    bnb_4bit_quant_type=\"nf4\",\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype=torch.bfloat16\n)\n\n# Load model with quantization config\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    \"mistralai/Mistral-7B-v0.1\",\n    quantization_config=bnb_config,\n    device_map=\"auto\"\n)",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Set `load_in_4bit` to True and use `\"nf4\"` as the quant_type.",
            "explanation": "NF4 is a specialized data type for weights that are normally distributed, ensuring minimal accuracy loss while crushing memory requirements."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Comparison: Transfer vs Adaptation vs Fine-Tuning",
            "explanation": "It is critical for a developer to know which tool to use. Use the table below as your engineering cheat sheet for model lifecycle management.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-family: sans-serif; background: #1e293b; color: #f8fafc; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;\"><thead><tr style=\"background: #d946ef; color: white;\"><th style=\"padding: 15px; text-align: left;\">Concept</th><th style=\"padding: 15px; text-align: left;\">Goal</th><th style=\"padding: 15px; text-align: left;\">Action</th><th style=\"padding: 15px; text-align: left;\">Compute Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid #334155;\"><td style=\"padding: 15px; font-weight: bold;\">Transfer Learning</td><td style=\"padding: 15px;\">Reuse general intelligence</td><td style=\"padding: 15px;\">Loading a Pretrained Base Model</td><td style=\"padding: 15px; color: #4ade80;\">Zero (Free)</td></tr><tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid #334155;\"><td style=\"padding: 15px; font-weight: bold;\">Adaptation</td><td style=\"padding: 15px;\">Domain Specialization</td><td style=\"padding: 15px;\">RAG, Prompting, or Fine-Tuning</td><td style=\"padding: 15px; color: #fbbf24;\">Low to Medium</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding: 15px; font-weight: bold;\">Fine-Tuning (PEFT/LoRA)</td><td style=\"padding: 15px;\">Internal Knowledge Update</td><td style=\"padding: 15px;\">Backpropagation on specific data</td><td style=\"padding: 15px; color: #f87171;\">Medium (GPU Req)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "speed_quiz",
          "timePerQuestion": 12,
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Which Fine-Tuning method involves freezing the base model and training tiny auxiliary matrices?",
              "options": [
                "Full Fine-Tuning",
                "LoRA",
                "RLHF",
                "Pretraining"
              ],
              "answer": 1
            },
            {
              "question": "What is a 'Base Model' in the context of Transfer Learning?",
              "options": [
                "A model that only speaks one language.",
                "A model pretrained on general data before specialization.",
                "A model that cannot write code.",
                "A model with 0 parameters."
              ],
              "answer": 1
            },
            {
              "question": "Which quantization format is specifically optimized for QLoRA fine-tuning?",
              "options": [
                "INT8",
                "FP16",
                "NF4",
                "GGUF"
              ],
              "answer": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M005-llm-lesson-7-build-from-scratch",
      "title": "Building a Mini GPT Model from scratch",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Build a Mini GPT?",
            "explanation": "Welcome back.So now we are going to build our own GPT model completely from scratch using PyTorch.\n\nBefore we start, let me ask you a question. Why don't we directly build ChatGPT or GPT-4?\n\nThe answer is simple. GPT-4 contains trillions of training tokens, massive datasets collected from the internet, thousands of GPUs, and billions of parameters. Training a model of that size would cost millions of dollars and require enormous computing resources.\n\nInstead, we build a Mini GPT. A Mini GPT uses the exact same core ideas used inside modern GPT models:\n\n• Tokenization\n• Embeddings\n• Positional Embeddings\n• Self-Attention\n• Multi-Head Attention\n• Transformer Blocks\n• Next Token Prediction\n• Autoregressive Generation\n\nThe only difference is scale.\n\nOur Mini GPT will learn from a very small dataset containing a few example sentences. After training, it will predict the next word and generate new text just like a real GPT model.\n\nBy the end of this lesson you will understand how GPT works internally and how companies scale the same architecture into powerful models like GPT-3, GPT-4, Llama, Claude, and Gemini.",
            "example": "Input: hello\n↓\nMini GPT predicts:\nhello friends how are you <END>\n\nSame architecture → Larger Data → More Parameters → GPT-4 Scale"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Problem We Want To Solve",
            "explanation": "Humans naturally predict the next word while reading a sentence.\n\nFor example:\n\n'I love drinking ____'\n\nMost people immediately think of words like coffee, or water.\n\nGPT models learn exactly this skill. They are trained to predict the next token repeatedly.\n\nIf the model becomes very good at predicting the next token, it eventually learns grammar, facts, reasoning patterns, language structure, and relationships between words.\n\nIn this lesson we will train a Mini GPT on a small custom dataset and teach it to predict the next word in a sequence.",
            "example": "Input Tokens:\nI love drinking\n\nTarget Token:\ncoffee\n\nAfter training:\n\nModel Prediction:\ncoffee ✅"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Training Data Used For Mini GPT",
            "explanation": "Unlike real GPT models which are trained on trillions of internet tokens, our Mini GPT learns from a very small collection of sentences.\n\nEach sentence ends with a special token called <END>. This tells the model where a sentence finishes.\n\nThe model reads all sentences, builds a vocabulary, converts words into numerical IDs, and learns patterns between words.\n\nEven though the dataset is tiny, it is enough to demonstrate every important GPT concept.",
            "example": "hello everyone how are you <END>\n\nthe coffee is very hot <END>\n\nmy name is Emma <END>\n\ni enjoy hiking in the mountains <END>\n\nnew york is a busy city <END>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Setup: Environment, Installation & File Structure",
            "explanation": "Before we start building our Mini GPT from scratch, let's prepare our development environment. We'll use Visual Studio Code (VS Code), Python, and PyTorch. Follow these setup steps once, and then we'll begin implementing each Transformer component.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Create Project Folder",
                    "body": "Create a new project folder named:\n\n```MiniGPT_From_Scratch```\n\nThis folder will contain all the source code for our Mini GPT implementation."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Open the Project in VS Code",
                    "body": "Launch Visual Studio Code and open the newly created project folder.\n\nVS Code provides an integrated terminal, debugger, and editor, making development much easier."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Create & Activate Virtual Environment",
                    "body": "Open the VS Code terminal and create a virtual environment.\n\n```python -m venv venv```\n\nActivate it:\n\nWindows:\n```venv\\Scripts\\activate```\n\nMac/Linux:\n```source venv/bin/activate```\n\nUsing a virtual environment keeps project dependencies isolated."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Install PyTorch",
                    "body": "Install the required deep learning libraries.\n\n```pip install torch torchvision torchaudio```\n\nVerify the installation:\n\n```python -c \"import torch; print(torch.__version__)\"```\n\nIf a version number is printed, PyTorch has been installed successfully."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Create Project Files",
                    "body": "Inside the project folder create the following files:\n\n```text\nMiniGPT_From_Scratch/\n├── transformer_blocks.py\n└── main.py\n```\n\nWe will write all our code inside these two files."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "File Responsibilities",
                    "body": "**transformer_blocks.py**\n\n• Self Attention Head\n• Multi Head Attention\n• Feed Forward Network\n• Transformer Block\n\n**main.py**\n\n• Dataset Creation\n• Vocabulary Building\n• Tokenization\n• MiniGPT Model\n• Training Loop\n• Text Generation"
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Ready to Build Mini GPT",
                    "body": "Our environment is now completely ready.\n\nIn the next slides, we'll implement every Transformer component one by one, connect them together, train the Mini GPT model, and finally generate new text from our trained model."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building the First Transformer Component: Self-Attention Head",
            "explanation": "Now it's time to start coding the Transformer architecture.\n\nOpen the file `transformer_blocks.py` and import the required PyTorch libraries.\n\nAs you already learned in the previous lessons, Self-Attention allows tokens to focus on important tokens in the sequence. Here we are implementing that same concept in code.\n\nCreate a class called `SelfAttentionHead`. Inside this class we define the Query, Key, and Value layers along with the causal mask used by GPT.\n\nDon't worry about every mathematical operation right now—we already discussed the theory. Focus on understanding how the attention mechanism is translated into code.\n\nAfter this step, we will have our first working Transformer component ready for use inside the GPT model.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># transformer_blocks.py</span><br><br>import torch<br>import torch.nn as nn<br>import torch.nn.functional as F<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Self-Attention Head</span><br>class SelfAttentionHead(nn.Module):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def __init__(self, embedding_dim, block_size, head_size):<br>&nbsp;super().__init__()<br>&nbsp;self.key = nn.Linear(embedding_dim, head_size, bias=False)<br>&nbsp;self.query = nn.Linear(embedding_dim, head_size, bias=False)<br>&nbsp;self.value = nn.Linear(embedding_dim, head_size, bias=False)<br>&nbsp;self.register_buffer('tril', torch.tril(torch.ones(block_size, block_size)))<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def forward(self, x):<br>&nbsp;B, T, C = x.shape<br>&nbsp;k = self.key(x)<br>&nbsp;q = self.query(x)<br>&nbsp;wei = q @ k.transpose(-2, -1) / (C ** 0.5)<br>&nbsp;wei = wei.masked_fill(self.tril[:T, :T] == 0, float('-inf'))<br>&nbsp;wei = F.softmax(wei, dim=-1)<br>&nbsp;v = self.value(x)<br>&nbsp;out = wei @ v<br>&nbsp;return out<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Self-Attention Head Created ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Implementing Multi-Head Attention",
            "explanation": "Continue working in the same `transformer_blocks.py` file.\n\nIn the previous step, we created a single Self-Attention Head. Now we will combine multiple attention heads together using a `MultiHeadAttention` class.\n\nAs you already learned in the theory section, different attention heads can focus on different relationships within the text. This allows the model to capture richer contextual information.\n\nHere we create multiple `SelfAttentionHead` objects, run them in parallel, concatenate their outputs, and finally project them back to the original embedding dimension.\n\nAfter completing this step, our Transformer will be able to learn multiple perspectives of the same input sequence simultaneously.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Continue in transformer_blocks.py</span><br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Multi-Head Attention</span><br>class MultiHeadAttention(nn.Module):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def __init__(self, embedding_dim, block_size, num_heads):<br>&nbsp;super().__init__()<br>&nbsp;head_size = embedding_dim // num_heads<br>&nbsp;self.heads = nn.ModuleList([SelfAttentionHead(embedding_dim, block_size, head_size) for _ in range(num_heads)])<br>&nbsp;self.proj = nn.Linear(num_heads * head_size, embedding_dim)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def forward(self, x):<br>&nbsp;out = torch.cat([h(x) for h in self.heads], dim=-1)<br>&nbsp;return self.proj(out)<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Multi-Head Attention Created ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Implementing the Feed Forward Network",
            "explanation": "Continue working in the same `transformer_blocks.py` file.\n\nSo far, we have implemented Self-Attention and Multi-Head Attention. Now let's add the Feed Forward Network.\n\nAs discussed earlier, Attention helps the model gather information from different tokens, while the Feed Forward Network processes that information and learns deeper representations.\n\nHere we create a simple neural network consisting of two Linear layers with a ReLU activation function in between. This component will later become part of every Transformer Block.\n\nAfter completing this step, we will have all the core components needed to build a complete Transformer Block.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Continue in transformer_blocks.py</span><br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Feed Forward Network</span><br>class FeedForward(nn.Module):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def __init__(self, n_embd):<br>&nbsp;super().__init__()<br>&nbsp;self.net = nn.Sequential(<br>nn.Linear(n_embd, 4 * n_embd),<br>nn.ReLU(),<br>nn.Linear(4 * n_embd, n_embd)<br>&nbsp;)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def forward(self, x):<br>&nbsp;return self.net(x)<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Feed Forward Network Created ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Implementing the Transformer Block",
            "explanation": "Continue working in the same `transformer_blocks.py` file.\n\nNow we are building the most important component of the entire Transformer architecture: the Transformer Block.\n\nAs you already learned earlier, a Transformer Block combines Multi-Head Attention, Feed Forward Networks, Residual Connections, and Layer Normalization into a single reusable unit.\n\nIn this implementation, we are creating a simplified educational version of a Transformer Block so that the architecture is easier to understand.\n\nReal production models such as GPT-4, Claude, Gemini, and Llama use more advanced techniques including:\n\n• Dropout layers for regularization\n• Better weight initialization strategies\n• Optimized attention implementations (Flash Attention)\n• RMSNorm instead of LayerNorm in some models\n• SwiGLU activation functions instead of simple ReLU\n• Mixture of Experts (MoE) architectures\n• Quantization and inference optimizations\n\nHowever, the core idea remains exactly the same. Every modern LLM is fundamentally built by stacking many Transformer Blocks together.\n\nHere we combine Multi-Head Attention and Feed Forward Networks using Residual Connections and Layer Normalization. After completing this step, we will have a complete Transformer Block ready to stack multiple times inside our GPT model.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Continue in transformer_blocks.py</span><br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Transformer Block</span><br>class Block(nn.Module):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def __init__(self, embedding_dim, block_size, n_heads):<br>&nbsp;super().__init__()<br>&nbsp;self.sa = MultiHeadAttention(embedding_dim, block_size, n_heads)<br>&nbsp;self.ffwd = FeedForward(embedding_dim)<br>&nbsp;self.ln1 = nn.LayerNorm(embedding_dim)<br>&nbsp;self.ln2 = nn.LayerNorm(embedding_dim)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def forward(self, x):<br>&nbsp;x = x + self.sa(self.ln1(x))<br>&nbsp;x = x + self.ffwd(self.ln2(x))<br>&nbsp;return x<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Transformer Block Created ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Creating the Main GPT Training Script",
            "explanation": "Now create a new file called `demo.py`. This file will act as the entry point of our Mini GPT project.\n\nIn the previous slides, we implemented all the reusable Transformer components inside `transformer_blocks.py`. Now we will start using those components to build and train an actual GPT model.\n\nFirst, import the required libraries and import the `Block` class that we created earlier.\n\nNext, we create a small training corpus. In real-world GPT models, companies use trillions of tokens collected from books, websites, code repositories, research papers, and many other sources. Since training on that scale requires thousands of GPUs and enormous computing resources, we will use a small custom dataset for learning purposes.\n\nAfter creating the dataset, we build the vocabulary. GPT cannot understand words directly, so every word must be converted into a numerical token ID. We create two mappings:\n\n• `word2idx` → Convert words into token IDs.\n• `idx2word` → Convert token IDs back into words.\n\nFinally, we define the model hyperparameters such as context window size, embedding dimensions, number of attention heads, number of Transformer blocks, learning rate, and training epochs. These settings control how large and powerful our Mini GPT will be.\n\nAfter this step, our dataset, vocabulary, and training configuration will be ready for model training.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># demo.py</span><br><br>import torch<br>import torch.nn as nn<br>import torch.nn.functional as F<br>import random<br><br>from transformer_blocks import Block<br><br>print('Torch version:', torch.__version__)<br>print('CUDA available:', torch.cuda.is_available())<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Small Training Corpus</span><br>corpus = [<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;'hello everyone welcome home',<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;'the coffee is very hot',<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;'my name is Emma',<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;'the streets of New York are busy',<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;'it is snowing in Colorado'<br>]<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Vocabulary Creation</span><br>corpus = [s + ' &lt;END&gt;' for s in corpus]<br>text = ' '.join(corpus)<br>words = list(set(text.split()))<br><br>word2idx = {w:i for i,w in enumerate(words)}<br>idx2word = {i:w for w,i in word2idx.items()}<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Tokenization</span><br>data = torch.tensor([word2idx[w] for w in text.split()], dtype=torch.long)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Hyperparameters</span><br>block_size = 6<br>embedding_dim = 32<br>n_heads = 2<br>n_layers = 2<br>lr = 1e-3<br>epochs = 1500<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Dataset & Vocabulary Ready ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Creating Training Batches",
            "explanation": "Continue working in the same `demo.py` file.\n\nNow we need a way to feed training data to our GPT model. Instead of sending the entire dataset at once, we randomly sample small chunks of text called batches.\n\nThis function creates the input sequence (`x`) and the target sequence (`y`). The target is simply the input shifted by one position.\n\nFor example:\n\nInput  : [hello, friends, how, are]\nTarget : [friends, how, are, you]\n\nThis is how GPT learns next-token prediction. Given the current words, the model tries to predict the next word in the sequence.\n\nIn large GPT models, batching is handled using sophisticated data pipelines and distributed training systems. For our Mini GPT, this simple batching function is enough to demonstrate the core training process.\n\nAfter this step, we will be able to generate random training samples whenever the model needs data.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Training Batch Generator</span><br><br>def get_batch(batch_size=16):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;ix = torch.randint(len(data) - block_size, (batch_size,))<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;x = torch.stack([data[i:i+block_size] for i in ix])<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;y = torch.stack([data[i+1:i+block_size+1] for i in ix])<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;return x, y<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Training Batch Function Created ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building, Training & Testing the Mini GPT Model",
            "explanation": "Continue working in the same `demo.py` file.\nNow we are finally assembling our complete Mini GPT model.\nIn this class, we create all the major GPT components:\n• Token Embeddings\n• Position Embeddings\n• Transformer Blocks\n• Layer Normalization\n• Vocabulary Prediction Head\n\nThe `forward()` method performs the training computations and calculates the loss, while the `generate()` method allows the model to generate new text one token at a time.\n\nAfter creating the model, we initialize the AdamW optimizer and start the training loop. During training, the model repeatedly receives batches of text and learns to predict the next token. As training progresses, the loss should gradually decrease, indicating that the model is learning language patterns from the dataset.\n\nFinally, we test the Mini GPT by providing a starting word and allowing the model to generate additional tokens.\n\nRemember, this is a very small educational GPT trained on only a few sentences. The generated text will not be as intelligent as ChatGPT or GPT-4. However, the underlying architecture is based on the same Transformer principles used by modern Large Language Models.\n\nOnce training is complete, you should see the training loss decreasing along with generated text similar to the patterns present in the training corpus.",
            "html_code": "<div style='background:#1e1e1e;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;font-family:Consolas,monospace;color:#d4d4d4;line-height:1.5;overflow:auto;'><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Mini GPT Model</span><br><br>class MiniGPT(nn.Module):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def __init__(self):<br>&nbsp;super().__init__()<br>&nbsp;self.token_embedding = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, embedding_dim)<br>&nbsp;self.position_embedding = nn.Embedding(block_size, embedding_dim)<br>&nbsp;self.blocks = nn.Sequential(*[Block(embedding_dim, block_size, n_heads) for _ in range(n_layers)])<br>&nbsp;self.ln_f = nn.LayerNorm(embedding_dim)<br>&nbsp;self.head = nn.Linear(embedding_dim, vocab_size)<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def forward(self, idx, targets=None):<br>&nbsp;# Training Forward Pass<br>&nbsp;return logits, loss<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;def generate(self, idx, max_new_tokens):<br>&nbsp;# Text Generation Logic<br>&nbsp;return idx<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Create Model & Optimizer</span><br>model = MiniGPT()<br>optimizer = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=lr)<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Training Loop</span><br>for step in range(epochs):<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;xb, yb = get_batch()<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;logits, loss = model(xb, yb)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;optimizer.zero_grad()<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;loss.backward()<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;optimizer.step()<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Test the Model</span><br>context = torch.tensor([[word2idx['hello']]], dtype=torch.long)<br>out = model.generate(context, max_new_tokens=15)<br><br>print('Generated Text:')<br>print(' '.join(idx2word[int(i)] for i in out[0]))<br><br><span style='color:#6A9955;'># Sample Output</span><br>hello friends how are you &lt;END&gt;<br><br><span style='color:#4EC9B0;'>Mini GPT Training & Testing Complete ✓</span></div>"
          }
        },
