    {
      "id": "M006-prompt-eng-02",
      "title": "Core Prompting Techniques",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#3b82f6",
        "accent": "#1d4ed8"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Zero-Shot: The Brave Explorer",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! In the previous lesson, we learned how to write better prompts. Now let's learn the first prompting technique used by AI engineers.\nImagine you are dropped into a kitchen with a basket of ingredients and told, 'Make dinner.' You have no recipe and no examples—just your prior knowledge. This is **Zero-Shot Prompting**.\n\nYou give the LLM a task with no previous context or examples. It relies entirely on its pre-training to figure it out. It's the fastest way to prompt, perfect for simple, standard tasks like summarizing a paragraph or translating a word.\n\n• **When to use:** Quick tasks, creative brainstorming, or testing a model's baseline intelligence.\n• **The Risk:** High variance. The model might format the answer in a way you didn't expect.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; background: #0f172a; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #3b82f6;\"><h4 style=\"color: #60a5fa; margin-top:0;\">Zero-Shot Visualizer</h4><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.05); padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px;\"><div style=\"background: #3b82f6; padding: 5px 10px; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 12px;\">User Prompt</div><div style=\"font-style: italic; font-size: 13px;\">\"Write a poem about a toaster.\"</div></div><div style=\"text-align: center; margin: 15px 0; color: #60a5fa;\"><i class=\"fas fa-arrow-down\"></i></div><div style=\"background: rgba(96, 165, 250, 0.1); padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #60a5fa;\"><p style=\"margin:0; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.4;\">The silver box sits on the shelf,<br>It toasts the bread all by itself.<br>A golden crunch, a sudden pop,<br>The morning hunger it will stop.</p></div><div style=\"font-size: 11px; color: #94a3b8; margin-top: 10px;\">Note: No examples provided. Model uses 100% internal logic.</div></div>",
            "example": "# Zero-Shot Prompt\nprompt = '''\nSummarize the following paragraph in one sentence.\n\nArtificial Intelligence is transforming healthcare by helping doctors diagnose diseases faster and more accurately.\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "One-Shot: The Map Maker",
            "explanation": "Sometimes Zero-Shot is not enough.Then we can use One-Short Prompting.\nImagine you want to teach a new employee how to write professional emails.\n If you simply say, 'Write a professional email,' they may not know exactly what style or format you expect. Instead, you show them one sample email and say, 'Write future emails like this.' By looking at that single example, they quickly understand the structure, tone, and writing style you want.\n\n **One-Shot Prompting** works in the same way. Instead of giving only an instruction like Zero-Shot Prompting, you provide one example of the task along with its correct output. The AI studies that example, learns the pattern, and then applies the same pattern to a new input.\nThis helps the model produce more accurate, consistent, and properly formatted responses because it has a clear reference to follow.",
            "example": "# One-Shot Prompt\nprompt = '''\nExample:\nInput: Apple\nOutput: Fruit\n\nNow classify:\nInput: Carrot\nOutput:\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)\n# Output: Vegetable",
            "html_code": "<iframe style=\"width:100%; min-height:240px; border:none; background:transparent;\" srcdoc=\"<style>body{margin:0;font-family:'Segoe UI',sans-serif;display:flex;justify-content:center;align-items:center;background:transparent;color:#f8fafc;} .container{background:linear-gradient(145deg, #1e1b4b, #2e1065);border:1px solid #6366f1;border-radius:12px;padding:20px;width:100%;max-width:550px;box-shadow:0 8px 32px rgba(99,102,241,0.2);} .header{text-align:center;color:#a5b4fc;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:15px;font-size:14px;letter-spacing:0.5px;} .layout{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px;} .box{background:rgba(15,23,42,0.8);border:1px solid #4f46e5;border-radius:8px;padding:15px;flex:1;} .prompt-title{color:#f472b6;font-size:12px;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:10px;text-transform:uppercase;} .code-line{font-family:monospace;font-size:12px;color:#cbd5e1;margin:6px 0;padding:6px;background:rgba(0,0,0,0.3);border-radius:4px;} .highlight{color:#4ade80;border-left:3px solid #4ade80;background:rgba(74, 222, 128, 0.05);} .target{color:#fcd34d;border-left:3px solid #fcd34d;} .arrow{color:#c084fc;font-size:24px;animation:pulse 1.5s infinite;} .result-box{background:rgba(16, 185, 129, 0.1);border:1px solid #10b981;text-align:center;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:center;height:104px;} .result-title{color:#34d399;font-size:12px;font-weight:bold;margin-bottom:8px;text-transform:uppercase;} .result-value{font-size:20px;font-weight:bold;color:#fff;} .badge{margin-top:10px;background:#10b981;color:#022c22;font-size:10px;font-weight:bold;padding:4px 10px;border-radius:12px;display:inline-block;} @keyframes pulse{0%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:0.5;}50%{transform:translateX(6px);opacity:1;}100%{transform:translateX(0);opacity:0.5;}}</style><div class='container'><div class='header'>🎯 One-Shot Pattern Recognition</div><div class='layout'><div class='box'><div class='prompt-title'>📝 The Prompt</div><div class='code-line'>Task: Convert to color</div><div class='code-line highlight'>Ex: Apple ➔ Red</div><div class='code-line target'>Q: Grape ➔</div></div><div class='arrow'>→</div><div class='box result-box'><div class='result-title'>✨ AI Output</div><div class='result-value'>Purple</div><div><span class='badge'>Pattern Locked ✔️</span></div></div></div></div>\"></iframe>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Few-Shot: The Pattern Architect",
            "explanation": "Great! But what if one example is still not enough?\nImagine you want to train a new employee to handle customer support emails. If you show only one example, they may understand the general pattern but still make mistakes in unusual situations.\nInstead, you show them several examples covering different customer requests and the correct responses for each one. After reviewing multiple examples, they begin to understand the rules, tone, structure, and decision-making process much more clearly.\n\n **Few-Shot Prompting** works in the same way. Instead of providing only one example, you provide a few examples (typically 3 to 5) of inputs and their expected outputs. The AI studies all of these examples, identifies common patterns, and learns how to handle similar tasks more accurately.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/prompt/in1.png]\n`Real Use Cases:` Classifying customer reviews as Positive, Negative, or Neutral, and teaching the AI a specific email format, brand voice, JSON structure, or customer support response style using multiple examples.",
            "example": "# Few-Shot Prompt\nprompt = '''\nInput: Apple\nOutput: Fruit\n\nInput: Carrot\nOutput: Vegetable\n\nInput: Rose\nOutput: Flower\n\nNow classify:\nInput: Mango\nOutput:\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)\n# Output: Fruit"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Role Prompting: The Expert Mask",
            "explanation": "We have taught the AI patterns.Now let's teach it how to think.\nImagine asking the same question to three different people: a Software Engineer, a Doctor, and a Marketing Manager. Even though the question is the same, each person will answer differently because of their knowledge, experience, vocabulary, and perspective.\n\n**Role Prompting** works in the same way. By telling the AI to act as a specific professional or expert, you guide it toward a particular style of thinking and responding. Instead of acting as a generic assistant, the model starts prioritizing information, tone, and reasoning that match the assigned role. This often leads to more focused, realistic, and task-specific responses.",
            "html_code": "<div style='font-family:Arial,sans-serif;max-width:800px;margin:auto;padding:20px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;text-align:center;'><h3 style='color:#0f172a;margin-top:0;'>Same Question, Different Roles</h3><div style='display:flex;justify-content:center;gap:15px;flex-wrap:wrap;'><div style='width:180px;background:#dbeafe;border:2px solid #3b82f6;border-radius:12px;padding:15px;'><div style='font-size:32px;'>💻</div><b style='color:#111827;'>Software Engineer</b><div style='margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;color:#334155;'>Focuses on code, performance, and scalability.</div></div><div style='width:180px;background:#dcfce7;border:2px solid #22c55e;border-radius:12px;padding:15px;'><div style='font-size:32px;'>🩺</div><b style='color:#111827;'>Doctor</b><div style='margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;color:#334155;'>Focuses on health, safety, and diagnosis.</div></div><div style='width:180px;background:#fef3c7;border:2px solid #eab308;border-radius:12px;padding:15px;'><div style='font-size:32px;'>📈</div><b style='color:#111827;'>Marketing Manager</b><div style='margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;color:#334155;'>Focuses on customers, branding, and growth.</div></div></div><div style='margin-top:20px;background:white;padding:12px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;color:#111827;font-weight:bold;'>Question → Assign Role → Role-Specific Thinking → Better Response</div></div>",
            "example": "# Role Prompting\nprompt = '''\nYou are an experienced Python instructor.\n\nExplain what a Python list is in simple words with one example.\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Challenge: Write a Zero-Shot Prompt",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Write a prompt that asks the AI to summarize a news article in 3 bullet points without providing any examples.",
            "starterCode": "Write a prompt: ''",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "• Main event\n• Important details\n• Final outcome",
            "hint": "Give only instructions. Do not provide any examples.",
            "solution": "Summarize the following news article in exactly 3 bullet points highlighting the most important information."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Persona Prompting: The Voice Actor",
            "explanation": "Amazing! Role Prompting changes expertise.But what if we want to change personality?\nImagine two people explaining the same topic: one is a friendly teacher and the other is a strict military officer. Even though the information is the same, the way they speak feels completely different.\n\n **Persona Prompting** works in the same way. Instead of changing the AI's expertise, it changes the AI's personality, tone, and communication style. You can make the model sound friendly, humorous, professional, motivational, empathetic, sarcastic, or even like a specific character.\n\n This technique is useful when you want responses to feel more natural, engaging, or aligned with a brand's personality. The goal is not to change what the AI knows, but how it communicates that knowledge.",
            "html_code": "<div style='font-family:Arial,sans-serif;max-width:850px;margin:auto;padding:20px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;text-align:center;'><h3 style='color:#0f172a;margin-top:0;'>Same Answer, Different Personality</h3><div style='display:flex;justify-content:center;gap:15px;flex-wrap:wrap;'><div style='width:220px;background:#dbeafe;border:2px solid #3b82f6;border-radius:12px;padding:15px;'><div style='font-size:32px;'>😊</div><b style='color:#111827;'>Friendly Teacher</b><div style='margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;color:#334155;'>\"Great question! Let's learn it step by step.\"</div></div><div style='width:220px;background:#dcfce7;border:2px solid #22c55e;border-radius:12px;padding:15px;'><div style='font-size:32px;'>💼</div><b style='color:#111827;'>Professional Consultant</b><div style='margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;color:#334155;'>\"Based on the available data, here is the recommended approach.\"</div></div><div style='width:220px;background:#fef3c7;border:2px solid #eab308;border-radius:12px;padding:15px;'><div style='font-size:32px;'>😂</div><b style='color:#111827;'>Funny Friend</b><div style='margin-top:8px;font-size:12px;color:#334155;'>\"Don't worry, this is easier than assembling IKEA furniture!\"</div></div></div><div style='margin-top:20px;background:white;padding:12px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;color:#111827;font-weight:bold;'>Question → Assign Persona → Change Tone & Style → More Human-Like Response</div></div>",
            "example": "# Persona Prompting\nprompt = '''\nYou are a motivational fitness coach.\n\nGive me advice on exercising every day.\n'''\n\nresponse = llm(prompt)\nprint(response)\n\n# Output:\n# You've got this! Start with just 15 minutes today.\n# Small daily steps lead to big results over time!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Format-Constrained Prompting",
            "explanation": "Outstanding! We can now control knowledge, perspective, and personality.But real-world software needs one more thing:\nIn real-world applications, AI responses are often consumed by software systems rather than humans. A chatbot can return plain text, but an AI Agent usually needs structured outputs that other applications can directly process.\n\n **Format-Constrained Prompting** is a technique where you instruct the model to generate responses in a specific format such as JSON, XML, Markdown, CSV, or SQL. Instead of producing lengthy explanations, the model focuses on following the required structure exactly.\n\n This makes the output predictable, machine-readable, and easy to integrate with APIs, databases, automation workflows, and software applications. By enforcing a fixed format, developers can reliably parse the AI response and trigger actions without additional manual processing.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: monospace; background: #000; color: #4ade80; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px;\"><span style=\"color: #f472b6;\">{</span><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;\"action\": <span style=\"color: #fbbf24;\">\"send_email\"</span>,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;\"recipient\": <span style=\"color: #fbbf24;\">\"sarah@company.com\"</span>,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;\"urgent\": <span style=\"color: #60a5fa;\">true</span><br><span style=\"color: #f472b6;\">}</span><div style=\"color: #ef4444; font-size: 11px; margin-top: 10px; border-top: 1px solid #333; padding-top: 5px;\">// No conversational text allowed. Only raw JSON data.</div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Challenge: Write a Few-Shot Prompt",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a prompt that classifies customer reviews as Positive or Negative using multiple examples.",
            "starterCode": "Write a prompt: ''",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "Negative",
            "hint": "Provide 3 examples before asking the AI to classify the final review.",
            "solution": "Review: 'Amazing product and great quality.' Sentiment: Positive. Review: 'Terrible experience and poor support.' Sentiment: Negative. Review: 'Fast delivery and excellent packaging.' Sentiment: Positive. Now classify: 'The product stopped working after one day.'"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "question": "Project: Agentic Resume Optimizer. Use a Role and Format-Constrained prompt to extract skills from a raw resume bio into a clean list.",
            "starterCode": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef optimize_resume(bio_text):\n    # TODO: Create a prompt that uses Role Prompting and Format Constraints\n    prompt = f\"\"\"\n    Role: You are a Senior Technical Recruiter.\n    Task: Extract a list of professional skills from the bio below.\n    Format: Output only a valid JSON array of strings.\n    \n    Bio: {bio_text}\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        temperature=0.0\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nbio = \"Joe has 5 years of experience in Python, React, and AWS Cloud architectures.\"\nprint(optimize_resume(bio))",
            "solution": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef optimize_resume(bio_text):\n    prompt = f\"\"\"\n    Role: You are a Senior Technical Recruiter.\n    Task: Extract a list of professional skills from the bio below.\n    Format: Output only a valid JSON array of strings.\n    \n    Bio: {bio_text}\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        temperature=0.0\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nbio = \"Joe has 5 years of experience in Python, React, and AWS Cloud architectures.\"\nprint(optimize_resume(bio))",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Set the role to 'Recruiter' and specify 'Output only a valid JSON array'.",
            "explanation": "By combining Role (expertise) and Format (JSON), you ensure the AI behaves like a backend service rather than a conversational assistant."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Professional Email Writer",
          "description": "You need to build a tool for a CEO who is very busy. The CEO wants to send emails that are 'Direct, professional, and never more than 3 sentences.' However, the AI keeps writing long, flowery introductions like 'I hope this email finds you well...'. How do you fix this using the techniques learned?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Use Zero-Shot prompting and hope the AI gets it right.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Zero-shot is too unpredictable for strict length and tone requirements.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Apply a 'Busy CEO' Persona and use Few-Shot prompting with 3 examples of short, direct emails.",
              "outcome": "Correct! Few-shot examples will visually demonstrate the '3-sentence rule' to the model better than any instruction.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "Just tell the AI to 'be concise' without giving any examples.",
              "outcome": "Partial success. 'Be concise' is subjective. The model might still write 5 sentences.",
              "score": 40
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the primary operational difference between Role Prompting and Persona Prompting?",
              "options": [
                "Role Prompting controls the technical domain knowledge boundaries, while Persona Prompting updates the tone, voice, and stylistic delivery traits.",
                "Role Prompting changes model parameter weights, whereas Persona Prompting only scales token length limits.",
                "Role Prompting requires few-shot example matrices, while Persona Prompting operates purely zero-shot.",
                "There is no difference; they are identical concepts within API orchestration layers."
              ],
              "answer": 0,
              "explanation": "Role defines specialized structural knowledge and professional accountability. Persona controls stylistic interaction design patterns and behavioral voice presentation."
            },
            {
              "question": "When building an automated data workflow, why are output templates and format constraints critical?",
              "options": [
                "They decrease the total token compute cost of the system by 90%.",
                "They prevent the model from generating conversational filler text, ensuring outputs can be cleanly parsed programmatically.",
                "They automatically increase the model's contextual memory window.",
                "They enforce creative randomness during inference phases."
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Format constraints remove natural text conversational elements from output buffers, enabling applications to directly pass data structures into JSON parsers or databases without syntax failures."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Broken CRM System",
          "description": "You are deploying an automated system that reads client feedback data records from a web dashboard and drafts email replies automatically. During beta testing, the model occasionally inserts pleasantries like 'Sure, here is that email draft for you:' or writes responses that sound overly apologetic and defensive when handling complaints. This upsets corporate users. How do you re-engineer the system prompt architecture to handle this reliably?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Increase the inference temperature parameter to 1.2 to give the system space to self-correct its style naturally.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Increasing temperature amplifies token distribution variance, leading to more erratic styling and less adherence to rules.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Inject an explicit Role and Persona configuration, use a One-Shot or Few-Shot format template example of a perfect response, and strictly instruct it to output only the raw email content block.",
              "outcome": "Correct! Providing explicit functional roles, balancing tone through persona controls, anchoring style using few-shot matrices, and formatting boundaries ensures zero conversational text leaks into production logs.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "Add more context text to the input prompt explaining the company's background philosophy.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Adding unrelated background text shifts internal attention vectors away from crucial structure rules, increasing formatting errors.",
              "score": 20
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M006-prompt-eng-03",
      "title": "Reasoning Prompting Techniques",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Chain of Thought (CoT): Showing Your Work",
            "explanation": "Fantastic! In the previous lesson, we learned how to give the AI better instructions. But what happens when the problem itself becomes difficult?\nRemember in grade school when your math teacher insisted you 'show your work' instead of just writing down the final answer? They did this so you wouldn't make careless mistakes. AI models need the exact same thing.\n\n**Chain of Thought (CoT)** is a technique where you provide few-shot examples that include the step-by-step reasoning process leading to the answer. If a model tries to answer a complex logic problem in one single jump, it often fails. By forcing it to output its intermediate reasoning steps, it effectively 'thinks out loud' and arrives at the correct conclusion.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/prompt/rp1.png]\n",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; background: #faf5ff; border: 1px solid #d8b4fe; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px;\"><div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px;\"><div style=\"background: #fee2e2; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #ef4444;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #991b1b;\">Standard Prompt (Fails)</strong><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin-top: 8px; color: #7f1d1d;\">Q: Roger has 5 tennis balls. He buys 2 more cans of tennis balls. Each can has 3 balls. How many does he have now?<br><br>A: The answer is 11.</p></div><div style=\"background: #dcfce7; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid #22c55e;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #166534;\">CoT Prompt (Succeeds)</strong><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin-top: 8px; color: #14532d;\">Q: Roger has 5 tennis balls...<br><br>A: Roger started with 5 balls. 2 cans of 3 tennis balls each is 6 tennis balls. 5 + 6 = 11. The answer is 11.</p></div></div><p style=\"font-size: 12px; margin-top: 15px; color: #6b21a8; font-weight: bold; text-align: center;\">By giving an example of the 'thought process', the AI learns to calculate before it concludes.</p></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Zero-Shot CoT: The Magic Phrase",
            "explanation": "What if you don't have the time to write out complex few-shot examples with reasoning steps? Enter the 'Magic Phrase'.\n\n**Zero-Shot CoT** or **Step-by-Step Reasoning** involves simply appending the phrase **'Let's think step by step'** to the end of your prompt. This simple instruction triggers the model's pre-trained reasoning capabilities, forcing it to break down the problem linearly before outputting a final answer. It is one of the most cost-effective ways to immediately improve a model's logic.",
            "tip": "Always use 'Let's think step by step' for math, logic puzzles, or complex data extraction tasks where zero-shot accuracy is low.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #1e1b4b; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; font-family: monospace; color: #a78bfa; text-align: center;\"><div style=\"font-size: 16px; margin-bottom: 10px;\">User Prompt</div><div style=\"background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; color: white; text-align: left;\">I went to the market and bought 10 apples. I gave 2 to the neighbor and 2 to the repairman. I then went and bought 5 more apples and ate 1. How many apples did I remain with?<br><br><span style=\"color: #fde047; font-weight: bold;\">Let's think step by step.</span></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tree of Thoughts (ToT): The Chess Grandmaster",
            "explanation": "Wonderful! Chain of Thought follows a single path.But real-world decision making is rarely that simple.\nImagine a chess grandmaster.Before making a move, they don't evaluate only one possibility.\nThey think:• What if I move my Queen?• What if I move my Knight?• What if I sacrifice a piece?\nThey explore multiple possible futures before selecting the best move.\nTree of Thoughts (ToT) applies the same idea to AI reasoning.\n\nInstead of generating one reasoning path, the model creates multiple possible reasoning branches.\nIt then:\n1. Generates several ideas.\n2. Evaluates each idea.\n3. Discards weak ideas.\n4. Expands strong ideas.\n5. Chooses the best solution.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/prompt/rp2.png]\nThis is especially useful for:\n• Strategic Planning\n• Creative Writing\n• Business Decisions\n• Puzzle Solving\n• Agent Planning Systems\n\n`Key Idea:`\nTree of Thoughts explores multiple solutions before choosing the best one."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Reasoning Prompting Techniques",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "ToT explores multiple reasoning ____.",
              "Bad paths are ____.",
              "CoT encourages the model to show ____ steps."
            ],
            "options": [
              "branches",
              "reasoning",
              "discarded",
              "complex",
              "tokens",
              "layers"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "branches",
              "blank_2": "discarded",
              "blank_3": "reasoning"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Self-Consistency: The Wisdom of the Crowd",
            "explanation": "Great! But even Tree of Thoughts can sometimes make mistakes.Let's imagine you ask one doctor for a diagnosis.That doctor might be wrong.Now imagine asking ten doctors independently.\nIf nine doctors give the same answer, your confidence increases dramatically.\nThis idea inspired Self-Consistency.\n\nInstead of generating one reasoning chain, we generate multiple reasoning chains.\nExample:\nReasoning Path 1 → Answer = 42\nReasoning Path 2 → Answer = 42\nReasoning Path 3 → Answer = 42\nReasoning Path 4 → Answer = 39\nReasoning Path 5 → Answer = 42\nThe majority answer wins.\nThis technique reduces:\n• Hallucinations\n• Random Mistakes\n• Arithmetic Errors\n• Logic Failures\n\nSelf-Consistency is often combined with Chain of Thought.\n\nKey Idea:\nIf multiple reasoning paths independently agree on the same answer, the answer is usually more reliable."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "ReAct Framework: Reason + Act",
            "explanation": "Amazing! So far, all reasoning techniques happen entirely inside the model's brain.But there is a problem.LLMs do not automatically know today's weather,live stock prices,database records Sometimes reasoning alone is not enough.\nThe model must take an action.This leads to ReAct (Reason + Act).\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/prompt/rp3.png]\n**ReAct (Reasoning and Acting)** solves this by teaching the model to alternate between \"Thinking\" and \"Acting\". The model generates a reasoning trace, realizes it needs outside information, and outputs a specific command to trigger an external tool (like a Web Search or API). Once the tool returns the data, the model observes it and continues reasoning.\nThe process works like this:\n•Step 1: Think about the problem.\n•Step 2: Decide what information is missing.\n•Step 3: Use a tool.\n•Step 4: Observe the result.\n•Step 5: Continue reasoning.\n•Step 6: Produce the final answer.\n**Example:**\nThought:I need today's weather.\nAction:Search Weather API\nObservation:28°C and Sunny\nThought:Now I have the information.\nFinal Answer:Today's weather is 28°C and sunny.\n\nThis reasoning-action loop powers modern AI agents.That we discuss in letter.",
            "example": "Thought: I need to find out who won the 2024 Super Bowl to answer the user's question.\nAction: Search[\"2024 Super Bowl winner\"]\nObservation: The Kansas City Chiefs won the 2024 Super Bowl.\nThought: Now I have the answer.\nAction: Finish[\"The Kansas City Chiefs won.\"]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
