    {
      "id": "M007-vector-db-05-rag-pipeline",
      "title": "Building a Semantic Search Engine",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#ec4899",
        "accent": "#be185d"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The End-to-End Architecture",
            "explanation": "So far, we have learned several important concepts.We have learned about embeddings, distance metrics, and vector databases. Now, we put them together into a complete system: A Semantic Search Engine, which is the foundational retrieval mechanism for **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)**.\n\nThe pipeline is split into two distinct phases:\n1. **Data Ingestion (Offline):** Preparing and storing your knowledge base.\n2. **Query Retrieval (Runtime):** Finding the right information when the user asks a question.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #0f172a; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #ec4899; color: white; font-family: sans-serif; text-align: center;\"><h4 style=\"color: #f472b6; margin-top: 0;\">⚙️ The Semantic Pipeline</h4><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-top: 15px; font-size: 12px;\"><div style=\"width: 45%; background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px; border-top: 3px solid #3b82f6;\"><b style=\"color: #60a5fa;\">Ingestion</b><div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #cbd5e1; text-align: left; line-height: 1.5;\">1. Get Raw Document<br>2. Chunk Document<br>3. Generate Embeddings<br>4. Store in Vector DB</div></div><div style=\"width: 45%; background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 8px; border-top: 3px solid #10b981;\"><b style=\"color: #34d399;\">Retrieval</b><div style=\"margin-top: 8px; color: #cbd5e1; text-align: left; line-height: 1.5;\">1. User types Query<br>2. Embed Query<br>3. Cosine Similarity Search<br>4. Return Top-K Chunks</div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Step 1: Document Chunking",
            "explanation": "Let's start with the first step.You cannot pass a 50-page PDF or a dense 3-page resume into an embedding model all at once. \n\nEmbedding models have strict token limits (often 512 or 8192 tokens). Furthermore, embedding a massive document dilutes its meaning. If an entire resume is one vector, a specific skill like 'Next.js' gets lost in the noise of the candidate's hobbies and education history.\n\n**Chunking** is the process of splitting a large document into smaller, semantically meaningful pieces (e.g., 500 characters per chunk, or splitting by paragraphs). Each chunk gets its own vector embedding.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #fdf2f8; border-left: 4px solid #db2777; padding: 15px; font-size: 13px; color: #831843;\"><b>Pro Tip - Chunk Overlap:</b> When slicing text into chunks, always configure a 10-20% 'overlap' between chunks. This ensures that a sentence split exactly down the middle doesn't lose its contextual meaning.</div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
