    {
      "id": "M008-rag-01-intro",
      "title": "Introduction to Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LLM Limitations and Hallucinations",
            "explanation": "Let's start with a simple question.Suppose you ask ChatGPT:'What was our company's revenue last month?'\nCan the model answer that question?\nThe answer is usually no.\nWhy?Because the model has never seen your company's private data.\nNow imagine you ask:'Who is the current CEO of Google?'\nIf the CEO changed recently, the model may not know that information either.\nThis happens because Large Language Models learn from data that was available during training.After training is completed, the model does not automatically learn new information.\nThis limitation is known as the Knowledge Cutoff.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/in1.png]\nAs a result, the model may:\n\n• Give outdated information\n• Say it does not know\n• Generate a confident but incorrect answer\nThat incorrect answer is called a Hallucination.\nBefore we solve this problem, we first need to understand the different types of knowledge that AI systems work with."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Static vs. Dynamic Knowledge",
            "explanation": "Not all information changes at the same speed.Some information stays the same for years.Other information changes every day.\nLet's look at both categories.\n\n1. **Static Knowledge:** Things that rarely change. (e.g., *How does a combustion engine work? What is the capital of France? How do you write a Python for-loop?*). LLMs are perfect at this. They memorize it during training.\n\n2. **Dynamic Knowledge:** Things that change constantly. (e.g., *Current interest rates, user account balances, yesterday's meeting notes, inventory levels*). LLMs are terrible at this. You cannot train an LLM fast enough to keep up with daily dynamic changes.",
            "tip": "Enterprise applications almost exclusively run on Dynamic Knowledge. Therefore, an out-of-the-box LLM is practically useless for a business without an external memory system."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Knowledge Limitations Review",
            "introduction": "Great! We now understand why LLMs sometimes provide incorrect answers and why accessing fresh information is important.",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
            "template": [
              "Outdated information can lead to ____.",
              "Information that rarely changes is called ____ knowledge.",
              "Company reports and meeting notes are examples of ____ knowledge."
            ],
            "options": [
              "training",
              "hallucinations",
              "static",
              "dynamic",
              "vectors",
              "metadata",
              "embeddings"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "blank_1": "hallucinations",
              "blank_2": "static",
              "blank_3": "dynamic"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why RAG Instead of Fine-Tuning?",
            "explanation": "Now let's look at a common question.If the model lacks information, why don't we simply train it again?\nAt first, this sounds like a good idea.But imagine a company that creates:\n• New reports every day\n• New policies every week\n• New documents every month\nWould we retrain the model every time something changes?\nThat would be extremely expensive and time-consuming.\nSuppose you hire a new employee. If you want them to learn a new writing style, company tone, or response format, you train them once—this is **Fine-Tuning**. But if you want them to answer questions using the latest company documents, policies, and reports, you give them access to a knowledge base every time they work—this is **RAG**.\n\n Fine-Tuning changes how the model behaves, while RAG changes what the model knows. Because company data changes frequently, most modern AI applications use RAG instead of repeatedly fine-tuning the model.",
            "html_code": "<div style='font-family:Arial,sans-serif;padding:16px;background:#ffffff;border:2px solid #d1d5db;border-radius:12px;'><h3 style='margin-top:0;color:#111827;text-align:center;'>RAG vs Fine-Tuning</h3><div style='display:flex;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;'><div style='flex:1;min-width:220px;background:#dbeafe;padding:14px;border-radius:10px;'><div style='font-size:18px;font-weight:bold;color:#111827;'>🎓 Fine-Tuning</div><div style='margin-top:8px;color:#111827;'>✓ Teach Tone</div><div style='color:#111827;'>✓ Teach Style</div><div style='color:#111827;'>✓ Teach Format</div><div style='color:#dc2626;'>✗ Latest Knowledge</div></div><div style='flex:1;min-width:220px;background:#dcfce7;padding:14px;border-radius:10px;'><div style='font-size:18px;font-weight:bold;color:#111827;'>📚 RAG</div><div style='margin-top:8px;color:#111827;'>✓ Latest Documents</div><div style='color:#111827;'>✓ Company Data</div><div style='color:#111827;'>✓ Real-Time Updates</div><div style='color:#111827;'>✓ Knowledge Retrieval</div></div></div><div style='margin-top:15px;padding:12px;background:#f3f4f6;border-radius:8px;text-align:center;color:#111827;font-weight:bold;'>Fine-Tuning = Teach Skills 🎓 | RAG = Provide Knowledge 📚</div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "The Fine-Tuning Trap",
          "description": "Your boss learns about the Knowledge Cutoff problem. They tell you: 'Just fine-tune the LLM! Every time we get a new company document, we will fine-tune the model so it learns it.' Why is your boss wrong?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "Fine-tuning doesn't work on proprietary company data.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. You can fine-tune on any data, but it is the wrong tool for the job.",
              "score": 0
            },
            {
              "text": "Fine-tuning is for teaching a model a new BEHAVIOR (like speaking like a pirate or outputting strict JSON). It is terrible for memorizing exact FACTS. Plus, retraining a model every single day is financially impossible.",
              "outcome": "Correct! Fine-tuning changes the 'style' of an LLM, not its factual memory. If you fine-tune a model on a new document, it might still hallucinate the details. We need a way to look up facts, not bake them into the brain.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "LLMs cannot be fine-tuned once they are deployed.",
              "outcome": "Incorrect. Models can be continuously fine-tuned, but it is slow and expensive.",
              "score": 10
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Introduction to RAG",
            "explanation": "If we can't bake dynamic facts into the LLM's brain, what do we do? We give the LLM an **open-book test**.\n\nThis is **Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)**.\nInstead of expecting the LLM to memorize everything, we let it 'Google' the answer in our private database before it speaks.\n\n**Real-World Examples:**\n• **Customer Support Bots:** The bot searches the company's internal wiki for the exact refund policy, reads it, and generates a polite response to the user.\n• **Legal AI:** The AI retrieves similar historical court cases from a database and uses them to draft a new legal argument.\n• **ChatGPT Web Search:** When ChatGPT searches the web to answer your question, it is performing RAG on the internet!",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #fdf4ff; border: 1px solid #fbcfe8; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; text-align: center; font-family: sans-serif;\"><h3 style=\"color: #db2777; margin-top: 0;\">The RAG Equation</h3><div style=\"font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; color: #831843; margin: 15px 0;\">Your Private Data + LLM Reasoning = RAG</div><div style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #be185d;\">We use the database for <b>Memory</b>, and the LLM purely for <b>Reasoning and Formatting</b>.</div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The End-to-End Workflow Explained",
            "explanation": "Let's walk through the complete RAG pipeline step by step and see how a simple user question is transformed into an accurate, context-aware response.\n**Step 1: User Query** The user asks a question such as 'What is Q3 Revenue?', which starts the RAG pipeline.\n**Step 2: Embedding Model** The query is converted into a vector embedding so the system can understand its semantic meaning.\n**Step 3: Vector Database** The embedding is searched against stored vectors to find the most relevant information.\n**Step 4: Retrieve Top-K Documents** The Vector Database returns the most similar documents related to the user's question.\n**Step 5: Context Injection** The retrieved documents are combined with the user's query to create an augmented prompt.\n**Step 6: LLM Processing** The LLM reads both the query and the retrieved context to understand the question accurately.\n**Step 7: Final Answer** The LLM generates a response using the provided context and returns the answer to the user.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/in2.png]\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building a Simple End-to-End RAG Chatbot",
            "explanation": "This example shows how to build a complete RAG pipeline using ChromaDB, Sentence Transformers, and OpenAI.\n\nThe workflow follows: Documents → Embeddings → Vector Database → Retrieval → Context Injection → LLM → Answer.",
            "example": "```python\n# 1. Install Dependencies\npip install openai chromadb sentence-transformers\n\n# 2. Import Libraries\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nimport chromadb\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\n# 3. Initialize Models\nclient = OpenAI(api_key='YOUR_API_KEY')\nembedding_model = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')\n\n# 4. Create Vector Database\ndb = chromadb.Client()\ncollection = db.create_collection('company_docs')\n\n# 5. Load Documents\ndocuments = [\n    'Office timing is 9 AM to 6 PM.',\n    'Guest WiFi password is coffee123.',\n    'Products can be returned within 45 days.'\n]\n\n# 6. Generate Document Embeddings\nembeddings = embedding_model.encode(documents).tolist()\n\n# 7. Store Embeddings in ChromaDB\ncollection.add(\n    ids=['1', '2', '3'],\n    documents=documents,\n    embeddings=embeddings\n)\n\n# 8. User Query\nquestion = 'What is the WiFi password?'\n\n# 9. Generate Query Embedding\nquery_embedding = embedding_model.encode(question).tolist()\n\n# 10. Retrieve Similar Document\nresults = collection.query(\n    query_embeddings=[query_embedding],\n    n_results=1\n)\n\ncontext = results['documents'][0][0]\n\n# 11. Build the RAG Prompt\nprompt = f'''\nAnswer only using the provided context.\n\nContext:\n{context}\n\nQuestion:\n{question}\n'''\n\n# 12. Generate Final Answer\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[\n        {\n            'role': 'user',\n            'content': prompt\n        }\n    ]\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)\n# Guest WiFi password is coffee123.\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Build an Employee Handbook RAG Assistant",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a simple Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) assistant. Generate embeddings for the employee handbook, retrieve the most relevant policy, and use GPT-4o-mini to answer the employee's question using only the retrieved context.",
            "starterCode": "from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# TODO: Load the embedding model\nembedding_model = \n\nknowledge_base = [\n    \"Employees may work from home up to three days per week.\",\n    \"Expense reimbursement requests must be submitted within 30 days.\",\n    \"The annual performance review takes place every December.\"\n]\n\nquestion = \"When should employees submit reimbursement requests?\"\n\n# TODO:\n# 1. Generate embeddings for all handbook documents.\n# 2. Generate an embedding for the user's question.\n# 3. Retrieve the most relevant policy.\n# 4. Build a RAG prompt.\n# 5. Send the prompt to GPT-4o-mini.\n",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "Expense reimbursement requests must be submitted within 30 days.",
            "hint": "Use the 'all-MiniLM-L6-v2' embedding model, cosine similarity for retrieval, and pass the retrieved policy as context to GPT-4o-mini.",
            "solution": "from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n# Load embedding model\nembedding_model = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')\n\n# Knowledge Base\nknowledge_base = [\n    \"Employees may work from home up to three days per week.\",\n    \"Expense reimbursement requests must be submitted within 30 days.\",\n    \"The annual performance review takes place every December.\"\n]\n\n# User Question\nquestion = \"When should employees submit reimbursement requests?\"\n\n# Step 1: Generate embeddings for the documents\ndocument_embeddings = embedding_model.encode(knowledge_base)\n\n# Step 2: Generate embedding for the user's question\nquery_embedding = embedding_model.encode([question])\n\n# Step 3: Retrieve the most relevant document\nsimilarities = cosine_similarity(query_embedding, document_embeddings)[0]\nbest_index = similarities.argmax()\nretrieved_context = knowledge_base[best_index]\n\n# Step 4: Build the RAG prompt\nprompt = \"\"\"\nAnswer the question using ONLY the provided context.\n\nContext:\n{retrieved_context}\n\nQuestion:\n{question}\n\"\"\"\n\n# Step 5: Generate the answer\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model=\"gpt-4o-mini\",\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}]\n)\n\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)"
          }
        },
