        {
          "type": "flashcard",
          "cards": [
            {
              "front": "What is RAG?",
              "back": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation is a technique where an LLM retrieves relevant external information before generating an answer."
            },
            {
              "front": "Why is RAG preferred over repeatedly fine-tuning a model with new company documents?",
              "back": "RAG provides real-time access to changing information, while fine-tuning is expensive and mainly changes model behavior rather than factual memory."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M008-rag-02-pipeline",
      "title": "The RAG Data Pipeline",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#14b8a6",
        "accent": "#0f766e"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Loading Documents: Gathering the Knowledge",
            "explanation": "Before an LLM can answer questions about your private data, you have to extract that data. An AI cannot natively 'read' a PDF file or a SQL database. It only understands raw text.\n\nThe first step of any RAG pipeline is the **Document Loader**. Frameworks like LangChain and LlamaIndex provide hundreds of pre-built loaders to extract raw text from:\n• **Unstructured Data:** PDFs, Word Documents (.docx), PowerPoints.\n• **Semi-Structured Data:** Websites (HTML scraping), Notion pages, Confluence.\n• **Structured Data:** SQL Databases, CSV files, JSON APIs.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #0f172a; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid #14b8a6; color: white; font-family: monospace;\"><h4 style=\"color: #5eead4; margin-top:0;\">// Data Ingestion Connectors</h4><div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 10px; font-size: 12px;\"><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #ef4444;\">📄 PyPDFLoader(\"report.pdf\")</div><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #3b82f6;\">🌐 WebBaseLoader(\"https://wiki\")</div><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #10b981;\">📊 CSVLoader(\"sales_data.csv\")</div><div style=\"background: #1e293b; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #f59e0b;\">🗄️ SQLDatabaseLoader(db_conn)</div></div><div style=\"color: #94a3b8; font-size: 11px; margin-top: 10px;\">Goal: Convert every format into one universal format -> <span style=\"color: #2dd4bf; font-weight: bold;\">Plain Text Strings</span></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Text Cleaning: Garbage In, Garbage Out",
            "explanation": "Before creating embeddings, the extracted text must be cleaned and normalized. Poor-quality text leads to poor retrieval results.\n\n **Text Cleaning Workflow:**\n • Extract Raw Text → Read content from PDFs, websites, Word files, or databases.\n • Remove HTML Tags → Remove tags such as div, nav, footer.\n • Remove Headers & Footers → Delete page numbers, copyright notices, and repeated document sections.\n • Fix Line Breaks → Merge sentences split by unnecessary \\n characters.\n • Remove Extra Spaces → Convert multiple spaces, tabs, and blank lines into clean text.\n • Remove Special Characters → Clean unwanted symbols and encoding issues.\n • Normalize Text → Standardize formatting, dates, punctuation, and casing if needed.\n • Remove Irrelevant Content → Remove menus, advertisements, navigation links, and boilerplate text.\n • Validate Content Quality → Ensure the remaining text contains useful information.\n • Send Clean Text for Chunking & Embedding → The cleaned text is now ready for the RAG pipeline.",
            "tip": "In RAG, 80% of your retrieval accuracy depends on how clean your text is before you embed it."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Chunking Strategies: Size & Overlap",
            "explanation": "We cannot send a 100-page book to the embedding model. We must split it into **Chunks**.\n\n• **Chunk Size:** How big is each piece? Usually 500 to 1,000 tokens (roughly 1-2 paragraphs). If the chunk is too big, the specific facts get diluted. If it's too small, the LLM won't have enough context to form a good answer.\n• **Chunk Overlap:** When you slice a document, you might accidentally cut a sentence in half! To prevent losing context, we configure an 'overlap' (e.g., 50 tokens). This means the end of Chunk 1 is duplicated at the beginning of Chunk 2.",
            "html_code": "<div style='font-family:Arial,sans-serif;padding:15px;background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d1d5db;border-radius:10px;'><h4 style='margin-top:0;color:#111827;'>Chunk Overlap</h4><div style='display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;'><div style='flex:1;background:#fef2f2;padding:10px;border-radius:8px;border-left:4px solid #ef4444;color:#111827;'><b>❌ No Overlap</b><br>Chunk 1: The new iPhone. It<br>Chunk 2: features a titanium body<br><span style='color:#b91c1c;'>Context Lost</span></div><div style='flex:1;background:#f0fdf4;padding:10px;border-radius:8px;border-left:4px solid #22c55e;color:#111827;'><b>✅ With Overlap</b><br>Chunk 1: The new iPhone. It<br>Chunk 2: <b>new iPhone. It</b> features...<br><span style='color:#166534;'>Context Preserved</span></div></div><div style='margin-top:10px;background:#eff6ff;padding:8px;border-radius:6px;text-align:center;color:#1e3a8a;font-size:13px;'><b>Overlap = Shared text between chunks.</b></div></div>",
            "example": "Practical Example:\nfrom langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\ntext = '''\nChunking is the process of breaking a large piece of text\ninto smaller, manageable pieces to make it easier to process and analyze.\n'''\n\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=40,\n    chunk_overlap=10\n)\n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\n\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(chunks, 1):\n    print(f'Chunk {i}:', chunk)\n\nOutput:\nChunk 1: Chunking is the process of breaking\nChunk 2: of breaking a large piece of text\nChunk 3: piece of text into smaller,\nChunk 4: into smaller, manageable pieces."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Types of Chunking Strategies",
            "explanation": "Chunking is the process of breaking large documents into smaller pieces before creating embeddings.\n`Types of Chunking statergies:`\n **Fixed-Size Chunking** splits text into equal-sized chunks,\n **Overlap Chunking** shares content between neighboring chunks to preserve context,\n **Sentence Chunking** splits text at sentence boundaries,\n **Paragraph Chunking** keeps complete paragraphs together, \n**Recursive Chunking** intelligently splits using paragraphs, sentences, and words,\n **Semantic Chunking** groups text based on meaning rather than size,\n **Document Structure Chunking** uses headings and sections as boundaries, \n**Token-Based Chunking** splits based on token count, and \n**Agentic Chunking** uses an LLM to decide optimal chunk boundaries. For most RAG applications, Recursive Chunking with Overlap is the recommended starting point.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/th1.png]\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Metadata Creation: Preparing Data for Retrieval",
            "explanation": "A chunk of text floating in a database is useless without context. When you create a chunk, you must attach **Metadata** (a JSON dictionary of facts about that chunk).\n\nIf the chunk says: *'Revenue grew by 20% this quarter.'*\nYour Metadata should say: `{\"source\": \"Q3_Financials.pdf\", \"date\": \"2023-10-01\", \"department\": \"Finance\", \"page\": 4}`\n\nMetadata is the secret weapon of enterprise RAG. It allows you to filter the database *before* running the semantic search, guaranteeing the LLM only reads the correct documents.",
            "example": "Without metadata, if a user asks 'What is our revenue?',\n the system might retrieve a report from 2018 instead of 2024!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Code: Chunking with LangChain",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Use LangChain's RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter to split a document with a chunk size of 100 characters and an overlap of 20 characters.",
            "starterCode": "from langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext = \"Artificial Intelligence is transforming the world. RAG makes LLMs smarter by giving them memory.\"\n\n# TODO: Initialize the text splitter with chunk_size=100 and chunk_overlap=20\nsplitter = \n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\nprint(f\"Created {len(chunks)} chunks.\")\nprint(f\"Chunk 1: {chunks[0]}\")",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "Created 2 chunks.\nChunk 1: Artificial Intelligence is transforming the world. RAG makes LLMs smarter by giving them memory.",
            "hint": "RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=100, chunk_overlap=20)",
            "solution": "from langchain.text_splitter import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext = \"Artificial Intelligence is transforming the world. RAG makes LLMs smarter by giving them memory.\"\n\n# Initialize the splitter\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=100, \n    chunk_overlap=20\n)\n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\nprint(f\"Created {len(chunks)} chunks.\")\nprint(f\"Chunk 1: {chunks[0]}\")"
          }
        },
