          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Vector Database Optimization: Sharding & Scaling",
            "explanation": "Even with IVF-PQ, a massive database must be distributed. \n\n• **Sharding (Horizontal Scaling):** You split your vector index into multiple pieces (shards) across different servers. When a user queries, the system searches all shards in parallel, then merges the top results.\n• **Replication:** You copy the exact same shard to multiple servers. This doesn't help store *more* data, but it handles high *throughput* (thousands of users searching at the same exact second).\n\nModern databases like Milvus, Pinecone, and Weaviate handle this Kubernetes-style sharding automatically under the hood.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #fafafa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; text-align: center;\"><b style=\"color: #475569;\">Architecture Rule of Thumb</b><div style=\"margin-top: 10px; font-size: 13px; color: #64748b;\">Out of Memory? -> <b>Add Shards</b></div><div style=\"margin-top: 5px; font-size: 13px; color: #64748b;\">High Latency / High Traffic? -> <b>Add Replicas</b></div></div>"
          }
        },
