      "id": "M008-rag-04-advanced-retrieval",
      "title": "Advanced Retrieval Techniques",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#3b82f6",
        "accent": "#1e40af"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hybrid & RRF",
            "explanation": "Welcome back to class. Imagine you are a Master Librarian trying to find a specific book. You have three ways to search:\n\n1. **Dense Retrieval:** You search by 'Meaning'. The user asks for 'space ships', and you find books about 'galactic cruisers'.\n2. **Sparse Retrieval (BM25):** You search by 'Exact Keywords'. The user asks for 'Model X-99', and you find the exact string 'X-99'. It creates sparse integer matrices based on word frequency.\n3. **Hybrid Retrieval:** The ultimate strategy. You run both searches simultaneously. \n\nBut how do you merge a Dense score of 0.85 with a BM25 score of 14.2? You use **Retrieval Fusion**, specifically **Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)**. RRF ignores the raw scores and looks at the *rankings*. If Document A is #1 in Dense and #4 in Sparse, RRF calculates a combined score using `1 / (k + rank)` to fairly crown the absolute best document.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 18px; border-radius: 10px; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; box-shadow: inset 0 2px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.02);\"><h4 style=\"color: #334155; margin: 0 0 10px 0; font-size: 16px;\">🏆 How RRF Crowns the Winner</h4><div style=\"background: #1e293b; color: #a5b4fc; padding: 10px; border-radius: 6px; font-family: monospace; font-size: 12px; margin-bottom: 15px;\">Score = 1 / (k + Rank_dense) + 1 / (k + Rank_sparse)</div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px;\"><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 8px 12px; background: white; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0;\"><strong style=\"color: #475569; font-size: 13px;\">Doc Alpha</strong><div style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px;\"><span style=\"background: #dbeafe; color: #1e40af; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 11px;\">Dense: #1</span><span style=\"background: #fee2e2; color: #991b1b; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 11px;\">Sparse: #45</span></div></div><div style=\"display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding: 8px 12px; background: #ecfdf5; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #a7f3d0; box-shadow: 0 2px 4px rgba(16,185,129,0.1);\"><strong style=\"color: #065f46; font-size: 13px;\">⭐ Doc Beta (Winner)</strong><div style=\"display: flex; gap: 6px;\"><span style=\"background: #dbeafe; color: #1e40af; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 11px;\">Dense: #3</span><span style=\"background: #fef3c7; color: #92400e; padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 11px;\">Sparse: #2</span></div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Query Transformation?",
            "explanation": "In the previous lessons, we learned that a Vector Database searches documents based on the user's question. But in real-world applications, users don't always ask clear or complete questions.\n\nFor example, a user might ask:\n\n**\"How do I fix it?\"**\n\nThe problem is that the question doesn't mention **what** needs to be fixed. Without enough information, the Vector Database may retrieve the wrong documents or no documents at all.\n\nTo solve this problem, we use **Query Transformation**.\n\nBefore searching the Vector Database, the user's question is first sent to an LLM. The LLM improves the query so that the search becomes more accurate.\n\nThere are three common Query Transformation techniques:\n\n### 1. Query Rewriting\n\nThe LLM rewrites the user's question into a clearer and more detailed query.\n\n**User Question:**\n\n\"How do I fix it?\"\n\n**Rewritten Query:**\n\n\"How to troubleshoot Acme Router Model 4?\"\n\nNow the Vector Database has enough information to retrieve the correct document.\n\n### 2. Query Expansion\n\nSometimes different documents use different words for the same concept.\n\nThe LLM automatically adds related keywords and synonyms.\n\n**User Question:**\n\n\"Find HR policy\"\n\n**Expanded Query:**\n\n\"Find HR policy, Human Resources policy, Employee Handbook, PTO Rules\"\n\nThis increases the chances of finding relevant documents.\n\n### 3. Multi-Query Search\n\nInstead of searching with only one query, the LLM creates multiple versions of the same question.\n\nExample:\n\n• What is the refund policy?\n\n• How can I return a product?\n\n• Product return guidelines.\n\n• Return and refund rules.\n\nThe Vector Database searches using all these queries and combines the results.\n\nThis helps retrieve more complete and accurate information.\n\n### Why is Query Transformation Important?\n\n• Improves search accuracy.\n\n• Handles vague or incomplete questions.\n\n• Retrieves more relevant documents.\n\n• Reduces the chances of missing useful information.\n\n**Key Idea:** Query Transformation improves the user's question **before** it reaches the Vector Database. A better query leads to better document retrieval, which ultimately produces more accurate RAG responses.",
            "tip": "Query Transformation usually adds a small amount of extra processing time, but it significantly improves retrieval accuracy in most RAG applications."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Retrieval Enhancement Review",
            "introduction": "Excellent! We have learned how Hybrid Retrieval combines multiple search methods and how Query Transformation improves user questions before retrieval begins.",
            "question": "Drag and drop the correct term into each blank.",
