        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings)",
            "explanation": "In a traditional RAG system, we convert the **user's question** into an embedding and search the Vector Database. This works well most of the time, but sometimes the user's question is short, vague, or uses different wording than the documents stored in the database.\n\nTo solve this problem, we use **HyDE (Hypothetical Document Embeddings)**.\n\nHyDE is an advanced retrieval technique where we first ask the LLM to **generate a hypothetical (fake) answer** without searching the database. Even if this answer is not completely correct, it usually has a writing style, vocabulary, and structure that is very similar to the real document.\n\nInstead of embedding the user's question, we embed this hypothetical answer and use that embedding to search the Vector Database. Since the hypothetical answer is much closer to the actual document than the original question, the Vector Database retrieves more relevant documents.\n\n### Where is HyDE Used?\n\n• Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)\n\n• Enterprise Knowledge Search\n\n• Customer Support Chatbots\n\n• Research Paper Search\n\n• Internal Company Documentation Search\n\n• Question Answering Systems\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/ar1.png]\n### How HyDE Works\n\nLet's understand the workflow using a customer support example.\n\n**Step 1:** A customer asks a question.Question:\"How do I reset my password?\"\n**Step 2:** Instead of searching immediately, the LLM generates a hypothetical answer without accessing the Vector Database.\n\nExample Hypothetical Answer:\n\n\"To reset your password, log in to your account, open the Settings page, click 'Reset Password', enter your registered email address, and follow the instructions sent to your inbox.\n**Step 3:** Convert this hypothetical answer into an embedding vector.\n**Step 4:** Search the Vector Database using the hypothetical embedding instead of the original question embedding.\n\nSince the hypothetical answer looks very similar to the real documents, the Vector Database retrieves the most relevant support articles.\n**Step 5:** Send the retrieved documents to the LLM.\n\nThe LLM reads the real documents and generates the final factual answer for the user.\n\n### Why Does HyDE Improve Retrieval?\n\nA user's question and the actual document are often written very differently.\n\nFor example:\n\n**Question:**\n\n\"How do I reset my password?\"\n\n**Actual Document:**\n\n\"Users can recover their account credentials by navigating to the Account Settings page and selecting the Reset Password option.\"\n\nAlthough both sentences mean the same thing, their wording is different.\n\nThe hypothetical answer generated by the LLM is much closer in style and vocabulary to the actual document, making semantic search more accurate.\n\n**Key Idea:** HyDE improves retrieval by generating a hypothetical answer first, embedding that answer, and then searching the Vector Database. The retrieved real documents are finally used to generate the correct response."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Keyhole Strategy (Small-to-Big)",
            "explanation": "If you break a book into massive 10-page chunks, the vector gets blurry and you can't find specific facts. But if you break the book into tiny 1-sentence chunks, the LLM loses all the context and doesn't know what chapter it's reading.\n\nThe solution is **Parent Document Retrieval** (also called **Small-to-Big Retrieval**).\n\nYou chunk the document twice. First, into massive Parent chunks (1000 tokens). Then, you split those into tiny Child chunks (100 tokens).\nYou ONLY embed and search the tiny Child chunks (peeking through the keyhole). But when you find a match, you don't send the child to the LLM. You retrieve its massive Parent chunk and send *that* to the LLM, providing perfect needle-in-a-haystack accuracy while maintaining complete narrative context.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-left: 4px solid #d946ef; padding: 16px; border-radius: 8px; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0,0,0,0.05);\"><div style=\"display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"><span style=\"background: #fdf4ff; color: #c026d3; padding: 4px 6px; border-radius: 6px; font-size: 14px;\">💡</span><b style=\"color: #86198f; font-size: 14px; margin: 0;\">Evolution: Contextual Retrieval</b></div><p style=\"color: #475569; font-size: 13px; margin: 0; line-height: 1.5;\">A newer spin on this is prepending the <b style=\"color: #334155;\">document summary</b> to every single chunk before embedding it. Now, even a tiny chunk 'remembers' the global context of the file it came from!</p></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Hop Retrieval ",
            "explanation": "Sometimes, the answer isn't in one document. \nImagine the query: *'Who is the CEO of the company that acquired WhatsApp?'*\n\nA standard RAG system searches for that exact sentence and fails. \n\n**Multi-Hop Retrieval (Recursive Retrieval)** acts like a detective following a trail:\n1. Hop 1: The agent searches 'Who acquired WhatsApp?' -> Retrieves 'Meta acquired WhatsApp.'\n2. The agent pauses, reads this, and realizes the question isn't fully answered. It writes a *new* query.\n3. Hop 2: The agent searches 'Who is the CEO of Meta?' -> Retrieves 'Mark Zuckerberg'.\n4. The agent compiles the evidence and delivers the final answer.",
            "example": "Multi-hop is essential for complex legal, financial, and medical reasoning where facts are spread across dozens of disparate files."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Laser Focus (Compression & Self-Query)",
            "explanation": "Finally, we must filter the noise.\n\n• **Self-Query Retrieval:** The user types 'Find me Apple earnings from 2023'. The LLM intercepts this, extracts the math, and automatically writes a Metadata Filter: `query='Apple earnings', metadata_filter={'year': 2023}`.\n• **Context Compression:** You searched the database and pulled 5 large documents. But only 2 sentences in those documents actually matter! Sending all 5 documents to the LLM costs money and causes hallucinations. Context Compression uses a tiny, fast LLM to 'highlight' only the relevant sentences in the retrieved text, throwing away the garbage *before* sending it to your expensive main LLM.",
            "tip": "Context Compression drastically reduces your OpenAI API bills by cutting down the number of tokens you feed into the final context window."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
