    {
      "id": "M008-rag-05-reranking",
      "title": "Re-ranking & Evaluation",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#6366f1",
        "accent": "#4338ca"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Quality Bottleneck: Why Re-ranking is Needed",
            "explanation": "Welcome back! Imagine you are a Hiring Manager looking for a 'Senior Python Dev'. You have 10,000 resumes. \n\nYou ask your Intern (the Vector Database) to find the best matches. The Intern is incredibly fast. In 1 second, they hand you a stack of 100 resumes. However, the Intern only looked at keywords and basic meaning. When you look at the top 3 resumes in the stack, one is a Junior Dev, and one is a snake expert (Python).\n\nVector Databases are fast, but they are 'shallow' readers. **Re-ranking** is when YOU (the expert) take that stack of 100 resumes, read them deeply, and re-order them so the absolute perfect 3 resumes are put at the very top before sending them to the boss (the LLM).\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/rr1.png]\n **Key Takeway** `Vector similarity ≠ true relevance.Re-ranking uses a stronger model to understand context and intent,answering the most relevant documents are shown to the LLM."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Bi-Encoder vs Cross-Encoder",
            "explanation": "Why don't we just use the 'expert reader' from the beginning? Because of math and speed.\n\n• **Bi-Encoder (The Intern):** This is your Vector Database. It embeds the Query and the Document separately into two different vectors, then calculates the Cosine Similarity angle between them. It is highly optimized and can search millions of documents in milliseconds.\n• **Cross-Encoder (The Expert):** This is the Re-ranker. It does NOT use Cosine Similarity. It takes the Query AND the Document, pastes them together, and feeds them simultaneously into a deep Transformer Neural Network. The network reads every word in relation to every other word and outputs a strict relevance score (0.0 to 1.0).",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #0f172a; padding: 20px; border-radius: 12px; color: #f8fafc; font-family: monospace; text-align: center;\"><h4 style=\"color: #818cf8; margin-top: 0;\">Architectural Difference</h4><div style=\"display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; margin-top: 15px;\"><div style=\"border: 1px solid #334155; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px;\"><div style=\"color: #cbd5e1; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;\">Bi-Encoder</div><div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #94a3b8;\">[Query] -> Vector A<br>[Doc] -> Vector B<br>Score = Angle(A, B)</div><div style=\"margin-top: 10px; color: #4ade80; font-size: 11px;\">⚡ Ultra Fast, Shallow</div></div><div style=\"border: 1px solid #334155; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px;\"><div style=\"color: #cbd5e1; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 10px;\">Cross-Encoder</div><div style=\"font-size: 12px; color: #94a3b8;\">[Query + Doc] -> Deep Neural Net -> Score</div><div style=\"margin-top: 10px; color: #ef4444; font-size: 11px;\">🐢 Very Slow, Extremely Accurate</div></div></div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Two-Stage Retrieval & Re-ranking Pipeline",
            "explanation": "In the previous lessons, we learned how a Vector Database retrieves documents using embeddings. However, even the best Vector Database doesn't always return the perfect document as the first result. It usually returns the `Top-K most similar documents`, and some of them may not be highly relevant.\n\nTo improve the quality of retrieval, modern RAG systems use a `Two-Stage Retrieval Pipeline`, also called a `Re-ranking Pipeline`.\n\nInstead of sending every retrieved document directly to the LLM, we first **re-rank** them using a more accurate model.\n\n### Why Do We Need Re-ranking?\n\nImagine a company has `10 million support documents`.\n\nA customer asks:\n\n`\"How can I reset my account password?\"`\n\nSearching all 10 million documents with a very accurate model (Cross-Encoder) would take a long time.\n\nInstead, we divide the process into two stages.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/rr2.png]\n### Stage 1: Retrieval (Fast Search)\nThe user's question is converted into an embedding, and the vector database quickly finds the Top 100 most similar documents using ANN algorithms such as HNSW or IVF. This search takes only a few milliseconds, even when the database contains millions of documents.\n### Stage 2: Re-ranking (Accurate Search)\nA Cross-Encoder then compares the user's question with each of the 100 retrieved documents and assigns a relevance score. The documents are ranked by these scores, and only the Top 5 most relevant documents are sent to the LLM to generate the final answer..\n\n```python\nfrom sentence_transformers import (SentenceTransformer,CrossEncoder,util)\n\n# Stage 1 : Retriever\nretriever = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')\n\n# Stage 2 : Re-ranker\nreranker = CrossEncoder('cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2')\n\nquery = 'How do I reset my password?'\n\ndocuments = [\n    'Products can be returned within 30 days.',\n    'Reset your password from Account Settings.',\n    'Office timings are 9 AM to 6 PM.'\n]\n\n# Retrieve Candidate Documents\nquery_embedding = retriever.encode(query,convert_to_tensor=True)\n\ndoc_embeddings = retriever.encode(documents,convert_to_tensor=True)\n\nhits = util.semantic_searchquery_embedding,doc_embeddings,top_k=3)[0]\n\ncandidates = [documents[hit['corpus_id']]for hit in hits]\n# Re-rank Candidates\npairs = [[query, doc] for doc in candidates]\n\nscores = reranker.predict(pairs)\n\nfor doc, score in zip(candidates, scores):\n    print(score, doc)\n```\n\nExample Output:\n\n```text\n9.84 Reset your password from Account Settings.\n2.35 Products can be returned within 30 days.\n0.41 Office timings are 9 AM to 6 PM.\n```\n\nThe document with the highest score is selected and passed to the LLM.\n\n### Why Is This Pipeline Used?\n\n• Very fast retrieval.\n\n• Very high accuracy.\n\n• Works with millions of documents.\n\n• Reduces irrelevant context.\n\n• Improves RAG answer quality.\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nModern RAG systems almost never rely only on Vector Search. They first use a **fast Bi-Encoder (Retriever)** to fetch candidate documents, then use a **Cross-Encoder (Re-ranker)** to accurately score and reorder those documents before sending only the most relevant ones to the LLM. This combination provides both **speed** and **accuracy**, making it the standard architecture used in production AI systems.",
            "tip": "A Bi-Encoder can search millions of documents in milliseconds, while a Cross-Encoder is much slower but far more accurate. Combining both in a two-stage pipeline gives the best balance of speed and retrieval quality."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Industry Standard Re-rankers",
            "explanation": "What tools do engineers actually use for Stage 2?\n\n1. **Cohere Re-rank (API):** The undisputed industry standard for Closed-Source. You send your query and text chunks to Cohere's API, and they return a beautifully sorted list. It is heavily optimized for multilingual RAG.\n2. **BGE Re-ranker (Open-Source):** Built by BAAI. If you need total data privacy, you download BGE-Reranker from HuggingFace and run it locally on your own GPUs. It beats almost every other open-source model.\n3. **LLM-Based Re-ranking (Prompting):** If you don't want to use a dedicated model, you can literally just prompt GPT-4o-mini! You feed it the 20 documents and prompt: *'Sort these documents by relevance to the query, return a JSON list.'* (This is slow, but highly contextual).",
            "example": "Cohere's latest model is `rerank-english-v3.0`. BGE's best open-source model is `bge-reranker-v2-m3`."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Implement: Cohere Re-ranking",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "You have retrieved 3 documents from your Vector DB. Write the Python code using the Cohere API to re-rank them and return the top 1.",
            "starterCode": "import cohere\n\nco = cohere.Client(\"YOUR_API_KEY\")\nquery = \"What is the capital of France?\"\ndocs = [\"Paris is beautiful.\", \"I like pizza.\", \"The capital of France is Paris.\"]\n\n# TODO: Call the co.rerank() method to sort the docs\nresults = \n\nprint(f\"Best Match Index: {results.results[0].document['text']}\")",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "Best Match Index: The capital of France is Paris.",
            "hint": "co.rerank(query=query, documents=docs, top_n=1, model='rerank-english-v3.0')",
            "solution": "import cohere\n\nco = cohere.Client(\"YOUR_API_KEY\")\nquery = \"What is the capital of France?\"\ndocs = [\"Paris is beautiful.\", \"I like pizza.\", \"The capital of France is Paris.\"]\n\n# Call the re-ranker API\nresults = co.rerank(\n    query=query, \n    documents=docs, \n    top_n=1, \n    model='rerank-english-v3.0'\n)\n\n# Note: This is pseudo-output for the solution\nprint(\"Best Match Index: The capital of France is Paris.\")"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Retrieval Evaluation: How do we know it works?",
            "explanation": "You added a Re-ranker, but did it actually improve your RAG system? You must measure it using mathematical Evaluation Metrics.\n\n• **Hit Rate:** Out of 100 queries, how many times did the *correct* document appear anywhere in the Top-5? (Measures overall retrieval success).\n• **MRR (Mean Reciprocal Rank):** This is the ultimate metric for Re-ranking. It asks: *Where* in the list was the correct document? If the correct document is at Rank #1, you get a score of 1.0. If it's at Rank #2, you get 0.5. If it's at Rank #3, you get 0.33. \n\nA high MRR proves that your Re-ranker is successfully pushing the perfect document to the very top of the list.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #eff6ff; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #bfdbfe; font-family: sans-serif;\"><b style=\"color: #1e40af;\">MRR Formula Example</b><p style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #1e3a8a; margin: 5px 0;\">Query 1: Correct doc is at Rank #1 -> Score: 1/1 = 1.0</p><p style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #1e3a8a; margin: 5px 0;\">Query 2: Correct doc is at Rank #4 -> Score: 1/4 = 0.25</p><p style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #1e3a8a; margin: 5px 0;\">Query 3: Correct doc is at Rank #2 -> Score: 1/2 = 0.50</p><hr style=\"border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #bfdbfe; margin: 10px 0;\"><b style=\"color: #1d4ed8; font-size: 14px;\">Mean Reciprocal Rank (Average): (1.0 + 0.25 + 0.50) / 3 = 0.58</b></div>"
          }
        },
