    {
      "id": "M008-rag-06-advanced-architectures",
      "title": "Advanced RAG Architectures",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#8b5cf6",
        "accent": "#6d28d9"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Limits of Naive RAG",
            "explanation": "Welcome back to class! So far, we've built 'Naive RAG'. The flow is simple: Retrieve documents -> Give to LLM -> Generate answer.\n\nBut what happens when things go wrong? \n1. **The Blind Retriever:** What if the Vector DB retrieves terrible, irrelevant documents? The LLM will use them anyway and give a bad answer.\n2. **The Confident Liar:** What if the LLM ignores the retrieved documents and hallucinates an answer anyway?\n3. **The Narrow Mind:** What if the user asks a global question like 'What is the main theme of this 500-page book?' Vector search fails here because the 'theme' isn't in one specific chunk; it's scattered everywhere.\n\nTo solve these, the industry invented Advanced RAG Architectures: **Self-RAG**, **CRAG**, and **GraphRAG**.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #fff1f2; border-left: 4px solid #e11d48; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; font-family: sans-serif;\"><h4 style=\"color: #be123c; margin-top: 0;\">The Naive RAG Failure State</h4><div style=\"font-size: 13px; color: #881337;\"><b>User:</b> \"How does AcmeCorp handle maternity leave?\"<br><br><b>Vector DB:</b> *Retrieves a document about paternity leave by mistake.*<br><br><b>LLM:</b> *Reads the wrong document, gets confused, and hallucinates a fake maternity policy.*<br><br><b>Result:</b> An HR disaster. We need systems that verify their own work!</div></div>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Self-RAG: The AI That Checks Its Own Work",
            "explanation": "Imagine a student writing an exam answer. A normal RAG system reads documents once and immediately writes an answer. But a good student reviews the answer before submitting it.\n**Self-RAG** works the same way. After generating an answer, a special Critic checks whether the retrieved documents are relevant, whether the answer is supported by the documents, and whether the answer is useful for the user. If the answer is poor or unsupported, the system searches again, retrieves better documents, and generates a new answer.\n **Main Components:** • Retriever → Finds relevant documents • LLM Generator → Creates the answer • Critic Model → Evaluates the answer • Feedback Loop → Triggers another retrieval if needed.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/adv1.png]\n**How It Works:**\n • User asks a question.\n • Retriever searches the Vector Database and fetches relevant documents.\n • LLM reads the retrieved documents and generates an initial answer.\n • Critic checks whether the retrieved documents are relevant to the question.\n • Critic verifies whether the answer is supported by the retrieved documents.\n • If the answer fails any check, the system retrieves better documents and generates a new answer.\n • Once all checks pass, the final verified answer is returned to the user.\n\n Self-RAG improves accuracy and reduces hallucinations because the answer is validated before being shown to the user."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "IDE: Implementing Self-RAG",
            "explanation": "To build Self-RAG, we use orchestration frameworks like **LangGraph**, which allow us to create loops and conditional edges. Here is how a lead engineer codes the Self-Reflection routing logic.",
            "html_code": "<div style=\"background: #1e1e1e; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid #333; overflow: hidden; font-family: 'Fira Code', Consolas, monospace; color: #d4d4d4; font-size: 13px; box-shadow: 0 10px 25px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);\"><div style=\"background: #2d2d2d; padding: 8px 15px; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; border-bottom: 1px solid #1e1e1e;\"><div style=\"width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 50%; background: #ff5f56;\"></div><div style=\"width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 50%; background: #ffbd2e;\"></div><div style=\"width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 50%; background: #27c93f;\"></div><span style=\"margin-left: 10px; color: #858585; font-size: 12px;\">self_rag_router.py</span></div><div style=\"padding: 15px; line-height: 1.6;\"><span style=\"color: #569cd6;\">def</span> <span style=\"color: #dcdcaa;\">hallucination_checker</span>(state):<br>&nbsp;<span style=\"color: #6a9955;\"># Critic LLM compares Draft vs Retrieved Documents</span><br>&nbsp;score = <span style=\"color: #dcdcaa;\">critic_llm</span>.invoke(draft, documents)<br><br>&nbsp;<span style=\"color: #c586c0;\">if</span> score == <span style=\"color: #ce9178;\">\"supported\"</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style=\"color: #c586c0;\">return</span> <span style=\"color: #ce9178;\">\"generate_final_answer\"</span><br>&nbsp;<span style=\"color: #c586c0;\">else</span>:<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style=\"color: #6a9955;\"># Draft is hallucinated! Loop back and try again.</span><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style=\"color: #c586c0;\">return</span> <span style=\"color: #ce9178;\">\"rewrite_query_and_retry\"</span><br><br><span style=\"color: #6a9955;\"># LangGraph Conditional Edge</span><br>workflow.<span style=\"color: #dcdcaa;\">add_conditional_edges</span>(<br>&nbsp;<span style=\"color: #ce9178;\">\"generate_draft\"</span>,<br>&nbsp;hallucination_checker<br>)</div></div>",
            "tip": "Don't worry about LangGraph yet—we'll build these workflows step by step in the LangGraph module."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Use Case: Self-RAG Hallucination Check",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "You are building a medical AI. The LLM drafted an answer. Write a grading function that checks if the word 'FDA_APPROVED' is in the source document. If the LLM drafted an answer containing 'FDA' but the source doesn't have it, return 'FAIL'. Otherwise, return 'PASS'.",
            "starterCode": "def grade_hallucination(draft_answer, source_document):\n    # TODO: Implement the safety check\n    pass\n\ndoc = \"The new drug X is in trial phase.\"\nans = \"Drug X is FDA_APPROVED and safe.\"\nprint(grade_hallucination(ans, doc))",
            "language": "python",
            "expected_output": "FAIL",
            "hint": "Check if 'FDA' is in draft_answer, and if 'FDA_APPROVED' is NOT in source_document.",
            "solution": "def grade_hallucination(draft_answer, source_document):\n    if \"FDA\" in draft_answer and \"FDA_APPROVED\" not in source_document:\n        return \"FAIL\"\n    return \"PASS\"\n\ndoc = \"The new drug X is in trial phase.\"\nans = \"Drug X is FDA_APPROVED and safe.\"\nprint(grade_hallucination(ans, doc))"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Corrective RAG (CRAG): Fixing Retrieval Mistakes",
            "explanation": "Traditional RAG retrieves documents and immediately sends them to the LLM, while **Self-RAG** checks the generated answer after it is created. **Corrective RAG (CRAG)** goes one step further by evaluating the retrieved documents **before** the answer is generated. In simple terms, **Self-RAG evaluates the answer, whereas CRAG evaluates the retrieval itself.**\n\nImagine your company's product database only contains information up to **2023**. A customer asks, **'What new AI features were added to Product X in 2024?'** Since the Vector Database has no 2024 data, it retrieves the closest matching document from 2023, which is outdated and may lead to an incorrect answer.\n\nTo solve this problem, CRAG introduces a **Knowledge Evaluator** immediately after retrieval. Instead of trusting the retrieved documents, it first checks whether they are reliable enough to answer the user's question.\n\n**Main Components:** • Retriever → Retrieves relevant documents • Knowledge Evaluator → Grades the retrieved documents • Web Search / External Knowledge Source → Fetches newer or missing information when required • LLM Generator → Generates the final answer.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/adv2.png]\n\n**How It Works:**\n• User asks a question.\n• Retriever searches the Vector Database and retrieves relevant documents.\n• Knowledge Evaluator checks the quality of the retrieved documents.\n• **Correct:** The documents are reliable, so they are sent directly to the LLM.\n• **Ambiguous:** The documents are partially relevant, so the system sends them to the LLM **and** performs a Web Search (using tools like Tavily or Google Search API) to gather additional information.\n• **Incorrect:** The retrieved documents are discarded completely, and the system relies entirely on a live Web Search to obtain accurate information.\n• The LLM combines the verified context and generates the final answer.\n\nBy correcting poor retrieval before generation, **CRAG reduces hallucinations, handles outdated knowledge, and produces more accurate answers than traditional RAG systems.**"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "IDE: Implementing CRAG",
            "explanation": "In CRAG, we use a simple routing mechanism. If the relevance score of the retrieved documents falls below a threshold, we pivot to a web search tool.",
            "example": "# CRAG Pipeline Core Functions (Python Pseudocode)\n\ndef retrieve(query, retriever, top_k=10):\n    \"\"\"Standard retrieval from vector store.\"\"\"\n    documents = retriever.invoke(query, top_k=top_k)\n    return documents\n\n\ndef evaluate_documents(query, documents, evaluator):\n    \"\"\"Grade each document for relevance to the query.\"\"\"\n    scores = []\n    for doc in documents:\n        score = evaluator.predict(query + ' [SEP] ' + doc.content)\n        scores.append({\n            'doc': doc,\n            'score': score,\n            'relevant': score >= RELEVANCE_THRESHOLD\n        })\n    return scores\n\n\ndef determine_action(scored_docs):\n    \"\"\"Classify retrieval quality as Correct, Incorrect, or Ambiguous.\"\"\"\n    max_score = max(s['score'] for s in scored_docs)\n\n    if max_score >= UPPER_THRESHOLD:\n        return 'CORRECT'\n    elif max_score <= LOWER_THRESHOLD:\n        return 'INCORRECT'\n    else:\n        return 'AMBIGUOUS'\n\n\ndef refine_knowledge(documents, query):\n    \"\"\"Extract only relevant knowledge.\"\"\"\n    strips = decompose_to_strips(documents)\n    relevant_strips = [s for s in strips if is_relevant(s, query)]\n    return recompose(relevant_strips)\n\n\ndef web_search_fallback(query, search_api):\n    \"\"\"Search the web if retrieval quality is poor.\"\"\"\n    keywords = rewrite_to_keywords(query)\n    results = search_api.search(keywords)\n    web_docs = extract_content(results)\n    return refine_knowledge(web_docs, query)\n\n\ndef crag_pipeline(query):\n    \"\"\"Main CRAG orchestration.\"\"\"\n    documents = retrieve(query, retriever)\n    scored_docs = evaluate_documents(query, documents, evaluator)\n    action = determine_action(scored_docs)\n\n    if action == 'CORRECT':\n        relevant_docs = [s['doc'] for s in scored_docs if s['relevant']]\n        knowledge = refine_knowledge(relevant_docs, query)\n\n    elif action == 'INCORRECT':\n        knowledge = web_search_fallback(query, search_api)\n\n    else:  # AMBIGUOUS\n        relevant_docs = [s['doc'] for s in scored_docs if s['relevant']]\n        internal = refine_knowledge(relevant_docs, query)\n        external = web_search_fallback(query, search_api)\n        knowledge = combine(internal, external)\n\n    response = llm.generate(query=query, context=knowledge)\n    return response"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Use Case: CRAG Routing Logic",
