          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "GraphRAG: Connecting Knowledge with Graphs",
            "explanation": "Traditional RAG retrieves individual document chunks, making it excellent for finding specific facts. However, it struggles with questions that require understanding relationships across many documents.\n\n**GraphRAG** is an advanced RAG technique that represents knowledge as a graph of entities and relationships, allowing the LLM to reason over connected information instead of isolated text chunks.\n\nImagine asking, **'What are all the business relationships between Microsoft and OpenAI over the last 10 years?'** A Vector Database may retrieve a few relevant paragraphs, but the complete answer is spread across hundreds of documents. Since no single chunk contains the full story, traditional RAG cannot easily connect everything together.\n\nGraphRAG solves this problem by first using an LLM to extract **Entities** (People, Companies, Products, Locations, etc.) and **Relationships** (CEO_OF, FOUNDED, ACQUIRED, PARTNERS_WITH, INVESTED_IN, COMPETES_WITH, etc.). These are stored inside a **Knowledge Graph**, where every entity is connected to related entities.\n\nWhen a user asks a complex question, GraphRAG doesn't simply search text. Instead, it traverses the graph, follows the relationships between entities, gathers all connected information, and provides the LLM with rich global context before generating the final answer.\n\n**Main Components:** • Document Loader → Loads documents • Entity Extractor (LLM) → Extracts entities and relationships • Knowledge Graph → Stores connected entities • Graph Retriever → Traverses the graph • LLM Generator → Produces the final answer.\n\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/adv3.png]\n\n**How It Works:**\n• Documents are loaded into the system.\n• An LLM extracts entities and their relationships.\n• The entities and relationships are stored in a Knowledge Graph.\n• The user asks a complex question.\n• GraphRAG traverses the graph to discover connected entities and relevant paths.\n• The retrieved graph context is sent to the LLM.\n• The LLM generates a final answer using the connected knowledge.\n\n**Neo4j** is the most popular graph database used with GraphRAG. It stores data as **nodes (entities)** and **edges (relationships)**, making it efficient to traverse and analyze complex connections between pieces of information."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
