      "id": "M008-vectorless-rag",
      "title": "Vectorless RAG",
      "theme": {
        "primary": "#d946ef",
        "accent": "#c026d3"
      },
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Traditional Vector RAG Has Limitations",
            "explanation": "Before learning **Vectorless RAG**, let's quickly understand why researchers started looking for an alternative to traditional **Vector RAG**.\n\nIn a typical Vector RAG system, documents are first divided into small **chunks**. These chunks are converted into **embeddings** using an embedding model and stored inside a **Vector Database** such as FAISS, Pinecone, ChromaDB, or Milvus. When a user asks a question, the same embedding model converts the query into a vector and performs a similarity search to retrieve the most relevant chunks.\n\nAlthough this approach works well, it introduces several challenges.\n\n**Common Limitations:**\n\n• **Chunking Breaks Context** → Important information may be split across multiple chunks.\n\n• **Embedding Cost** → Every document must be converted into embeddings, which increases processing time and API cost.\n\n• **Vector Database Dependency** → You need an additional database just to store embeddings.\n\n• **Similarity Isn't Understanding** → Embeddings retrieve text based on similarity, not true reasoning.\n\n• **Re-indexing Cost** → Whenever documents change, embeddings often need to be regenerated.\n\nA simplified Vector RAG pipeline looks like this:\n\n```python\n# Traditional Vector RAG Pipeline\n\nDocuments\n    ↓\nChunking\n    ↓\nEmbedding Model\n    ↓\nVector Database\n    ↓\nSimilarity Search\n    ↓\nRelevant Chunks\n    ↓\nLLM\n    ↓\nFinal Answer\n```\n\nAs document collections become larger, maintaining embeddings and vector databases becomes more expensive. This motivated researchers to explore **Vectorless RAG**, which removes the dependency on embeddings while still retrieving relevant information."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Vectorless RAG?",
            "explanation": "**Vectorless RAG** is a Retrieval-Augmented Generation technique that retrieves information **without creating embeddings or using a Vector Database**.\n\nInstead of searching documents using vector similarity, Vectorless RAG relies on the **structure of the document** and the **reasoning ability of Large Language Models (LLMs)** to locate the required information.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/vec1.png]\nRather than asking,\n\n`'Which chunk is mathematically similar to my question?'`\nit asks,\n`'Which section or page is most likely to contain the answer?`\nThis makes retrieval more natural because the LLM navigates the document similarly to how a human would.\n\n### How Vectorless RAG Works\n\n• Documents remain in their original structure.\n\n• A document index or hierarchy is created instead of embeddings.\n\n• The LLM examines the document structure.\n\n• It decides which section or page should be opened.\n\n• Only the required content is retrieved.\n\n• The retrieved content is passed to the LLM for answer generation.\n\nThe overall workflow looks like this:\n\n**Why is it called 'Vectorless'?**\n\nBecause there are **no embeddings**, **no vector similarity search**, and **no Vector Database** involved. The LLM uses reasoning together with the document's structure to locate the correct information.\n\nThis approach is especially useful for **large PDFs, books, manuals, research papers, legal documents, and technical documentation**, where preserving the original document structure is often more valuable than splitting everything into small chunks."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "How Vectorless RAG Works",
            "explanation": "Now that we know what **Vectorless RAG** is, let's understand how it answers a user's question.\n\nUnlike traditional RAG, Vectorless RAG does **not** search through thousands of embeddings. Instead, it lets the **LLM reason** about the document structure and decide where the answer is most likely located.\n\nImagine a company has uploaded a Product User Manual containing hundreds of pages.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/vec2.png]\n**Step-by-Step Workflow**\n**Step 1: Load the Product Manual**\nThe system first loads the complete product manual.\nExample Sections:Product Overview,Installation Guide,Warranty Policy,Shipping Information,Return & Refund Policy,Troubleshooting\n**Step 2: Create the Document Structure**\nInstead of creating embeddings, Vectorless RAG creates a simple document index using the page titles and section headings.This is similar to the **Table of Contents** of a book.\n**Step 3: Customer Asks a Question**\nA customer asks: `Can I return my laptop after 30 days if it has a manufacturing defect?`\n**Step 4: LLM Reasons About the Sections**\nThe LLM looks at the available sections and thinks:• Product Overview,Installation Guide,Shipping Information,**Return & Refund Policy**\nThe LLM decides that the answer is most likely inside the **Return & Refund Policy** section.\n**Step 5: Retrieve Only That Section**\nInstead of searching the entire manual, the system retrieves only the **Return & Refund Policy** page.\n\nExample Retrieved Content:\n\"Products with manufacturing defects can be returned within 60 days for a full refund or replacement.\"\n**Step 6: Generate the Final Answer**\nThe retrieved content is sent to the LLM.The LLM generates the final answer:`\"Yes. Since the laptop has a manufacturing defect, it can be returned within 60 days for a full refund or replacement.\"`\nA simplified implementation looks like this:\nNotice that Vectorless RAG never creates embeddings and never performs vector similarity search. It simply uses the document structure to find the correct section, retrieves only that content, and lets the LLM generate the final answer."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Vector RAG vs Vectorless RAG",
            "explanation": "Both approaches solve the same problem—providing external knowledge to an LLM—but they retrieve information differently.\n\nThe comparison below highlights the major differences.\n\n```html\n<table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:Segoe UI;font-size:14px;\">\n<tr style=\"background:#2563eb;color:white;\">\n<th style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Feature</th>\n<th style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Vector RAG</th>\n<th style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Vectorless RAG</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\"><b>Embeddings</b></td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Required</td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Not Required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\"><b>Vector Database</b></td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Required</td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Not Required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\"><b>Retrieval Method</b></td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Similarity Search</td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">LLM Reasoning + Document Structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\"><b>Context</b></td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Small Chunks</td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Entire Sections or Pages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\"><b>Infrastructure</b></td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Embedding Model + Vector DB</td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Only LLM + Document Index</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\"><b>Best For</b></td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">Millions of Small Documents</td>\n<td style=\"padding:10px;border:1px solid #ddd;\">PDFs, Books, Manuals & Reports</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n```\n\n### Which One Should You Use?\n\n**Choose Vector RAG when:**\n\n• You have millions of independent documents.\n\n• Semantic similarity is important.\n\n• Fast retrieval at scale is required.\n\n**Choose Vectorless RAG when:**\n\n• Documents already have a clear structure.\n\n• Maintaining full context is important.\n\n• You want to avoid embedding generation and vector databases.\n\n### Key Takeaway\n\nVector RAG retrieves information using **mathematical similarity**, while Vectorless RAG retrieves information using **reasoning over document structure**. Both are powerful techniques, and choosing the right one depends on your use case."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is PageIndex?",
            "explanation": "Before building a **Vectorless RAG** application, we need a tool that can understand the structure of a document. **PageIndex** is an AI-powered document indexing platform designed specifically for **Vectorless Retrieval**.\n\nUnlike traditional RAG systems, PageIndex **does not create embeddings or require a Vector Database**. Instead, it analyzes the document, identifies its **pages, headings, sections, subsections, and hierarchy**, and builds a structured index. When a user asks a question, PageIndex retrieves the most relevant sections using the document structure, and the retrieved content is then passed to a Large Language Model (LLM) to generate the final answer.\n\n### Why Use PageIndex?\n\n• No embedding generation required.\n\n• No Vector Database (FAISS, Pinecone, Chroma, Milvus) required.\n\n• Preserves the complete document structure.\n\n• Works well with PDFs, books, manuals, research papers, and technical documentation.\n\n• Retrieves only the most relevant sections before sending them to the LLM.\n[IMAGE:/images/genai/rag/vec3.png]\n### Requirements\n\nTo build a Vectorless RAG application using PageIndex, you'll need:\n\n• Python 3.10 or later.\n\n• A PageIndex API Key. (To Create API Keys go to page index official website)\n\n• A PDF document to index.\n\n### Install Required Libraries -`pip install pageindex` \n\n###Follow below snippet for implemenation\n\n```python\n#Import Required Libraries\nfrom pageindex import PageIndexClient\n#Initialize the PageIndex Client\nPAGEINDEX_API_KEY = \"YOUR_PAGEINDEX_API_KEY\"\n\npi_client = PageIndexClient(\n    api_key=PAGEINDEX_API_KEY\n)\n```\n\nOnce the client is initialized, you can upload any PDF document to PageIndex. It automatically analyzes the document, builds a hierarchical index, and prepares it for **Vectorless Retrieval**, allowing the LLM to answer questions without using embeddings or a Vector Database."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
