    {
      "id": "M009-openai-huggingface",
      "title": "Module 9: OpenAI & Hugging Face",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The OpenAI API: The Industry Standard",
            "explanation": "The OpenAI API is the most widely used LLM API in the world. Understanding its Chat Completions endpoint, function calling, streaming, and structured outputs is essential for any Generative AI Application Engineer.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Chat Completions API",
                    "body": "The core endpoint. You send a list of messages (system, user, assistant, tool) and receive a completion. The system message sets the AI's persona, user messages are the human's input, and assistant messages are the AI's previous responses."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Model Selection",
                    "body": "GPT-4o: Best all-around model for complex reasoning and tool calling. GPT-4o-mini: 100x cheaper, great for simple classification, extraction, and routing. o1/o3: Deep reasoning models that think before answering — ideal for math, science, and complex analysis."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Function Calling",
                    "body": "You define functions as JSON Schema objects and pass them in the 'tools' parameter. The model decides when to call them and returns structured arguments. This is the backbone of all OpenAI-powered agents."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Structured Outputs",
                    "body": "OpenAI's Structured Outputs guarantee the model's response exactly matches a provided JSON Schema — no validation errors, no malformed JSON. This is critical for production systems where a single parsing failure breaks the pipeline."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Streaming",
                    "body": "Instead of waiting for the full response, use stream=True to receive tokens as they are generated. This creates a real-time typing effect. For tool calls, the stream includes partial JSON that assembles into the full function call."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Embeddings API",
                    "body": "The Embeddings endpoint converts text into numerical vectors for semantic search. The text-embedding-3-small model produces 1536-dimensional vectors. These embeddings power RAG systems, similarity search, and clustering."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Rate Limits & Cost Management",
                    "body": "OpenAI enforces rate limits (tokens per minute, requests per minute). Production apps must implement: exponential backoff retries, token counting before sending requests, caching frequent queries, and batching where possible."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "from openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n  model=\"gpt-4\",\n  messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Hello!\"}]\n)\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Structured Outputs & Error Recovery",
            "explanation": "In production, you can't just ask an LLM to 'return JSON' and hope for the best. Unstructured text outputs break downstream parsers, corrupt databases, and cause silent failures. Structured Outputs force the model to respond in a machine-verifiable format (JSON Schema, Pydantic models) that is guaranteed to parse correctly. When combined with Error Recovery patterns, you get a self-healing pipeline that automatically detects and fixes malformed outputs without human intervention.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "JSON Mode vs JSON Schema Mode",
                    "body": "JSON Mode (`response_format={'type': 'json_object'}`) forces the model to output valid JSON, but doesn't enforce a specific structure — it might return `{\"foo\": 1}` when you expected `{\"name\": \"...\", \"age\": ...}`. JSON Schema Mode (`response_format={'type': 'json_schema', 'json_schema': {...}}`) constrains the output to match an exact schema with required fields, types, and enums. Always use Schema Mode in production."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Pydantic Integration",
                    "body": "Define your expected output as a Pydantic model and pass it directly as the response_format. OpenAI's `client.beta.chat.completions.parse(response_format=MyModel)` automatically converts the Pydantic model to a JSON schema, sends it to the API, and parses the response back into a validated Pydantic object. If the response doesn't match the schema, it raises a clear exception instead of silently corrupting your data."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Nested & Complex Schemas",
                    "body": "Real-world outputs are often deeply nested: a list of users, each with a list of orders, each with a list of items. Pydantic handles this naturally with nested models. Tip: keep schemas as flat as possible — deeply nested schemas increase the chance of the model making structural errors, even in Schema Mode."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Error Recovery: Self-Correction Pattern",
                    "body": "When a structured output fails validation (e.g., a required field is missing, a value is out of range), don't just crash. Instead:\n\n1. Catch the validation error.\n2. Format the error message into a clear prompt: 'Your output was invalid. Error: field age must be positive. Please fix and try again.'\n3. Send the error back to the LLM in a follow-up message.\n4. Parse the corrected output. This self-correction loop resolves 90%+ of errors automatically."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Retry Budget & Fallback",
                    "body": "Self-correction shouldn't loop forever. Set a max retry count (typically 2-3). If the model still can't produce valid output after 3 attempts, fall back to:\n\n1. A different model (e.g., switch from GPT-4o-mini to GPT-4o).\n2. A more explicit prompt with an example.\n3. Return a default/empty value and flag for human review."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Streaming with Structured Outputs",
                    "body": "When streaming structured outputs, you receive partial JSON tokens. You can't validate until the stream is complete. Use partial parsing libraries that accumulate tokens and validate incrementally, or buffer the entire stream and validate at the end. OpenAI's SDK handles this with `stream=True` and partial Pydantic model hydration."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Testing Structured Outputs",
                    "body": "Write unit tests that verify your structured output pipeline handles:\n\n1. Valid responses.\n2. Responses with extra fields (should be ignored).\n3. Responses with missing required fields (should trigger retry).\n4. Responses with wrong types (string instead of int).\n5. Empty responses.\n6. Responses that are valid JSON but don't match the schema. Test with both real API calls and mocked responses."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Enforce Structured Output",
