        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Function Calling Internals",
            "explanation": "Function calling (tool use) is the mechanism that transforms an LLM from a text generator into an agent that can interact with the real world — searching the web, querying databases, calling APIs, and executing code. Understanding how function calling actually works under the hood — from schema definition to execution and error handling — is essential for building reliable, production-grade tool-using agents.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "How the Model Selects Tools",
                    "body": "When you provide tool schemas in the API call, the model uses its attention mechanism to compare the user's request against each tool's name, description, and parameter descriptions. Vague or ambiguous descriptions cause the model to select the wrong tool. Always write tool descriptions as if you're explaining them to a junior engineer who has never seen the tool before."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Schema Design Best Practices",
                    "body": "Each tool schema defines: name (verb_noun format: 'search_web', 'get_weather'), description (one clear sentence), and parameters (JSON Schema with types, descriptions, enums, and required fields). Anti-patterns:\n\n1. Tools with 10+ parameters — break into smaller tools.\n2. Generic descriptions ('does stuff') — be specific.\n3. Overlapping tools — the model can't distinguish between them."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Parallel Tool Calls",
                    "body": "Modern models (GPT-4o, Claude 3.5) can generate multiple tool calls in a single response. For example, if the user asks 'What's the weather in Tokyo and New York?', the model generates two parallel `get_weather` calls. Your executor must handle these concurrently (using asyncio.gather or threading) to avoid serial latency. The results are then sent back as separate tool response messages."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Tool Call Execution Loop",
                    "body": "The standard execution loop:\n\n1. Send user message + tool schemas to LLM.\n2. If response contains tool_calls, extract each call's name and arguments.\n3. Execute the corresponding function with the provided arguments.\n4. Format the result as a tool response message.\n5. Send the tool response back to the LLM.\n6. Repeat from step 2 until the LLM responds with a regular text message (no tool calls). This loop is the heart of every agent framework."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Error Handling: Tool Execution Failures",
                    "body": "When a tool call fails (API timeout, invalid arguments, permission denied), you must decide:\n\n1. Return the error to the LLM as a tool response ('Error: API returned 500'). The LLM will often self-correct — retrying with different parameters or choosing a different tool.\n2. Implement automatic retries with exponential backoff on the execution side, transparent to the LLM.\n3. For fatal errors, return a clear message and let the LLM inform the user gracefully."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Tool Call Validation",
                    "body": "Never blindly execute tool calls without validation. The model might:\n\n1. Pass the wrong argument types (string instead of int).\n2. Pass values outside valid ranges.\n3. Call a tool with a destructive action that needs approval. Validate every tool call's arguments against the schema before execution. For destructive tools, implement an approval gate (human-in-the-loop) before execution."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Forced Tool Calling",
                    "body": "Sometimes you want to guarantee the model calls a specific tool (e.g., always search before answering). Use `tool_choice={'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'search_web'}}` to force the model to call that tool. Use `tool_choice='required'` to force the model to call at least one tool (any tool). Use `tool_choice='none'` to prevent tool calls entirely (useful for the final summarization step)."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M010-ai-agents",
      "title": "Module 10: Fine-Tuning",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Fine-Tuning?",
            "explanation": "Okay, let's start from the very beginning. Imagine you just hired a brand-new employee. This person is incredibly smart — they went to the best university in the world, read millions of books, and they know a little bit about literally everything. History, science, cooking, law, medicine — you name it.\n\nThat's exactly what a **Foundation Model** is. Models like GPT-4, Llama 3, and Gemini are trained on billions of web pages, books, and articles. They're brilliant generalists — they know a little about everything.\n\nBut here's the problem.\n\nLet's say you run a hospital, and you need this employee to be a **heart surgeon**. Sure, they read about hearts in med school, but would you trust them to perform open-heart surgery on day one? Of course not. You'd send them to a **Residency** — months or years of intense, focused training where they ONLY practice heart surgery, over and over, until they become a specialist.\n\n**Fine-tuning is the residency for AI models.**\n\nYou take that brilliant generalist model, and you train it further — but this time, you ONLY feed it YOUR specific data. Maybe it's thousands of customer support conversations, or legal contracts, or medical diagnoses. After this extra training, the model doesn't just 'know about' your field — it speaks your language, follows your format, and responds exactly the way you want it to.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m10 fine tunning/m10_fine_tuning_overview.png\" alt=\"m10 fine tuning overview\" />\n\n\n\n**Example Analogy:**\nImagine you have a super-smart parrot that can speak every language in the world. But you want it to ONLY speak like a friendly doctor. So you spend a week only talking to it in 'friendly doctor' language. After that week, the parrot naturally starts speaking like a friendly doctor — that's fine-tuning!\n\n**So what actually happens during fine-tuning? Let me walk you through it:**\n\n1. **Start with a pre-trained Foundation Model** — This is your smart employee on day one. The model already has billions of parameters (think of parameters as tiny brain cells) that were trained on the internet.\n\n2. **Feed it YOUR domain-specific dataset** — This is the residency. You give it anywhere from 100 to 10,000 examples of exactly how you want it to respond. Each example is like a flashcard: 'When someone asks THIS, you should respond like THAT.'\n\n3. **Run additional training passes (called epochs)** — An epoch means the model reads through ALL your examples once. You typically run 1–5 epochs, so the model reads your data multiple times to really absorb the patterns.\n\n4. **The model's weights get updated** — Remember those billions of tiny brain cells? Some of them shift slightly to learn YOUR patterns. The model now 'thinks' a little differently.\n\nNow, here's something super important that trips up a lot of beginners. Let me be very clear about what fine-tuning CAN and CANNOT do:\n\n**✅ What Fine-Tuning CAN Do:**\n• Teach a model to ALWAYS respond in a specific format (e.g., always output valid JSON, always use bullet points)\n• Give a model a consistent personality or tone (e.g., always be formal, always be empathetic, always sound like a pirate)\n• Teach domain-specific terminology (legal jargon, medical abbreviations, financial lingo)\n• Make a model follow complex multi-step instructions reliably every single time\n\n**❌ What Fine-Tuning CANNOT Do:**\n• Give the model NEW factual knowledge that wasn't in its training data. If you want the model to know about your company's latest product that launched yesterday, fine-tuning won't help — the model will forget it quickly. For that, you need **RAG** (Retrieval-Augmented Generation), which we covered in Module 8.\n• Fix fundamental reasoning limitations of small models. A 1-billion parameter model won't suddenly become as smart as GPT-4 just because you fine-tuned it.\n• Replace the need for prompt engineering entirely. Fine-tuning and prompting work together, not as replacements.\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule of Fine-Tuning:**\nFine-tuning changes **HOW** the model thinks and responds, not **WHAT** it knows. It's about behavior modification, not knowledge injection. Think of it like teaching someone manners — you're not making them smarter, you're teaching them how to behave in a specific way.\n\nAlright, now that you understand what fine-tuning IS, the next natural question is: 'When should I actually use it?' Let's talk about that."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "When to Fine-Tune vs. Prompt Engineering vs. RAG",
            "explanation": "Okay, so now you know what fine-tuning is. But here's a secret that will save you hundreds of dollars and days of work:\n\n**Don't fine-tune unless you absolutely have to.**\n\nSeriously. Fine-tuning is powerful, but it's also the most expensive and time-consuming option. Before you jump to fine-tuning, there's a hierarchy of solutions you should try. Think of it like fixing a leaky faucet — you don't knock down the whole wall first, right? You start with the simplest fix.\n\nLet me walk you through the three tools in your AI toolkit, from simplest to most powerful:\n\n---\n\n**🔧 Tool 1: Prompt Engineering (Try This FIRST — Always)**\n\nThis is the easiest and cheapest option. You don't change the model at all — you just write a better instruction for it.\n\n**Example Analogy:** It's like telling your smart parrot 'Speak like a pirate!' — you didn't retrain the parrot, you just gave it better instructions.\n\n**When to use it:**\n• One-off format changes ('Output your response as JSON')\n• Simple persona changes ('You are a friendly customer support agent')\n• Tasks that GPT-4 or Claude already handles well with the right prompt\n\n**Cost:** $0 — it's free! You're just typing a better prompt.\n**Time:** Minutes. You write a prompt, test it, refine it.\n\n---\n\n**📚 Tool 2: RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)**\n\nRemember from Module 8? RAG is when the model SEARCHES your documents in real-time and reads them before answering. The model itself doesn't change — it just gets access to your latest information at query time.\n\n**Example Analogy:** Imagine your parrot has a bookshelf. Every time someone asks a question, the parrot first flies to the bookshelf, reads the relevant page, and THEN answers. The parrot doesn't memorize the books — it reads them on demand.\n\n**When to use it:**\n• The FACTS change frequently (company HR policies, product catalogs, news)\n• You need the model to cite specific sources\n• Your data is too large to fit into a prompt\n\n**Cost:** Low — you pay for an embedding model and a vector database.\n**Time:** A few hours to set up.\n\n---\n\n**⚡ Tool 3: Fine-Tuning (The Nuclear Option)**\n\nThis is when you actually retrain the model's brain. You change its weights — its internal wiring — so it permanently behaves differently.\n\n**Example Analogy:** Instead of giving the parrot instructions or a bookshelf, you actually TEACH the parrot a new language by spending a week speaking only that language. After training, the parrot speaks that language naturally, without needing instructions.\n\n**When to use it:**\n• You need the model to learn a NEW SKILL or STYLE that prompting can't achieve\n• The model needs to output perfectly structured reports every single time, with zero failures\n• You want the model to sound EXACTLY like your brand's voice across thousands of interactions\n• You need the model to work with a proprietary internal language or format\n\n**Cost:** $5–$500+ (GPU rental).\n**Time:** Hours to days.\n\n---\n\nHere's a simple decision tree you can use every time:\n\n```python\n# Decision tree in code:\ndef choose_approach(task):\n    if task.needs_latest_facts:\n        return 'RAG'          # Facts change? Use RAG.\n    elif task.needs_new_skill_or_style:\n        return 'Fine-Tuning'  # New behavior? Fine-tune.\n    else:\n        return 'Prompt Engineering'  # Start here always!\n```\n\nGot it? Great. Let's test your understanding with some real-world scenarios."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scenario Practice",
            "description": "You are building a bot that summarizes the morning news. The news changes every single day. Which technique should you use?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "text": "Fine-Tuning — retrain the model every morning with new articles.",
                "score": 0,
                "outcome": "Incorrect! Fine-tuning is slow and expensive. You cannot retrain your model every single morning. The model would also forget yesterday's training."
              },
              {
                "text": "RAG — retrieve the latest news articles at query time.",
                "score": 100,
                "outcome": "Correct! RAG allows the model to fetch and read the latest news articles dynamically without any retraining. The facts change daily, making RAG the perfect choice."
              },
              {
                "text": "Prompt Engineering — just tell the model to summarize today's news.",
                "score": 20,
                "outcome": "Close, but the model doesn't KNOW today's news. It was trained months ago. You need RAG to feed it the actual articles."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scenario Practice",
            "description": "Your marketing team wants ALL AI-generated emails to perfectly match the company's quirky, Gen-Z, emoji-heavy brand voice. Prompt engineering sort of works, but the model keeps slipping back into formal language. What do you do?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "text": "Fine-tune the model on 1,000 examples of emails written in the brand voice.",
                "score": 100,
                "outcome": "Correct! When you need the model to CONSISTENTLY adopt a specific style that prompt engineering can't reliably maintain, fine-tuning bakes that behavior directly into the model's weights."
              },
              {
                "text": "Use RAG to retrieve brand voice guidelines.",
                "score": 30,
                "outcome": "RAG helps inject knowledge, but style/voice is a BEHAVIOR problem, not a knowledge problem. The model needs to learn the pattern through training, not just read about it."
              },
              {
                "text": "Write a longer, more detailed system prompt.",
                "score": 40,
                "outcome": "Longer prompts help but have diminishing returns. The model will still 'forget' the style during long conversations. Fine-tuning makes the style permanent."
