        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) — The Foundation of All Fine-Tuning",
            "explanation": "Alright, now that you understand ALL the adapter methods available (LoRA, QLoRA, Prefix Tuning, etc.), let's zoom into the actual training process itself. Because here's the thing — LoRA is just the adapter. The TRAINING PROCESS that uses those adapters is called **Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT)**.\n\nThink of it this way: LoRA decides WHICH parts of the model to update. SFT decides HOW to update them.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m10 fine tunning/m10_lora_vs_sft.png\" alt=\"LoRA vs SFT\" />\n\n**🧒 The Music Lesson Analogy:**\n\nLoRA is like choosing which fingers to train on the piano — maybe just the right hand (target modules). SFT is the actual practice session — sitting at the piano, playing scales, getting feedback, and improving. You need BOTH — the right fingers AND the practice.\n\n**What is SFT exactly?**\n\nSFT stands for Supervised Fine-Tuning. 'Supervised' means you're showing the model **input-output pairs** — 'when you see THIS input, produce THIS output.' It's like giving a student a set of perfect example answers and saying 'learn to answer like this.'\n\nThis is different from the model's original pretraining, where it just read the internet without any supervision. SFT adds the human judgment layer — 'THIS is what a good response looks like.'\n\n---\n\n**SFT vs. Continued Pretraining — The Critical Difference:**\n\nA lot of beginners confuse these two. They are NOT the same thing:\n\n| | Continued Pretraining | Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Goal** | Teach the model NEW knowledge | Teach the model HOW to respond |\n| **Data** | Raw text (books, articles, code) | Input-output pairs (Q&A, instructions) |\n| **Learns** | Facts, language patterns | Behavior, format, style |\n| **Example** | Feed it all of Wikipedia | Feed it 1000 customer support conversations |\n| **Loss Function** | Next-token prediction on raw text | Next-token prediction on responses only |\n\nThe key insight: during SFT, we typically compute loss **ONLY on the assistant's response**, not on the user's question. The model already knows how to read questions — we want it to learn how to ANSWER them.\n\n---\n\n**The SFTTrainer — Your Training Workhorse:**\n\nThe `trl` library's `SFTTrainer` is the standard tool everyone uses for SFT. Let me show you its most important configuration options:\n\n```python\nfrom trl import SFTTrainer\n\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=model,\n    args=training_args,\n    train_dataset=dataset,\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    peft_config=lora_config,      # Your LoRA/QLoRA config\n    max_seq_length=2048,          # Maximum tokens per example\n    dataset_text_field='text',    # Column with formatted text\n    packing=False,                # Pack multiple examples? (advanced)\n)\n```\n\n**Key parameters explained:**\n\n• **max_seq_length** — The maximum number of tokens per training example. Longer = more context but more VRAM. For most tasks, 512–2048 is enough. If your customer support conversations are short, 512 works. If they're multi-turn, use 1024–2048.\n\n• **packing** — When True, SFTTrainer packs multiple short examples into a single sequence to fill the max_seq_length efficiently. This can speed up training by 2-3x but requires careful attention masking. Start with False, try True once comfortable.\n\n• **dataset_text_field** — The column in your dataset that contains the already-formatted text (with chat templates applied). If you're using a formatting function instead, use the `formatting_func` parameter.\n\n**Using a Formatting Function:**\n\nInstead of pre-formatting your data, you can pass a function that formats each example on-the-fly:\n\n```python\ndef formatting_func(example):\n    messages = [\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a helpful assistant.\"},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": example[\"question\"]},\n        {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": example[\"answer\"]}\n    ]\n    return tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False)\n\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=model,\n    args=training_args,\n    train_dataset=dataset,\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    peft_config=lora_config,\n    formatting_func=formatting_func,  # Format on-the-fly!\n    max_seq_length=1024,\n)\n```\n\nThis is cleaner because your dataset stays in its original format, and the formatting happens automatically during training.\n\n**🔑 Key Takeaway:** SFT is the process, LoRA is the adapter method, SFTTrainer is the tool. You need all three working together.\n\nNow let's talk about the most critical input to SFT — your training data. And specifically, how to generate it at scale."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "SFTTrainer with Formatting Function",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create a formatting function called 'format_example' that takes an example dict with 'instruction' and 'response' keys. It should build a messages list with a system message ('You are a helpful AI assistant.'), a user message (the instruction), and an assistant message (the response). Apply the tokenizer's chat template and return the formatted string. Then create an SFTTrainer with this formatting function.",
            "starterCode": "from trl import SFTTrainer\n\ndef format_example(example):\n    '''Format a single training example using chat template.'''\n    messages = [\n        ___\n    ]\n    return tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False)\n\n# Create trainer with formatting function\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=model,\n    args=training_args,\n    train_dataset=dataset,\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    peft_config=lora_config,\n    formatting_func=___,\n    max_seq_length=1024\n)",
            "solution": "from trl import SFTTrainer\n\ndef format_example(example):\n    '''Format a single training example using chat template.'''\n    messages = [\n        {'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a helpful AI assistant.'},\n        {'role': 'user', 'content': example['instruction']},\n        {'role': 'assistant', 'content': example['response']}\n    ]\n    return tokenizer.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False)\n\ntrainer = SFTTrainer(\n    model=model,\n    args=training_args,\n    train_dataset=dataset,\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n    peft_config=lora_config,\n    formatting_func=format_example,\n    max_seq_length=1024\n)",
            "hint": "Build a messages list with system/user/assistant dicts, then pass format_example as the formatting_func.",
            "explanation": "The formatting function converts raw dataset examples into the model's expected chat format on-the-fly. This keeps your dataset clean and lets SFTTrainer handle tokenization efficiently. The chat template ensures correct special tokens for the model.",
            "concepts": [
              "SFTTrainer",
              "formatting_func",
              "Chat Templates"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def format_example.*messages.*system.*user.*assistant.*formatting_func=format_example"
            },
            "hints": [
              "Build a messages list with system/user/assistant dicts, then pass format_example as the formatting_func.",
              "Focus on these key concepts: SFTTrainer, formatting_func, Chat Templates.",
              "Check the parameter names carefully — Python is case-sensitive and typos will cause failures."
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Data Engineering — The Part That Makes or Breaks Everything",
            "explanation": "Alright, now we're getting into the part that separates real AI engineers from tutorial followers. And I need you to really listen to this next sentence:\n\n**Data preparation is 90% of the actual work in fine-tuning.**\n\n<img src=\"/images/m10 fine tunning/m10_data_pipeline_flowchart.png\" alt=\"Data Engineering Pipeline Flowchart\" />\n\nSeriously. The LoRA configuration? That's 5 lines of code. The training loop? Another 10 lines. But preparing good, clean, high-quality training data? That's where you'll spend most of your time, and it's where most projects succeed or fail.\n\n**Example Analogy:** Imagine you're teaching a child to cook. If you give them a cookbook full of wrong recipes (sugar instead of salt, oven at 1000°), they'll make terrible food. But if every recipe is perfect, they'll become a great cook. Your model is the child, and your data is the cookbook.\n\n**The #1 Rule: Quality Over Quantity**\n\n1,000 perfectly crafted, hand-reviewed examples will create a MUCH smarter model than 100,000 messy, scraped examples. I've seen startups spend $500 on 100K garbage examples from web scraping, and a solo developer beat them with 500 handwritten examples.\n\n**Garbage In = Garbage Out.** There are no shortcuts here.\n\n---\n\nOkay, so what format should your data be in? The model can't read PDFs or Excel files. Everything needs to be in **JSONL format** — that's JSON Lines, where each line is one complete training example.\n\nThere are two standard formats everyone uses:\n\n**Format 1: Alpaca Format (For Single-Turn Tasks)**\n\nThis is the simplest format. Each example is one task:\n\n```json\n{\"instruction\": \"Summarize this article\", \"input\": \"The stock market rose 2% today after...\", \"output\": \"Markets rose 2% following positive earnings reports.\"}\n```\n\nThree keys:\n- `instruction` — WHAT to do (the task description)\n- `input` — the context or data to work with\n- `output` — the expected response\n\n**When to use:** Single-turn tasks like summarization, translation, classification, code generation.\n\n**Format 2: ShareGPT Format (For Multi-Turn Conversations)**\n\nThis stores entire conversations with alternating speakers:\n\n```json\n{\"conversations\": [\n  {\"from\": \"human\", \"value\": \"What is machine learning?\"},\n  {\"from\": \"gpt\", \"value\": \"Machine learning is a subset of AI where...\"}\n]}\n```\n\n**When to use:** Chatbots, multi-turn assistants, conversational AI — anything where context from previous messages matters.\n\n---\n\n**The Lazy Engineer's Secret: Synthetic Data**\n\nWriting 1,000 examples by hand is exhausting. Here's the modern approach that every top AI lab uses:\n\n1. **Write 10–20 seed examples by hand** — Make these PERFECT. They're the foundation.\n2. **Feed them to GPT-4 or Claude** and ask: 'Generate 1,000 variations of these examples, with different topics, styles, and difficulty levels.'\n3. **Human-review the generated data** — Remove anything that's wrong, weird, or repetitive.\n4. **Use the cleaned synthetic data** for fine-tuning your smaller model.\n\nThis 'distillation' approach lets you create GPT-4-quality training data at scale, then use it to teach a smaller, cheaper model.\n\nNow, there's one more critical thing about data preparation that trips up almost everyone. Let me tell you about it."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Chat Templates: The #1 Mistake That Kills Fine-Tuning Projects",
            "explanation": "Okay, this is the part where I need your full attention. I've seen MORE fine-tuning projects fail because of this one mistake than any other. And it's so subtle that most tutorials don't even mention it.\n\nHere's the problem: You have your beautiful JSONL data with `role` and `content` fields. But the AI model CANNOT read JSON keys. It can only read a single, continuous string of text — just raw characters, one after another.\n\nSo how does the model know where the user's message ends and the assistant's message begins? How does it know when to STOP generating text?\n\n**The answer: Chat Templates with Special Control Tokens.**\n\n**Example Analogy:** Imagine two people writing letters back and forth. To know who wrote what, each person puts a special colored stamp at the start and end of their section. The blue stamp means 'user starts here,' and the red stamp means 'assistant starts here.' Without these stamps, you'd have one giant blob of text with no way to tell who said what.\n\nThese 'stamps' are called **Special Tokens**, and EVERY model uses DIFFERENT ones. This is crucial:\n\n```python\n# How your JSON looks:\nmessages = [\n    {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Hello!'},\n    {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Hi there!'}\n]\n\n# How Llama 3 actually sees it during training:\n# <|begin_of_text|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n# Hello!<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n# Hi there!<|eot_id|>\n```\n\nSee those weird `<|start_header_id|>` and `<|eot_id|>` tokens? Those are Llama 3's special stamps. And here's the dangerous part:\n\n**Every model has DIFFERENT stamps:**\n• **Llama 3:** Uses `<|start_header_id|>` and `<|eot_id|>`\n• **ChatML (Mistral, etc.):** Uses `<|im_start|>` and `<|im_end|>`\n• **Gemma:** Uses `<start_of_turn>` and `<end_of_turn>`\n\n**If you use the wrong template, your model will produce garbage.** It won't know when to stop talking, it won't know who said what, and it will hallucinate uncontrollably. I've seen people waste days debugging their model's outputs, only to realize they used the wrong chat template.\n\n**The fix is simple — let the tokenizer handle it automatically:**\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained('meta-llama/Llama-3-8B-Instruct')\n\n# Apply the correct chat template automatically\nformatted = tokenizer.apply_chat_template(\n    messages,\n    tokenize=False  # Return a readable string, not token IDs\n)\nprint(formatted)\n```\n\n**⚠️ THE CRITICAL RULE:** Always load the tokenizer from the EXACT SAME model you're fine-tuning. Never mix tokenizers and models. A Llama tokenizer on a Mistral model = instant disaster.\n\nLet me give you some practice with this."
          }
        },
        {
