          "content": {
            "title": "Synthetic Data Generation — Building Training Data at Scale",
            "explanation": "Alright, you now know what format your data should be in (Alpaca or ShareGPT) and how to apply chat templates. But here's the elephant in the room:\n\n**Where do you get 1,000+ high-quality training examples?**\n\nWriting them by hand is brutal. Trust me — after writing 50 examples of customer support conversations, you'll want to throw your laptop out the window. Every example needs to be unique, realistic, and follow the exact style you want.\n\nThis is where **Synthetic Data Generation** comes in — and it's not a hack. It's literally what every top AI lab in the world does, including OpenAI, Google, and Meta.\n\n**🧒 The Cooking Class Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're a master chef teaching a cooking class. You could cook 1,000 different dishes by hand — but that would take months. Instead, you cook 20 PERFECT example dishes, then hire assistant chefs (GPT-4) to create variations: 'Make this pasta dish, but swap the sauce, change the protein, adjust for different dietary restrictions.' Now you have 1,000 unique dishes — all based on your 20 masterpieces.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m10 fine tunning/m10_synthetic_data_analogy.png\" alt=\"Synthetic Data Cooking Analogy\" />\n\n**The Self-Instruct Pipeline:**\n\nThis is the exact technique that created the Alpaca dataset (which kicked off the open-source fine-tuning revolution):\n\n1. **Write 15-20 seed examples by hand** — Make these PERFECT. They set the quality bar.\n2. **Feed them to GPT-4 or Claude as few-shot examples** — 'Here are 15 examples of how our customer support bot should respond. Generate 50 more like these, covering different scenarios.'\n3. **Filter the generated data** — Remove duplicates, check for quality, fix any errors.\n4. **Repeat** — Use the best generated examples as new seeds for the next batch.\n\n```python\nimport openai\nimport json\n\n# Your 5 seed examples (hand-written, perfect quality)\nseed_examples = [\n    {\n        \"instruction\": \"Customer asks about return policy\",\n        \"input\": \"Hi, I bought a laptop last week and I'm not happy with it. Can I return it?\",\n        \"output\": \"Hi there! Absolutely — we have a 30-day return policy for all electronics. Since you purchased it last week, you're well within the return window. Would you like me to start the return process for you?\"\n    },\n    # ... more seed examples\n]\n\ndef generate_synthetic_batch(seeds, num_examples=50):\n    \"\"\"Generate synthetic training data using GPT-4.\"\"\"\n    prompt = f\"\"\"You are a training data generator. Below are {len(seeds)} example customer support conversations.\n\nGenerate {num_examples} NEW, unique conversations following the EXACT same style, tone, and format.\n\nRules:\n- Cover diverse topics: shipping, returns, product questions, complaints, account issues\n- Vary the customer's tone: polite, frustrated, confused, urgent\n- Keep responses 2-4 sentences, friendly and professional\n- Include specific details like order numbers (#TM-XXXX), product names, and dates\n\nSEED EXAMPLES:\n{json.dumps(seeds, indent=2)}\n\nGenerate {num_examples} new examples in the same JSON format. Output ONLY valid JSON array.\"\"\"\n\n    response = openai.chat.completions.create(\n        model=\"gpt-4o\",\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": prompt}],\n        temperature=0.9,  # Higher temp = more variety\n        max_tokens=4000\n    )\n    \n    return json.loads(response.choices[0].message.content)\n```\n\n---\n\n**The Evol-Instruct Method (Used by WizardLM):**\n\nInstead of just generating variations, Evol-Instruct makes examples progressively MORE complex:\n\n1. Start with a simple instruction: 'Write a Python function to add two numbers'\n2. **Evolve it**: 'Write a Python function that adds two numbers, handles edge cases like None inputs and non-numeric types, includes type hints, and raises custom exceptions'\n3. **Evolve again**: 'Write a production-ready Python module with a function that performs arithmetic operations, includes comprehensive error handling, logging, unit tests, and documentation'\n\nEach evolution creates a harder, more realistic training example. This is how WizardLM outperformed models trained on 10x more data — by making the data HARDER, not just bigger.\n\n---\n\n**Quality Filtering — The Secret Sauce:**\n\nRaw synthetic data always has issues. You MUST filter it:\n\n```python\ndef filter_quality(examples):\n    \"\"\"Remove low-quality synthetic examples.\"\"\"\n    filtered = []\n    seen_instructions = set()\n    \n    for ex in examples:\n        instruction = ex.get('instruction', '').strip()\n        output = ex.get('output', '').strip()\n        \n        # Skip empty or too-short responses\n        if len(output) < 20:\n            continue\n        \n        # Skip near-duplicate instructions\n        if instruction.lower() in seen_instructions:\n            continue\n        seen_instructions.add(instruction.lower())\n        \n        # Skip if output is just a copy of the instruction\n        if output.lower().startswith(instruction.lower()[:50]):\n            continue\n        \n        filtered.append(ex)\n    \n    return filtered\n```\n\n**📊 The Scale Strategy:**\n\n| Phase | Examples | Method |\n|-------|----------|--------|\n| Phase 1 | 20 | Hand-written seeds (perfect quality) |\n| Phase 2 | 200 | GPT-4 generation from seeds |\n| Phase 3 | 200 | Hand-review + quality filter |\n| Phase 4 | 1000 | Second round of GPT-4 generation |\n| Phase 5 | 800 | Final quality filter + deduplication |\n| **Total** | **~800** | **High-quality, diverse dataset** |\n\n**🔑 Key Insight:** 800 high-quality synthetic examples will outperform 10,000 low-quality scraped examples. Always prioritize QUALITY over quantity.\n\nLet's practice building a data quality filter."
          }
        },
