        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "DPO In Practice — Hands-On Preference Optimization",
            "explanation": "You just learned that DPO (Direct Preference Optimization) lets you align a model using simple chosen/rejected pairs — no reward model, no reinforcement learning. Now let's get our hands dirty and actually BUILD a DPO pipeline.\n\n**🧒 The Restaurant Review Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're training a new chef. Instead of just showing them how to cook (SFT), you also show them two dishes side by side: 'This one got a 5-star review. This one got a 1-star review. Can you tell the difference?' Over time, the chef naturally starts cooking more like the 5-star dish. That's DPO.\n\n**Step 1: Build a Preference Dataset**\n\nDPO needs three things per example:\n- **prompt** — The question or instruction\n- **chosen** — The response you WANT the model to produce\n- **rejected** — The response you DON'T want\n\nWhere do you get chosen/rejected pairs? Several strategies:\n\n```python\n# Strategy 1: Generate both responses from your current model\n# and have humans pick the better one\nprompt = \"How do I return a product?\"\n\nchosen = \"I'd be happy to help with your return! Our policy allows returns \" \\\n         \"within 30 days of purchase. Please visit your account page and \" \\\n         \"click 'Start Return' next to the item. Is there anything else?\"\n\nrejected = \"Returns are possible. Go to the website. Find the return \" \\\n           \"section. Fill the form. Wait for processing.\"\n\n# Strategy 2: Use the base model output as 'rejected'\n# and your fine-tuned model output as 'chosen'\n# This teaches the model to prefer its improved responses\n```\n\n**Step 2: Set Up the DPOTrainer**\n\nThe `trl` library makes DPO training almost as easy as SFT:\n\n```python\nfrom trl import DPOTrainer, DPOConfig\n\n# DPO configuration\ndpo_config = DPOConfig(\n    output_dir=\"./dpo_output\",\n    num_train_epochs=1,          # DPO usually needs fewer epochs\n    per_device_train_batch_size=2,\n    gradient_accumulation_steps=8,\n    learning_rate=5e-7,          # MUCH lower than SFT!\n    beta=0.1,                    # Controls preference strength\n    lr_scheduler_type=\"cosine\",\n    logging_steps=10,\n    fp16=True,\n    optim=\"paged_adamw_8bit\",\n)\n\ntrainer = DPOTrainer(\n    model=model,                 # Your SFT-trained model\n    ref_model=None,              # None = use implicit reference\n    args=dpo_config,\n    train_dataset=dpo_dataset,\n    tokenizer=tokenizer,\n)\n\ntrainer.train()\n```\n\n**The beta parameter is crucial:**\n- **beta=0.1** (default) — Moderate preference enforcement. Good starting point.\n- **beta=0.5** — Strong enforcement. Model aggressively avoids rejected responses but may become less creative.\n- **beta=0.01** — Weak enforcement. Model barely changes. Use if DPO is hurting quality.\n\n**Example Analogy:** beta is like the strictness of a teacher. A strict teacher (high beta) gives you zero tolerance for bad answers. A lenient teacher (low beta) gently suggests improvements without being harsh.\n\n---\n\n**The Complete DPO Workflow:**\n\n1. **First:** Train with SFT on your instruction dataset → You get a model that CAN respond\n2. **Then:** Train with DPO on preference pairs → You teach it WHICH responses are BETTER\n3. **Result:** A model that not only responds correctly but responds with the PREFERRED style and quality\n\nThis SFT → DPO pipeline is exactly what Meta used to create Llama 2 Chat, and what most production models follow today.\n\n**🔑 Key Insight:** DPO learning rate should be 10-100x LOWER than SFT learning rate. If SFT uses 2e-4, DPO should use 5e-7 to 5e-6. Why? Because DPO is fine-tuning an already-trained model — big updates would destroy what SFT learned."
          }
        },
