          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building the Complete Pipeline — Putting It All Together",
            "explanation": "Alright, you've learned all the individual pieces. Now let's put them together into a complete, working fine-tuning pipeline. I'm going to walk you through it step by step, like we're pair-programming together.\n\n**Think of it like building a car.** We've learned about engines (LoRA), fuel (data), and steering (alignment). Now we're assembling the whole vehicle.\n\nHere are the 7 steps, in order:\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Install Your Tools**\n\nFirst, you need to install the Python libraries. Think of these as your workshop tools:\n\n```bash\npip install torch transformers peft trl datasets bitsandbytes accelerate\n```\n\nQuick rundown:\n- `torch` — The engine (PyTorch, the deep learning framework)\n- `transformers` — The steering wheel (loads and manages models)\n- `peft` — The LoRA toolkit (parameter-efficient fine-tuning)\n- `trl` — The training assistant (makes training easy)\n- `datasets` — The fuel pump (loads and manages data)\n- `bitsandbytes` — The compressor (4-bit quantization)\n- `accelerate` — The turbo (speeds up training)\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Load the Base Model in 4-bit (QLoRA)**\n\nThis is where we load our smart-but-general-purpose model and compress it to fit in GPU memory:\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, BitsAndBytesConfig\n\nbnb_config = BitsAndBytesConfig(\n    load_in_4bit=True,\n    bnb_4bit_quant_type='nf4',\n    bnb_4bit_compute_dtype='float16',\n    bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True\n)\n\nmodel = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    'meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct',\n    quantization_config=bnb_config,\n    device_map='auto'  # Automatically use the GPU\n)\n```\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Load the Tokenizer**\n\nThe tokenizer converts human text into numbers that the model can understand:\n\n```python\nfrom transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\n    'meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct'\n)\ntokenizer.pad_token = tokenizer.eos_token\n```\n\n**Why `pad_token = eos_token`?** Many models don't have a dedicated padding token. Padding is used to make all examples in a batch the same length. We reuse the end-of-sequence token for this purpose. If you skip this line, training will crash with a cryptic error.\n\nThe remaining steps (LoRA config, dataset loading, training arguments, trainer assembly) are what you'll practice in the code challenges coming up. Let's make sure you can wire each piece correctly."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Hardware, VRAM Math & Cost Realities",
            "explanation": "Before we start training, let's talk money. As an AI engineer, you NEED to know what GPU to rent and how much it will cost. Your boss will ask. Your client will ask.\n\n**The VRAM Rule of Thumb:**\n\n1 Billion parameters ≈ 2 GB of VRAM (at 16-bit precision)\n\n**Example Analogy:** Think of VRAM like the size of your desk. A bigger model needs a bigger desk. If the desk is too small, the model falls off and crashes.\n\nHere's the complete cheat sheet:\n\n| Model Size | 16-bit VRAM | 4-bit VRAM | QLoRA Training |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1B | 2 GB | 0.5 GB | ~2 GB |\n| 7-8B | 16 GB | 4 GB | ~6 GB |\n| 13B | 26 GB | 6.5 GB | ~12 GB |\n| 70B | 140 GB | 35 GB | ~48 GB |\n\n**What do GPUs actually cost in 2024-2025?**\n\n• **RTX 3090/4090 (24GB):** $0.40–$0.60/hour → Perfect for 8B models. This is what most solo developers use.\n• **A10G (24GB):** $0.75/hour → Available on AWS SageMaker.\n• **A100 (80GB):** $1.50–$2.00/hour → Needed for 70B models or large batch sizes.\n• **H100 (80GB):** $3.00–$4.00/hour → The fastest, for when time is money.\n\n**Real cost example:** Fine-tuning a 500-example dataset on Llama 8B with QLoRA takes about 1–2 hours on an RTX 4090. Total cost: **$0.50–$1.20** — literally less than a cup of coffee.\n\n---\n\nNow, here's a sneaky trick that every pro uses:\n\n**Gradient Accumulation — The 'Fake Big Batch' Trick**\n\nYou want a batch size of 128 for stable training (bigger batches = smoother gradients = more stable learning). But your GPU can only fit 4 samples at a time.\n\nSolution: Process 4 samples, save the gradients (DON'T update the weights yet), repeat 32 times, THEN update. The model sees 4 × 32 = 128 samples before each weight update — exactly as if you had a massive GPU!\n\n```python\nper_device_train_batch_size = 4        # Fit 4 in VRAM\ngradient_accumulation_steps = 32       # 4 × 32 = 128 effective batch\n```\n\n**Example Analogy:** Imagine you're moving boxes from a truck to a warehouse. You can only carry 4 boxes at a time. But instead of unpacking after every trip, you make 32 trips and THEN unpack everything at once. Same result as carrying 128 boxes at once, but you only needed strength for 4!"
          }
        },
        {
