        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Deployment: Getting Your Model Into the Real World",
            "explanation": "Your model is trained, evaluated, and ready. Now the final question: how do you actually USE it?\n\nYou have three options depending on your use case. Let me walk you through each one.\n\n---\n\n**Option 1: Cloud Serving with vLLM (For Production Web Apps)**\n\nIf you're building a product that lots of people will use — like a customer support chatbot on your website — you need a fast, scalable server.\n\n**vLLM** is the industry standard. It creates an API server that's compatible with the OpenAI SDK, which means any code written for GPT-4 works with YOUR model too — just change the base URL.\n\n```bash\npip install vllm\n\n# Start an OpenAI-compatible API server\npython -m vllm.entrypoints.openai.api_server \\\\\n    --model ./fine_tuned_model \\\\\n    --port 8000\n```\n\nNow any OpenAI client can call your model:\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI(base_url='http://localhost:8000/v1')\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='./fine_tuned_model',\n    messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Hello!'}]\n)\n```\n\n**Example Analogy:** Your model becomes like a restaurant. People (users) walk in, place an order (send a message), and get served (receive a response). vLLM is the restaurant manager who handles everything efficiently.\n\n---\n\n**Option 2: Local Inference with Ollama (For Privacy)**\n\nIf your data is sensitive (medical, legal, financial) and you can't send it to the cloud, run the model entirely offline on your laptop:\n\n1. Convert to GGUF format: `python convert.py ./fine_tuned_model`\n2. Load in Ollama: `ollama create mymodel -f Modelfile`\n3. Chat: `ollama run mymodel 'Hello!'`\n\nEverything runs locally. Zero data leaves your machine.\n\n---\n\n**Option 3: Push to HuggingFace Hub (For Sharing)**\n\nWant to share your model with the world? Two lines of code:\n\n```python\nmerged_model.push_to_hub('your-username/my-fine-tuned-model')\ntokenizer.push_to_hub('your-username/my-fine-tuned-model')\n```\n\nAnyone can now download and use your model. This is how the open-source AI community works — people share their fine-tuned models for specific tasks, and others build on top of them.\n\nAlright, you now know the complete lifecycle: data → training → evaluation → deployment. Let's make sure everything's locked in with some practice."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Model Merging & Export — From Adapter to Production",
            "explanation": "Your model is trained and evaluated. Now let's talk about the final step before deployment — getting your model into the right FORMAT for production use.\n\n**🧒 The Recipe Book Analogy:**\n\nYou've been cooking with sticky notes attached to a master recipe book (LoRA adapters on a base model). Now you need to decide: do you publish the book WITH the sticky notes pasted in (merge), or do you want to create a POCKET-SIZED version for camping (quantize)?\n\n---\n\n**Part 1: Merging LoRA Adapters**\n\nWhen you finish LoRA training, your model is actually TWO things: the original frozen base model + the tiny adapter weights. For production, you usually want to MERGE them into a single model:\n\n```python\nfrom peft import PeftModel\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM\n\n# Load the base model (full precision this time)\nbase_model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    \"meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct\",\n    torch_dtype=torch.float16,\n    device_map=\"auto\"\n)\n\n# Load the adapter on top\nmodel = PeftModel.from_pretrained(base_model, \"./my_lora_adapter\")\n\n# Merge adapter into base model permanently\nmerged_model = model.merge_and_unload()\n\n# Save the merged model\nmerged_model.save_pretrained(\"./merged_model\")\ntokenizer.save_pretrained(\"./merged_model\")\n```\n\nAfter merging, the adapter is permanently fused into the model weights. The result is a standard model that doesn't need the PEFT library to run.\n\n---\n\n**Part 2: Model Merging — Combining Multiple Fine-Tuned Models**\n\nThis is an advanced technique where you combine MULTIPLE fine-tuned models into one. Think of it like merging the knowledge of two specialists:\n\n**SLERP (Spherical Linear Interpolation):**\nSmoothly blends two models by interpolating their weights along a sphere. Best for merging two models that were trained on related tasks.\n\n**TIES (Trim, Elect, Sign & Merge):**\nSmarter merging that resolves conflicts between models. When two models disagree on a weight value, TIES uses majority voting.\n\n```python\n# Using mergekit (the standard tool)\n# Install: pip install mergekit\n\n# merge_config.yaml\n# models:\n#   - model: ./customer_support_model\n#     parameters:\n#       weight: 0.6\n#   - model: ./technical_writing_model\n#     parameters:\n#       weight: 0.4\n# merge_method: slerp\n# dtype: float16\n```\n\n```bash\nmergekit-yaml merge_config.yaml ./merged_output\n```\n\n---\n\n**Part 3: Quantization for Deployment — GGUF Format**\n\nFor local deployment with Ollama or llama.cpp, you need to convert your model to GGUF format with quantization:\n\n```bash\n# Install llama.cpp\ngit clone https://github.com/ggerganov/llama.cpp\ncd llama.cpp && make\n\n# Convert HuggingFace model to GGUF\npython convert_hf_to_gguf.py ./merged_model --outtype f16\n\n# Quantize to different precision levels\n./llama-quantize ./merged_model.gguf ./model-Q4_K_M.gguf Q4_K_M\n```\n\n**Quantization levels explained:**\n\n| Format | Size (7B) | Quality | Speed | Best For |\n|--------|-----------|---------|-------|----------|\n| **F16** | ~14 GB | Perfect | Slow | Reference / testing |\n| **Q8_0** | ~7 GB | Excellent | Fast | High-quality local |\n| **Q5_K_M** | ~5 GB | Very good | Faster | Balanced choice |\n| **Q4_K_M** | ~4 GB | Good | Fastest | Most local deployments |\n| **Q2_K** | ~2.5 GB | Acceptable | Fastest | Ultra-constrained devices |\n\n**Q4_K_M is the sweet spot** — it reduces model size by ~75% with minimal quality loss. This is what most Ollama users run.\n\n**Deploy to Ollama:**\n```bash\n# Create a Modelfile\necho 'FROM ./model-Q4_K_M.gguf\nSYSTEM \"You are a helpful customer support assistant for TechMart.\"' > Modelfile\n\n# Import into Ollama\nollama create my-model -f Modelfile\n\n# Chat!\nollama run my-model \"How do I return a laptop?\"\n```\n\n**🔑 The Export Decision Tree:**\n- Need an API server? → Merge + deploy with vLLM (HF format)\n- Need local/offline? → Merge + convert to GGUF + Ollama\n- Need to share publicly? → Push to HuggingFace Hub\n- Need maximum speed? → Quantize to Q4_K_M\n- Need maximum quality? → Keep F16 or Q8_0"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Merging and Exporting a LoRA Model",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a complete model merging pipeline. Load a base model from 'meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct' in float16, load a LoRA adapter from './my_adapter' using PeftModel, merge the adapter with merge_and_unload(), and save both the merged model and tokenizer to './production_model'.",
            "starterCode": "from peft import PeftModel\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nimport torch\n\n# Step 1: Load base model\nbase_model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    ___,\n    torch_dtype=___,\n    device_map='auto'\n)\n\n# Step 2: Load adapter\nmodel = PeftModel.from_pretrained(___, ___)\n\n# Step 3: Merge\nmerged = model.___\n\n# Step 4: Save\nmerged.save_pretrained(___)\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained('meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct')\ntokenizer.save_pretrained(___)",
            "solution": "from peft import PeftModel\nfrom transformers import AutoModelForCausalLM, AutoTokenizer\nimport torch\n\nbase_model = AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\n    'meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct',\n    torch_dtype=torch.float16,\n    device_map='auto'\n)\n\nmodel = PeftModel.from_pretrained(base_model, './my_adapter')\n\nmerged = model.merge_and_unload()\n\nmerged.save_pretrained('./production_model')\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained('meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct')\ntokenizer.save_pretrained('./production_model')",
            "hint": "Load base model in float16, use PeftModel.from_pretrained(base_model, adapter_path), then merge_and_unload(), then save_pretrained.",
            "explanation": "This pipeline converts a LoRA adapter + base model into a single standalone model. Loading in float16 keeps quality high. merge_and_unload() permanently fuses the adapter weights. Saving both model and tokenizer together ensures they stay matched for deployment.",
            "concepts": [
              "PeftModel",
              "merge_and_unload",
              "Model Export"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
