        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "PromptTemplates: Reusable Prompt Design",
            "explanation": "You can now send structured messages to any LLM. But are you going to hardcode every single prompt? Of course not. That would be like a chef who memorizes every recipe instead of writing them down — it doesn't scale.\n\n**🧒 The Cookie Cutter Analogy:**\n\nThink of a PromptTemplate as a cookie cutter. The cookie cutter has a fixed SHAPE (the template), but you use different DOUGH (variables) each time. One cookie cutter, unlimited cookies.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_prompt_template_cookie.png\" alt=\"PromptTemplate Cookie Cutter Analogy\" />\n\nWithout templates, you end up writing messy string formatting:\n\n```python\n# This gets ugly fast 🤮\nprompt = f'You are a {role}. Explain {topic} to a {audience} in {language}.'\n```\n\nThis is fragile, hard to test, and impossible to share across your team. One misplaced curly brace and your entire app crashes.\n\n**✨ ChatPromptTemplate — The Proper Way:**\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import ChatPromptTemplate\n\nprompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([\n    ('system', 'You are a {role}. Always respond in {language}.'),\n    ('human', 'Explain {topic} in simple terms.')\n])\n\n# Use it with any variables — like a cookie cutter!\nformatted = prompt.invoke({\n    'role': 'teacher', 'language': 'English', 'topic': 'recursion'\n})\n```\n\nSee those `{role}`, `{language}`, and `{topic}` placeholders? They're your variable slots. When you call `.invoke()`, LangChain fills them in automatically.\n\n**✅ Why Templates Beat f-strings:**\n• **Reusable** — Define once, use with different variables forever\n• **Type-safe** — LangChain validates your variables (forget one? It tells you!)\n• **Composable** — Templates snap into LCEL chains with the `|` pipe operator (we'll learn this next!)\n• **Serializable** — Save prompts to files and load them later\n\n**🚀 Quick Template Shortcut:**\nFor simple single-message prompts:\n\n```python\nprompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_template('Explain {topic} in simple terms.')\n```\n\nThis creates a HumanMessage template — perfect for quick prototyping.\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule of Prompts:**\nIf you're copy-pasting the same prompt structure more than twice, make it a template. Your future self will thank you.\n\nNow let's build some templates with real code."
          }
        },
