          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Memory: Making Your Chatbot Remember",
            "explanation": "<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_memory_types.png\" alt=\"Memory: Making Your Chatbot Remember\" className=\"w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-6 shadow-xl border border-[#30363d]\" />\n\nYour LCEL chain works beautifully — prompt flows to model flows to parser in one clean line. But try having a conversation: tell it 'My name is Alice', then ask 'What's my name?' It has absolutely no idea. Every call starts completely fresh.\n\n**🧒 The Goldfish Analogy:**\n\nYou know the joke about goldfish having a 3-second memory? LLMs are WORSE. They have a ZERO-second memory. Every time you call `.invoke()`, the model starts from scratch — completely blank. It's like talking to someone who forgets you exist every time you blink.\n\nSo how do chatbots like ChatGPT seem to remember? They CHEAT. They don't actually \"remember\" anything. Instead, they replay the ENTIRE conversation history before every new message. It's like writing down everything that was said on a notepad and reading the entire notepad out loud before asking a new question.\n\n**That's exactly what LangChain Memory does.**\n\nBefore each new call, LangChain loads the conversation history and prepends it to the new message. The LLM then sees the entire conversation and can reference earlier exchanges. It's not memory — it's replay.\n\n**📝 InMemoryChatMessageHistory — The Notepad:**\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.chat_history import InMemoryChatMessageHistory\n\nhistory = InMemoryChatMessageHistory()\nhistory.add_user_message('My name is Alice')\nhistory.add_ai_message('Nice to meet you, Alice!')\nhistory.add_user_message('What is my name?')\n\n# history.messages now contains all 3 messages\n```\n\n**🔑 Session IDs — Separating Notebooks:**\nIn production, you need separate notebooks per user. You don't want Alice's conversation leaking into Bob's!\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.runnables.history import RunnableWithMessageHistory\n\nstore = {}  # session_id → InMemoryChatMessageHistory\n\ndef get_session_history(session_id: str):\n    if session_id not in store:\n        store[session_id] = InMemoryChatMessageHistory()\n    return store[session_id]\n\nchain_with_memory = RunnableWithMessageHistory(\n    chain, get_session_history\n)\n```\n\nDifferent `session_id` = different conversation. Same `session_id` = continued conversation. It's like having labeled notebooks — one for Alice, one for Bob, one for Charlie.\n\n**⚠️ IMPORTANT:** In-memory storage vanishes when your app restarts. For production, use database-backed storage (Redis, PostgreSQL). We'll cover production-grade persistence when we learn about LangGraph checkpointers later.\n\nLet's practice building memory systems."
          }
        },
        {
