        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LangGraph — From Chains to Agents",
            "explanation": "Everything you've built so far — chains, memory, tools — follows a straight line: input goes in, processing happens, output comes out, done. One direction, one pass, finished.\n\nBut real-world AI needs to be smarter than that.\n\n**🧒 The GPS Navigation Analogy:**\n\nThink about a GPS. When you type in a destination, it plans a route. But what happens when there's a traffic jam? The GPS doesn't just blindly follow the original route — it RETHINKS, RECALCULATES, and finds a new path. It loops back, makes decisions, and keeps adjusting until you arrive.\n\nLCEL chains are like old paper maps — they give you ONE fixed route. LangGraph is like a modern GPS — it can think, reroute, loop back, and make decisions dynamically.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_langgraph_gps.png\" alt=\"LangGraph GPS Analogy\" />\n\n**Why Not Just Chains?**\nLCEL chains are linear pipelines: A → B → C → Done. They execute exactly once, left to right. But real agents need to:\n• **LOOP** — Call a tool, check the result, maybe call another tool\n• **DECIDE** — Should I search the web or query the database?\n• **RETRY** — That didn't work, let me try a different approach\n\n**🏗️ What is LangGraph?**\nLangGraph models your agent as a **graph** with three building blocks:\n• **Nodes** — Functions that do work (call LLM, execute tool, process data)\n• **Edges** — Connections between nodes (including conditional routing!)\n• **State** — Shared data that flows through every node\n\n**🔑 The Key Difference:**\nChains execute once: `Prompt → LLM → Output`\nAgents LOOP: `Think → Act → Observe → Think Again → Repeat Until Done`\n\nThis \"Think-Act-Observe\" loop is called the **ReAct pattern**, and it's how every modern AI agent works — from ChatGPT's code interpreter to GitHub Copilot's workspace agent.\n\nLangGraph is a separate package: `pip install langgraph`\n\nLet's start building agents by learning the most important concept first — State."
          }
        },
