        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "State Design: The Heart of Every LangGraph Agent",
            "explanation": "Before building a LangGraph agent, we need to design its **State**. This might sound boring, but trust me — State design makes or breaks your agent. Get it right, and everything flows smoothly. Get it wrong, and you'll be debugging for hours.\n\n**🧒 The Clipboard Analogy:**\n\nImagine a team of office workers passing a clipboard around. Each person reads what's on the clipboard, does their work, writes their results on the clipboard, and passes it to the next person.\n\nThat clipboard IS the State. It's the shared data structure that flows through every node in your graph. Every node can READ from it and WRITE to it.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_state_design.png\" alt=\"LangGraph State Clipboard Analogy\" />\n\n**State is a TypedDict:**\nEvery LangGraph agent starts with a State definition:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import TypedDict, Annotated\nfrom langgraph.graph.message import add_messages\n\nclass AgentState(TypedDict):\n    messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]\n    user_query: str\n    search_results: list\n```\n\nThis defines the 'shape' of your clipboard — what fields it has, and what type each field is.\n\n**⚠️ The Overwrite Problem (THIS TRIPS UP EVERYONE):**\n\nWhen a node returns `{'messages': [new_msg]}`, should it REPLACE the entire list or APPEND to it? By default, LangGraph **OVERWRITES**. Your entire conversation history gets deleted and replaced with just the new message!\n\n**🔧 Reducers Fix This:**\nA Reducer is a function that controls HOW state updates are merged. Think of it as a \"merge policy\":\n\n```python\nclass AgentState(TypedDict):\n    messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]  # APPEND (reducer)\n    user_query: str                          # OVERWRITE (default)\n```\n\n`add_messages` is the most important built-in reducer. It APPENDS new messages to the existing list instead of replacing it. This is how you preserve conversation history.\n\n**Writing Your Own Reducer:**\nIt's just a function that takes old and new values and returns the merged result:\n\n```python\ndef add_to_set(existing: set, new_items: set) -> set:\n    return existing | new_items\n\nclass State(TypedDict):\n    visited_urls: Annotated[set, add_to_set]\n```\n\n**📋 State Design Best Practices:**\n1. **ALWAYS** use `Annotated[list, add_messages]` for message fields\n2. Keep state flat — avoid deeply nested dicts\n3. Only include data that multiple nodes need to share\n4. Use descriptive field names\n5. Add type hints to every field\n\nNow let's practice designing state."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
