        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Edges & Routing: Controlling Agent Flow",
            "explanation": "You've built a basic graph with nodes and entry/exit points — awesome! But right now, your agent is like a train on a fixed track. It goes from A to B and that's it. No turns, no decisions, no flexibility.\n\nThe real power of LangGraph lies in its **edges** — especially **conditional edges** that let your agent make decisions at runtime.\n\n**🧒 The Choose-Your-Own-Adventure Analogy:**\n\nRemember those books where you'd read a page and then choose: \"If you want to fight the dragon, turn to page 42. If you want to run away, turn to page 67\"? That's exactly what conditional edges do — they let your agent CHOOSE which path to take based on what it sees.\n\n**📋 Three Types of Edges:**\n```python\ngraph.add_edge('a', 'b')          # Standard — ALWAYS go A → B\ngraph.add_conditional_edges(...)   # Conditional — CHOOSE at runtime\ngraph.set_entry_point('start')     # Entry — where execution begins\n```\n\n**⚡ Conditional Edges (The Power Feature):**\nA routing function inspects the current state and returns the name of the next node:\n\n```python\ndef router(state):\n    last_msg = state['messages'][-1]\n    if hasattr(last_msg, 'tool_calls') and last_msg.tool_calls:\n        return 'tools'     # LLM wants to use a tool\n    return 'end'           # LLM has the final answer\n\ngraph.add_conditional_edges(\n    'agent', router,\n    {'tools': 'tool_node', 'end': END}\n)\n```\n\n**🔄 The Agent Loop (The REAL Magic):**\nCombine a conditional edge with a loop-back edge to create the fundamental agent pattern:\n\n```python\ngraph.add_edge('tool_node', 'agent')  # Loop back!\ngraph.add_conditional_edges('agent', router, ...)\n```\n\nThis creates: Agent → Router → Tool → Agent → Router → ... → END\n\nThe agent keeps thinking and acting until it decides it has enough information. This is the heartbeat of EVERY modern AI agent.\n\n**The END Constant:**\nImport `END` from `langgraph.graph` to terminate the graph. When the router returns a key mapped to `END`, execution stops and the final state is returned.\n\nLet's practice writing routing functions."
          }
        },
