        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Streaming: Real-Time Agent Execution",
            "explanation": "Our agents now persist conversations across calls. But here's a UX problem — users are staring at a BLANK SCREEN while the agent thinks, calls tools, and composes answers. That's terrible user experience.\n\n**🧒 The Kitchen Window Analogy:**\n\nImagine ordering food at a restaurant. Option A: you sit in a dark room for 20 minutes, then food appears. Option B: you sit at a sushi bar where you can WATCH the chef prepare your food in real time — slicing, rolling, plating.\n\nWhich experience is better? Obviously the sushi bar! Even though the wait time is the same, SEEING the progress makes it feel faster and more engaging.\n\nStreaming is the sushi bar window for your AI agent. Instead of waiting in silence, users see every step as it happens.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_streaming_sushi.png\" alt=\"Streaming Sushi Bar Analogy\" />\n\n**Without Streaming:**\n`graph.invoke()` → blank screen for 10 seconds → full answer appears all at once.\n\n**With Streaming:**\n`graph.stream()` → 'Thinking...' → 'Searching...' → 'Found 3 results...' → 'Here's your answer.'\n\n**The .stream() Method:**\n```python\nfor event in graph.stream({'messages': [('human', 'Hello!')]}):\n    for node_name, output in event.items():\n        print(f'[{node_name}]: {output}')\n```\n\n**📋 Stream Modes:**\n• `'values'` — Full state after each node (default). Like getting a full photo after each step.\n• `'updates'` — Only what CHANGED at each node. Like getting a diff.\n• `'messages'` — Individual LLM tokens as they generate. This is the ChatGPT typing effect!\n\n**⌨️ Token-Level Streaming (The ChatGPT Effect):**\n```python\nfor event in graph.stream(input, stream_mode='messages'):\n    msg, meta = event\n    print(msg.content, end='', flush=True)\n```\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule:**\nALWAYS use `.stream()` in production. Better UX, lower perceived latency, and easier debugging. Reserve `.invoke()` for scripts and testing.\n\nLet's try streaming."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Expert Concept: Human-in-the-Loop Safety Gates",
            "explanation": "Streaming shows users what the agent is doing in real-time. But what about actions that are too RISKY to run automatically? Sending emails, making purchases, deleting data, executing code — you probably want a HUMAN to approve these before the agent pulls the trigger.\n\n**🧒 The Nuclear Launch Key Analogy:**\n\nIn movies, launching a nuclear missile requires TWO people to turn their keys simultaneously. No single person can launch alone. Human-in-the-Loop is like that second key — the agent proposes an action, but a human must approve it before it executes.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m11 langchain/m11_human_in_loop.png\" alt=\"Nuclear Launch Key Analogy for HITL\" />\n\n**🛑 interrupt_before — Pause BEFORE a Node:**\n```python\ngraph = graph_builder.compile(\n    checkpointer=checkpointer,\n    interrupt_before=['send_email']  # Pause here!\n)\n```\n\nWhen the graph reaches `send_email`, it STOPS and returns the current state. The human reviews it, and then decides: approve or reject.\n\n**▶️ Resuming After Approval:**\nAfter reviewing, resume by passing `None`:\n\n```python\n# Agent pauses at interrupt...\n# Human reviews and approves...\n\n# Resume from where it paused\nfor event in graph.stream(None, config):\n    print(event)\n```\n\n**✏️ Modifying State Before Resuming:**\nYou can even CHANGE the state before letting the agent continue:\n\n```python\ngraph.update_state(config, {'messages': [('human', 'Change the subject line to something shorter')]})\n```\n\n**⚠️ CRITICAL REQUIREMENT:** Human-in-the-Loop ONLY works with a checkpointer. The checkpointer saves the paused state so you can resume later — even minutes or hours later.\n\n**📋 Common Interrupt Points:**\n• Before tool execution — review which tool and arguments\n• Before sending data — review emails, API calls, database writes\n• After research — review findings before generating a report\n• Before payment — approve purchases and financial transactions\n\n**🔑 The Golden Rule of Safety:**\nAny action with real-world consequences should have a human gate. Your agent should PROPOSE actions; humans should APPROVE them.\n\nLet's implement a safety gate in code."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Streaming a Graph Execution",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Write code to stream the execution of a graph (already defined). Use graph.stream() with a human message 'Tell me about AI'. Loop through the events and print each node_name and its output.",
            "starterCode": "# Assume 'graph' is already compiled\ninput_data = {'messages': [('human', 'Tell me about AI')]}\n\n# Stream and print each step\nfor event in ___:\n    for node_name, output in event.items():\n        print(f'Node: {node_name}')\n        print(f'Output: {output}')\n        print('---')",
            "solution": "input_data = {'messages': [('human', 'Tell me about AI')]}\n\nfor event in graph.stream(input_data):\n    for node_name, output in event.items():\n        print(f'Node: {node_name}')\n        print(f'Output: {output}')\n        print('---')",
            "hint": "Use graph.stream(input_data) instead of graph.invoke(input_data).",
            "explanation": "graph.stream() returns an iterator of events. Each event is a dictionary where keys are node names and values are that node's output. This lets you show real-time progress to users.",
