        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Mini Project: Multi-Tool Research Agent with LangChain",
            "explanation": "**🎯 Your Mini Project Mission:**\n\nIt's time to put EVERYTHING together. You're going to build a multi-tool research agent using LangChain and LangGraph that can autonomously research any topic by searching the web, summarizing articles, and compiling a structured report.\n\n**What You Will Build:**\nA \"Research Assistant Agent\" that takes a topic as input and produces a comprehensive research brief. The agent will use multiple tools (web search, Wikipedia, calculator) and implement the ReAct reasoning loop to decide which tool to use at each step.\n\n**📋 Project Requirements:**\n\n1. **Agent Architecture** — Design an agent using LangGraph with:\n   - A planning node that breaks down the research question\n   - A tool-selection node that picks the right tool for each sub-task\n   - A synthesis node that compiles findings into a report\n\n2. **Tool Integration** — Connect at least 3 tools:\n   - Web search (using Tavily or SerpAPI)\n   - Wikipedia lookup for background context\n   - A text summarizer for long articles\n\n3. **Memory & State** — Implement conversation memory so the agent:\n   - Remembers what it has already researched\n   - Avoids duplicate searches\n   - Builds on previous findings\n\n4. **Output Format** — Generate a structured markdown report with:\n   - Executive summary\n   - Key findings (with sources)\n   - Areas needing further research\n\n5. **Error Handling** — Gracefully handle:\n   - Failed API calls\n   - Irrelevant search results\n   - Timeout scenarios\n\n**🌟 Stretch Goals:**\n- Add a human-in-the-loop checkpoint where the agent asks for confirmation before deep-diving\n- Implement parallel tool execution for faster research\n- Add citation tracking with proper source attribution\n\nThis project brings together everything you learned about LangChain chains, LangGraph state machines, tool binding, and agent memory!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Walkthrough: Building the Agent Step by Step",
            "explanation": "**Step-by-Step Implementation Guide**\n\nLet me walk you through building this agent like we've been doing throughout this module — one clear step at a time.\n\n**📋 Step 1: Set Up the State Graph (Your Blueprint)**\n\n```python\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, END\nfrom typing import TypedDict, List, Annotated\nimport operator\n\nclass ResearchState(TypedDict):\n    topic: str\n    sub_questions: List[str]\n    findings: Annotated[List[str], operator.add]\n    sources: Annotated[List[str], operator.add]\n    final_report: str\n    iteration: int\n\n# Define the graph\nworkflow = StateGraph(ResearchState)\n```\n\nNotice how `findings` and `sources` use the `operator.add` reducer — so each node's results get APPENDED, not overwritten. This is what we learned in State Design!\n\n**📋 Step 2: Create Your Tool Arsenal**\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_community.tools import TavilySearchResults, WikipediaQueryRun\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\nsearch_tool = TavilySearchResults(max_results=3)\nwiki_tool = WikipediaQueryRun()\n\n@tool\ndef summarize_text(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Summarize a long piece of text into key points.\"\"\"\n    return llm.invoke(f\"Summarize this in 3 bullet points: {text}\")\n```\n\n**📋 Step 3: Wire the Graph (Connect the Dots)**\n\n```python\nworkflow.add_node(\"plan\", plan_research)\nworkflow.add_node(\"search\", execute_search)\nworkflow.add_node(\"synthesize\", compile_report)\n\nworkflow.set_entry_point(\"plan\")\nworkflow.add_edge(\"plan\", \"search\")\nworkflow.add_conditional_edges(\"search\", should_continue, {\n    \"continue\": \"search\",\n    \"done\": \"synthesize\"\n})\nworkflow.add_edge(\"synthesize\", END)\n```\n\nSee that conditional edge? The agent loops back to search MORE until it decides it has enough information. Then it moves to synthesis. This is the Think-Act-Observe loop in action!\n\n**📋 Step 4: Test Your Agent**\n\nRun it on these test topics and verify the quality:\n1. \"Latest advances in quantum computing 2024\"\n2. \"How does mRNA vaccine technology work?\"\n3. \"Compare React vs Vue vs Svelte for web development\"\n\n**📋 Evaluation Criteria:**\n- Does it find relevant, accurate information?\n- Does it cite sources properly?\n- Does the report flow logically?\n- Does it handle ambiguous queries gracefully?\n\n**🎉 Congratulations!** You have mastered LangChain AND LangGraph — from simple chains to multi-agent systems. In the next module, we'll take these agent skills even further with AI Agents architecture patterns!"
          }
        }
      ]
