      "id": "M012-ai-agents",
      "title": "Module 12: AI Agents",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What Are AI Agents?",
            "explanation": "Welcome to Module 12 — the module that changes everything you thought you knew about AI.\n\nLet me paint a picture for you. Imagine you hire the smartest intern in the world. They've read every textbook, memorized every Wikipedia article, and can write beautiful prose in 20 languages. Impressive, right? But here's the catch — this intern is **chained to a desk**. They can only answer questions you type to them. They can't pick up the phone, open a web browser, check your calendar, or even look out the window to see if it's raining. All they can do is talk.\n\nThat's what an LLM is without agency. A brilliant mind trapped in a text box.\n\n**Now imagine you unchain them.** You give them a phone, a computer, access to your company's databases, a web browser, and permission to take actions in the real world. Suddenly, that intern doesn't just ANSWER your question about the weather — they CHECK the weather API, look at your calendar, and proactively suggest: \"It's going to rain during your 3 PM outdoor meeting. Want me to move it indoors and notify the attendees?\"\n\n**That's an AI Agent.** An LLM that can perceive its environment, reason about what to do, take actions using tools, and iterate until a goal is achieved.\n\n<img src=\"/images/agent m12/what is agents.png\" alt=\"what is agents\" />\n\n**The Technical Definition:**\nAn AI Agent is a system where an LLM operates in a loop — perceiving inputs, reasoning about what to do next, taking actions (like calling APIs or running code), observing the results, and repeating until the task is complete. The key difference from a chatbot is that **the LLM decides what to do**, not the programmer.\n\n**Why This Module Will Change How You Think About AI:**\n\nHere's a secret most courses won't tell you: the future of AI is not bigger models. It's **agents**. Andrew Ng, one of the most influential figures in AI, put it perfectly: \"I think AI agent workflows will drive massive AI progress this year — perhaps even more than the next generation of foundation models.\"\n\nCompanies like Google, OpenAI, Anthropic, and Microsoft are all betting billions on agent architectures. Devin (the AI coding agent), ChatGPT's tool use, and Google's Gemini Agents are all built on the exact patterns you'll learn in this module.\n\n**What You'll Build in This Module:**\nBy the end of these 90 stages, you'll be able to:\n✅ Build agents that autonomously research, code, and analyze data\n✅ Design multi-agent systems where specialized AI workers collaborate\n✅ Implement safety guardrails that prevent your agents from going rogue\n✅ Evaluate and debug agent performance like a production engineer\n❌ You will NOT just learn theory — every pattern comes with working Python code\n\nLet's start by understanding the most fundamental concept in all of agent engineering: the agent loop."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the KEY difference between a chatbot and an AI Agent?",
              "options": [
                "An agent uses a more expensive LLM model",
                "A chatbot responds to one prompt and waits; an agent autonomously works toward a goal using tools",
                "An agent doesn't need an LLM — it's pure code",
