        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Agent Loop: Perceive → Think → Act",
            "explanation": "<img src=\"/images/agent m12/m12_agent_loop.png\" alt=\"m12 agent loop\" />\n\nLet me tell you about the most important concept in this entire module. If you understand this ONE pattern, you understand 90% of how every AI agent works — from simple chatbots to the most sophisticated multi-agent systems powering companies like Perplexity and Devin.\n\n**The Restaurant Kitchen Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're a head chef in a busy restaurant kitchen. A ticket comes in: \"Table 7 wants a medium-rare steak with a side of garlic mashed potatoes.\" Here's what you do:\n\n1. **PERCEIVE** — You read the ticket and check what ingredients you have\n2. **THINK** — You decide: \"I'll start the steak first since it takes longer, then prep the potatoes\"\n3. **ACT** — You fire up the grill and start cooking\n\nBut you don't just do this once. After the steak hits the grill, you **loop back**: Is it the right temperature? Does it need more seasoning? Should I start the potatoes now? You keep perceiving, thinking, and acting until the dish is perfect and ready to serve.\n\n**That's exactly how an AI agent works.** It's a loop — not a single call.\n\n**The Simplest Possible Agent:**\nHere's a minimal agent in plain Python — no frameworks, no magic:\n\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef simple_agent(goal):\n    messages = [\n        {'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a helpful assistant.'},\n        {'role': 'user', 'content': goal}\n    ]\n    \n    while True:  # ← This is the agent loop!\n        response = client.chat.completions.create(\n            model='gpt-4o', messages=messages\n        )\n        reply = response.choices[0].message\n        \n        # If the LLM wants to call a tool → execute it\n        if reply.tool_calls:\n            # Execute tool, add result to messages\n            # Loop continues...\n            pass\n        else:\n            # No tool call → we have the final answer\n            return reply.content\n```\n\n**Why `while True`? — Let Me Be Very Clear About This:**\n\nThe loop is THE fundamental insight. Here's the difference:\n- A **chatbot** calls the LLM once and returns. One question, one answer. Done.\n- An **agent** calls the LLM, checks if it wants to use a tool, executes that tool, feeds the result back, and calls the LLM **again**. This loop continues until the LLM decides it has enough information to give a final answer.\n\nIt's like the difference between asking someone a question and walking away, versus having an actual back-and-forth conversation where they can research, verify, and refine their answer.\n\n**What Happens Inside Each Iteration:**\n1. **PERCEIVE** — The LLM receives all messages so far (including previous tool results)\n2. **THINK** — The LLM reasons about what to do next\n3. **ACT** — Either call a tool (loop continues) OR return a final answer (loop ends)\n\n**The Magic of This Pattern:**\nThis simple loop can solve incredibly complex problems. Need to research a topic? The agent searches, reads, searches again, reads more, and synthesizes — all from this one loop. Need to debug code? The agent reads the error, tries a fix, runs the code, reads the new error, tries another fix — same loop. The intelligence comes from the LLM's ability to decide what tool to call and when to stop.\n\n**Why This Matters in the Real World:**\nEvery agent framework you'll encounter — LangGraph, CrewAI, OpenAI Agents SDK, AutoGen — is just a fancy wrapper around this loop. If you understand Perceive → Think → Act, you can build agents from scratch and debug any framework.\n\n**Safety Note:**\nNever use a bare `while True` in production! Always add:\n• A `max_iterations` counter (typically 10-25)\n• A token/cost budget\n• A timeout\n\nWe'll cover safety guardrails in Phase 9. But first, let's test your understanding of this pattern with a hands-on exercise."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Your First Agent Loop",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Complete the agent loop below. The `llm_call(messages)` function returns a dictionary with:\n- `type`: either 'tool_call' or 'final_answer'\n- `content`: the response text\n\nYour loop should:\n1. Call `llm_call(messages)` in each iteration\n2. If type is 'tool_call', print 'Calling tool...' and append the result to messages\n3. If type is 'final_answer', print the content and break",
            "starterCode": "def llm_call(messages):\n    # Mock LLM that returns {'type': 'tool_call'|'final_answer', 'content': '...'}\n    pass\n\nmessages = [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the weather in Tokyo?'}]\nmax_iterations = 10\niteration = 0\n\n# TODO: Write the agent loop\n# Use while iteration < max_iterations\n",
            "solution": "def llm_call(messages):\n    pass\n\nmessages = [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the weather in Tokyo?'}]\nmax_iterations = 10\niteration = 0\n\nwhile iteration < max_iterations:\n    response = llm_call(messages)\n    iteration += 1\n    \n    if response['type'] == 'tool_call':\n        print('Calling tool...')\n        messages.append({'role': 'tool', 'content': 'Tool result here'})\n    elif response['type'] == 'final_answer':\n        print(response['content'])\n        break",
            "hint": "Use a while loop with a counter. Check response['type'] to decide whether to continue looping or break.",
            "explanation": "This is the core agent pattern: loop, call LLM, check if it wants a tool, execute tool, feed result back. The max_iterations guard prevents infinite loops — a critical safety measure.",
            "concepts": [
              "Agent Loop",
              "Tool Calling",
              "Safety Guards"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "while.*iteration.*max_iterations.*llm_call.*response.*type.*final_answer.*break"
            }
          }
