        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Agents vs. Chatbots vs. Pipelines — When to Use What",
            "explanation": "<img src=\"/images/m12_embedding_space.png\" alt=\"m12 embedding space\" />\n\nI need you to really listen to this one, because choosing the wrong approach here will cost you weeks of wasted work and hundreds of dollars in unnecessary API calls.\n\nNot every AI application needs an agent. Let me say that again: **not every AI application needs an agent.** Using an agent when a simple API call would suffice is like hiring a full-time detective to tell you the time — absurdly expensive and painfully slow for something a watch can do.\n\nHere's how to think about it. Imagine you run a company with three types of workers:\n\n**🔌 The Light Switch (Direct LLM Call)**\nYou flip it, the light turns on. One action, one result. No thinking required.\n```python\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o-mini',\n    messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize this text...'}]\n)\n```\n✅ Use for: Simple one-shot tasks — summarization, translation, classification, sentiment analysis\n❌ Don't use for: Anything requiring multiple steps, external data, or decision-making\n\n**🏭 The Assembly Line (Chain / Pipeline)**\nEvery product goes through the same fixed steps in the same order. Predictable, efficient, scalable.\n```python\n# RAG Pipeline: always the same steps, no decisions\nquery → embed → search vector DB → format context → LLM → answer\n```\n✅ Use for: Predictable workflows — RAG, content generation, data processing pipelines\n❌ Don't use for: Tasks where you don't know the steps in advance\n\n**🕵️ The Detective (Agent)**\nGiven a case, they decide what to investigate, what questions to ask, which leads to follow. Every case is different.\n```python\nwhile not done:\n    action = llm.decide(state)   # LLM chooses what to do\n    result = execute(action)     # Execute the chosen action\n    state.update(result)         # Update with new information\n```\n✅ Use for: Open-ended tasks, research, multi-step reasoning, dynamic tool selection\n❌ Don't use for: Simple tasks where a pipeline would be 10x cheaper and faster\n\n**The Decision Matrix — Print This Out:**\n\n| Question | Direct Call | Pipeline | Agent |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Do you know all the steps in advance? | Yes (1 step) | Yes (N steps) | No |\n| Does the LLM need to make decisions? | No | No | Yes |\n| Does it need external tools? | No | Maybe | Yes |\n| Is the output deterministic? | Mostly | Yes | No |\n| Typical cost per run | $0.001 | $0.01-0.10 | $0.10-5.00 |\n| Typical latency | <2 sec | 2-10 sec | 10-120 sec |\n\n**Real-World Examples:**\n- **Stripe** uses direct LLM calls for auto-completing support responses\n- **Notion** uses pipelines for their AI writing assistant (fixed steps: expand → improve → format)\n- **Perplexity** uses agents for web research (unpredictable: search → read → search more → synthesize)\n\n**The Golden Rule:**\nStart with the simplest approach that works. Try a direct call first. If that's not enough, build a pipeline. Only reach for an agent when the task truly requires dynamic decision-making.\n\nIn practice, most production systems combine all three: an agent orchestrates the overall workflow, calling pipelines for well-defined sub-tasks and direct LLM calls for simple operations.\n\nNow that you know WHEN to use an agent, let's learn the specific framework that powers most modern agents: ReAct."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Choosing the Right Architecture",
            "description": "Your company wants to build an AI system that answers customer questions by searching a knowledge base, checking order status via an API, and sometimes escalating to a human. Which architecture is most appropriate?",
