        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Tool Calling — Giving Agents Superpowers",
            "explanation": "Imagine you have the world's most brilliant brain surgeon — someone with encyclopedic medical knowledge, perfect reasoning, and years of training. But they have no hands. They can describe the surgery in exquisite detail, tell you exactly which arteries to clamp, but they can't actually pick up a scalpel.\n\nThat's an LLM without tools. A genius locked in an empty room — incredibly smart but unable to DO anything in the real world. Tool calling is what gives the LLM its hands.\n\n**The Core Problem — And Why It's Harder Than You Think:**\nLLMs generate text. But text alone can't book a flight, check the weather, query a database, or send an email. We need a way for the LLM to say: \"I want to call this function with these arguments.\" And we need that mechanism to be reliable, structured, and safe.\n\n**How Tool Calling Works — The Swiss Army Knife Pattern:**\n\nThink of it like a Swiss Army knife. The knife (your agent) has many tools built in — a blade, scissors, a screwdriver. But the knife doesn't randomly deploy all tools at once. Based on the situation, it selects the RIGHT tool for the job.\n\n**Step 1: You describe your tools to the LLM** (like labeling each tool on the knife)\n```python\ntools = [{\n    'type': 'function',\n    'function': {\n        'name': 'get_weather',\n        'description': 'Get current weather for a city',\n        'parameters': {\n            'type': 'object',\n            'properties': {\n                'city': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'City name'}\n            },\n            'required': ['city']\n        }\n    }\n}]\n```\n\n**Step 2: The LLM decides to call a tool** (it picks the right one)\nWhen the user asks \"What's the weather in Tokyo?\", the LLM doesn't answer from memory — it returns a special `tool_calls` field: `get_weather(city='Tokyo')`\n\n**Step 3: YOUR code executes the tool** (the critical part)\nThis is crucial — **the LLM does NOT execute anything.** It just says WHAT it wants to call. Your Python code actually runs the function, gets the result, and sends it back to the LLM.\n\n**Why This Separation Is Brilliant:**\n\n✅ **Safety** — Your code is the gatekeeper. The LLM can request any tool call, but your code decides whether to actually execute it\n✅ **Reliability** — No more hoping the LLM outputs valid JSON. Modern models are specifically trained to produce clean function call JSON\n✅ **Flexibility** — Your tools can do ANYTHING: call APIs, run SQL queries, execute Python code, send emails, deploy code\n\n❌ **Old Approach (Don't Do This)**: Trick the LLM into outputting JSON via clever prompts → unreliable, breaks frequently\n✅ **Modern Approach**: Native function calling built into the model's training → reliable, structured, consistent\n\n**Who Supports Native Tool Calling:**\nGPT-4o, Claude 3.5, Gemini 1.5, Llama 3.1, Mistral Large — all major models now support it. This isn't a niche feature; it's the standard way agents interact with the world.\n\n**Why This Matters For Your Career:**\nAccording to a 2024 survey by LangChain, **87% of production AI agents** use tool calling as their primary mechanism for interacting with external systems. If you can design good tool schemas and handle tool execution pipelines, you're in the top 10% of AI engineers.\n\nNow let's learn how to write those tool schemas properly — because a bad schema is like giving someone a Swiss Army knife with no labels."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
