        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Defining Tool Schemas with JSON Schema",
            "explanation": "Here's something that might surprise you: the #1 reason agents call the wrong tool or pass wrong arguments is NOT because the LLM is dumb. It's because the developer wrote a bad tool description.\n\nLet me explain with an analogy. Think of a tool schema as a **job posting**. If your job posting says \"Do stuff with data,\" you'll get terrible candidates. But if it says \"Senior Data Analyst — analyze quarterly sales data from our PostgreSQL database, generate CSV reports with year-over-year comparisons, requires SQL proficiency\" — now you'll attract exactly the right person.\n\nThe LLM reads your tool descriptions the same way a job candidate reads a posting. The clearer and more specific you are, the better the LLM performs.\n\n**The Five Parts of a Tool Schema:**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"function\",\n  \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"search_products\",\n    \"description\": \"Search for products in our e-commerce catalog by name, category, or price range. Returns up to 10 results sorted by relevance. Example: search_products(query='wireless headphones', max_price=100)\",\n    \"parameters\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"query\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\",\n          \"description\": \"Search query — e.g., 'wireless headphones', 'running shoes'\"\n        },\n        \"max_price\": {\n          \"type\": \"number\",\n          \"description\": \"Maximum price filter in USD. If omitted, no price filter is applied.\"\n        },\n        \"category\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\",\n          \"enum\": [\"electronics\", \"clothing\", \"home\", \"sports\"],\n          \"description\": \"Product category to filter by\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"required\": [\"query\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**The Golden Rules for Tool Descriptions — Memorize These:**\n\n| Rule | ❌ Bad Example | ✅ Good Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Be specific | \"Search stuff\" | \"Search our product catalog by name or category\" |\n| Include examples | (no examples) | \"e.g., 'AAPL', 'MSFT', 'GOOGL'\" |\n| State limitations | (silent) | \"Only supports US stock tickers\" |\n| Describe return format | (mystery) | \"Returns JSON with price, volume, and change_percent\" |\n| Name clearly | \"do_thing\" | \"search_products\" |\n\n**Common Mistakes I See All the Time:**\n\n1. **Vague descriptions** → The LLM uses the tool for the wrong purpose\n2. **No examples in parameter descriptions** → The LLM formats arguments incorrectly\n3. **Missing `enum` for fixed choices** → The LLM invents invalid options\n4. **No `required` field** → The LLM omits critical parameters\n\n**Common Parameter Types:**\n• `string` — text inputs (names, queries, IDs)\n• `number` — numeric inputs (prices, quantities)\n• `boolean` — true/false flags\n• `array` — lists of values (`{\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}`)\n• `enum` — fixed set of allowed values (prevents the LLM from inventing invalid options)\n\n**Pro Tip That Will Save You Hours:**\nThe `description` field on each **parameter** matters as much as the tool description. The LLM reads EVERYTHING in the schema to decide what values to pass. I've seen agents go from 60% accuracy to 95% accuracy just by improving parameter descriptions.\n\n**Why This Matters:**\nAt companies like Stripe and Shopify, tool schema quality is treated as seriously as API documentation. A poorly defined schema doesn't just cause errors — it causes the agent to hallucinate tool calls that look plausible but are completely wrong.\n\nNow that you know how to define tools, let's see the complete pipeline — how a tool call flows from the user's question all the way to the final answer."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Writing a Complete Tool Schema",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Define a tool schema for a `get_stock_price` function. It should:\n1. Have the name 'get_stock_price'\n2. Have a clear description\n3. Accept a required `ticker` parameter (string) with description 'Stock ticker symbol, e.g., AAPL'\n4. Accept an optional `exchange` parameter (string)",
            "starterCode": "tool_schema = {\n    'type': 'function',\n    'function': {\n        'name': '___',\n        'description': '___',\n        'parameters': {\n            'type': 'object',\n            'properties': {\n                # Define ticker and exchange parameters\n            },\n            'required': [___]\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nprint(tool_schema)",
            "solution": "tool_schema = {\n    'type': 'function',\n    'function': {\n        'name': 'get_stock_price',\n        'description': 'Get the current stock price for a given ticker symbol.',\n        'parameters': {\n            'type': 'object',\n            'properties': {\n                'ticker': {\n                    'type': 'string',\n                    'description': 'Stock ticker symbol, e.g., AAPL'\n                },\n                'exchange': {\n                    'type': 'string',\n                    'description': 'Stock exchange name, e.g., NASDAQ'\n                }\n            },\n            'required': ['ticker']\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nprint(tool_schema)",
            "hint": "Name it 'get_stock_price', add a 'ticker' property of type 'string' and an 'exchange' property. Only 'ticker' goes in the required array.",
            "explanation": "The schema tells the LLM exactly how to call your function. The description is critical — the LLM reads it to decide WHEN to use this tool. Optional parameters (like exchange) are not in the required array.",
            "concepts": [
              "Tool Schema",
              "JSON Schema",
              "Function Parameters"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "get_stock_price.*ticker.*type.*string.*required.*ticker"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Tool Execution Pipeline",
            "explanation": "Now let's see the complete picture. Think of it like an assembly line in a factory — each station does one thing, and the product flows from start to finish in a predictable sequence.\n\n**The Tool Call Assembly Line — 5 Stations:**\n\n**Station 1: Send the order** (user message + tool menu → LLM)\n```python\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o',\n    messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Weather in Tokyo?'}],\n    tools=tool_schemas  # ← Give the LLM the menu of available tools\n)\n```\n\n**Station 2: Read the LLM's decision** (did it want to call a tool?)\n```python\nmessage = response.choices[0].message\nif message.tool_calls:  # ← LLM decided to use a tool!\n    tool_call = message.tool_calls[0]\n    name = tool_call.function.name       # 'get_weather'\n    args = json.loads(tool_call.function.arguments)  # {'city': 'Tokyo'}\n```\n\n**Station 3: YOUR code executes the tool** (you're the gatekeeper)\n```python\n# Map tool names to actual Python functions\ntool_functions = {'get_weather': get_weather, 'search': search}\nresult = tool_functions[name](**args)  # ← Your code runs the function!\n```\n\n**Station 4: Send the result back** (feed the observation to the LLM)\n```python\nmessages.append(message)  # The assistant's tool_call message\nmessages.append({\n    'role': 'tool',\n    'tool_call_id': tool_call.id,  # Must match the original call!\n    'content': json.dumps(result)\n})\n```\n\n**Station 5: LLM speaks** (final answer using real data)\n```python\nfinal = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o', messages=messages\n)\nprint(final.choices[0].message.content)\n# → 'The weather in Tokyo is 22°C and sunny.'\n```\n\n**The Three Things That Go Wrong Most Often:**\n\n| Problem | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `KeyError: 'get_wether'` | LLM misspelled the tool name | Better tool names + validation |\n| `json.JSONDecodeError` | LLM returned malformed args | Use `try/except` + retry |\n| Wrong tool chosen | Ambiguous tool descriptions | Clearer, more specific descriptions |\n\n**Key Insight — Burn This Into Your Memory:**\nThe LLM NEVER touches your code or APIs directly. It only outputs JSON describing what it WANTS to call. Your code is always the gatekeeper. This separation is what makes tool calling safe and controllable.\n\n**A Real Production Pattern — Error Handling Included:**\n```python\ndef execute_tool_call(tool_call, available_tools):\n    name = tool_call.function.name\n    \n    # Validate tool exists\n    if name not in available_tools:\n        return f\"ERROR: Unknown tool '{name}'. Available: {list(available_tools.keys())}\"\n    \n    # Parse arguments safely\n    try:\n        args = json.loads(tool_call.function.arguments)\n    except json.JSONDecodeError:\n        return \"ERROR: Could not parse tool arguments. Please try again.\"\n    \n    # Execute with error handling\n    try:\n        return available_tools[name](**args)\n    except Exception as e:\n        return f\"ERROR: {e}. Please try a different approach.\"\n```\n\nThis pattern handles the three most common failures gracefully, and feeds errors back to the LLM so it can self-correct (just like we learned in the ReAct section).\n\nNow let's level up — modern LLMs can call multiple tools at once. Let's see how parallel tool calls work."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building the Full Tool Execution Loop",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Complete the tool execution pipeline. Given a `tool_call` object from the OpenAI API:\n1. Extract the function name and arguments (parse JSON)\n2. Look up the function in `available_tools` dict\n3. Execute the function with the parsed arguments\n4. Create the tool response message with the correct role and tool_call_id",
            "starterCode": "import json\n\ndef get_weather(city):\n    return {'temp': '22°C', 'condition': 'Sunny', 'city': city}\n\navailable_tools = {'get_weather': get_weather}\n\n# Simulated tool_call from OpenAI API\nclass ToolCall:\n    id = 'call_abc123'\n    class function:\n        name = 'get_weather'\n        arguments = '{\"city\": \"Tokyo\"}'\n\ntool_call = ToolCall()\n\n# TODO: Extract name and args\nname = ___\nargs = ___\n\n# TODO: Execute the tool\nresult = ___\n\n# TODO: Create tool response message\ntool_message = {\n    'role': '___',\n    'tool_call_id': ___,\n    'content': ___\n}",
            "solution": "import json\n\ndef get_weather(city):\n    return {'temp': '22°C', 'condition': 'Sunny', 'city': city}\n\navailable_tools = {'get_weather': get_weather}\n\nclass ToolCall:\n    id = 'call_abc123'\n    class function:\n        name = 'get_weather'\n        arguments = '{\"city\": \"Tokyo\"}'\n\ntool_call = ToolCall()\n\nname = tool_call.function.name\nargs = json.loads(tool_call.function.arguments)\n\nresult = available_tools[name](**args)\n\ntool_message = {\n    'role': 'tool',\n    'tool_call_id': tool_call.id,\n    'content': json.dumps(result)\n}",
            "hint": "Use tool_call.function.name for the name, json.loads() to parse the arguments, available_tools[name](**args) to call it, and role 'tool' with tool_call.id.",
            "explanation": "This is the exact pattern every agent framework uses under the hood: extract → parse → execute → format response. The tool_call_id must match so the LLM knows which tool call this result belongs to.",
            "concepts": [
              "Tool Execution",
              "JSON Parsing",
              "Function Dispatch"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "tool_call\\.function\\.name.*json\\.loads.*available_tools.*role.*tool.*tool_call\\.id"
            }
          }
