        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Parallel Tool Calls & Structured Outputs",
            "explanation": "Imagine you're cooking dinner and you need to chop vegetables AND boil water. A beginner cook does them sequentially — chop first, then boil. An experienced cook starts the water boiling and chops while waiting. Same end result, but twice as fast.\n\nModern LLMs like GPT-4o can do the same thing with tool calls — request **multiple tools in a single response** and execute them in parallel. This is a game-changer for performance.\n\n**Parallel Tool Calls in Action:**\nUser: \"What's the weather in both London AND Paris?\"\n\nInstead of making two sequential round-trips to the LLM, it returns both requests at once:\n```python\nmessage.tool_calls = [\n    ToolCall(function=Function(name='get_weather', arguments='{\"city\": \"London\"}')),\n    ToolCall(function=Function(name='get_weather', arguments='{\"city\": \"Paris\"}'))\n]\n```\n\nYou execute both tools (potentially in parallel with `asyncio`), send both results back, and the LLM combines them into one unified answer.\n\n**Handling Multiple Tool Calls — The Complete Pattern:**\n```python\nif message.tool_calls:\n    messages.append(message)  # Add the assistant message first\n    \n    for tool_call in message.tool_calls:  # Loop over ALL calls\n        name = tool_call.function.name\n        args = json.loads(tool_call.function.arguments)\n        result = available_tools[name](**args)\n        \n        messages.append({\n            'role': 'tool',\n            'tool_call_id': tool_call.id,  # Each has a unique ID!\n            'content': json.dumps(result)\n        })\n```\n\n**The Performance Difference Is Massive:**\n\n| Approach | Tool Calls | Round-trips to LLM | Total Time |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sequential | 2 separate requests | 4 (ask + answer × 2) | ~8 seconds |\n| Parallel | 1 request, 2 tools | 2 (ask + answer) | ~3 seconds |\n\nFor agents that need 5-10 tools per task, parallel calls cut execution time by 60-80%.\n\n**Async Execution for Maximum Speed:**\n```python\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def execute_tools_parallel(tool_calls, available_tools):\n    tasks = []\n    for tc in tool_calls:\n        name = tc.function.name\n        args = json.loads(tc.function.arguments)\n        tasks.append(asyncio.to_thread(available_tools[name], **args))\n    \n    results = await asyncio.gather(*tasks)\n    return results\n```\n\n**Structured Outputs for Agent Responses:**\nSometimes you want the LLM to return structured data (not just call tools). Use `response_format` to force valid JSON:\n```python\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='gpt-4o',\n    messages=messages,\n    response_format={'type': 'json_object'}  # Forces valid JSON output\n)\n```\n\nThis is incredibly useful when your agent needs to return structured data like:\n• A plan with numbered steps\n• An analysis with scores and categories\n• A structured report with sections\n\n**When to Use What:**\n✅ **Parallel tool calls** — When you need multiple pieces of data that don't depend on each other\n❌ **Sequential tool calls** — When the second tool needs the result of the first (step 2 depends on step 1)\n✅ **Structured outputs** — When you need the agent to return data in a specific format for downstream processing\n\nNow that you've mastered tool calling, it's time to learn about planning — because the best agents think before they act."
          }
