        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Agent Systems — Why One Agent Isn't Enough",
            "explanation": "<img src=\"/images/m12_multi_agent.png\" alt=\"m12 multi agent\" />\n\nLet me tell you a story about a developer named Sarah. She built a single agent to handle her company's customer support. This one agent needed to: answer billing questions, troubleshoot technical issues, process refunds, escalate urgent cases, and update the CRM. It worked... for about a week. Then the prompt grew to 4,000 words, the agent started confusing billing procedures with technical troubleshooting, and refunds were being processed with the wrong amounts. Sarah's agent wasn't stupid — it was **overwhelmed**.\n\n**Here's the lesson: a single agent trying to do everything is like a single person trying to be the CEO, accountant, developer, and receptionist at the same time.** They'll do each job poorly.\n\n**The Solution: Specialization**\n\nInstead of one overwhelmed agent, Sarah built four specialized agents:\n• **Triage Agent** — Classifies incoming requests and routes them\n• **Billing Agent** — Handles invoices, payments, and refunds (with access to billing tools)\n• **Tech Support Agent** — Troubleshoots issues (with access to logs and documentation)\n• **Escalation Agent** — Handles angry customers and urgent cases (with human-in-the-loop)\n\nEach agent had a focused system prompt, specific tools, and clear boundaries. The result? 90% fewer errors, faster response times, and happy customers.\n\n**Why Multi-Agent > Single Agent:**\n\n| Dimension | Single Agent | Multi-Agent |\n|---|---|---|\n| Prompt complexity | 4,000+ words (confusion) | 200-500 words each (clarity) |\n| Tool conflicts | 20+ tools (wrong selection) | 3-5 tools each (precision) |\n| Error blast radius | One error affects everything | Errors are isolated to one agent |\n| Debugging | \"Which of 20 things went wrong?\" | \"The billing agent failed at step 3\" |\n| Scaling | Rewrite the whole agent | Add/modify individual agents |\n\n**The Three Multi-Agent Architectures:**\n1. **Supervisor** — One manager agent delegates to worker agents (most common)\n2. **Peer-to-Peer** — Agents collaborate as equals, passing work to each other\n3. **Debate** — Agents argue and a judge picks the best answer (for accuracy)\n\n**When to Use Multi-Agent:**\n✅ Task requires expertise in multiple domains (billing + tech + legal)\n✅ Single agent prompt exceeds ~1,000 words\n✅ You need to add new capabilities without breaking existing ones\n✅ Different parts of the task need different tools\n\n**When a Single Agent Is Fine:**\n❌ Task is simple and well-defined (research, Q&A, summarization)\n❌ All steps use the same tools and expertise\n❌ You're prototyping and need speed over reliability\n\n**Real-World Multi-Agent Systems:**\n• **Devin** (AI coder) — Planner + Coder + Tester + Debugger agents\n• **ChatGPT** — Routing agent + Code interpreter + DALL·E + Browse agents\n• **Customer support platforms** — Triage + Billing + Technical + Escalation agents\n\nLet's start with the most common pattern: the Supervisor."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Sequential Multi-Agent Pipeline",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a sequential 3-agent pipeline where:\n1. Agent 1 (Researcher) processes the user query\n2. Agent 2 (Writer) takes the research and writes content\n3. Agent 3 (Editor) polishes the final output\n\nComplete the `run_pipeline()` function.",
            "starterCode": "def run_agent(system_prompt, user_message):\n    # Mock LLM call\n    return f'[{system_prompt[:20]}...] processed: {user_message[:50]}'\n\ndef run_pipeline(user_query):\n    # Agent 1: Researcher\n    research = run_agent(\n        '___',\n        f'Research this topic: {user_query}'\n    )\n    print(f'Research: {research}')\n    \n    # Agent 2: Writer — uses research as input\n    draft = run_agent(\n        '___',\n        f'Write content based on: {___}'\n    )\n    print(f'Draft: {draft}')\n    \n    # Agent 3: Editor — polishes the draft\n    final = run_agent(\n        '___',\n        f'Edit and polish: {___}'\n    )\n    print(f'Final: {final}')\n    \n    return final\n\nrun_pipeline('AI agents in healthcare')",
            "solution": "def run_agent(system_prompt, user_message):\n    return f'[{system_prompt[:20]}...] processed: {user_message[:50]}'\n\ndef run_pipeline(user_query):\n    research = run_agent(\n        'You are a research analyst. Find key facts and data.',\n        f'Research this topic: {user_query}'\n    )\n    print(f'Research: {research}')\n    \n    draft = run_agent(\n        'You are a content writer. Write clear, engaging content.',\n        f'Write content based on: {research}'\n    )\n    print(f'Draft: {draft}')\n    \n    final = run_agent(\n        'You are an editor. Fix errors and improve clarity.',\n        f'Edit and polish: {draft}'\n    )\n    print(f'Final: {final}')\n    \n    return final\n\nrun_pipeline('AI agents in healthcare')",
            "hint": "Give each agent a descriptive system prompt. Pass 'research' to the writer and 'draft' to the editor.",
            "explanation": "This is the simplest multi-agent pattern: sequential handoff. Each agent has a focused role and passes its output to the next. The key is that each agent's system prompt is specialized.",
            "concepts": [
              "Sequential Pipeline",
              "Multi-Agent",
              "Agent Handoff"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "run_agent.*research.*run_agent.*research.*run_agent.*draft"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Supervisor Pattern: Manager + Workers",
            "explanation": "The Supervisor pattern is the most common production architecture for multi-agent systems. And the best way to understand it is with an analogy you already know.\n\n**The Hospital Triage Analogy:**\n\nWhen you walk into an emergency room, you don't go directly to a surgeon. First, a **triage nurse** assesses your condition:\n- Broken bone? → Send to **Orthopedics**\n- Chest pain? → Send to **Cardiology** (URGENT)\n- Rash? → Send to **Dermatology**\n- Common cold? → Send to **General Practice**\n\nThe triage nurse doesn't treat you — they **route** you to the right specialist. And each specialist has their own equipment, expertise, and procedures.\n\n**That's exactly how the Supervisor pattern works:**\n\n```python\n# The Supervisor (Triage Nurse)\nsupervisor = Agent(\n    name='Supervisor',\n    instructions='''You are a customer support supervisor.\n    Analyze the customer's message and delegate to the right agent:\n    - Billing questions → billing_agent\n    - Technical issues → tech_agent  \n    - General questions → general_agent\n    Never try to answer directly — always delegate.''',\n    handoffs=[billing_agent, tech_agent, general_agent]\n)\n```\n\n**How It Works — Step by Step:**\n1. User sends a message to the Supervisor\n2. Supervisor analyzes the message and decides which worker to call\n3. Worker executes the task using its specialized tools\n4. Worker returns the result to the Supervisor\n5. Supervisor sends the final response to the user (or delegates to another worker)\n\n**The Three Flavors of Supervisor:**\n\n**Flavor 1: Router** (simplest)\nThe supervisor just routes — it doesn't process the result.\n```\nUser → Supervisor → Worker A → User\n```\n\n**Flavor 2: Orchestrator** (most common)\nThe supervisor routes, receives results, and may combine or process them.\n```\nUser → Supervisor → Worker A → Supervisor → Worker B → Supervisor → User\n```\n\n**Flavor 3: Hierarchical** (most complex)\nSupervisors manage sub-supervisors who manage workers.\n```\nCEO Supervisor → Department Supervisors → Worker Agents\n```\n\n**Supervisor Design Best Practices:**\n\n| Practice | Why |\n|---|---|\n| Keep the supervisor prompt focused on routing | It should classify, not process |\n| Give clear criteria for each worker | \"Send billing questions to billing_agent\" |\n| Define a fallback worker | \"If uncertain, send to general_agent\" |\n| Log every routing decision | Essential for debugging |\n| Add confidence thresholds | \"If <70% confident about routing, ask the user to clarify\" |\n\n**Common Supervisor Mistakes:**\n❌ Supervisor tries to answer questions itself (it should only route)\n❌ Unclear routing criteria (agents overlap in responsibility)\n❌ No fallback for ambiguous requests\n❌ Missing logging (impossible to debug routing errors)\n\n**Why This Pattern Dominates Production:**\nAt companies like Intercom, Zendesk, and Salesforce, the Supervisor pattern handles millions of customer interactions daily. It scales beautifully: need a new capability? Just add a new worker agent and update the supervisor's routing rules. No need to rewrite anything.\n\nNext, let's explore a very different pattern — what happens when you need two agents to argue with each other to find the truth."
          }
