          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Four Agentic Design Patterns",
            "explanation": "In early 2024, Andrew Ng — one of the most influential figures in AI and co-founder of Google Brain — gave a talk that changed how the industry thinks about AI agents. He identified four foundational design patterns that every AI engineer must know. These aren't theoretical — they're the building blocks used by every production agent system in the world.\n\n**Think of these patterns as the four elements of agent engineering:**\n\n**🪞 Pattern 1: Reflection**\nThe agent generates output, then critically reviews its own work.\n→ \"Write code, then check if it compiles, then fix bugs\"\n→ Like a writer who re-reads their own draft with fresh eyes\n\n**🔧 Pattern 2: Tool Use**\nThe agent calls external tools to interact with the real world.\n→ \"Search the web, run code, query databases\"\n→ Like giving the agent hands, eyes, and ears\n\n**📋 Pattern 3: Planning**\nThe agent creates a step-by-step plan before executing.\n→ \"Break this research into 5 steps, then execute each\"\n→ Like a project manager creating a Gantt chart before the team starts building\n\n**👥 Pattern 4: Multi-Agent**\nMultiple specialized agents collaborate on a task.\n→ \"Researcher finds data, Writer creates report, Editor polishes\"\n→ Like a company with departments instead of one person doing everything\n\n**The Power Matrix — When to Use Each:**\n\n| Pattern | Best For | Typical Improvement | Cost Multiplier |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Reflection | Code generation, writing, data extraction | +15-30% quality | 2-3x |\n| Tool Use | Real-world data, computation, external APIs | Enables new capabilities | 1.5-2x |\n| Planning | Complex multi-step tasks, research | +40-60% task completion | 1.2-1.5x |\n| Multi-Agent | Domain-specific expertise, scaling | +25-40% on complex tasks | 2-5x |\n\n**How They Combine — The Real Magic:**\nMost production systems use ALL FOUR simultaneously:\n• A **Planner** decomposes the goal (Planning)\n• A **Supervisor** delegates to Workers (Multi-Agent)\n• Each Worker calls **Tools** (Tool Use)\n• Each Worker **Reflects** on its output before returning (Reflection)\n\n```\nUser Goal → Planner (Planning)\n  → Supervisor (Multi-Agent)\n    → Worker A: uses tools (Tool Use) → reflects (Reflection) → returns\n    → Worker B: uses tools (Tool Use) → reflects (Reflection) → returns\n  → Supervisor combines results → Final Answer\n```\n\n**The Key Insight:**\nThese patterns aren't mutually exclusive — they're **composable building blocks**. The best agents layer them together: plan → delegate → execute with tools → reflect → iterate. Understanding these four patterns gives you a mental framework to analyze and design ANY agent system.\n\nWe've already covered each pattern in depth in previous phases. In the next few stages, we'll see how each one works as a standalone design pattern, with practical guidelines for when to apply each one."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Reflection Pattern: Self-Improvement Loop",
            "explanation": "Let me tell you why Reflection is the highest-ROI pattern you'll ever implement.\n\n**The Code Review Analogy:**\n\nImagine a developer who writes code and immediately ships it to production without ever reviewing it. No tests, no self-review, no nothing. Terrifying, right? Now imagine they write code, then spend 5 minutes re-reading it as if they were a code reviewer seeing it for the first time. They catch bugs, improve variable names, add edge case handling. Same developer, dramatically better code — just from taking a second look.\n\n**That's Reflection.** The agent generates something, then asks itself: \"Is this good? How can I improve it?\" It's the simplest pattern but often the most impactful.\n\n**The Reflection Loop:**\n```python\ndef reflect_and_improve(task, max_rounds=3):\n    # Generate initial output\n    output = generate(task)\n    \n    for round in range(max_rounds):\n        # Critique the output (be specific!)\n        feedback = critique(output)\n        # → 'The code has a bug on line 5: undefined variable \"result\"'\n        \n        # Check if it's good enough\n        if 'no issues found' in feedback.lower():\n            break\n        \n        # Improve based on feedback\n        output = improve(output, feedback)\n    \n    return output\n```\n\n**Real-World Impact — Numbers That Matter:**\n\n| Task | Without Reflection | With Reflection (2 rounds) | Improvement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Code generation | 65% pass rate | 85% pass rate | +31% |\n| Essay writing | 3.2/5 human rating | 4.1/5 human rating | +28% |\n| Data extraction | 72% accuracy | 91% accuracy | +26% |\n| SQL generation | 70% correct queries | 88% correct queries | +26% |\n\nThese numbers come from real benchmarks. Two extra LLM calls can improve quality by 25-30%.\n\n**When Reflection Works Best:**\n✅ Code generation (does it compile? pass tests? handle edge cases?)\n✅ Writing (is the tone consistent? are there grammar errors? is the structure logical?)\n✅ Data extraction (does the JSON match the expected schema? are all fields filled?)\n✅ SQL queries (does the query return the right results? is it optimized?)\n\n**When Reflection Doesn't Help:**\n❌ Subjective creative tasks (no clear \"correct\" answer to check against)\n❌ Tasks where the LLM can't verify its own output (it can't check live data)\n❌ Simple one-shot tasks where the first answer is almost always right (reflection adds unnecessary latency)\n\n**Pro Tips That Will Save You Money:**\n\n1. **Use a cheaper model for the critic.** GPT-4o-mini is a fantastic critic — fast, cheap, and surprisingly good at catching bugs. Use GPT-4o for generation, GPT-4o-mini for critique.\n\n2. **Be specific in the critique prompt.** Don't say \"review this code.\" Say \"check this code for: (1) syntax errors, (2) undefined variables, (3) missing edge cases, (4) security vulnerabilities.\"\n\n3. **Set a max rounds limit.** Most improvements happen in rounds 1-2. By round 3, you're getting diminishing returns. Don't waste money on rounds 4+.\n\n4. **Use concrete verification when possible.** Instead of asking the LLM \"is this code correct?\", actually RUN the code and check if it passes tests. Concrete feedback beats LLM opinions every time.\n\nNow let's look at the Tool Use pattern — the one that gives agents their superpowers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
