          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Agent Safety — What Can Go Wrong Will Go Wrong",
            "explanation": "I need you to really listen to this one, because what I'm about to tell you could save your company from a career-ending disaster.\n\nAgents are powerful. Agents are also **dangerous**. An unsupervised agent can:\n• 💸 Get stuck in an infinite loop, burning **$500 in API costs** in 3 hours\n• 💀 Execute destructive database queries (`DELETE FROM users WHERE 1=1`)\n• 🔓 Leak sensitive customer data (PII, credit card numbers) in its responses\n• 📧 Send embarrassing or legally problematic emails to your CEO, clients, or regulators\n• 🔥 Deploy broken code to production, taking down your entire service\n\n**Agent Safety is NOT Optional.** It's the difference between a demo that impresses your boss and a production system that doesn't get you fired.\n\n**The Horror Stories — All Real, All Preventable:**\n\n**Story 1: The $200 Research Agent**\nA developer built a research agent and left it running overnight. The agent got stuck in a loop — searching, reading, searching, reading — making 2,000+ API calls over 3 hours. It cost $200 and produced a rambling, incoherent report. The fix? A simple `max_iterations=25` would have stopped it in 2 minutes.\n\n**Story 2: The Database Destroyer**\nA coding agent was given SQL execution privileges for \"data analysis.\" During a routine query optimization task, it decided to \"clean up\" the database and ran `DROP TABLE users_backup`. The table contained the only backup of 50,000 user records. The fix? A tool whitelist that only allowed `SELECT` statements.\n\n**Story 3: The Generous Customer Support Bot**\nA customer service agent was trained to \"resolve customer complaints.\" A clever customer complained about a $50 charge, and the agent — trying to be helpful — promised a $500 refund, a free year of service, and a personal apology from the CEO. All of this in writing, creating a legal obligation. The fix? Human-in-the-loop for any action involving money or commitments.\n\n**Story 4: The Data Leaker**\nA medical AI agent was asked to summarize a patient's history. It included the patient's full SSN, home address, and insurance details in a response that was emailed to a research team. HIPAA violation. The fix? Output guardrails that scan for PII before returning responses.\n\n**Every one of these was preventable with proper guardrails.** In the next stages, you'll learn the five guardrails every agent needs, plus human-in-the-loop patterns for high-stakes actions.\n\nLet me be honest with you: if you skip this phase, you WILL experience one of these horror stories. The question isn't if, it's when. So let's make sure you're protected."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Five Guardrails Every Agent Needs",
            "explanation": "Think of guardrails like the safety systems in a nuclear power plant. Each one independently prevents a different type of disaster. You don't pick one — you layer ALL of them, because when one fails, the others catch it.\n\n**Here are the five guardrails, from simplest to most sophisticated:**\n\n**Guardrail 1: Iteration Limit** 🔢\nThe simplest and most important. Set a hard maximum on loop iterations.\n```python\nMAX_ITERATIONS = 25  # Never while True in production!\n\nfor step in range(MAX_ITERATIONS):\n    result = agent.step()\n    if result.is_final:\n        break\nelse:\n    # Hit the limit — return best results so far\n    return \"Reached maximum iterations. Here's what I found so far...\"\n```\n**Why 25?** Most legitimate tasks complete in 5-15 iterations. If you're past 25, the agent is almost certainly stuck in a loop.\n\n**Guardrail 2: Token/Cost Budget** 💰\nTrack total tokens consumed. Stop when the budget is exceeded.\n```python\ntotal_tokens = 0\nMAX_TOKENS = 100_000  # ~$0.30 for GPT-4o\n\nfor step in range(MAX_ITERATIONS):\n    result = agent.step()\n    total_tokens += result.usage.total_tokens\n    \n    if total_tokens > MAX_TOKENS:\n        return f'Budget exceeded ({total_tokens} tokens). Returning best results.'\n```\n**Pro tip:** Set alerts at 50% and 80% of budget. This gives the agent a chance to wrap up cleanly instead of being cut off mid-sentence.\n\n**Guardrail 3: Timeout** ⏱️\nSet a wall-clock time limit. This catches cases where the tools themselves are slow (network timeouts, large file processing).\n```python\nimport time\nstart_time = time.time()\nMAX_SECONDS = 300  # 5 minutes\n\nfor step in range(MAX_ITERATIONS):\n    if time.time() - start_time > MAX_SECONDS:\n        return 'Timeout reached. Returning partial results.'\n    result = agent.step()\n```\n\n**Guardrail 4: Tool Whitelist** ✅\nOnly allow pre-approved tools. Reject ANYTHING not on the list.\n```python\nALLOWED_TOOLS = {'web_search', 'read_file', 'calculate'}\n\ndef execute_tool(tool_name, args):\n    if tool_name not in ALLOWED_TOOLS:\n        raise SecurityError(f\"Tool '{tool_name}' is not allowed. Available: {ALLOWED_TOOLS}\")\n    return tools[tool_name](**args)\n```\n**Critical for SQL agents:** Whitelist only `SELECT` statements. NEVER allow `DELETE`, `DROP`, `UPDATE`, or `INSERT` without explicit human approval.\n\n**Guardrail 5: Output Scanning** 🛡️\nScan agent outputs before returning them to the user:\n```python\nimport re\n\ndef scan_output(text):\n    # Check for PII\n    if re.search(r'\\b\\d{3}-\\d{2}-\\d{4}\\b', text):  # SSN pattern\n        raise SecurityError('Output contains potential SSN')\n    if re.search(r'\\b\\d{16}\\b', text):  # Credit card pattern\n        raise SecurityError('Output contains potential credit card number')\n    \n    # Check for dangerous content\n    if any(phrase in text.lower() for phrase in ['drop table', 'delete from', 'rm -rf']):\n        raise SecurityError('Output contains potentially dangerous commands')\n    \n    return text  # Safe to return\n```\n\n**The Minimum Viable Safety Stack:**\n\n| Risk Level | Required Guardrails |\n|---|---|\n| Demo / Internal | Iteration limit + Cost budget |\n| Production (low risk) | All of the above + Timeout + Tool whitelist |\n| Production (high risk) | All of the above + Output scanning + Human-in-the-loop |\n| Financial / Medical / Legal | ALL guardrails + Full audit logging + Human approval for every action |\n\n**Rule of Thumb:** Every production agent should have AT LEAST guardrails 1-4. Add 5 for any application handling sensitive data.\n\nNow let's talk about the ultimate safety net — when you need a human to approve an action before it executes."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Implementing Agent Guardrails",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Add three guardrails to the agent loop:\n1. Maximum iterations (10)\n2. Token budget (max 5000 tokens)\n3. Tool whitelist (only 'search' and 'calculate' allowed)",
            "starterCode": "ALLOWED_TOOLS = {'search', 'calculate'}\nMAX_ITERATIONS = 10\nMAX_TOKENS = 5000\n\ndef agent_with_guardrails(query):\n    messages = [{'role': 'user', 'content': query}]\n    total_tokens = 0\n    \n    for step in range(___):\n        response = llm_call(messages)  # Returns {'type', 'tool', 'tokens_used'}\n        total_tokens += response.get('tokens_used', 100)\n        \n        # Guardrail: Token budget\n        if total_tokens > ___:\n            return 'Budget exceeded. Returning partial results.'\n        \n        # Guardrail: Tool whitelist\n        if response.get('tool') and response['tool'] not in ___:\n            return f'Blocked: tool {response[\"tool\"]} not allowed'\n        \n        if response['type'] == 'final_answer':\n            return response['content']\n    \n    return 'Max iterations reached. Returning best results.'",
            "solution": "ALLOWED_TOOLS = {'search', 'calculate'}\nMAX_ITERATIONS = 10\nMAX_TOKENS = 5000\n\ndef agent_with_guardrails(query):\n    messages = [{'role': 'user', 'content': query}]\n    total_tokens = 0\n    \n    for step in range(MAX_ITERATIONS):\n        response = llm_call(messages)\n        total_tokens += response.get('tokens_used', 100)\n        \n        if total_tokens > MAX_TOKENS:\n            return 'Budget exceeded. Returning partial results.'\n        \n        if response.get('tool') and response['tool'] not in ALLOWED_TOOLS:\n            return f'Blocked: tool {response[\"tool\"]} not allowed'\n        \n        if response['type'] == 'final_answer':\n            return response['content']\n    \n    return 'Max iterations reached. Returning best results.'",
            "hint": "Use MAX_ITERATIONS for the loop range, MAX_TOKENS for the budget check, and ALLOWED_TOOLS for the whitelist.",
            "explanation": "These three guardrails form the minimum safety layer for any agent: iteration limits prevent infinite loops, token budgets prevent cost overruns, and tool whitelists prevent unauthorized actions.",
            "concepts": [
              "Guardrails",
              "Safety",
              "Rate Limiting"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "MAX_ITERATIONS.*MAX_TOKENS.*ALLOWED_TOOLS"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Human-in-the-Loop: The Ultimate Safety Net",
            "explanation": "For high-stakes actions, no amount of automated guardrails is enough. You need a **human** to approve the action before it executes. This isn't a sign of weakness in your agent — it's a sign of mature engineering.\n\n**Real-World HITL Examples From Industry:**\n\n| Company | Agent Action | HITL Trigger |\n|---|---|---|\n| **GitHub Copilot** | Suggests code changes | Developer reviews and accepts/rejects each suggestion |\n| **Salesforce Einstein** | Proposes email to customer | Sales rep reviews before sending |\n| **Stripe** | Processes refund > $100 | Manager approval required |\n| **Tesla Autopilot** | Changes lanes | Driver must confirm (hands on wheel) |\n| **Medical AI** | Suggests diagnosis | Doctor reviews and signs off |\n\nIn every case, the AI does the heavy lifting, but a human makes the final call on consequential actions.\n\n**The HITL Pattern:**\n```python\nDANGEROUS_ACTIONS = {\n    'send_email',      # Can't unsend\n    'delete_record',   # Data loss\n    'make_payment',    # Financial impact\n    'deploy_code',     # Production impact\n    'update_customer', # Legal/compliance\n}\n\nasync def execute_with_approval(tool_name, args):\n    if tool_name in DANGEROUS_ACTIONS:\n        # Pause and present the proposed action to the human\n        print(f'⚠️  Agent wants to execute: {tool_name}')\n        print(f'   Arguments: {json.dumps(args, indent=2)}')\n        print(f'   This action cannot be easily undone.')\n        \n        approval = input('Approve? (yes/no/modify): ')\n        \n        if approval.lower() == 'no':\n            return 'Action rejected by human reviewer.'\n        elif approval.lower() == 'modify':\n            args = get_modified_args()  # Let human edit the arguments\n    \n    # Execute the tool (either auto-approved or human-approved)\n    return execute_tool(tool_name, args)\n```\n\n**LangGraph's Built-In HITL — The Best Implementation:**\nLangGraph has native support for human-in-the-loop with the `interrupt()` function:\n```python\nfrom langgraph.types import interrupt\n\ndef send_email_node(state):\n    email_draft = state['draft']\n    \n    # Pause the graph and ask the human\n    approval = interrupt({\n        'question': 'Should I send this email?',\n        'draft': email_draft,\n        'to': state['recipient']\n    })\n    \n    if approval == 'yes':\n        send_email(email_draft)\n        return {'status': 'sent'}\n    else:\n        return {'status': 'cancelled by human'}\n```\n\nThe graph literally pauses execution, serializes its state, and waits for the human to respond. When they do, it resumes exactly where it left off.\n\n**The HITL Spectrum — Not All Actions Need the Same Level of Approval:**\n\n| Risk Level | Approval Type | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Low | Auto-approve (log only) | Web search, read file |\n| Medium | Notify (approve within 5 min or auto-proceed) | Create draft, update note |\n| High | Require approval (block until human responds) | Send email, process payment |\n| Critical | Require approval + 2nd reviewer | Delete data, deploy to production |\n\n**When to Use HITL:**\n✅ Sending emails or messages to customers\n✅ Modifying database records\n✅ Making financial transactions\n✅ Deploying code to production\n✅ Any action that can't be easily undone\n✅ Any action with legal or compliance implications\n\n**The Productivity Equation:**\nHITL doesn't slow agents down — it makes them trustworthy. An agent that processes 100 requests/hour with 5% errors (5 disasters/hour) is WORSE than an agent that processes 80 requests/hour with 0.1% errors (human catches the rest). Trust = adoption = value.\n\nNow let's learn how to diagnose and fix agent failures when they DO happen — because even with guardrails, things will occasionally go wrong."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Human-in-the-Loop Agent",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Add human approval to the agent's tool execution. If the tool is in REQUIRES_APPROVAL, print the proposed action and check `get_approval()` before executing.",
            "starterCode": "REQUIRES_APPROVAL = {'send_email', 'delete_record'}\n\ndef get_approval(tool_name, args):\n    # In production, this would be a UI prompt\n    print(f'Agent wants to: {tool_name}({args})')\n    return True  # Auto-approve for demo\n\ndef execute_tool(name, args):\n    return f'Executed {name} with {args}'\n\ndef safe_execute(tool_name, args):\n    # Check if approval is needed\n    if tool_name in ___:\n        approved = ___(tool_name, args)\n        if not approved:\n            return '___'\n    \n    return execute_tool(tool_name, args)\n\nprint(safe_execute('search', {'query': 'AI agents'}))\nprint(safe_execute('send_email', {'to': 'boss@company.com'}))",
            "solution": "REQUIRES_APPROVAL = {'send_email', 'delete_record'}\n\ndef get_approval(tool_name, args):\n    print(f'Agent wants to: {tool_name}({args})')\n    return True\n\ndef execute_tool(name, args):\n    return f'Executed {name} with {args}'\n\ndef safe_execute(tool_name, args):\n    if tool_name in REQUIRES_APPROVAL:\n        approved = get_approval(tool_name, args)\n        if not approved:\n            return 'Action rejected by human reviewer.'\n    \n    return execute_tool(tool_name, args)\n\nprint(safe_execute('search', {'query': 'AI agents'}))\nprint(safe_execute('send_email', {'to': 'boss@company.com'}))",
            "hint": "Check if tool_name is in REQUIRES_APPROVAL. Call get_approval() and return a rejection message if not approved.",
            "explanation": "Human-in-the-loop is the ultimate safety mechanism. Safe tools (like search) execute immediately, while dangerous tools (like send_email) require human approval first.",
