          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project — Research Agent Architecture",
            "explanation": "Let's design a real-world **Research Agent** — the kind used by tools like GPT-Researcher and Perplexity. This project combines everything you've learned: ReAct, tool calling, planning, multi-agent, memory, safety, and observability.\n\n**What It Does:**\nTakes a broad topic, autonomously researches it across multiple sources, and produces a comprehensive report with citations. Think of it as your own personal research assistant that can read the entire internet.\n\n**Architecture Overview — 5 Sub-Agents Working Together:**\n\n```\nUser: 'Impact of AI on healthcare in 2024'\n    ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│  1. Planner Agent (GPT-4o)      │  ← Planning pattern\n│  Generates 5 research questions │\n└──────────────┬──────────────────┘\n               ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│  2. Searcher Agent (×5, parallel)│  ← Tool Use + Parallel execution\n│  Tools: web_search, news_search │\n│  Searches web for each question │\n└──────────────┬──────────────────┘\n               ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│  3. Reader Agent                │  ← Tool Use\n│  Tools: web_scraper, pdf_reader │\n│  Reads full articles from URLs  │\n│  Extracts key findings          │\n└──────────────┬──────────────────┘\n               ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│  4. Synthesizer Agent           │  ← Reflection pattern\n│  Combines all findings          │\n│  Adds proper citations          │\n│  Self-reviews for coherence     │\n└──────────────┬──────────────────┘\n               ↓\n┌─────────────────────────────────┐\n│  5. Reviewer Agent              │  ← Reflection + HITL\n│  Checks for hallucinations      │\n│  Verifies all citations exist   │\n│  Returns final report or revise │\n└─────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n**Tools Needed:**\n• 🔍 **Web Search API** — Tavily ($5/month), Serper, or Brave Search\n• 📄 **Web Scraper** — BeautifulSoup or Playwright for reading full articles\n• 📕 **PDF Reader** — PyPDF2 for academic papers\n• 📝 **Note-taking** — Vector store for storing and retrieving findings\n\n**Key Design Decisions:**\n1. **Parallel search** — Run all 5 searches simultaneously (saves 60% time)\n2. **Dedup at the reader level** — Don't read the same article twice\n3. **Citation tracking** — Every finding is linked to its source URL\n4. **Cost budgeting** — Set a max of $0.50 per report\n\n**Guardrails:**\n• Max 25 searches per report (cost control)\n• Max 10 articles read (prevents runaway reading)\n• 5-minute timeout for the entire pipeline\n• Output scanning to prevent hallucinated citations\n\n**Estimated Cost Per Report:**\n| Component | API Calls | Estimated Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Planner (GPT-4o) | 1 | $0.03 |\n| Searcher (GPT-4o-mini) | 5 | $0.02 |\n| Reader (GPT-4o-mini) | 10 | $0.05 |\n| Synthesizer (GPT-4o) | 2 | $0.06 |\n| Reviewer (GPT-4o) | 1 | $0.03 |\n| Search API calls | 5 | $0.05 |\n| **Total** | | **~$0.24** |\n\nThat's a comprehensive, cited research report for under $0.25. Try getting that from a human research assistant!\n\nNow let's build a very different kind of agent — one that works with real-time financial data."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Research Agent",
            "difficulty": "Hard",
            "question": "Build a simplified research agent that:\n1. Takes a topic and generates research questions using `generate_questions(topic)`\n2. Searches for each question using `search(query)`\n3. Synthesizes findings into a report using `synthesize(findings)`",
            "starterCode": "def generate_questions(topic):\n    return [\n        f'What is {topic}?',\n        f'What are the latest trends in {topic}?',\n        f'What are the challenges of {topic}?'\n    ]\n\ndef search(query):\n    return f'Found 3 articles about: {query}'\n\ndef synthesize(findings):\n    return 'Research Report:\\n' + '\\n'.join(f'- {f}' for f in findings)\n\ndef research_agent(topic):\n    print(f'Researching: {topic}')\n    \n    # Step 1: Generate research questions\n    questions = ___\n    print(f'Questions: {questions}')\n    \n    # Step 2: Search for each question\n    findings = []\n    for q in questions:\n        result = ___\n        findings.append(result)\n    \n    # Step 3: Synthesize\n    report = ___\n    return report\n\nprint(research_agent('AI agents in healthcare'))",
            "solution": "def generate_questions(topic):\n    return [\n        f'What is {topic}?',\n        f'What are the latest trends in {topic}?',\n        f'What are the challenges of {topic}?'\n    ]\n\ndef search(query):\n    return f'Found 3 articles about: {query}'\n\ndef synthesize(findings):\n    return 'Research Report:\\n' + '\\n'.join(f'- {f}' for f in findings)\n\ndef research_agent(topic):\n    print(f'Researching: {topic}')\n    \n    questions = generate_questions(topic)\n    print(f'Questions: {questions}')\n    \n    findings = []\n    for q in questions:\n        result = search(q)\n        findings.append(result)\n    \n    report = synthesize(findings)\n    return report\n\nprint(research_agent('AI agents in healthcare'))",
            "hint": "Call generate_questions(topic), search(q) for each question, and synthesize(findings) at the end.",
            "explanation": "This is the core flow of any research agent: decompose → search → synthesize. Production versions add parallel search, web scraping, citation verification, and iterative refinement.",
            "concepts": [
              "Research Agent",
              "Task Decomposition",
              "Information Synthesis"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "generate_questions.*search.*findings.*synthesize"
            }
          }
