          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Capstone Project: Build an Autonomous Task Manager Agent",
            "explanation": "**Your Capstone Mission:**\n\nBuild an autonomous AI agent that manages a to-do list by understanding natural language commands, breaking down complex tasks, prioritizing work, and providing status updates — all using the agent architectures you learned in this module.\n\n**What You Will Build:**\nA \"Smart Task Manager Agent\" that goes beyond simple CRUD operations. It understands context, suggests task breakdowns, identifies dependencies, and proactively reminds you about deadlines.\n\n**Why This Is the Perfect Capstone:**\nThis project touches EVERY concept from the module:\n• **ReAct Loop** → The core agent cycle for understanding and acting on commands\n• **Tool Calling** → Calendar API, priority scoring, dependency resolution\n• **Planning** → Breaking complex tasks into subtasks\n• **Multi-Agent** → Parser + Planner + Reminder sub-agents\n• **Memory** → Remembering tasks, history, and user preferences\n• **Safety** → Never deleting tasks without confirmation\n• **Evaluation** → Testing that commands are understood correctly\n\n**Project Requirements — Build All Five:**\n\n**1. ReAct Loop Implementation** — Your agent must use the Perceive → Think → Act cycle:\n   - **Perceive**: Parse natural language commands (\"Add buy groceries for the party on Saturday\")\n   - **Think**: Determine intent, extract entities (task, deadline, priority)\n   - **Act**: Execute the appropriate action (add, complete, reschedule, etc.)\n\n**2. Multi-Agent Architecture** — Implement at least 2 specialized sub-agents:\n   - **Parser Agent**: Extracts structured data from natural language\n   - **Planner Agent**: Breaks complex tasks into subtasks and identifies dependencies\n   - **Reminder Agent** (optional): Monitors deadlines and sends proactive alerts\n\n**3. Tool Usage** — Connect these tools:\n   - Calendar API (or mock) for scheduling\n   - Priority scoring algorithm\n   - Task dependency resolver\n\n**4. Memory System** — Maintain:\n   - Short-term memory (current conversation context)\n   - Long-term memory (all tasks, history of completions)\n   - Episodic memory (past similar tasks for suggestions)\n\n**5. Self-Correction** — When the agent misunderstands a command:\n   - Detect confusion and ask clarifying questions\n   - Learn from corrections\n\n**Example Interactions:**\n```\nUser: \"I need to prepare for the product launch next Friday\"\nAgent: \"I'll break this down for you:\n  1. Finalize product demo (by Wednesday) - HIGH priority\n  2. Prepare presentation slides (by Thursday) - HIGH priority  \n  3. Send team reminder email (by Thursday) - MEDIUM priority\n  4. Set up launch day checklist (by Friday morning) - HIGH priority\n  Shall I add all of these to your task list?\"\n\nUser: \"Yes, and mark the demo as dependent on getting API access\"\nAgent: \"Done! I've added a dependency: 'Finalize product demo' is blocked by \n  'Get API access'. I notice 'Get API access' isn't in your tasks yet. \n  Should I add it as an urgent item?\"\n```\n\n**Stretch Goals:**\n- Add a Streamlit UI with a chat interface\n- Implement natural language date parsing (\"next Tuesday\", \"end of month\")\n- Add team task assignment with multi-user support\n- Implement the Reflection pattern: agent reviews its own task breakdowns for quality\n\n**Evaluation Criteria:**\nYour agent should handle at least these 10 test commands correctly:\n1. \"Add a task to buy milk\" (simple add)\n2. \"What's on my list?\" (query)\n3. \"Mark buy milk as done\" (update)\n4. \"I need to plan a birthday party next Saturday\" (complex decomposition)\n5. \"What's overdue?\" (time-aware query)\n6. \"Reschedule the presentation to Monday\" (date modification)\n7. \"Delete the milk task\" (delete with confirmation)\n8. \"What did I complete this week?\" (episodic memory)\n9. \"Prioritize my tasks\" (sorting/analysis)\n10. \"Buy eggs\" with later correction \"I meant bread, not eggs\" (self-correction)\n\nThis capstone combines ReAct, multi-agent systems, tool use, memory, and self-correction — everything from this module. Build it, and you'll have a portfolio project that demonstrates genuine AI engineering expertise.\n\n**Congratulations on completing Module 12!** 🎉 You now understand how to build, evaluate, and deploy production-grade AI agents. The future of AI is agentic — and you're ready to build it."
          }
        }
      ]
