      "id": "M013-mcp",
      "title": "Module 13: MCP (Model Context Protocol)",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is the Model Context Protocol (MCP)?",
            "explanation": "Imagine you just bought a brand-new smartphone. It's brilliant — fast processor, gorgeous screen, amazing camera. But there's a catch: it can only talk to ONE app. Want to check email? You need a special cable. Want to browse the web? Different cable. Want to play music? Yet ANOTHER cable. Every single service requires its own proprietary connector, its own protocol, its own integration. You'd need 50 different cables hanging off your phone.\n\nSounds absurd, right? But that's EXACTLY how AI tools work today without MCP.\n\n**The Problem MCP Solves:**\n\nRight now, if you want your AI agent to connect to GitHub, Slack, a database, and a file system, you write FOUR separate custom integrations. Each one has its own authentication, its own request format, its own error handling. And if another team wants to connect their AI app to the same GitHub? They write their OWN integration from scratch. It's copy-paste engineering at its worst.\n\nMCP is the **USB-C for AI tools**. Just like USB-C provides one universal port that works with any device (chargers, monitors, drives, phones), MCP provides one universal protocol that connects any AI application to any data source or tool.\n\n**The Technical Definition:**\nMCP (Model Context Protocol) is an open standard created by **Anthropic** in late 2024 that defines how AI applications (called **Hosts**) connect to external tools and data sources (called **Servers**) through a standardized JSON-RPC protocol. Build a tool server once, and ANY MCP-compatible AI app can discover and use it automatically.\n\n**Why This Changes Everything — A Real Story:**\n\nBefore MCP, the team at Cursor (the AI code editor) had to write custom integrations for every tool — GitHub, Jira, Slack, databases. Each integration took 2-3 weeks. After adopting MCP, they plug into ANY MCP server in minutes. The community builds the servers; Cursor just connects to them.\n\n**Who's Using MCP Today:**\n- **Claude Desktop** — Anthropic's flagship product, MCP-native\n- **Cursor** — The AI code editor, uses MCP for tool integrations\n- **VS Code (Copilot)** — GitHub's AI assistant, now supports MCP\n- **Windsurf** — AI coding tool, MCP-compatible\n- **Zed Editor** — Next-gen code editor with MCP support\n- **Sourcegraph Cody** — AI coding assistant, MCP-powered\n\n**The Three MCP Primitives — Everything You Can Expose:**\n\n| Primitive | What It Does | Analogy |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tools** | Functions the AI can CALL | The buttons on a remote control |\n| **Resources** | Read-only data the AI can ACCESS | The channels the TV can show |\n| **Prompts** | Reusable templates for the AI | Pre-programmed macros on the remote |\n\n**When to Use MCP:**\n\\u2705 You want to connect AI apps to external tools/data with a standard protocol\n\\u2705 You want to build a tool ONCE and have it work with Claude, Cursor, VS Code, etc.\n\\u2705 You're building an enterprise system where multiple AI apps need the same integrations\n\\u2705 You want dynamic tool discovery (tools are found at runtime, not hardcoded)\n\n**When MCP is NOT the Right Choice:**\n\\u274c Simple one-off scripts that only need one API call\n\\u274c Real-time streaming of binary data (video, audio)\n\\u274c You only have one AI app and one tool (direct integration is simpler)\n\nIn this module, you'll go from understanding the protocol to building production-ready MCP servers and clients with real Python code. Let's start by understanding how MCP compares to the traditional way of connecting AI to tools."
          }
        },
        {
