          "type": "flashcard",
          "cards": [
            {
              "front": "What are the three core primitives that an MCP server exposes?",
              "back": "1. Tools — callable functions the AI can invoke (e.g., search_database)\n2. Resources — read-only data the AI can access (e.g., file contents)\n3. Prompts — reusable prompt templates with arguments"
            },
            {
              "front": "What does MCP stand for?",
              "back": "Model Context Protocol — an open standard by Anthropic that defines a universal way for AI applications to connect to external tools and data sources."
            },
            {
              "front": "What is the 'USB-C analogy' for MCP?",
              "back": "Just as USB-C standardized the physical connector so any cable works with any device, MCP standardizes the protocol so any MCP client (AI app) works with any MCP server (tool wrapper)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP vs Traditional APIs",
            "explanation": "Here's a question that every AI engineer needs to answer: \"Should I build a custom tool integration, or should I use MCP?\" Let me help you make the right call — because choosing wrong will cost you weeks of unnecessary work.\n\n**The Power Outlet Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're an electrician. A client asks you to wire their house. You could design a custom plug shape for every single appliance — one shape for the fridge, another for the TV, another for the toaster. It would work... but it's insane. Instead, you use a **standard outlet**. Any appliance, any manufacturer, one plug shape.\n\nTraditional AI tool integration = custom plug per appliance.\nMCP = standard outlet.\n\n**Traditional REST API Integration — The Old Way:**\nWhen you connect an AI agent to a REST API, you write a custom LangChain Tool that hardcodes the endpoint, authentication, request format, and response parsing:\n\n```python\n# Traditional approach: Custom tool per API, per app\nimport requests\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef get_stock_price(ticker: str) -> str:\n    '''Get the current stock price.'''\n    headers = {'Authorization': 'Bearer sk-xxx'}\n    response = requests.get(\n        f'https://api.stocks.com/v1/price/{ticker}',\n        headers=headers\n    )\n    data = response.json()\n    return f'{ticker}: {data[\"price\"]}'\n```\n\nThis tool is **permanently welded** to this specific API AND this specific app. If you want another AI app (say, Claude Desktop) to use the same stock API, you write the entire integration from scratch.\n\n**MCP Approach — The New Way:**\nWith MCP, you wrap the API in a server ONCE. Any MCP client discovers and uses it automatically:\n\n```python\n# MCP approach: Build the server once, use everywhere\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport requests\n\nmcp = FastMCP('StockServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_stock_price(ticker: str) -> str:\n    '''Get the current stock price for a ticker symbol.\n    Use when the user asks about stock prices, market data,\n    or financial quotes. Returns the current price in USD.'''\n    headers = {'Authorization': 'Bearer sk-xxx'}\n    response = requests.get(\n        f'https://api.stocks.com/v1/price/{ticker}',\n        headers=headers\n    )\n    data = response.json()\n    return f'{ticker}: {data[\"price\"]}'\n```\n\nThe code is nearly identical — but now ANY MCP-compatible client (Claude Desktop, Cursor, your custom agent) can discover and call `get_stock_price` without writing any integration code.\n\n**Side-by-Side Comparison:**\n\n| Feature | Traditional API Tool | MCP Server |\n|---|---|---|\n| Discovery | Hardcoded in agent code | Dynamic via capability negotiation |\n| Reusability | One app only | Any MCP-compatible client |\n| Protocol | Custom HTTP per API | Standardized JSON-RPC |\n| Schema | Manual documentation | Auto-generated from type hints |\n| Adding to new app | Rewrite integration | Just connect to the server |\n| Tool listing | Manually maintained | `tools/list` auto-returns all tools |\n| Error format | Ad hoc | Standardized JSON-RPC errors |\n\n**The Economics Are Compelling:**\n\n| Scenario | Traditional | MCP |\n|---|---|---|\n| Connect 1 tool to 1 app | 4 hours | 4 hours (same) |\n| Connect 1 tool to 5 apps | 20 hours (4h × 5) | 4 hours (build once) |\n| Connect 10 tools to 5 apps | 200 hours | 40 hours |\n| Community contributes tools | Not possible | \\u2705 Hundreds of pre-built servers |\n\n**The Key Insight — Memorize This:**\nMCP doesn't replace REST APIs — it **wraps** them. Your MCP server still calls REST APIs, databases, or any other backend service under the hood. MCP just standardizes HOW AI clients discover and invoke those capabilities. It's an abstraction layer, not a replacement.\n\n**When to Use MCP vs. Direct Integration:**\n```python\ndef choose_approach(scenario):\n    if scenario.num_apps == 1 and scenario.num_tools == 1:\n        return 'Direct integration'  # MCP overhead not worth it\n    elif scenario.tools_shared_across_apps:\n        return 'MCP'                 # Build once, connect many\n    elif scenario.want_community_tools:\n        return 'MCP'                 # Huge ecosystem of pre-built servers\n    elif scenario.enterprise_with_many_teams:\n        return 'MCP'                 # Standardization pays off enormously\n    else:\n        return 'Start direct, migrate to MCP when you add a 2nd app'\n```\n\nNow that you understand WHY MCP exists and WHEN to use it, let's dive into HOW it actually works under the hood."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Converting a REST Tool to MCP",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Below is a traditional LangChain tool that calls a weather API. Convert it into an MCP tool using FastMCP. Keep the same function name and logic, but use the MCP decorator instead of LangChain's @tool. Initialize the server as 'WeatherServer'.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Initialize the MCP server\nmcp = ___\n\n# Convert this LangChain tool to an MCP tool:\n# @tool\n# def get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n#     '''Get current weather for a city.'''\n#     return f'Weather in {city}: Sunny, 25°C'\n\n# Write the MCP version below:\n",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('WeatherServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n    '''Get current weather for a city.'''\n    return f'Weather in {city}: Sunny, 25°C'",
            "hint": "Replace @tool with @mcp.tool() and initialize FastMCP('WeatherServer'). The function itself stays the same!",
            "explanation": "The conversion is minimal: swap @tool for @mcp.tool() and add a FastMCP server instance. The function signature, type hints, docstring, and logic are identical. This shows how MCP builds on familiar patterns — you're not learning a new language, just a new protocol.",
            "concepts": [
              "FastMCP",
              "@mcp.tool()",
              "REST to MCP conversion"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "@mcp\\.tool\\(\\)\\s*\\ndef get_weather\\(city:\\s*str\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "When should you use an MCP server instead of a direct REST API integration? (Select the correct option)",
              "options": [
                "When you need the fastest possible HTTP response times",
                "When multiple AI applications need to access the same tool and you want plug-and-play discovery",
