        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP Architecture Deep Dive",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand the WHY of MCP, let's dive into HOW it works. And I'm going to explain it with an analogy that will make the entire architecture click instantly.\n\n**The Embassy Analogy:**\n\nImagine you're the president of a country (the **Host**). You want to communicate with other countries (external services like GitHub, Slack, databases). You don't fly to each country personally — you send **ambassadors** (Clients). Each ambassador lives in an embassy in one specific country and handles all communication with that country's government (the **Server**).\n\n- **Host** = The President's office (your AI application)\n- **Client** = An ambassador (one per foreign country/server)\n- **Server** = A foreign government (wraps an external service)\n- **JSON-RPC** = The diplomatic language they all speak\n\n**The Three Components in Detail:**\n\n**1. MCP Host (The President's Office)**\nThe top-level application the user interacts with — Claude Desktop, Cursor IDE, VS Code, or YOUR custom AI app. The Host:\n\\u2022 Creates and manages MCP Client instances (sends out ambassadors)\n\\u2022 Handles user authorization and consent\n\\u2022 Enforces security policies\n\\u2022 Provides the LLM that processes tool results\n\n**2. MCP Client (The Ambassador)**\nLives inside the Host and maintains a **1:1 connection** to a single MCP Server. It:\n\\u2022 Handles JSON-RPC protocol communication\n\\u2022 Performs capability negotiation on connect (\"What tools do you offer?\")\n\\u2022 Converts server tool schemas to the LLM's native format\n\\u2022 Routes the LLM's tool calls to the correct server\n\nHere's the critical architectural decision: **each server connection gets its own dedicated client.** If your Host connects to 3 servers (GitHub, Slack, PostgreSQL), it creates 3 MCP Client instances. This isolation prevents one buggy server from crashing your entire system.\n\n**3. MCP Server (The Foreign Government)**\nA lightweight program that wraps a specific external service. It:\n\\u2022 Implements the MCP protocol\n\\u2022 Exposes tools, resources, and prompts\n\\u2022 Handles tool execution and returns results\n\\u2022 Is designed to be small, focused, and composable\n\n**The Communication Flow:**\n```\nUser \\u2192 Host \\u2192 Client \\u2190JSON-RPC\\u2192 Server \\u2192 External Service\n                                            (GitHub API,\n                                             Database,\n                                             File System)\n```\n\n**Transport Layers — How They Actually Talk:**\nMCP supports two transport mechanisms, and choosing the right one matters:\n\n| Transport | How It Works | When to Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| **stdio** | Client spawns server as a subprocess, communicates via stdin/stdout | Local development, local tools (file system, local DB) |\n| **Streamable HTTP** | Client-to-server via HTTP POST, server-to-client via Server-Sent Events | Production, cloud-hosted servers, shared across machines |\n\n```python\n# stdio transport: local server as subprocess\nserver_params = StdioServerParameters(\n    command='python',\n    args=['my_mcp_server.py']\n)\n\n# HTTP transport: remote server\n# Client connects to: http://my-server.com:8000/mcp\n```\n\n**Why stdio for Development?**\nZero network latency, no firewall issues, no CORS headaches. The client literally spawns the server as a child process — it's as fast and secure as communication gets.\n\n**Why HTTP for Production?**\nYou can deploy the server on a different machine, share it across teams, put it behind a load balancer, and scale it independently. Multiple AI apps across your organization can connect to the same server.\n\n**Why This Architecture Is Brilliant:**\nThe 1:1 Client-Server relationship means you can mix and match freely. Want to add a new tool? Deploy a new server and point your client at it. Want to remove one? Disconnect the client. No code changes to your Host application. It's the same composability that made microservices powerful — but for AI tools.\n\nNow let's look at the wire protocol that makes all this communication possible: JSON-RPC 2.0."
          }
        },
        {
