        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "JSON-RPC 2.0: The Wire Protocol",
            "explanation": "Every time an MCP client talks to an MCP server, they speak a language called **JSON-RPC 2.0**. Think of it as the diplomatic language in our embassy analogy — both sides MUST speak it, and it has very strict grammar rules.\n\n**The Phone Call Analogy:**\n\nJSON-RPC is like a formal phone call protocol:\n1. You **dial** (send a request with an ID)\n2. You **state your business** (the method and parameters)\n3. The other person **responds** using your call ID (so you know which call they're answering)\n4. If something goes wrong, they tell you the **error code** (not just \"something broke\")\n\n**A JSON-RPC Request — What the Client Sends:**\n```json\n{\n  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n  \"id\": 1,\n  \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n  \"params\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n    \"arguments\": {\n      \"city\": \"San Francisco\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**A JSON-RPC Response — What the Server Returns:**\n```json\n{\n  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n  \"id\": 1,\n  \"result\": {\n    \"content\": [\n      {\n        \"type\": \"text\",\n        \"text\": \"Weather in San Francisco: Sunny, 18\\u00b0C\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**A JSON-RPC Error — When Things Go Wrong:**\n```json\n{\n  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n  \"id\": 1,\n  \"error\": {\n    \"code\": -32602,\n    \"message\": \"Invalid params: 'city' is required but was not provided\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Key JSON-RPC Fields — Know These Cold:**\n\n| Field | What It Does | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `jsonrpc` | Protocol version (always \"2.0\") | `\"2.0\"` |\n| `id` | Matches responses to requests | `1`, `2`, `\"abc\"` |\n| `method` | The MCP method being called | `\"tools/call\"`, `\"tools/list\"`, `\"initialize\"` |\n| `params` | Arguments for the method | `{\"name\": \"get_weather\", ...}` |\n| `result` | Success response data | `{\"content\": [...]}` |\n| `error` | Error response with code and message | `{\"code\": -32602, ...}` |\n\n**The MCP Methods You'll Use Most:**\n\n| Method | Direction | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `initialize` | Client \\u2192 Server | Start connection, exchange capabilities |\n| `notifications/initialized` | Client \\u2192 Server | Confirm initialization complete |\n| `tools/list` | Client \\u2192 Server | Discover available tools |\n| `tools/call` | Client \\u2192 Server | Execute a specific tool |\n| `resources/list` | Client \\u2192 Server | Discover available resources |\n| `resources/read` | Client \\u2192 Server | Read a specific resource |\n\n**The Initialization Handshake — The First Conversation:**\nWhen a client first connects, they perform a three-step handshake:\n\n```\nStep 1: Client \\u2192 Server: \"Hi, I support these capabilities...\"\n        {\"method\": \"initialize\", \"params\": {\"capabilities\": {...}}}\n\nStep 2: Server \\u2192 Client: \"Great! I support tools and resources...\"\n        {\"result\": {\"capabilities\": {\"tools\": {}, \"resources\": {}}}}\n\nStep 3: Client \\u2192 Server: \"Perfect, we're good to go!\"\n        {\"method\": \"notifications/initialized\"}\n```\n\nThis capability negotiation tells each side what the other supports. If the server only supports tools (not resources), the client won't try to read resources. It's like two people agreeing on which languages they both speak before starting a conversation.\n\n**Why You Need to Know This:**\nMost of the time, the MCP SDK handles JSON-RPC for you automatically. But when something breaks — and it will — you'll need to read raw JSON-RPC messages to debug. The MCP Inspector shows you these messages in real-time, and understanding them is the key to fixing connection issues fast.\n\nLet's continue with the complete MCP lifecycle — what happens from the moment a connection opens to when it closes."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Parsing a JSON-RPC MCP Response",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write a function called 'parse_mcp_response' that takes a JSON-RPC response dictionary and extracts the tool result text. If the response contains an 'error' key, return the error message instead. If the result has 'content', return the 'text' field of the first content item.",
            "starterCode": "def parse_mcp_response(response: dict) -> str:\n    '''Parse an MCP JSON-RPC response and extract the result text.'''\n    # Check for errors first\n    ___\n    # Extract the text from the result content\n    ___",
            "solution": "def parse_mcp_response(response: dict) -> str:\n    '''Parse an MCP JSON-RPC response and extract the result text.'''\n    if 'error' in response:\n        return f'Error: {response[\"error\"][\"message\"]}'\n    result = response.get('result', {})\n    content = result.get('content', [])\n    if content:\n        return content[0].get('text', 'No text in response')\n    return 'Empty response'",
            "hint": "Check for 'error' key first. Then navigate: response['result']['content'][0]['text'].",
            "explanation": "MCP responses follow JSON-RPC 2.0 format. The tool result is nested inside result.content[0].text. Error responses have an 'error' object with 'code' and 'message'. Understanding this structure helps you debug MCP integrations.",
            "concepts": [
              "JSON-RPC",
              "MCP Response Format",
              "Error Handling"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def parse_mcp_response.*response.*dict.*error.*result.*content"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The MCP Lifecycle: From Connection to Shutdown",
            "explanation": "Every MCP session follows a strict lifecycle, and understanding this sequence is your secret weapon for debugging connection issues. When something breaks, 90% of the time it's because one of these steps failed silently.\n\n**The Restaurant Shift Analogy:**\n\nThink of an MCP session like a restaurant shift:\n1. **Opening** (Initialization) — Turn on the lights, fire up the ovens, stock the ingredients\n2. **Menu Review** (Discovery) — Print today's menu, tell the waitstaff what's available\n3. **Service** (Operation) — Take orders, cook food, serve customers, repeat\n4. **Closing** (Shutdown) — Clean the kitchen, turn off equipment, lock up\n\n**Phase 1 — Initialization (Opening the Restaurant):**\n1. Host starts and reads its MCP server configuration file\n2. Host creates an MCP Client for each configured server\n3. Each Client connects to its Server via the configured transport (stdio or HTTP)\n4. Client sends `initialize` request with its capabilities\n5. Server responds with ITS capabilities (which tools, resources, prompts it supports)\n6. Client sends `notifications/initialized` to confirm\n\n**What Goes Wrong Here:**\n\\u274c Server process fails to start (wrong Python path, missing dependencies)\n\\u274c Capability negotiation hangs (version mismatch between SDK versions)\n\\u274c Config file has wrong path to server script\n\n**Phase 2 — Discovery (Printing the Menu):**\n7. Client calls `tools/list` to get all available tools with their JSON schemas\n8. Client calls `resources/list` to get available resources (if supported)\n9. Client calls `prompts/list` to get available prompts (if supported)\n10. Client converts tool schemas to the LLM's native function-calling format\n\n**What Goes Wrong Here:**\n\\u274c Tools defined AFTER `mcp.run()` is called (they won't be registered!)\n\\u274c Missing `@mcp.tool()` decorator (function exists but isn't exposed)\n\\u274c Import errors inside tool handler functions\n\n**Phase 3 — Operation (Serving Customers):**\n11. User asks a question\n12. LLM sees the available tools and decides to call one\n13. Client sends `tools/call` request to the server\n14. Server executes the tool and returns the result\n15. Client passes the result back to the LLM\n16. LLM generates a response using the tool result\n17. Steps 11-16 repeat as needed (the agent loop!)\n\n**What Goes Wrong Here:**\n\\u274c Tool receives wrong argument types (LLM sends string, tool expects int)\n\\u274c Unhandled exceptions crash the server process\n\\u274c stdio servers accidentally `print()` to stdout (corrupts the protocol!)\n\n**Phase 4 — Shutdown (Closing the Restaurant):**\n18. Host sends shutdown signal\n19. Clients close connections gracefully\n20. Servers clean up resources (close DB connections, file handles, etc.)\n\n**The Complete Flow — Visualized:**\n```\nHost starts\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Create Client A \\u2192 Connect to GitHub Server\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Create Client B \\u2192 Connect to Slack Server\n  \\u2514\\u2500 Create Client C \\u2192 Connect to Database Server\n\nEach Client:\n  initialize \\u2192 capabilities \\u2192 tools/list \\u2192 ready!\n\nUser asks question:\n  LLM decides: \"I need the GitHub tool\"\n  Host \\u2192 Client A \\u2192 GitHub Server \\u2192 GitHub API \\u2192 result\n  Result \\u2192 Client A \\u2192 Host \\u2192 LLM \\u2192 User gets answer\n\nHost shuts down:\n  Close Client A, B, C \\u2192 Servers clean up \\u2192 Done\n```\n\n**Pro Debugging Tip:**\nWhen your MCP server isn't working, trace the lifecycle step by step. Ask: \"Which phase failed?\" If initialization fails, check your config. If discovery fails, check your tool decorators. If operation fails, check your tool code. This systematic approach saves hours of guesswork.\n\nNow let's start building! In the next stage, you'll create your first MCP server from scratch."
          }
        },
        {
