          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building an MCP Server",
            "explanation": "Alright, enough theory — let's build something real. In the next few minutes, you'll create a working MCP server that any AI application can connect to. And I promise you, it's easier than you think.\n\n**The Vending Machine Analogy:**\n\nBuilding an MCP server is like building a vending machine. You need:\n1. A **machine** (the FastMCP server instance)\n2. **Products** inside it (tools, resources, prompts)\n3. A **power cable** (the transport — stdio or HTTP)\n4. **Labels** on each button (descriptions so people know what each product does)\n\nOnce the machine is plugged in, anyone with coins (an MCP client) can walk up and use it.\n\n**The Simplest MCP Server — 5 Lines of Meaningful Code:**\n\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Step 1: Create the vending machine\nmcp = FastMCP('HelloServer')\n\n# Step 2: Add a product (tool)\n@mcp.tool()\ndef greet(name: str) -> str:\n    '''Greet someone by name. Use when the user wants to say hello.'''\n    return f'Hello, {name}! Welcome to MCP.'\n\n# Step 3: Plug it in (start the server)\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()  # Runs on stdio transport by default\n```\n\nSave this as `hello_server.py` and you have a complete, working MCP server. That's it. Seriously.\n\n**What Happens When This Server Starts:**\n1. `mcp.run()` starts listening on stdin for JSON-RPC messages\n2. When a client connects, the server responds with its capabilities: \"I have one tool called `greet`\"\n3. The client can then call `tools/list` to see the tool's schema (auto-generated from the type hints!)\n4. When the LLM decides to call `greet`, the client sends a `tools/call` request\n5. The server executes the function and returns the result\n\n**The FastMCP Framework — Why It's Magical:**\n\nFastMCP (part of the official MCP Python SDK) handles ALL the protocol boilerplate for you:\n\\u2705 JSON-RPC serialization/deserialization\n\\u2705 JSON Schema generation from Python type hints\n\\u2705 Capability negotiation\n\\u2705 Transport handling (stdio and HTTP)\n\\u2705 Error formatting\n\nWithout FastMCP, you'd write 200+ lines of protocol handling code. With FastMCP, you write Python functions and add decorators.\n\n**Installing the MCP SDK:**\n```bash\npip install mcp[cli]\n```\n\nThis installs the MCP SDK, FastMCP, and the MCP Inspector CLI tool.\n\n**Running Your Server:**\n```bash\n# Option 1: Run directly (stdio mode)\npython hello_server.py\n\n# Option 2: Run with the MCP Inspector (for debugging)\nnpx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector python hello_server.py\n```\n\n**The Three Things Every MCP Server Needs:**\n1. A `FastMCP` instance with a descriptive name\n2. At least one tool, resource, or prompt (decorated functions)\n3. A `mcp.run()` call to start the server\n\n**Common Beginner Mistakes:**\n\\u274c Forgetting `mcp.run()` — the server never starts\n\\u274c Putting tool definitions AFTER `mcp.run()` — they won't be registered\n\\u274c Using `print()` in stdio mode — it corrupts the JSON-RPC stream (use `logging` to stderr instead!)\n\nNow let's add the real meat — tools. A server without tools is like a vending machine with no products inside."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Creating a Minimal MCP Server",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Create a minimal MCP server using FastMCP. Name it 'CalculatorServer'. Include the if __name__ == '__main__' guard to run it.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\n# Create the server\nmcp = ___\n\n# Run the server\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('CalculatorServer')\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "hint": "Initialize FastMCP with the name 'CalculatorServer', then call mcp.run() inside the __main__ guard.",
            "explanation": "This is the minimal MCP server. FastMCP('CalculatorServer') creates a server named 'CalculatorServer'. mcp.run() starts listening for client connections. The server handles all JSON-RPC protocol details automatically.",
            "concepts": [
              "FastMCP",
              "Server Initialization",
              "mcp.run()"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "FastMCP\\('CalculatorServer'\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Adding Tools to Your Server",
            "explanation": "A server without tools is like a restaurant without a menu — technically open, but nobody can order anything. Tools are the heart of any MCP server. Let me show you how to build them right.\n\n**The Three Magic Ingredients:**\n\nEvery MCP tool needs exactly three things, and if you get any of them wrong, the LLM will misuse (or ignore) your tool:\n\n**1. Type Hints are REQUIRED** — FastMCP reads `a: int, b: int` to auto-generate the tool's JSON Schema. Without type hints, the LLM won't know what parameters to pass.\n\n**2. Docstrings are Descriptions** — The LLM reads your docstring to decide WHEN to use this tool. This isn't documentation for humans — it's a prompt for the AI.\n\n**3. Return Values** — Tools should return strings (or types that can be serialized to text). The return value is sent back to the LLM as the tool's result.\n\n**The \\u0040mcp.tool() Decorator in Action:**\n```python\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Add two numbers together and return the sum.\n    Use this when the user asks to add, sum, or total numbers.'''\n    return a + b\n```\n\nThat single decorator turns a plain Python function into a network-accessible tool that any MCP client can discover and call.\n\n**What FastMCP Generates Behind the Scenes:**\nFrom your type hints and docstring, FastMCP automatically creates this JSON Schema:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"add\",\n  \"description\": \"Add two numbers together and return the sum. Use this when the user asks to add, sum, or total numbers.\",\n  \"inputSchema\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"a\": {\"type\": \"integer\"},\n      \"b\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}\n    },\n    \"required\": [\"a\", \"b\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nZero manual schema writing. The types flow from Python to JSON automatically.\n\n**Building a Multi-Tool Server:**\nA single server can (and should) expose multiple related tools:\n\n```python\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Add two numbers together.'''\n    return a + b\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Multiply two numbers together.'''\n    return a * b\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef divide(a: float, b: float) -> str:\n    '''Divide a by b. Returns error if b is zero.'''\n    if b == 0:\n        return 'Error: Cannot divide by zero. Please provide a non-zero divisor.'\n    return str(a / b)\n```\n\nWhen a client connects, it calls `tools/list` and receives the schemas for ALL three tools. The LLM then chooses which one to call based on the user's question.\n\n**Docstring Engineering — The #1 Skill for MCP:**\n\n| Quality | Docstring | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| \\u274c Bad | `'''Does math stuff.'''` | LLM ignores the tool or uses it wrong |\n| \\u26a0\\ufe0f Okay | `'''Add two numbers.'''` | LLM uses it but may confuse with similar tools |\n| \\u2705 Great | `'''Add two numbers and return the sum. Use when the user asks to add, sum, or total. Returns an integer.'''` | LLM uses it perfectly |\n\n**Pro Tips for Tool Design:**\n1. **One tool, one job** — Don't create a `do_math(operation, a, b)` god-tool. Make separate `add`, `subtract`, `multiply` tools. The LLM picks more accurately when tools are specific.\n2. **Include examples in descriptions** — \"e.g., add(5, 3) returns 8\" helps the LLM understand expected formats.\n3. **State what it returns** — \"Returns a JSON string with keys: name, email, role\" prevents the LLM from guessing.\n4. **State when NOT to use it** — \"Do NOT use for currency conversion\" prevents misuse.\n\nNow let's learn about the second MCP primitive — Resources, which let AI read data instead of calling functions."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a Multi-Tool MCP Server",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Create a FastMCP server named 'MathServer' with two tools: 'add' (takes two ints, returns their sum) and 'subtract' (takes two ints, returns their difference). Include docstrings and type hints for both.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n# Tool 1: Addition\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    ___\n\n# Tool 2: Subtraction\n___\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef add(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Add two numbers together and return the sum.'''\n    return a + b\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef subtract(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    '''Subtract b from a and return the difference.'''\n    return a - b\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "hint": "Each tool needs @mcp.tool(), type hints for all parameters, a docstring, and a return statement.",
            "explanation": "Each @mcp.tool() decorated function becomes a callable tool. FastMCP auto-generates JSON schemas from the type hints (a: int, b: int -> int) and uses the docstrings as tool descriptions for the LLM.",
            "concepts": [
              "Multiple Tools",
              "@mcp.tool()",
              "Type Hints"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "@mcp\\.tool\\(\\)\\s*\\ndef add.*@mcp\\.tool\\(\\)\\s*\\ndef subtract"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Resources: Exposing Data to the AI",
            "explanation": "Tools let the AI DO things. Resources let the AI READ things. Think of the difference like this: a tool is a **vending machine button** (press it, something happens), while a resource is a **display window** (look through it, see what's inside).\n\n**The Library Analogy:**\n\nImagine your MCP server is a library:\n- **Tools** are the librarian — you ask them to search for a book, check it out, or return it (actions with side effects)\n- **Resources** are the bookshelves — you walk up, browse, and read whatever's there (read-only access to data)\n\n**Static Resources — Fixed Data at a Fixed Address:**\nExpose data at a fixed URI that never changes:\n\n```python\n@mcp.resource('config://app/settings')\ndef get_settings() -> str:\n    '''Read the current application configuration.'''\n    return json.dumps({\n        'version': '2.1.0',\n        'debug': False,\n        'max_retries': 3,\n        'region': 'us-east-1'\n    })\n```\n\nThe AI can request `config://app/settings` anytime it needs to know the app's configuration. No side effects, no modifications — just data.\n\n**Dynamic Resource Templates — Parameterized Data:**\nUse URI templates for resources that depend on a parameter:\n\n```python\n@mcp.resource('users://{user_id}/profile')\ndef get_user_profile(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Get a user profile by their unique ID.'''\n    user = database.find_user(user_id)\n    return json.dumps({\n        'id': user_id,\n        'name': user.name,\n        'email': user.email,\n        'role': user.role\n    })\n```\n\nThe `{user_id}` placeholder makes this dynamic — the client can request `users://alice/profile` or `users://bob/profile`.\n\n**Tool vs Resource — The Decision Guide:**\n\n| Dimension | Tool | Resource |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purpose | Execute an ACTION | Provide DATA |\n| Side effects? | Yes (can modify state) | No (read-only) |\n| Who triggers it? | The LLM decides when to call | Explicitly requested by client |\n| Example | `send_email(to, body)` | `config://email/smtp-settings` |\n| Analogy | The librarian | The bookshelf |\n\n**When to Use Resources:**\n\\u2705 Application configuration that the AI needs to reference\n\\u2705 Documentation or knowledge base content\n\\u2705 Database records that provide context (user profiles, product catalogs)\n\\u2705 Log files or system status information\n\n**When to Use Tools Instead:**\n\\u2705 Searching (the query changes every time)\n\\u2705 Creating, updating, or deleting records\n\\u2705 Calling external APIs with varying parameters\n\\u2705 Any operation with side effects\n\n**A Real-World Example — Documentation Server:**\n```python\nmcp = FastMCP('DocsServer')\n\n# Static resource: company handbook\n@mcp.resource('docs://handbook/leave-policy')\ndef get_leave_policy() -> str:\n    '''Company leave and vacation policy.'''\n    return open('docs/leave_policy.md').read()\n\n# Dynamic resource: API docs by version\n@mcp.resource('docs://api/{version}/endpoints')\ndef get_api_docs(version: str) -> str:\n    '''API endpoint documentation for a specific version.'''\n    return open(f'docs/api/{version}/endpoints.md').read()\n\n# Tool: search docs (this is an ACTION, not data)\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_docs(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search all documentation for matching content.'''\n    results = doc_search_engine.search(query)\n    return json.dumps(results)\n```\n\nNotice how the static content is a Resource (read-only), but the search functionality is a Tool (dynamic action).\n\nNow let's look at the third MCP primitive — Prompts, the least used but sometimes very handy feature."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Creating an MCP Resource",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Add a static resource to the MathServer that exposes the server's version info at the URI 'info://server/version'. It should return a JSON string with keys 'name', 'version', and 'tools_count'.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n# Add a resource at 'info://server/version'\n___\ndef get_version() -> str:\n    '''Get server version information.'''\n    return json.dumps(___)",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('MathServer')\n\n@mcp.resource('info://server/version')\ndef get_version() -> str:\n    '''Get server version information.'''\n    return json.dumps({'name': 'MathServer', 'version': '1.0.0', 'tools_count': 2})",
            "hint": "Use @mcp.resource('info://server/version') decorator and return json.dumps() with the required keys.",
            "explanation": "Resources use the @mcp.resource() decorator with a URI string. The function returns string data that the AI can read. Unlike tools, resources are passive data providers — they don't take action, they provide context.",
            "concepts": [
              "@mcp.resource()",
              "Static Resources",
              "URI Schemes"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "@mcp\\.resource\\(.*info://server/version.*\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Prompts: Reusable Templates for the AI",
            "explanation": "The third MCP primitive is Prompts — and let me be honest with you: they're the least used of the three primitives. But when you need them, they're incredibly useful.\n\n**The Macro Button Analogy:**\n\nThink of Prompts like macro buttons on a gaming keyboard. Instead of pressing 10 keys in sequence every time, you program a macro that does it all with one button press. MCP Prompts work the same way — they're pre-built, optimized instruction templates that clients can invoke with one call.\n\n**Creating a Prompt:**\n```python\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('DevToolsServer')\n\n@mcp.prompt()\ndef code_review(language: str, code: str) -> str:\n    '''Generate a detailed code review prompt for the given code.'''\n    return f'''You are an expert {language} code reviewer with 15 years of experience.\nReview the following code for:\n1. Bugs and logical errors\n2. Performance issues and bottlenecks\n3. Security vulnerabilities (SQL injection, XSS, etc.)\n4. Code style and best practices\n5. Test coverage suggestions\n\nBe specific — reference line numbers and suggest concrete fixes.\n\nCode to review:\n\\u0060\\u0060\\u0060{language}\n{code}\n\\u0060\\u0060\\u0060'''\n```\n\n**When to Use Prompts:**\n\\u2705 Your MCP server wraps a domain-specific tool, and you want to provide optimized prompts for common workflows\n\\u2705 You want to standardize how AI assistants interact with your tools across your organization\n\\u2705 You want to version-control prompt templates alongside the tools they complement\n\\u2705 You have complex, multi-step prompt templates that would be tedious to recreate each time\n\n**When NOT to Use Prompts:**\n\\u274c Simple tools that don't need special instruction templates\n\\u274c One-off interactions where a regular system prompt suffices\n\\u274c When you're just starting out (focus on Tools first)\n\n**Prompts vs Tools — Clear the Confusion:**\n\n| Dimension | Tool | Prompt |\n|---|---|---|\n| What it does | Executes code, returns data | Returns a text template |\n| Who calls it | The LLM decides | The user/client explicitly requests |\n| Side effects | Can have side effects | Never — just returns text |\n| Usage frequency | Very common | Rare in practice |\n| Analogy | Vending machine button | Macro button on a keyboard |\n\n**A More Practical Example — SQL Query Helper:**\n```python\n@mcp.prompt()\ndef write_sql(table_name: str, task: str) -> str:\n    '''Generate a prompt for writing a safe SQL query.'''\n    schema = get_table_schema(table_name)  # Fetch actual table schema\n    return f'''You are a PostgreSQL expert. Write a SQL query for the following task.\n\nTable schema:\n{schema}\n\nTask: {task}\n\nRules:\n- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection\n- Add appropriate WHERE clauses to avoid full table scans\n- Include LIMIT if the result set could be large\n- Add comments explaining complex joins or subqueries'''\n```\n\n**The Honest Truth About Prompts:**\nIn the current MCP ecosystem, Tools are used 10x more frequently than Prompts. Most production MCP servers have 5-20 tools and 0-2 prompts. Focus your energy on mastering Tools first — they're where the real value is. Add Prompts later when you find yourself repeatedly constructing the same complex prompts.\n\nWith all three primitives understood (Tools, Resources, Prompts), you're ready to level up your tool-building skills. Let's write production-quality MCP tools."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Complete MCP Server with Tools, Resources, and Prompts",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Build a complete MCP server named 'DevToolsServer' that has:\n1. A tool called 'count_words' that takes text (str) and returns the word count as a string\n2. A resource at 'info://help' that returns usage instructions\n3. Run the server with mcp.run()\n\nStore the server in a variable called 'mcp'.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('DevToolsServer')\n\n# Tool: count words in text\n___\n\n# Resource: help info\n___\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('DevToolsServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef count_words(text: str) -> str:\n    '''Count the number of words in the given text.'''\n    count = len(text.split())\n    return f'The text contains {count} words.'\n\n@mcp.resource('info://help')\ndef get_help() -> str:\n    '''Get usage instructions for the DevTools server.'''\n    return 'Available tools: count_words(text) - counts words in text.'\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    mcp.run()",
            "hint": "Use @mcp.tool() for the word counter and @mcp.resource('info://help') for the help resource. Both need docstrings and type hints.",
            "explanation": "This server exposes both a tool (count_words) and a resource (help info). When a client connects, it discovers both capabilities. The LLM can call count_words when users ask about word counts, and read the help resource for context about available features.",
            "concepts": [
              "Complete MCP Server",
              "Tools + Resources",
              "Server Composition"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "@mcp\\.tool\\(\\).*count_words.*@mcp\\.resource"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
