        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building an MCP Client & Tool Integration",
            "explanation": "So far, you've been building MCP servers — the tool providers. Now let's flip to the other side and build an MCP **client** — the tool consumer. This is where things get really powerful, because your client can connect to ANY MCP server and dynamically discover its tools.\n\n**The Universal Remote Analogy:**\n\nThink of an MCP client as a **universal remote control**. You point it at any TV (MCP server) and it automatically learns all the buttons (tools). You don't need to program each button manually — the remote asks the TV: \"What can you do?\" and the TV responds with its full list of capabilities.\n\n**The Simplest MCP Client:**\n\n```python\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def main():\n    # Connect to a local MCP server\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['math_server.py']\n    )\n    \n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            # Step 1: Initialize the connection\n            await session.initialize()\n            \n            # Step 2: Discover available tools\n            tools = await session.list_tools()\n            print(f'Server has {len(tools.tools)} tools:')\n            for tool in tools.tools:\n                print(f'  - {tool.name}: {tool.description}')\n            \n            # Step 3: Call a tool\n            result = await session.call_tool('add', {'a': 5, 'b': 3})\n            print(f'Result: {result.content[0].text}')  # \"8\"\n```\n\n**What's Happening Under the Hood:**\n1. `stdio_client` spawns `python math_server.py` as a subprocess\n2. `session.initialize()` sends the JSON-RPC `initialize` handshake\n3. `session.list_tools()` sends `tools/list` and receives all tool schemas\n4. `session.call_tool()` sends `tools/call` with the tool name and arguments\n5. The result comes back as structured content\n\n**Connecting to Multiple Servers:**\n\nThe real power of MCP is connecting one client to MANY servers simultaneously:\n\n```python\nservers = {\n    'math': StdioServerParameters(command='python', args=['math_server.py']),\n    'weather': StdioServerParameters(command='python', args=['weather_server.py']),\n    'github': StdioServerParameters(command='python', args=['github_server.py']),\n}\n\nall_tools = []\nsessions = {}\n\nfor name, params in servers.items():\n    # Each server gets its own client session\n    read, write = await stdio_client(params).__aenter__()\n    session = await ClientSession(read, write).__aenter__()\n    await session.initialize()\n    \n    tools = await session.list_tools()\n    all_tools.extend(tools.tools)\n    sessions[name] = session\n    \n    print(f'{name}: {len(tools.tools)} tools')\n\nprint(f'Total tools available: {len(all_tools)}')\n```\n\nNow your client has access to math tools, weather tools, and GitHub tools — all from separate servers, all discoverable at runtime.\n\n**The Architecture Pattern:**\n```\nYour AI App (Host)\n  \\u251c\\u2500 MCP Client A \\u2194 Math Server     (add, multiply, divide)\n  \\u251c\\u2500 MCP Client B \\u2194 Weather Server  (get_weather, get_forecast)\n  \\u2514\\u2500 MCP Client C \\u2194 GitHub Server   (search_repos, create_issue)\n  \n  LLM sees ALL tools from ALL servers\n  LLM decides which tool to call\n  Client routes the call to the correct server\n```\n\n**Why This Is Powerful:**\n- Add a new capability (e.g., Slack) by deploying a new server — zero code changes to your app\n- Remove a capability by disconnecting the server — no code changes\n- Each server is independently deployable, testable, and scalable\n- Community-built servers plug right in\n\nNow let's see the ultimate integration — connecting MCP tools to a LangChain/LangGraph agent for autonomous tool use."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Building a stdio MCP Client",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write an async function called 'connect_and_list_tools' that connects to a local MCP server (running as 'python server.py'), initializes the session, lists all available tools, and returns a list of tool names (strings).",
            "starterCode": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def connect_and_list_tools() -> list:\n    '''Connect to MCP server and return list of tool names.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            # Initialize the session\n            ___\n            # List tools and return their names\n            ___",
            "solution": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def connect_and_list_tools() -> list:\n    '''Connect to MCP server and return list of tool names.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            tools = await session.list_tools()\n            return [tool.name for tool in tools.tools]",
            "hint": "Call await session.initialize() first, then await session.list_tools(). Use a list comprehension to extract tool.name from each tool.",
            "explanation": "The client flow is: connect → initialize → discover → use. session.initialize() performs the JSON-RPC handshake. session.list_tools() returns tool objects with .name and .description attributes. We extract just the names into a list.",
            "concepts": [
              "MCP Client",
              "stdio_client",
              "Tool Discovery"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "await session\\.initialize\\(\\).*await session\\.list_tools\\(\\)"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Multi-Server Orchestration & LangChain Integration",
            "explanation": "Here's where everything comes together. You've built MCP servers. You've built MCP clients. Now let's connect them to a LangGraph agent that can autonomously discover and use tools from multiple servers. This is the production pattern that companies like Cursor and Sourcegraph use.\n\n**The Orchestra Analogy:**\n\nThink of a symphony orchestra. The **conductor** (your LangGraph agent) doesn't play any instruments — they coordinate. Each **section** (MCP server) has specialized musicians (tools). The conductor reads the score (user's request), decides which section to cue (which tool to call), and creates beautiful music (a coherent response) from all the parts.\n\n**Converting MCP Tools to LangChain Tools:**\n\nThe bridge between MCP and LangChain is straightforward — you convert MCP tool schemas into LangChain StructuredTool objects:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.tools import StructuredTool\n\ndef mcp_to_langchain_tool(session, mcp_tool):\n    '''Convert an MCP tool into a LangChain-compatible tool.'''\n    async def call_tool(**kwargs):\n        result = await session.call_tool(mcp_tool.name, kwargs)\n        return result.content[0].text\n\n    return StructuredTool(\n        name=mcp_tool.name,\n        description=mcp_tool.description,\n        func=call_tool,\n        args_schema=mcp_tool.inputSchema\n    )\n```\n\n**The Easy Way — langchain-mcp-adapters:**\n\nFor production, use the official adapter package that handles everything automatically:\n\n```bash\npip install langchain-mcp-adapters\n```\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_mcp_adapters.client import MultiServerMCPClient\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import create_react_agent\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\n\n# Connect to multiple MCP servers at once\nclient = MultiServerMCPClient({\n    'math': {\n        'command': 'python',\n        'args': ['math_server.py'],\n        'transport': 'stdio'\n    },\n    'weather': {\n        'url': 'http://weather-server:8000/mcp',\n        'transport': 'streamable_http'\n    },\n    'github': {\n        'command': 'python',\n        'args': ['github_server.py'],\n        'transport': 'stdio'\n    }\n})\n\n# Get ALL tools from ALL servers — dynamically discovered!\ntools = await client.get_tools()\nprint(f'Agent has {len(tools)} tools from 3 servers')\n\n# Create a ReAct agent with all the tools\nagent = create_react_agent(ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o'), tools)\n\n# The agent can now use any tool from any server\nresult = await agent.ainvoke({\n    'messages': [('user', 'What is 5+3 and what is the weather in Tokyo?')]\n})\n```\n\n**What Happens At Runtime:**\n1. `MultiServerMCPClient` connects to all 3 servers\n2. Each server reports its tools via `tools/list`\n3. The adapter converts all MCP tools to LangChain tools\n4. `create_react_agent` gives the LLM access to all tools\n5. User asks a question spanning multiple servers\n6. LLM calls `add(5, 3)` from the math server AND `get_weather(\"Tokyo\")` from the weather server\n7. Results flow back through the agent to the user\n\n**The Production Architecture:**\n```\nUser Question\n  \\u2193\nLangGraph ReAct Agent (GPT-4o)\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Discovers 15 tools from 4 MCP servers\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Decides which tools to call\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Calls tools via MCP protocol\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Receives results\n  \\u2514\\u2500 Generates unified answer\n```\n\n**Mixing Transports — The Best of Both Worlds:**\nNotice how in the example, the math server uses stdio (local, fast) while the weather server uses HTTP (remote, shared). You can mix and match transports freely:\n\n| Server | Transport | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| File system tools | stdio | Security — never expose to network |\n| Database tools | stdio | Performance — zero network latency |\n| Shared API tools | HTTP | Shared across team, centrally managed |\n| Third-party tools | HTTP | Hosted by the tool provider |\n\n**Why This Pattern Is the Future:**\nWith MCP + LangGraph, adding a new capability to your agent is literally one config line. No code changes. No redeployment. Just point your client at a new server. This is why the MCP ecosystem is growing so fast — every new server instantly benefits every MCP-compatible app.\n\nNow let's talk about something critical: security. Because connecting AI to external tools is powerful but also dangerous if done wrong."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Calling an MCP Tool from Client",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Write an async function called 'call_mcp_tool' that connects to a local MCP server ('python calc_server.py'), initializes the session, and calls a tool named 'add' with arguments a=10 and b=20. Return the text content of the result.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def call_mcp_tool() -> str:\n    '''Connect to server and call the add tool.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['calc_server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            # Call the 'add' tool with a=10, b=20\n            result = ___\n            # Return the text content\n            return ___",
            "solution": "from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def call_mcp_tool() -> str:\n    '''Connect to server and call the add tool.'''\n    server_params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command='python',\n        args=['calc_server.py']\n    )\n\n    async with stdio_client(server_params) as (read, write):\n        async with ClientSession(read, write) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            result = await session.call_tool('add', {'a': 10, 'b': 20})\n            return result.content[0].text",
            "hint": "Use await session.call_tool('add', {'a': 10, 'b': 20}). The result text is at result.content[0].text.",
            "explanation": "session.call_tool() sends a JSON-RPC tools/call request to the server. The first argument is the tool name, the second is a dictionary of arguments. The result follows MCP's content format — the text is at result.content[0].text.",
            "concepts": [
              "session.call_tool()",
              "Tool Invocation",
              "Result Parsing"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "await session\\.call_tool\\('add'.*\\{.*'a'.*10.*'b'.*20.*\\}"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
