          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP Security & Authentication",
            "explanation": "I need you to really listen to this one, because a misconfigured MCP server can turn your helpful AI assistant into an **attack vector** that leaks data, deletes records, or gets exploited by prompt injection.\n\n**The Bank Vault Analogy:**\n\nYour MCP server is a bank vault. It contains access to valuable things — databases, APIs, file systems, email services. The MCP protocol is the door. Right now we need to make sure that door has proper locks, cameras, and guards — not just a \"please close the door behind you\" sign.\n\n**The Unique Threat Model — What Makes MCP Different:**\n\nMCP introduces a security challenge that traditional APIs don't have: an **AI model** (which can hallucinate and be manipulated) decides which tools to call and with what arguments. You must protect against threats that come from BOTH the outside (attackers) and the inside (the LLM itself):\n\n| Threat | How It Happens | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Prompt Injection** | Attacker embeds instructions in data the LLM reads | Malicious text in a webpage tricks the LLM into calling `delete_all_users` |\n| **Parameter Manipulation** | LLM hallucinates dangerous inputs | LLM sends `../../etc/passwd` as a filename (path traversal) |\n| **Unauthorized Access** | Unauthenticated client connects to your server | Anyone on the network can call your tools |\n| **Privilege Escalation** | Tool exposes more capability than intended | A \"read file\" tool gets tricked into reading `/etc/shadow` |\n\n**Authentication for Remote Servers:**\n\nLocal servers (stdio) are relatively safe — they run on the same machine as the host. But remote servers (HTTP/SSE) are exposed to the network and MUST have authentication:\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('SecureServer')\n\n# API key authentication middleware\nAPI_KEY = os.environ['MCP_API_KEY']\n\ndef validate_auth(request_headers):\n    '''Validate the API key from request headers.'''\n    key = request_headers.get('Authorization', '').replace('Bearer ', '')\n    if key != API_KEY:\n        raise PermissionError('Invalid or missing API key')\n```\n\nThe MCP specification recommends:\n- **OAuth 2.0 with PKCE** for browser-based clients\n- **API keys or client credentials** for server-to-server connections\n- **mTLS** for high-security enterprise environments\n\n**The Six Security Commandments:**\n\n1. **Validate ALL tool inputs server-side** — Never trust the LLM. It can hallucinate `rm -rf /` as an argument.\n```python\nALLOWED_TABLES = ['users', 'products', 'orders']\nif table not in ALLOWED_TABLES:\n    return f'Error: Invalid table. Allowed: {ALLOWED_TABLES}'\n```\n\n2. **Use allowlists, not blocklists** — Don't try to block bad inputs. Only allow known-good ones.\n\n3. **Implement rate limiting** — Prevent tool-calling loops from overwhelming your backends.\n```python\nfrom functools import lru_cache\nimport time\n\ncall_counts = {}\nMAX_CALLS_PER_MINUTE = 30\n\ndef check_rate_limit(tool_name):\n    now = time.time()\n    calls = call_counts.get(tool_name, [])\n    recent = [t for t in calls if now - t < 60]\n    if len(recent) >= MAX_CALLS_PER_MINUTE:\n        return False\n    recent.append(now)\n    call_counts[tool_name] = recent\n    return True\n```\n\n4. **Log everything** — Every tool call, every argument, every result. You need an audit trail.\n\n5. **Principle of Least Privilege** — Each tool should have the minimum permissions needed. A \"search\" tool shouldn't have write access to the database.\n\n6. **Use environment variables for secrets** — Never hardcode API keys, tokens, or passwords.\n\n**Why This Matters — The Real Risk:**\nIn 2024, security researchers demonstrated prompt injection attacks against MCP-connected agents where malicious content in a webpage caused the agent to exfiltrate data through tool calls. This isn't theoretical — it's a real attack vector. The defenses above protect against it.\n\nNow let's go deeper into trust boundaries — because not every tool carries the same risk."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Secure Tool with Input Validation",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create an MCP tool called 'read_file' that takes a file_path (str). Implement security validation:\n1. Check that the path doesn't contain '..' (path traversal protection)\n2. Check that the path starts with '/workspace/' (restrict to safe directory)\n3. Return appropriate error messages for violations\n4. Return mock file contents for valid paths",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('FileServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef read_file(file_path: str) -> str:\n    '''Read the contents of a file within the /workspace/ directory.\n    Only files within /workspace/ are accessible for security.'''\n    # Security validation\n    ___\n    # Mock file reading\n    return f'Contents of {file_path}: [file data here]'",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('FileServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef read_file(file_path: str) -> str:\n    '''Read the contents of a file within the /workspace/ directory.\n    Only files within /workspace/ are accessible for security.'''\n    if '..' in file_path:\n        return 'Error: Path traversal detected. \"..\" is not allowed in file paths.'\n    if not file_path.startswith('/workspace/'):\n        return f'Error: Access denied. Only files in /workspace/ are readable. Got: {file_path}'\n    return f'Contents of {file_path}: [file data here]'",
            "hint": "Check for '..' first (path traversal). Then check startswith('/workspace/'). Return specific error messages for each violation.",
            "explanation": "This demonstrates two critical security patterns: path traversal prevention (blocking '..') and directory restriction (only allowing /workspace/). Both checks happen server-side because the LLM might hallucinate dangerous paths.",
            "concepts": [
              "Path Traversal Prevention",
              "Directory Restriction",
              "Input Validation"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def read_file\\(file_path:\\s*str\\).*\\.\\.'.*in.*file_path.*startswith"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Trust Boundaries & Tool Isolation",
            "explanation": "Not every tool carries the same risk. Reading a weather API is harmless. Deleting database records could end careers. MCP security architecture requires you to think about **trust boundaries** — invisible lines that separate safe operations from dangerous ones.\n\n**The Hospital Zones Analogy:**\n\nThink of a hospital. It has zones with different access levels:\n- **Public lobby** — Anyone can enter (read-only tools like search, weather)\n- **Patient floors** — Staff badge required (tools that access user data)\n- **Operating rooms** — Specialized access only (tools that modify data)\n- **Pharmacy vault** — Dual-key access required (tools that handle payments, delete records)\n\nYour MCP architecture should have the same zoning.\n\n**The Trust Boundary Pattern — Separate Servers by Risk:**\n\n```python\n# LOW RISK: Read-only operations (public lobby)\nsafe_server = FastMCP('ReadOnlyTools')\n\n@safe_server.tool()\ndef search_docs(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search documentation. Read-only, no side effects.'''\n    return doc_search(query)\n\n@safe_server.tool()\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n    '''Get current weather. Read-only, no sensitive data.'''\n    return weather_api.get(city)\n```\n\n```python\n# HIGH RISK: Write operations (operating room - separate process!)\ndanger_server = FastMCP('WriteTools')\n\n@danger_server.tool()\ndef delete_record(table: str, record_id: int) -> str:\n    '''Delete a database record. DESTRUCTIVE — cannot be undone.'''\n    logger.warning(f'DELETE requested: {table}/{record_id}')\n    # Validate, authorize, then delete...\n    return f'Deleted record {record_id} from {table}'\n\n@danger_server.tool()\ndef send_email(to: str, subject: str, body: str) -> str:\n    '''Send an email. Cannot be unsent once delivered.'''\n    logger.warning(f'EMAIL requested to: {to}')\n    # Validate, authorize, then send...\n    return f'Email sent to {to}'\n```\n\n**Why Separate Servers?**\n\n| Reason | Explanation |\n|---|---|\n| **Different auth requirements** | Read-only: API key. Write: OAuth + human approval |\n| **Different rate limits** | Reads: 100/min. Writes: 5/min |\n| **Different audit levels** | Reads: basic logging. Writes: full audit trail with user attribution |\n| **Isolation** | Disable the dangerous server without affecting reads |\n| **Access control** | Only senior devs can deploy write servers |\n| **Blast radius** | If the write server crashes, read-only tools keep working |\n\n**Comprehensive Audit Logging — Non-Negotiable for Write Operations:**\n\n```python\nimport logging\nimport json\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\naudit_logger = logging.getLogger('mcp_audit')\n\ndef audit_log(tool_name, args, result, user_id=None):\n    '''Log every tool invocation for security auditing.'''\n    entry = {\n        'timestamp': datetime.now().isoformat(),\n        'tool': tool_name,\n        'arguments': args,\n        'result_preview': str(result)[:200],\n        'user_id': user_id,\n    }\n    audit_logger.info(json.dumps(entry))\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef delete_user(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Delete a user account. This action is irreversible.'''\n    audit_log('delete_user', {'user_id': user_id}, 'pending')\n    \n    # ... perform deletion ...\n    \n    audit_log('delete_user', {'user_id': user_id}, 'completed')\n    return f'User {user_id} deleted successfully'\n```\n\n**Secret Management — The Basics:**\n```python\nimport os\n\n# GOOD: Load from environment variables\nDB_URL = os.environ['DATABASE_URL']\nAPI_KEY = os.environ['STRIPE_API_KEY']\n\n# BAD: Hardcoded secrets (NEVER do this!)\n# DB_URL = 'postgresql://admin:password123@prod-db:5432/main'\n```\n\n**The Security Checklist for Production MCP Servers:**\n\n| Requirement | Read-Only Server | Read-Write Server |\n|---|---|---|\n| Input validation | \\u2705 Required | \\u2705 Required |\n| Authentication | \\u26a0\\ufe0f Recommended | \\u2705 Required |\n| Rate limiting | \\u26a0\\ufe0f Recommended | \\u2705 Required |\n| Audit logging | \\u26a0\\ufe0f Basic | \\u2705 Comprehensive |\n| Human-in-the-loop | \\u274c Not needed | \\u2705 For destructive operations |\n| Separate deployment | \\u274c Optional | \\u2705 Required |\n| Environment secrets | \\u2705 Required | \\u2705 Required |\n\nWith security locked down, let's move on to running MCP in production — patterns, scaling, and monitoring."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Tool with Rate Limiting",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create an MCP tool called 'search_api' that includes basic rate limiting. Use a module-level counter to track calls. If called more than 10 times, return an error asking the agent to wait. Otherwise, increment the counter and return mock results.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('SearchServer')\n\n# Simple rate limit counter\ncall_count = 0\nMAX_CALLS = 10\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_api(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search the external API. Rate limited to 10 calls per session.'''\n    global call_count\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\n\nmcp = FastMCP('SearchServer')\n\ncall_count = 0\nMAX_CALLS = 10\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_api(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search the external API. Rate limited to 10 calls per session.'''\n    global call_count\n    if call_count >= MAX_CALLS:\n        return f'Error: Rate limit exceeded ({MAX_CALLS} calls). Please wait before searching again.'\n    call_count += 1\n    return f'Search results for \"{query}\": [result 1, result 2, result 3]'",
            "hint": "Check if call_count >= MAX_CALLS first. If so, return an error. Otherwise, increment and return results.",
            "explanation": "Rate limiting prevents an AI agent stuck in a tool-calling loop from overwhelming your backend. The error message tells the agent to stop calling, which a well-designed agent will respect. In production, use a proper rate limiter (e.g., token bucket algorithm).",
            "concepts": [
              "Rate Limiting",
              "Global State",
              "Agent Loop Prevention"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "call_count.*>=.*MAX_CALLS.*Error.*Rate limit"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Choosing Authentication for Remote MCP",
            "description": "You're deploying an MCP server to the cloud that wraps your company's internal CRM system. Multiple AI assistants across the organization will connect to it. What authentication should you use?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "text": "No authentication — the server is behind a VPN.",
