        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "MCP in Production: Patterns & Best Practices",
            "explanation": "Building an MCP server that works on your laptop is easy. Building one that's reliable, maintainable, and scalable in production requires patterns that have emerged from real-world enterprise deployments. Let me share the ones that matter most.\n\n**The Restaurant Chain Analogy:**\n\nRunning one restaurant (dev server) is simple. Running a chain of 50 restaurants (production) requires standard operating procedures, supply chain management, quality control, and brand consistency. These production patterns are your standard operating procedures.\n\n**Pattern 1: Server Registry**\n\nIn an enterprise with 20 MCP servers across 5 teams, someone needs to know what's available. Maintain a central registry:\n\n```python\n# server_registry.json — the master list of all MCP servers\n{\n  \"servers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"GitHubServer\",\n      \"transport\": \"stdio\",\n      \"command\": \"python\",\n      \"args\": [\"github_server.py\"],\n      \"description\": \"GitHub repository operations — search repos, create issues, read PRs\",\n      \"owner\": \"platform-team\",\n      \"auth\": \"github_token\",\n      \"risk_level\": \"medium\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"SlackServer\",\n      \"transport\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://mcp.internal.company.com/slack\",\n      \"description\": \"Slack messaging — send messages, read channels, manage threads\",\n      \"owner\": \"comms-team\",\n      \"auth\": \"oauth2\",\n      \"risk_level\": \"high\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nAI hosts query the registry to discover servers dynamically. Adding a new server is one JSON entry — no code changes to any AI app.\n\n**Pattern 2: Tool Description Engineering at Scale**\n\nAt enterprise scale, tool descriptions become your most critical documentation. They're not just for developers — they're the **AI's instruction manual**:\n\n```python\n# MEDIOCRE — The LLM might confuse this with other search tools\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search(query: str) -> str:\n    '''Search for information.'''\n\n# PRODUCTION-GRADE — The LLM knows exactly when and how to use this\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_company_wiki(query: str, max_results: int = 5) -> str:\n    '''Search the internal company wiki for articles matching the query.\n    USE this when the user asks about company policies, onboarding\n    procedures, HR guidelines, or internal documentation.\n    DO NOT use this for general knowledge questions — use web_search instead.\n    DO NOT use this for code documentation — use search_code_docs instead.\n    Returns up to max_results articles with titles and content snippets.\n    Example: search_company_wiki(\"vacation policy\") returns PTO guidelines.'''\n```\n\n**Pattern 3: Structured Error Responses**\n\nErrors that help the LLM self-correct are worth their weight in gold:\n\n```python\n# BAD — The LLM learns nothing from this\nreturn 'Error: Internal Server Error'\n\n# GOOD — The LLM knows exactly how to recover\nreturn ('Error: User not found with email \"bob@test.com\". '\n        'Please verify the email address. '\n        'Tip: Try searching by name using the search_users tool instead.')\n```\n\n**Pattern 4: Versioning & Backward Compatibility**\n\nUse semantic versioning. When breaking changes are unavoidable, run old and new versions in parallel:\n```python\nmcp = FastMCP('GitHubServer', version='2.1.0')\n\n# Keep v1 tools working while adding v2 tools\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_repos_v2(query: str, language: str = None, sort: str = 'relevance') -> str:\n    '''Search GitHub repositories. V2: now supports language filter and sorting.'''\n    ...\n```\n\n**Pattern 5: Health Checks & Readiness**\n\nProduction servers need health endpoints:\n```python\n@mcp.tool()\ndef health_check() -> str:\n    '''Check if this MCP server is healthy and all dependencies are reachable.\n    The AI should NOT call this — it is for infrastructure monitoring.'''\n    checks = {\n        'database': check_db_connection(),\n        'api_key_valid': check_api_key(),\n        'disk_space': check_disk_space()\n    }\n    status = 'healthy' if all(checks.values()) else 'degraded'\n    return json.dumps({'status': status, 'checks': checks})\n```\n\n**The Production Deployment Checklist:**\n\\u2705 All tools have production-quality descriptions with usage/non-usage guidance\n\\u2705 Error responses are actionable (not generic)\n\\u2705 Server is registered in the central registry\n\\u2705 Health check tool exists\n\\u2705 Versioning strategy defined\n\\u2705 Monitoring and alerting configured\n\nLet's dive into the operational side — scaling, monitoring, and testing your MCP servers."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Health Check Endpoint",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Add a health check tool to your MCP server. Create a tool called 'health_check' that returns a JSON string with 'status' ('healthy'), 'version' ('1.0.0'), and 'tools_available' (the number 3). This lets monitoring systems verify the server is running.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('ProductionServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef health_check() -> str:\n    '''Check if the server is healthy and responding.\n    Returns server status, version, and number of available tools.'''\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('ProductionServer')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef health_check() -> str:\n    '''Check if the server is healthy and responding.\n    Returns server status, version, and number of available tools.'''\n    return json.dumps({\n        'status': 'healthy',\n        'version': '1.0.0',\n        'tools_available': 3\n    })",
            "hint": "Use json.dumps() to return a dictionary with status, version, and tools_available as a JSON string.",
            "explanation": "Health check tools let monitoring systems verify MCP servers are running and responsive. In production, you'd also check database connections, external API availability, and memory usage. The structured JSON response is easy for monitoring tools to parse.",
            "concepts": [
              "Health Checks",
              "Production Monitoring",
              "JSON Responses"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "def health_check.*json\\.dumps.*status.*healthy.*version"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scaling, Monitoring & Error Design",
            "explanation": "Your MCP server works locally. Now it needs to handle 100 concurrent connections, recover from crashes, and alert you when something's wrong. Let's talk about operations.\n\n**The Power Grid Analogy:**\n\nA power grid doesn't just generate electricity — it monitors load, balances demand, detects failures, and reroutes power automatically. Your MCP infrastructure needs the same operational maturity.\n\n**Scaling MCP Servers:**\n\nThe scaling strategy depends on your transport:\n\n| Transport | Scaling Model | How It Works |\n|---|---|---|\n| **stdio** | Per-user processes | The host spawns a new server process per connection. Scales naturally — each user gets their own server instance |\n| **HTTP** | Standard web scaling | Kubernetes, Docker, auto-scaling groups behind a load balancer. Multiple replicas handle concurrent connections |\n\n```yaml\n# docker-compose.yml for scaling an HTTP MCP server\nservices:\n  mcp-github:\n    build: ./mcp-github-server\n    ports:\n      - '8001:8000'\n    environment:\n      - GITHUB_TOKEN=${GITHUB_TOKEN}\n    deploy:\n      replicas: 3       # 3 instances behind a load balancer\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD\", \"curl\", \"-f\", \"http://localhost:8000/health\"]\n      interval: 30s\n      timeout: 10s\n      retries: 3\n```\n\n**The Four Metrics You Must Monitor:**\n\n| Metric | What It Tells You | Alert Threshold |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Request latency** (p50, p95, p99) | How long tool calls take | p95 > 5 seconds |\n| **Error rate** | Percentage of tool calls that fail | > 5% in 5 minutes |\n| **Call volume** | How many tool calls per minute | Sudden spike (3x normal) |\n| **Active connections** | How many clients are connected | Near server capacity |\n\n**Monitoring Implementation:**\n```python\nimport time\nimport logging\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nmetrics = defaultdict(list)\n\ndef track_tool_call(tool_name, func):\n    '''Decorator to track tool call metrics.'''\n    async def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):\n        start = time.time()\n        try:\n            result = await func(*args, **kwargs)\n            duration = time.time() - start\n            metrics['latency'].append((tool_name, duration))\n            metrics['success'].append(tool_name)\n            return result\n        except Exception as e:\n            metrics['errors'].append((tool_name, str(e)))\n            raise\n    return wrapper\n```\n\n**Testing MCP Servers at Three Levels:**\n\n**Level 1: Unit Tests** — Test individual tool functions with pytest\n```python\nimport pytest\n\ndef test_add_tool():\n    result = add(5, 3)\n    assert result == 8\n\ndef test_add_negative():\n    result = add(-5, 3)\n    assert result == -2\n\ndef test_divide_by_zero():\n    result = divide(10, 0)\n    assert 'Error' in result  # Should return error, not crash\n```\n\n**Level 2: Integration Tests** — Use the MCP Inspector to verify protocol compliance\n```bash\nnpx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector python my_server.py\n# Then manually test: initialize, tools/list, tools/call\n```\n\n**Level 3: End-to-End Tests** — Connect a real LLM client and verify the full flow\n```python\nasync def test_e2e():\n    '''Full end-to-end test with a real LLM.'''\n    client = MultiServerMCPClient({'math': {'command': 'python', 'args': ['math_server.py']}})\n    tools = await client.get_tools()\n    \n    agent = create_react_agent(ChatOpenAI(model='gpt-4o-mini'), tools)\n    result = await agent.ainvoke({'messages': [('user', 'What is 15 + 27?')]})\n    \n    assert '42' in result['messages'][-1].content\n```\n\n**Error Design Philosophy:**\nIn production MCP, errors are not failures — they're **communication**. Every error message is a message to the LLM about what went wrong and how to fix it. Design your errors as carefully as you design your tools.\n\n**The Operations Checklist:**\n\\u2705 Metrics collection (latency, error rate, call volume)\n\\u2705 Alerting on anomalies (error spikes, latency degradation)\n\\u2705 Unit tests for all tool functions\n\\u2705 Integration tests with MCP Inspector\n\\u2705 Docker/K8s deployment for HTTP servers\n\\u2705 Health check endpoints\n\\u2705 Graceful shutdown handling\n\nNow let's learn the most practical skill for day-to-day MCP development — debugging with the Inspector tool."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Structured Error Response Tool",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "question": "Create an MCP tool called 'get_user_profile' that takes a user_id (str). If the user_id is empty, return an error suggesting the LLM ask the user for their ID. If user_id is 'admin', return a mock admin profile. For any other user_id, return an error suggesting the LLM try the 'search_users' tool instead.",
            "starterCode": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('UserService')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_user_profile(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Get a user profile by their unique ID.\n    Returns user details or a helpful error message.'''\n    ___",
            "solution": "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP\nimport json\n\nmcp = FastMCP('UserService')\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef get_user_profile(user_id: str) -> str:\n    '''Get a user profile by their unique ID.\n    Returns user details or a helpful error message.'''\n    if not user_id.strip():\n        return 'Error: user_id is empty. Please ask the user for their ID.'\n    if user_id == 'admin':\n        return json.dumps({'id': 'admin', 'name': 'Admin User', 'role': 'administrator'})\n    return f'Error: No user found with ID \"{user_id}\". Try using the search_users tool to find users by name instead.'",
            "hint": "Check for empty user_id first, then handle the 'admin' case with json.dumps, then return a helpful error for unknown users suggesting an alternative tool.",
            "explanation": "Good error messages tell the LLM what went wrong AND what to do next. 'Try using search_users' gives the agent a concrete next step. This self-correcting behavior makes your MCP tools much more useful in agentic workflows.",
            "concepts": [
