          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Capstone Project: Build a RAG-Powered Documentation Assistant",
            "explanation": "**Your Capstone Mission:**\n\nBuild a complete RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) system exposed as an MCP server, letting any MCP-compatible AI app ask questions about technical documentation and get accurate, cited answers. This project combines everything you've learned in this module — server building, tool design, error handling, security, and production patterns.\n\n**Why This Is the Perfect MCP Capstone:**\nUnlike a standalone RAG app, your documentation assistant will be an MCP server. This means Claude Desktop, Cursor, VS Code, and any other MCP client can instantly use it. Build once, use everywhere — that's the MCP promise in action.\n\n**What You Will Build:**\nA \"Documentation Assistant MCP Server\" that ingests technical docs, chunks and embeds them, stores them in a vector database, and exposes search and Q&A tools via MCP.\n\n**Architecture:**\n```\nMCP Server: DocAssistant\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Tool: ingest_document(url_or_path)\n  \\u2502     \\u2514\\u2500 Load \\u2192 Chunk \\u2192 Embed \\u2192 Store in ChromaDB\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Tool: search_docs(query, top_k=5)\n  \\u2502     \\u2514\\u2500 Embed query \\u2192 Vector search \\u2192 Return relevant chunks\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Tool: ask_docs(question)\n  \\u2502     \\u2514\\u2500 Search \\u2192 Build context \\u2192 LLM generates answer with citations\n  \\u251c\\u2500 Resource: docs://stats\n  \\u2502     \\u2514\\u2500 Total documents, chunks, last updated\n  \\u2514\\u2500 Prompt: detailed_analysis(topic)\n        \\u2514\\u2500 Template for in-depth documentation analysis\n```\n\n**Project Requirements — Build All Five:**\n\n**1. Document Ingestion Pipeline:**\n   - Load documents from multiple formats (PDF, Markdown, HTML)\n   - Implement intelligent chunking (respect section boundaries, not just fixed-size)\n   - Handle tables, code blocks, and nested headers properly\n   - Deduplicate chunks to prevent redundant storage\n\n**2. Embedding & Storage:**\n   - Generate embeddings using `text-embedding-3-small` (or similar)\n   - Store in ChromaDB (local, easy to set up)\n   - Add metadata: source file, section heading, page number, chunk index\n\n**3. MCP Tools (at minimum):**\n```python\n@mcp.tool()\nasync def ingest_document(source: str) -> str:\n    '''Ingest a document into the knowledge base.\n    source: URL or file path to a document (PDF, MD, HTML).\n    Returns the number of chunks created and stored.'''\n    ...\n\n@mcp.tool()\ndef search_docs(query: str, top_k: int = 5) -> str:\n    '''Search the documentation for relevant passages.\n    Use for finding specific information in the knowledge base.\n    Returns the top matching chunks with source citations.'''\n    ...\n\n@mcp.tool()\nasync def ask_docs(question: str) -> str:\n    '''Ask a question about the documentation and get an answer.\n    Uses RAG: retrieves relevant context, then generates an answer.\n    Includes citations. Returns \"I don't know\" if the docs don't cover the topic.'''\n    ...\n```\n\n**4. Error Handling & Security:**\n   - Validate all file paths (prevent path traversal attacks)\n   - Rate limit ingestion (prevent DoS via massive document uploads)\n   - Return actionable error messages for the LLM\n   - Log all tool calls to stderr\n\n**5. Evaluation:**\n   - Create 20 test questions with known answers from your documentation\n   - Measure retrieval accuracy (are the right chunks found?)\n   - Measure answer quality (does the LLM answer correctly with proper citations?)\n\n**Example Interaction (via Claude Desktop):**\n```\nUser: \"How do I create a virtual environment in Python?\"\nClaude (using your MCP server):\n  \"To create a virtual environment in Python, use the venv module:\n\n    python -m venv myenv\n\n  To activate it:\n  - Windows: myenv\\\\Scripts\\\\activate\n  - Mac/Linux: source myenv/bin/activate\n\n  [Source: python-docs/tutorial/venv.md, Section: Creating Virtual Environments]\"\n```\n\n**Stretch Goals:**\n- Add conversational memory (follow-up questions use previous context)\n- Implement a document update pipeline (re-index when files change)\n- Add a Streamlit web UI alongside the MCP interface\n- Implement hybrid search (semantic + keyword) with re-ranking\n\n**How This Ties Everything Together:**\n\n| Module Concept | How It's Used |\n|---|---|\n| FastMCP server | The foundation of the entire project |\n| Tool design | search_docs, ask_docs, ingest_document |\n| Resources | docs://stats for metadata |\n| Prompts | detailed_analysis template |\n| Error handling | Validate inputs, actionable error messages |\n| Security | Path validation, rate limiting |\n| Logging | stderr logging for debugging |\n| Inspector | Testing and debugging during development |\n\nThis capstone covers the complete MCP development lifecycle — from server creation to production deployment. Build it, and you'll have both a portfolio piece AND a genuinely useful tool that any MCP client can use.\n\n**Congratulations on completing Module 13!** \\ud83c\\udf89 You now understand how to build, test, secure, and deploy MCP servers. The MCP ecosystem is growing explosively — every server you build makes the entire AI tool ecosystem richer."
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M014-backend-development",
      "title": "Module 14: Backend Development",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is a Server? (The Restaurant Analogy)",
            "explanation": "Before we write a single line of backend code, let's understand the fundamental concept: What IS a server, and why do you need one?\n\nLet me paint a picture. Right now, your AI models — those brilliant LangChain chains, those fine-tuned models — they all live on YOUR laptop. They only work when YOUR computer is running. That's like a world-famous chef who only cooks in their own kitchen and never opens a restaurant. Nobody else gets to taste their food!\n\n**🧒 The Restaurant Analogy:**\n\nThink of the internet as a giant food court.\n\n- **You (the client)** = A hungry customer sitting at a table\n- **The server** = The kitchen that prepares your food\n- **The API** = The waiter who takes your order and brings back food\n- **The request** = Your order (\"I want a pizza\")\n- **The response** = The food that arrives at your table\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_server_restaurant.png\" alt=\"Server Restaurant Analogy\" />\n\n**What a Server Actually Does:**\nA server is just a computer that's always on, always connected to the internet, and always listening for requests. When you visit `chatgpt.com`, your browser sends a request to OpenAI's server. That server processes your request, generates a response, and sends it back to your browser.\n\nHere's the key insight: **a server is not a special machine.** It's just a regular computer running a program that listens on a port. Your laptop could be a server right now — and in a few minutes, it will be!\n\n**Why AI Engineers Need Backend Skills:**\nYou've built amazing AI models and chains with LangChain. But right now, they only work on YOUR laptop. To let other people use your AI, you need a server that:\n\n1. **Listens** for incoming requests 24/7\n2. **Receives** user inputs (questions, images, documents)\n3. **Runs** your AI model on those inputs\n4. **Sends back** the AI's response\n\nThat's exactly what we'll build in this module — a production-ready backend that serves your AI to the world.\n\n**The Full Flow:**\n```\nUser's Browser → HTTP Request → Your Server → AI Model → Response → User's Browser\n```\n\nEvery AI product you use — ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Midjourney — works exactly this way. A frontend sends requests to a backend server, the server runs the AI, and sends back results. By the end of this module, you'll know how to build this entire pipeline yourself."
          }
        },
