        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is an API? (The Waiter Analogy)",
            "explanation": "Okay, so you understand that a server is a kitchen that cooks food. But how does the customer (your frontend) actually communicate with the kitchen? They can't just walk into the kitchen and start grabbing food — that would be chaos!\n\nThey need a **waiter** — someone who speaks both languages. The customer says \"I'd like the pasta,\" the waiter translates that into a kitchen ticket, the kitchen cooks it, and the waiter brings back the plate.\n\n**🧒 The Waiter Analogy:**\n\nAn **API (Application Programming Interface)** is that waiter. It's the messenger that takes requests from one piece of software and delivers them to another.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_api_waiter.png\" alt=\"API Waiter Analogy\" />\n\n**Real-World APIs You Use Every Day:**\nEvery time you check the weather on your phone, your weather app sends a request to a weather API. Every time you log in with Google, the website talks to Google's authentication API. Every time ChatGPT responds to you, your browser talks to OpenAI's chat API.\n\n**What Makes a Good API?**\nThink of it like a restaurant menu. A good menu:\n- Lists exactly what's available (endpoints)\n- Describes each dish (documentation)\n- Tells you the price (rate limits)\n- Has clear categories (URL structure)\n\nA good API does the same:\n```\nGET    /menu              → See all available dishes (list items)\nPOST   /order             → Place a new order (create item)\nGET    /order/42          → Check on order #42 (read item)\nPUT    /order/42          → Change order #42 (update item)\nDELETE /order/42          → Cancel order #42 (delete item)\n```\n\n**🔍 The Key Insight:**\nAPIs are just **contracts**. They say: \"If you send me THIS kind of request, I promise to send you THAT kind of response.\" Your frontend and backend don't need to know ANYTHING about each other's internals — they just need to agree on the contract.\n\nThis is incredibly powerful. It means you can build your AI backend in Python, and your frontend in React, and they'll work together perfectly — as long as the API contract is clear.\n\n**REST (Representational State Transfer):**\nThe most common API style is called REST. It uses standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and URLs to organize resources. Every API you'll build in this module follows REST principles."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive_simulation",
