          "type": "reflection",
          "content": {
            "title": "Reflect: The Client-Server Model",
            "prompt": "Think about an AI application you use daily (like ChatGPT, Google Search, or a smart assistant). Can you trace the client-server flow?\n\n1. What is the 'client' in this case?\n2. What data does it send to the server?\n3. What does the server do with that data?\n4. What comes back in the response?\n\nWrite your thoughts below — understanding this flow is the foundation of everything we'll build!",
            "minLength": 50
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is FastAPI? Your First Backend Framework",
            "explanation": "Alright, you understand servers, APIs, HTTP methods, and JSON. Now let's pick the tool we'll actually BUILD with. And the choice is easy — **FastAPI**.\n\n**🧒 The Power Tool Analogy:**\n\nYou COULD build a house with a hand saw and a hammer. That's Flask — it works, it's simple, but you do everything manually. Or you could use power tools — a circular saw, a nail gun, laser levels. That's FastAPI — same job, 10x faster, with built-in safety features.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_fastapi_framework.png\" alt=\"FastAPI Framework\" />\n\nFastAPI was created in 2018 by Sebastián Ramírez, and it exploded in popularity because it solved every pain point that Flask developers had been complaining about for years.\n\n**Why FastAPI is Perfect for AI Engineers:**\n\n1. **🚀 Blazing Fast** — FastAPI is one of the fastest Python frameworks (on par with Node.js and Go). When your AI model takes 3 seconds to respond, you don't want the framework adding another 500ms.\n\n2. **📚 Auto Documentation** — Visit `/docs` and you get a beautiful, interactive API documentation page FOR FREE. You can test your endpoints right in the browser.\n\n3. **🛡️ Type Safety with Pydantic** — If someone sends `\"temperature\": \"hot\"` when your endpoint expects a float, FastAPI rejects it automatically with a clear error message. No more debugging mysterious crashes.\n\n4. **⚡ Async by Default** — FastAPI supports `async/await` natively. This is CRITICAL for AI apps because your server can handle hundreds of users while waiting for slow AI API calls.\n\n**Your First FastAPI App (5 lines!):**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.get('/hello')\ndef say_hello():\n    return {'message': 'Hello, World!'}\n```\n\n**Running It:**\n```bash\npip install fastapi uvicorn\nuvicorn main:app --reload\n```\n\nVisit `http://localhost:8000/hello` and you'll see:\n```json\n{\"message\": \"Hello, World!\"}\n```\n\nVisit `http://localhost:8000/docs` and you'll see beautiful, auto-generated Swagger documentation.\n\n**That's it.** Five lines of code and you have a production-grade API server running. Compare that to setting up Express.js in Node or Spring Boot in Java — FastAPI is in a league of its own for developer experience."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "Anatomy of Your First FastAPI App",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "Line 1: Import",
                "text": "'from fastapi import FastAPI' — This brings the FastAPI tool into your code. Think of it like grabbing the restaurant blueprint from the shelf."
              },
              {
                "label": "Line 2: Create the App",
                "text": "'app = FastAPI()' — This creates your API server. You just opened your restaurant! The variable 'app' IS your server."
              },
              {
                "label": "Line 3: The Decorator",
                "text": "'@app.get(\"/\")' — This is called a decorator. It says: 'When someone visits the path /, run the function below.' Think of it as adding an item to your menu."
              },
              {
                "label": "Line 4: The Function",
