        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "POST Requests & Pydantic Models (Placing an Order)",
            "explanation": "So far, all our endpoints have been GET requests — the server just SENDS data. But what about when the user needs to SEND data TO the server? Like submitting a chat message, uploading a file, or creating an account?\n\nThat's where **POST requests** come in. And FastAPI has a secret weapon for handling incoming data safely: **Pydantic models**.\n\n**🧒 The Order Form Analogy:**\n\nImagine a restaurant where customers don't just say what they want — they fill out an order form. The form has specific fields: Dish Name (text), Quantity (number), Special Instructions (optional text). If someone writes \"banana\" in the Quantity field, the waiter rejects the form immediately: \"Sir, that's not a number.\"\n\nPydantic models ARE that order form. They define exactly what data your endpoint expects, what type each field should be, and which fields are required.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_post_pydantic.png\" alt=\"POST Requests and Pydantic Models\" />\n\n**Defining Your Data Model:**\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom typing import Optional\n\nclass ChatRequest(BaseModel):\n    message: str                          # Required\n    model: str = 'gpt-4o'               # Optional, has default\n    temperature: float = 0.7             # Optional, has default\n    max_tokens: Optional[int] = None     # Optional, no default\n```\n\n**Using It in an Endpoint:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\napp = FastAPI()\nclient = OpenAI()\n\n@app.post('/chat')\ndef chat(request: ChatRequest):\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=request.model,\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': request.message}],\n        temperature=request.temperature,\n        max_tokens=request.max_tokens\n    )\n    return {\n        'response': response.choices[0].message.content,\n        'model': request.model,\n        'tokens_used': response.usage.total_tokens\n    }\n```\n\n**What Happens When Someone Sends Bad Data:**\nIf a user sends `{\"message\": 123, \"temperature\": \"hot\"}`, Pydantic immediately rejects it:\n```json\n{\n    \"detail\": [\n        {\"loc\": [\"body\", \"message\"], \"msg\": \"str type expected\", \"type\": \"type_error.str\"},\n        {\"loc\": [\"body\", \"temperature\"], \"msg\": \"value is not a valid float\", \"type\": \"type_error.float\"}\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n**🔑 Why This is CRITICAL for AI Apps:**\nWithout Pydantic, bad input would reach your AI model, waste API credits, and potentially crash your app. With Pydantic, invalid requests get caught at the DOOR — before any expensive AI processing happens. Think of it as a free security guard.\n\n**GET vs POST — When to Use Which:**\n- **GET** = Fetching data. Parameters go in the URL: `/search?query=AI`\n- **POST** = Sending data. Parameters go in the request BODY (hidden from the URL)\n\nUse POST whenever the data is complex, sensitive, or longer than a URL can handle. Chat messages, login credentials, file uploads — always POST."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
