        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Async & Await — Handling Multiple Customers at Once",
            "explanation": "This is one of the most important concepts in backend development, and it's ESPECIALLY critical for AI applications. Here's why.\n\nWhen your server calls the OpenAI API, it takes 2-5 seconds to get a response. During those 5 seconds, what does your server do? With **synchronous** code, it sits there. Frozen. Doing absolutely nothing. And if 100 users send requests at the same time? 99 of them are just... waiting.\n\n**🧒 The Restaurant Waiter Analogy (Again!):**\n\nImagine a restaurant with ONE waiter.\n\n**Synchronous waiter (sync):** Takes an order from Table 1, walks to the kitchen, STANDS THERE watching the chef cook for 20 minutes, brings the food back, THEN walks to Table 2. Tables 3-10 are furious.\n\n**Asynchronous waiter (async):** Takes an order from Table 1, drops it in the kitchen, immediately walks to Table 2 to take their order, drops it in the kitchen, moves to Table 3... When Table 1's food is ready, they grab it on the way past. All 10 tables get served efficiently by ONE waiter.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_async_await.png\" alt=\"Sync vs Async\" />\n\n**The Code Difference:**\n```python\n# ❌ SYNCHRONOUS — Server is FROZEN during the AI call\n@app.post('/chat')\ndef chat_sync(message: str):\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(  # Blocks for 3 seconds\n        model='gpt-4o',\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': message}]\n    )\n    return {'response': response.choices[0].message.content}\n\n# ✅ ASYNCHRONOUS — Server handles other requests while waiting\n@app.post('/chat')\nasync def chat_async(message: str):\n    response = await async_client.chat.completions.create(  # Non-blocking!\n        model='gpt-4o',\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': message}]\n    )\n    return {'response': response.choices[0].message.content}\n```\n\n**The Two Magic Keywords:**\n1. `async` — Put this before `def` to declare that this function CAN pause and resume\n2. `await` — Put this before any slow operation to say \"start this, but let other requests run while I wait\"\n\n**🔍 Why This MATTERS for AI:**\nAI API calls are the SLOWEST thing your server does. A database query takes 5ms. An OpenAI call takes 3000ms. That's 600x slower. Without async, your server can only handle ONE AI request at a time. With async, it can handle HUNDREDS simultaneously.\n\n**The Math:**\n- Sync server, 1 worker: 1 request every 3 seconds = 20 requests/minute\n- Async server, 1 worker: 100 concurrent requests = 2,000 requests/minute\n\nThat's a 100x improvement with ZERO extra hardware. This is why FastAPI's async support is a game-changer for AI applications.\n\n**⚠️ Common Mistake:**\nDon't mix sync and async carelessly. If you use `async def` but then call a synchronous function inside (without await), you'll block the entire event loop and lose all the benefits."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "board_video",
          "content": {
            "title": "Sync vs Async — The Restaurant Example",
            "avatar": "Human",
            "voice": true,
            "items": [
