        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "FastAPI vs Flask — Choosing Your Tool",
            "explanation": "You might be wondering: \"Why FastAPI and not Flask? Isn't Flask more popular?\" Great question. Let me give you the honest comparison.\n\n**🧒 The Tool Comparison Analogy:**\n\nFlask is like a Swiss Army knife — it's been around forever (2010), it's reliable, everyone knows how to use it, and it gets the job done. But it doesn't have power tools built in.\n\nFastAPI is like a modern multi-tool with a built-in laser level, digital display, and USB charging — newer (2018), more features out of the box, and designed for modern use cases.\n\n**The Honest Comparison:**\n\n| Feature | Flask | FastAPI |\n|---------|-------|---------|\n| **Speed** | Moderate | One of the fastest |\n| **Async Support** | Add-on (hacky) | Native, first-class |\n| **Data Validation** | Manual (you write it) | Automatic (Pydantic) |\n| **API Documentation** | Manual (Swagger add-on) | Auto-generated at /docs |\n| **Type Hints** | Optional | Required (and useful!) |\n| **Learning Curve** | Very gentle | Gentle (slightly steeper) |\n| **Community** | Massive (13+ years) | Growing fast (6+ years) |\n| **Best For** | Simple web apps, MVPs | APIs, AI backends, microservices |\n\n**When to Choose Flask:**\n- Building a simple web app with HTML templates\n- Your team already knows Flask\n- You need maximum ecosystem/plugin support\n\n**When to Choose FastAPI (our choice):**\n- Building an AI backend or API\n- You need async for handling slow AI calls\n- You want automatic request validation\n- You want auto-generated API docs\n- You're starting a new project\n\n**The Trend Is Clear:**\nFastAPI is now the #1 most-loved Python web framework on Stack Overflow. It's used by Microsoft, Netflix, Uber, and most AI startups. For AI engineering specifically, it's the obvious choice because of async support and Pydantic integration.\n\n**💡 The Good News:**\nIf you learn FastAPI, you can learn Flask in an afternoon. They share 80% of the same concepts. The reverse isn't quite as easy because FastAPI adds async patterns and type safety that Flask doesn't have.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "vsComparison",
                "content": {
                  "left": {
                    "title": "Flask",
                    "points": [
                      "Synchronous by default — blocks during AI calls",
                      "Manual request parsing with request.get_json()",
                      "No built-in validation — you write your own checks",
                      "No auto-generated docs — needs Flask-Swagger extension",
                      "Mature ecosystem with thousands of extensions",
                      "Simpler learning curve for basic web apps"
                    ]
                  },
                  "right": {
                    "title": "FastAPI",
                    "points": [
                      "Async native — handles 1000s of concurrent AI calls",
                      "Pydantic models auto-parse and validate requests",
                      "Type-safe with full IDE autocomplete support",
                      "Auto-generated Swagger docs at /docs",
                      "Built for modern APIs and microservices",
                      "3x faster performance (Starlette + Uvicorn)"
                    ]
                  }
                }
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Streaming Responses — Why AI Feels Instant",
            "explanation": "Have you ever noticed how ChatGPT's responses appear word by word, like someone is typing? That's not a gimmick — it's a critical engineering technique called **streaming**. And it completely transforms the user experience.\n\n**🧒 The Sushi Conveyor Belt Analogy:**\n\nOption A: You order 10 pieces of sushi. The chef makes ALL 10 in the kitchen, then brings them out together 15 minutes later. You sit there hungry, staring at nothing.\n\nOption B: The chef puts each piece on a conveyor belt AS IT'S READY. The first piece arrives in 30 seconds. You start eating while the rest are still being made.\n\nSame total wait time. But Option B FEELS instant because you get immediate feedback.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_streaming.png\" alt=\"Streaming vs Normal Response\" />\n\n**Why Streaming Matters for AI:**\nWithout streaming, your user sees:\n```\n[User sends message] → [3 second blank screen] → [BOOM: entire response appears at once]\n```\n\nWith streaming, your user sees:\n```\n[User sends message] → [0.3s] \"The\" → \"answer\" → \"to\" → \"your\" → \"question\" → \"is...\"\n```\n\n**How Streaming Works in FastAPI:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nimport asyncio\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nasync def generate_words():\n    \"\"\"Yield one word at a time, simulating AI streaming.\"\"\"\n    words = \"The answer to your question is that AI streaming works by sending data in chunks\".split()\n    for word in words:\n        yield word + \" \"\n        await asyncio.sleep(0.1)  # Simulate processing time\n\n@app.get('/stream')\nasync def stream_response():\n    return StreamingResponse(\n        generate_words(),\n        media_type='text/plain'\n    )\n```\n\n**🔍 The Key Concept: `yield` vs `return`**\n- `return` = \"Here's ALL the data. I'm done.\" (Normal response)\n- `yield` = \"Here's a PIECE of data. I'll give you more soon.\" (Streaming)\n\nA function with `yield` is called a **generator**. It pauses after each yield, sends the chunk to the client, then resumes from where it left off. This is the backbone of every streaming AI application.\n\n**Server-Sent Events (SSE) — The Production Pattern:**\nFor real AI streaming, we use SSE (Server-Sent Events). It's a standard protocol designed for servers to push data to clients:\n```python\nasync def stream_ai_response(message: str):\n    response = await client.chat.completions.create(\n        model='gpt-4o',\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': message}],\n        stream=True  # This is the magic flag!\n    )\n    async for chunk in response:\n        if chunk.choices[0].delta.content:\n            yield f\"data: {chunk.choices[0].delta.content}\\n\\n\"\n```\n\nThis is exactly how ChatGPT, Claude, and every modern AI chat interface works behind the scenes."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Normal Response vs Streaming Response",
            "description": "Streaming doesn't make the AI faster — it makes the EXPERIENCE faster. Users start reading immediately instead of staring at a loading spinner.",
            "type": "lcel_pipeline",
            "steps": [
              {
                "name": "Normal Response (No Streaming)",
                "input": "User: \"Explain Python in detail\"",
                "processing": "AI generates entire answer (10 seconds)...",
                "output": "User waits... waits... waits...\n⏳ 10 seconds later → FULL answer appears at once",
                "description": "Without streaming, the user stares at a blank screen until the ENTIRE response is ready. Time to first word: ~10 seconds.",
                "outputType": "BLOCKING"
              },
              {
                "name": "Streaming Response (SSE)",
                "input": "User: \"Explain Python in detail\"",
                "processing": "AI generates tokens one at a time...",
                "output": "⚡ 0.5s → \"Python\"\n⚡ 0.6s → \"Python is\"\n⚡ 0.7s → \"Python is a\"\n⚡ ...building up word by word...",
                "description": "With streaming, words appear in real-time as the AI generates them. Time to first word: ~0.5 seconds!",
                "outputType": "STREAMING"
              }
            ],
            "codePanel": {
              "title": "Streaming Code",
              "code": "from fastapi.responses import (\n    StreamingResponse\n)\n\nasync def stream_ai():\n    stream = await client.chat\\\n        .completions.create(\n        model='gpt-4o-mini',\n        messages=[...],\n        stream=True\n    )\n    async for chunk in stream:\n        content = chunk.choices[0]\\\n            .delta.content\n        if content:\n            yield f'data: {content}\\n\\n'\n    yield 'data: [DONE]\\n\\n'"
            },
            "typeFlowPanel": {
              "title": "Comparison",
              "flow": [
                "Normal: 10s to first word",
                "Streaming: 0.5s to first word",
                "Same total time",
                "Streaming feels 20x faster",
                "All AI chatbots use streaming"
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "teacher_vis",
          "content": {
            "title": "Understanding yield vs return",
            "sections": [
              {
                "label": "return",
                "text": "A normal function uses 'return'. It gives you the ENTIRE answer at once and the function ENDS. Like a chef who cooks the ENTIRE meal and brings it all out at once."
              },
              {
                "label": "yield",
                "text": "A generator function uses 'yield'. It gives you ONE PIECE at a time. After each yield, the function PAUSES (not ends). When you ask for the next piece, it continues from where it left off."
              },
              {
                "label": "Why yield for streaming?",
                "text": "Streaming needs to send data piece by piece. 'yield' is perfect because it lets you send one word, pause, send the next word, pause, and so on — instead of waiting for the whole response."
              },
              {
                "label": "Example",
                "code": "# Regular function with return:\ndef get_all_words():\n    return ['Hello', 'World']  # Everything at once\n\n# Generator function with yield:\ndef get_words_one_by_one():\n    yield 'Hello'  # Send first word, pause\n    yield 'World'  # Send second word, pause"
              },
              {
                "label": "SSE Format",
                "text": "Each piece must follow the SSE format: start with 'data: ', contain the content, and end with TWO newlines (\\n\\n). Example: 'data: Hello\\n\\n'. This format tells the browser where each piece starts and ends."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Build a Streaming Countdown",
            "question": "Create a streaming endpoint at GET '/countdown' that streams numbers from 5 down to 1, one at a time, using the SSE format. Each yielded message should be: 'data: {number}\\n\\n'. After the countdown, yield 'data: Blast off!\\n\\n'.",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nimport time\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef countdown_generator():\n    # TODO: yield numbers 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 in SSE format\n    # Then yield 'Blast off!' in SSE format\n    pass\n\n@app.get(\"/countdown\")\ndef countdown():\n    return StreamingResponse(\n        countdown_generator(),\n        media_type=\"text/event-stream\"\n    )",
            "solution": "from fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nimport time\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\ndef countdown_generator():\n    for i in range(5, 0, -1):\n        yield f\"data: {i}\\n\\n\"\n        time.sleep(1)\n    yield \"data: Blast off!\\n\\n\"\n\n@app.get(\"/countdown\")\ndef countdown():\n    return StreamingResponse(\n        countdown_generator(),\n        media_type=\"text/event-stream\"\n    )",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use a for loop with range(5, 0, -1) to count down. Use yield with f-strings to format each number in SSE format: f\"data: {number}\\n\\n\""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Streaming AI Responses — The Real Thing",
            "explanation": "You've learned the theory of streaming. Now let's build the REAL thing — streaming responses from OpenAI through your FastAPI server to a frontend. This is exactly how ChatGPT works.\n\n**The Full Stack Streaming Pipeline:**\n```\nUser types message → React Frontend → FastAPI Server → OpenAI API\n                                                          ↓\nUser sees words appear ← React Frontend ← FastAPI streams ← OpenAI streams tokens\n```\n\n**Server-Side: Streaming from OpenAI:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\n\napp = FastAPI()\nclient = AsyncOpenAI()\n\nasync def stream_openai(message: str):\n    \"\"\"Stream tokens from OpenAI one at a time.\"\"\"\n    response = await client.chat.completions.create(\n        model='gpt-4o',\n        messages=[\n            {'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a helpful assistant.'},\n            {'role': 'user', 'content': message}\n        ],\n        stream=True  # This is the magic flag!\n    )\n\n    async for chunk in response:\n        content = chunk.choices[0].delta.content\n        if content:\n            yield f\"data: {content}\\n\\n\"  # SSE format\n\n    yield \"data: [DONE]\\n\\n\"  # Signal that streaming is complete\n\n@app.post('/chat/stream')\nasync def chat_stream(message: str):\n    return StreamingResponse(\n        stream_openai(message),\n        media_type='text/event-stream',\n        headers={\n            'Cache-Control': 'no-cache',\n            'Connection': 'keep-alive'\n        }\n    )\n```\n\n**Client-Side: Reading the Stream in JavaScript:**\n```javascript\nconst response = await fetch('/chat/stream', {\n    method: 'POST',\n    headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},\n    body: JSON.stringify({message: 'Explain quantum computing'})\n});\n\nconst reader = response.body.getReader();\nconst decoder = new TextDecoder();\n\nwhile (true) {\n    const {done, value} = await reader.read();\n    if (done) break;\n\n    const text = decoder.decode(value);\n    // Append each chunk to the UI as it arrives\n    document.getElementById('response').textContent += text;\n}\n```\n\n**🔍 What's Happening Under the Hood:**\n1. Your frontend sends a POST request to `/chat/stream`\n2. FastAPI opens a connection to OpenAI with `stream=True`\n3. OpenAI sends tokens one at a time (as they're generated by the model)\n4. FastAPI immediately forwards each token to the frontend (no buffering)\n5. The frontend appends each token to the UI in real-time\n6. When OpenAI sends `[DONE]`, the stream ends\n\n**💡 Pro Tip: Token Counting During Streaming**\nDuring streaming, you don't get the full `usage` object until the stream ends. If you need to track token usage, count tokens manually using `tiktoken` or wait for the final chunk which sometimes includes usage stats.\n\nThis streaming pattern is the backbone of every modern AI chat interface. Master it, and you can build ChatGPT-quality experiences."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "WebSockets — Real-Time Two-Way Chat",
            "explanation": "Streaming with SSE is great for one-way communication (server → client). But what about a REAL chat interface where BOTH sides need to send messages at any time? Like a real-time AI assistant where you can interrupt the AI mid-response?\n\nThat's where **WebSockets** come in.\n\n**🧒 The Phone Call vs. Letters Analogy:**\n\nRegular HTTP is like sending letters. You write a letter (request), mail it, wait for a reply (response). Each letter is a separate, independent transaction. You can't have a back-and-forth conversation without a lot of waiting.\n\nWebSockets are like a phone call. You dial once (open connection), and then both sides can talk at ANY TIME. No waiting for letters. Real-time, two-way communication.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_websockets.png\" alt=\"WebSockets Real-Time Communication\" />\n\n**WebSocket Chat in FastAPI:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, WebSocket, WebSocketDisconnect\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.websocket('/ws/chat')\nasync def chat_websocket(websocket: WebSocket):\n    await websocket.accept()  # \"Pick up the phone\"\n\n    try:\n        while True:  # Keep the line open\n            # Wait for user to send a message\n            message = await websocket.receive_text()\n\n            # Send a response back\n            ai_response = await get_ai_response(message)\n            await websocket.send_text(ai_response)\n\n    except WebSocketDisconnect:\n        print(\"User hung up the phone\")\n```\n\n**When to Use What:**\n| Feature | HTTP (REST) | SSE (Streaming) | WebSocket |\n|---------|-------------|-----------------|-----------|\n| Direction | Client → Server → Client | Server → Client | Both ways |\n| Connection | Opens and closes each time | Server pushes, client listens | Always open |\n| Best for | CRUD operations | AI token streaming | Real-time chat, games |\n| Complexity | Simple | Moderate | Higher |\n\n**🔍 For AI Applications:**\n- **REST** — Use for simple Q&A endpoints, file uploads, user management\n- **SSE** — Use for streaming AI responses (ChatGPT-style word-by-word output)\n- **WebSocket** — Use for interactive AI agents that need real-time back-and-forth (voice assistants, collaborative AI tools)\n\n**💡 Pro Tip:**\nMost AI chat apps actually use SSE for the AI response streaming and regular REST for everything else (login, history, settings). WebSockets are only needed when you truly need bidirectional real-time communication — like multiplayer games or collaborative document editing with AI."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What is the key difference between Server-Sent Events (SSE) and WebSockets?",
              "options": [
                "SSE is faster than WebSockets",
                "SSE is one-way (server → client), WebSockets are two-way",
                "WebSockets only work with AI applications",
                "SSE requires a special browser plugin"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "SSE (Server-Sent Events) is a one-way channel where only the server sends data to the client. WebSockets create a persistent two-way connection."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Authentication — Locking the Door to Your API",
            "explanation": "Imagine you built an amazing AI chatbot API. Each AI response costs you $0.01. Now imagine someone discovers your API URL and writes a script that sends 1 million requests. That's **$10,000** gone in minutes. This actually happens. All the time.\n\n**🧒 The Bouncer at a Club Analogy:**\n\nYour API is a VIP nightclub. Without a bouncer, anyone can walk in and drink your expensive champagne (make API calls on your dime). Authentication is the bouncer — they check IDs before letting anyone through the door.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_auth_bouncer.png\" alt=\"API Authentication Bouncer Analogy\" />\n\n**The Three Levels of Authentication:**\n\n**Level 1: API Keys (Simplest)**\nLike a physical key to a building. You give each user a unique key, and they include it in every request.\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Header\n\napp = FastAPI()\nVALID_API_KEYS = {'sk-abc123', 'sk-def456'}\n\n@app.post('/chat')\nasync def chat(\n    message: str,\n    x_api_key: str = Header(..., alias='X-API-Key')\n):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_API_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(\n            status_code=401,\n            detail='Invalid API key'\n        )\n    return {'response': 'Hello!'}\n```\n\n**How Clients Send the Key:**\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"http://localhost:8000/chat\" \\\n     -H \"X-API-Key: sk-abc123\" \\\n     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n     -d '{\"message\": \"Hello\"}'\n```\n\n**Level 2: JWT Tokens (For User Login)**\nMore sophisticated — we'll cover this in the next lesson.\n\n**Level 3: OAuth 2.0 (For \"Login with Google\")**\nThe most complex — used when you want users to log in with their Google/GitHub accounts.\n\n**🔑 When to Use What:**\n- **API Keys** — Machine-to-machine communication, developer access, simple apps\n- **JWT Tokens** — User login systems, mobile apps, web apps with sessions\n- **OAuth** — \"Sign in with Google/GitHub\" flows, third-party integrations\n\nFor most AI backends, you'll start with API keys (simple and effective) and add JWT when you need user accounts."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "JWT (JSON Web Tokens) — How Login Works",
            "explanation": "API keys work great for machine-to-machine communication. But for human users logging into your AI app — where they have usernames, passwords, and profiles — we use something much more elegant: **JWT (JSON Web Tokens)**.\n\n**🧒 The Concert Wristband Analogy:**\n\nWhen you go to a music festival, you show your ticket at the gate once. They give you a wristband. For the rest of the festival, you just flash your wristband — no need to show your ticket again. The wristband PROVES you paid, and it has your access level encoded on it (VIP vs General Admission).\n\nA JWT token IS that wristband. After login, the server creates a token (wristband) that contains the user's info. The user shows this token on every future request. The server can verify it WITHOUT checking a database — the signature on the token itself proves it's authentic.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_jwt_token.png\" alt=\"JWT Token Wristband Analogy\" />\n\n**The Login Flow:**\n1. User sends username + password\n2. Server verifies credentials against the database\n3. Server creates a JWT token and sends it back\n4. User includes this token in every future request\n5. Server validates the token (no database lookup needed!)\n\n**What's Inside a JWT?**\nA JWT has 3 parts separated by dots: `header.payload.signature`\n\n```python\n# The payload contains user info:\n{\n    \"user_id\": 42,\n    \"email\": \"alice@example.com\",\n    \"role\": \"premium\",\n    \"exp\": 1700000000  # Expiration timestamp\n}\n```\n\n**Creating JWTs in FastAPI:**\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\nimport jwt  # pip install PyJWT\n\nSECRET_KEY = 'your-secret-key-keep-this-safe'\nALGORITHM = 'HS256'\n\ndef create_token(user_id: int, email: str, role: str) -> str:\n    payload = {\n        'user_id': user_id,\n        'email': email,\n        'role': role,\n        'exp': datetime.utcnow() + timedelta(hours=24)\n    }\n    return jwt.encode(payload, SECRET_KEY, algorithm=ALGORITHM)\n\ndef verify_token(token: str) -> dict:\n    try:\n        return jwt.decode(token, SECRET_KEY, algorithms=[ALGORITHM])\n    except jwt.ExpiredSignatureError:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail='Token expired')\n    except jwt.InvalidTokenError:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail='Invalid token')\n```\n\n**🔍 Why JWTs are Perfect for AI Apps:**\n- **Stateless** — No need to store sessions in a database. The token itself contains all the info.\n- **Scalable** — Works perfectly with multiple servers (no shared session store needed)\n- **Contains user data** — You can put the user's role in the token to control access (e.g., free users get 10 requests/day, premium users get unlimited)\n\n**⚠️ Security Rules:**\n1. NEVER put the SECRET_KEY in your code — use environment variables\n2. ALWAYS set an expiration time — tokens should expire after 24 hours\n3. NEVER store sensitive data in the payload — JWTs are base64-encoded, not encrypted. Anyone can decode them."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Managing Secrets & Environment Variables (.env)",
            "explanation": "Okay, quick story time. In 2023, a developer at Samsung accidentally uploaded their entire codebase — including API keys — to ChatGPT. Those keys gave access to their internal servers. Game over. Career-defining mistake.\n\nAnd this happens ALL THE TIME. GitHub has bots that scan every single commit for exposed API keys. The moment you push your OpenAI key to a public repo, it gets stolen within MINUTES.\n\n**🧒 The Spare House Key Analogy:**\n\nImagine you have a spare key to your house. You wouldn't tape it to the front door with a sign saying \"KEY HERE,\" right? You'd hide it under a specific rock that only YOU know about.\n\nThat's exactly what a `.env` file is — your secret hiding spot. Your code knows where to find it, but it NEVER gets uploaded to GitHub.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_env_secrets.png\" alt=\"Environment Variables Security Analogy\" />\n\n**Step 1: Create a .env file:**\n```bash\n# .env (this file stays on YOUR machine only)\nOPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-abc123...\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/mydb\nJWT_SECRET=super-secret-key-never-share-this\nREDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379\nENVIRONMENT=development\n```\n\n**Step 2: Add .env to .gitignore (CRITICAL!):**\n```bash\n# .gitignore\n.env\n.env.local\n.env.production\n```\n\n**Step 3: Load secrets in your FastAPI app:**\n```python\nfrom pydantic_settings import BaseSettings\n\nclass Settings(BaseSettings):\n    openai_api_key: str\n    database_url: str\n    jwt_secret: str\n    redis_url: str = 'redis://localhost:6379'\n    environment: str = 'development'\n\n    class Config:\n        env_file = '.env'\n\nsettings = Settings()\n\n# Now use them safely:\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\nclient = AsyncOpenAI(api_key=settings.openai_api_key)\n```\n\n**Why Pydantic Settings is Better Than python-dotenv:**\n- ✅ **Type validation** — If `OPENAI_API_KEY` is missing, your app crashes at startup with a clear error, not 30 minutes later when a user hits the chat endpoint\n- ✅ **Default values** — Set sensible defaults for development\n- ✅ **Auto-documentation** — Your settings class IS the documentation\n\n**🔒 The Production Setup:**\nIn production (AWS, GCP, Railway), you don't use `.env` files at all. You set environment variables directly in the cloud dashboard. Your code doesn't change — `pydantic_settings` reads from the actual environment if no `.env` file exists.\n\nThis is the professional way every company handles secrets. No exceptions."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive_simulation",
          "mode": "pipeline",
          "content": {
            "title": "JWT Authentication Flow",
            "description": "Walk through the JWT login process and see how tokens are created and verified."
          },
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "Submit Credentials",
              "action": "login",
              "description": "User submits email and password via POST request."
            },
            {
              "label": "Verify & Sign",
              "action": "verify",
              "description": "Server verifies password and signs a new JWT token."
            },
            {
              "label": "Issue Token",
              "action": "issue",
              "description": "Server sends JWT token back to the client."
            },
            {
              "label": "Attach Header",
              "action": "attach",
              "description": "Client attaches 'Bearer <token>' in the Authorization header for future requests."
            },
            {
              "label": "Grant Access",
              "action": "access",
              "description": "Server validates token signature and grants access to protected routes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Build a Protected API Endpoint",
            "question": "Complete the 'verify_api_key' function that checks if the provided API key is in the VALID_KEYS dictionary. If the key is invalid, raise an HTTPException with status_code 401. If valid, return the user's name.",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Header\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nVALID_KEYS = {\n    \"key_123\": \"Alice\",\n    \"key_456\": \"Bob\"\n}\n\ndef verify_api_key(x_api_key: str = Header()):\n    # TODO: Check if x_api_key is in VALID_KEYS\n    # If not, raise HTTPException with status_code=401\n    # If yes, return the user's name\n    pass\n\n@app.get(\"/secret\")\ndef secret_endpoint(x_api_key: str = Header()):\n    user = verify_api_key(x_api_key)\n    return {\"message\": f\"Welcome, {user}!\"}",
            "solution": "from fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Header\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\nVALID_KEYS = {\n    \"key_123\": \"Alice\",\n    \"key_456\": \"Bob\"\n}\n\ndef verify_api_key(x_api_key: str = Header()):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail=\"Invalid API key\")\n    return VALID_KEYS[x_api_key]\n\n@app.get(\"/secret\")\ndef secret_endpoint(x_api_key: str = Header()):\n    user = verify_api_key(x_api_key)\n    return {\"message\": f\"Welcome, {user}!\"}",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Use 'if x_api_key not in VALID_KEYS:' to check. If invalid, use 'raise HTTPException(status_code=401, detail=\"Invalid API key\")'. If valid, return VALID_KEYS[x_api_key]."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Rate Limiting — Don't Let Anyone Bankrupt You",
            "explanation": "Even with authentication, you have a critical problem. What if a valid, paying user sends 10,000 requests per minute? Each AI request costs money. Without limits, one user — even one with good intentions — could drain your entire monthly budget in an hour.\n\n**🧒 The Speed Limit Analogy:**\n\nHighways have speed limits. Not because cars CAN'T go faster, but because it keeps everyone safe and the road functional. Without speed limits, one person going 200mph ruins it for everyone else.\n\nRate limiting is the speed limit for your API. It ensures fair usage and protects your budget.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_rate_limiting.png\" alt=\"Rate Limiting Analogy\" />\n\n**Simple In-Memory Rate Limiter:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Request\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nimport time\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# Track requests per IP\nrequest_counts: dict[str, list[float]] = defaultdict(list)\nRATE_LIMIT = 10  # max requests\nWINDOW = 60      # per 60 seconds\n\n@app.middleware('http')\nasync def rate_limit_middleware(request: Request, call_next):\n    client_ip = request.client.host\n    now = time.time()\n\n    # Remove old timestamps outside the window\n    request_counts[client_ip] = [\n        t for t in request_counts[client_ip]\n        if now - t < WINDOW\n    ]\n\n    # Check if over limit\n    if len(request_counts[client_ip]) >= RATE_LIMIT:\n        raise HTTPException(\n            status_code=429,\n            detail={\n                'error': 'rate_limit_exceeded',\n                'message': f'Max {RATE_LIMIT} requests per {WINDOW} seconds',\n                'retry_after': WINDOW\n            }\n        )\n\n    # Record this request\n    request_counts[client_ip].append(now)\n    return await call_next(request)\n```\n\n**💡 Different Limits for Different Users:**\n```python\nRATE_LIMITS = {\n    'free': {'requests': 10, 'window': 60},      # 10/min\n    'pro': {'requests': 100, 'window': 60},       # 100/min\n    'enterprise': {'requests': 1000, 'window': 60} # 1000/min\n}\n```\n\n**The Real-World Math:**\n- OpenAI charges ~$0.01 per request\n- Without rate limiting: A bot sends 100,000 requests = **$1,000 bill**\n- With rate limiting (100/min per user): Maximum damage = **$14.40/day per user**\n\n**⚠️ Production Warning:**\nThe in-memory approach above works for a single server. In production with multiple servers, use **Redis** for shared rate limiting (we'll cover Redis later in this module). Redis ensures that if a user hits Server A 5 times and Server B 5 times, the total count is 10 — not 5 on each."
          }
        },
        {
