              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Build a Background Task System",
            "question": "Create a FastAPI app with two endpoints:\n1. POST /tasks — accepts a Pydantic model 'TaskRequest' with a 'description' field, generates a random task_id, adds a background task, and returns the task_id immediately.\n2. GET /tasks/{task_id} — returns the status of the task.",
            "starterCode": "from fastapi import FastAPI, BackgroundTasks\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nimport uuid\n\napp = FastAPI()\ntask_statuses = {}\n\nclass TaskRequest(BaseModel):\n    description: str\n\ndef process_task(task_id: str, description: str):\n    task_statuses[task_id] = {\"status\": \"complete\", \"description\": description}\n\n# TODO: Create POST /tasks endpoint\n\n# TODO: Create GET /tasks/{task_id} endpoint",
            "solution": "from fastapi import FastAPI, BackgroundTasks\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nimport uuid\n\napp = FastAPI()\ntask_statuses = {}\n\nclass TaskRequest(BaseModel):\n    description: str\n\ndef process_task(task_id: str, description: str):\n    task_statuses[task_id] = {\"status\": \"complete\", \"description\": description}\n\n@app.post(\"/tasks\")\ndef create_task(request: TaskRequest, background_tasks: BackgroundTasks):\n    task_id = str(uuid.uuid4())[:8]\n    task_statuses[task_id] = {\"status\": \"processing\"}\n    background_tasks.add_task(process_task, task_id, request.description)\n    return {\"task_id\": task_id}\n\n@app.get(\"/tasks/{task_id}\")\ndef get_task(task_id: str):\n    if task_id not in task_statuses:\n        return {\"error\": \"Task not found\"}\n    return task_statuses[task_id]",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "For POST /tasks: generate a task_id with uuid, set initial status, use background_tasks.add_task() to run process_task in background, return the task_id. For GET /tasks/{task_id}: look up and return the status."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Connecting FastAPI to PostgreSQL (SQLAlchemy & SQLModel)",
            "explanation": "So far, all our data disappears the moment we restart the server. Every chat message, every user account — gone. Poof. That's like a restaurant where the chef forgets every order the second a customer leaves. Not exactly production-ready.\n\n**🧒 The Filing Cabinet Analogy:**\n\nRight now, your API is writing notes on a whiteboard. It works fine while the whiteboard is up, but the moment someone erases it (server restart), everything is lost.\n\nA database is like replacing that whiteboard with a massive steel filing cabinet. Data goes IN, the cabinet is locked, and it stays there forever — even if you turn off the lights and go home. When you come back, everything is exactly where you left it.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_database_filing.png\" alt=\"Database Filing Cabinet Analogy\" />\n\n**PostgreSQL is the gold standard for AI applications.** It's free, incredibly reliable, handles millions of records, and (as you'll see later) even supports vector embeddings for AI search.\n\n**Setting Up the Connection:**\n```python\n# database.py\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import sessionmaker, DeclarativeBase\n\nDATABASE_URL = \"postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/ai_app\"\n\nengine = create_engine(DATABASE_URL)\nSessionLocal = sessionmaker(bind=engine, autocommit=False, autoflush=False)\n\nclass Base(DeclarativeBase):\n    pass\n\n# Dependency for FastAPI endpoints\ndef get_db():\n    db = SessionLocal()\n    try:\n        yield db\n    finally:\n        db.close()\n```\n\n**Defining Your First Model (Table):**\n```python\n# models.py\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, String, DateTime, Text\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom database import Base\n\nclass ChatMessage(Base):\n    __tablename__ = 'chat_messages'\n\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True, index=True)\n    user_id = Column(String, index=True, nullable=False)\n    role = Column(String, nullable=False)       # 'user' or 'assistant'\n    content = Column(Text, nullable=False)\n    model = Column(String, default='gpt-4o')\n    tokens_used = Column(Integer, default=0)\n    created_at = Column(DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow)\n\nclass User(Base):\n    __tablename__ = 'users'\n\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True, index=True)\n    email = Column(String, unique=True, index=True)\n    hashed_password = Column(String)\n    api_calls_today = Column(Integer, default=0)\n    created_at = Column(DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow)\n```\n\n**Using the Database in Endpoints:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Depends\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session\nfrom database import get_db\nfrom models import ChatMessage\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.post('/chat')\nasync def chat(message: str, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    # Save the user's message\n    db_message = ChatMessage(\n        user_id='user-123',\n        role='user',\n        content=message\n    )\n    db.add(db_message)\n    db.commit()\n\n    # Get AI response\n    ai_response = await get_ai_response(message)\n\n    # Save the AI's response too\n    ai_message = ChatMessage(\n        user_id='user-123',\n        role='assistant',\n        content=ai_response\n    )\n    db.add(ai_message)\n    db.commit()\n\n    return {'response': ai_response}\n```\n\n**🔍 Why `Depends(get_db)`?**\nRemember Dependency Injection? FastAPI automatically creates a database session for each request and closes it when the request is done. No database connection leaks, no forgotten cleanups. It's beautiful."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Performing Database CRUD Operations",
            "explanation": "Now that your database is connected, let's learn the four operations that EVERY application needs. They spell out a handy acronym: **CRUD**.\n\n**🧒 The Library Card Catalog Analogy:**\n\nThink of your database as a giant library:\n- **C**reate = Adding a new book to the shelf and filing a card for it\n- **R**ead = Looking up a book using the card catalog and pulling it from the shelf\n- **U**pdate = Crossing out old information on a card and writing new details\n- **D**elete = Removing a book from the shelf and shredding its card\n\nEvery app you've ever used — Instagram, Spotify, ChatGPT — is just CRUD on a database with a pretty frontend.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_crud_library.png\" alt=\"CRUD Library Analogy\" />\n\n**The Complete CRUD for a Notes API:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Depends, HTTPException\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom typing import Optional\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n# --- Pydantic schemas (what the user sends/receives) ---\nclass NoteCreate(BaseModel):\n    title: str\n    content: str\n    tags: Optional[list[str]] = []\n\nclass NoteResponse(BaseModel):\n    id: int\n    title: str\n    content: str\n    tags: list[str]\n    created_at: str\n\n    class Config:\n        from_attributes = True\n\n# --- CREATE ---\n@app.post('/notes', response_model=NoteResponse)\ndef create_note(note: NoteCreate, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    db_note = Note(**note.model_dump())\n    db.add(db_note)\n    db.commit()\n    db.refresh(db_note)\n    return db_note\n\n# --- READ (all) ---\n@app.get('/notes', response_model=list[NoteResponse])\ndef list_notes(skip: int = 0, limit: int = 20, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    return db.query(Note).offset(skip).limit(limit).all()\n\n# --- READ (one) ---\n@app.get('/notes/{note_id}', response_model=NoteResponse)\ndef get_note(note_id: int, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    note = db.query(Note).filter(Note.id == note_id).first()\n    if not note:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail='Note not found')\n    return note\n\n# --- UPDATE ---\n@app.put('/notes/{note_id}', response_model=NoteResponse)\ndef update_note(note_id: int, updates: NoteCreate, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    note = db.query(Note).filter(Note.id == note_id).first()\n    if not note:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail='Note not found')\n    for key, value in updates.model_dump().items():\n        setattr(note, key, value)\n    db.commit()\n    db.refresh(note)\n    return note\n\n# --- DELETE ---\n@app.delete('/notes/{note_id}')\ndef delete_note(note_id: int, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    note = db.query(Note).filter(Note.id == note_id).first()\n    if not note:\n        raise HTTPException(status_code=404, detail='Note not found')\n    db.delete(note)\n    db.commit()\n    return {'message': f'Note {note_id} deleted successfully'}\n```\n\n**💡 Pro Tip: Always Use Pydantic Schemas**\nNotice that we have separate models for the database (`Note`) and the API (`NoteCreate`, `NoteResponse`). This is intentional:\n- The **database model** has auto-generated fields like `id` and `created_at`\n- The **request model** (`NoteCreate`) only includes fields the user should provide\n- The **response model** (`NoteResponse`) controls exactly what gets sent back\n\nThis separation protects you from accidentally exposing internal fields (like hashed passwords) in API responses."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Database Migrations with Alembic",
            "explanation": "Here's a situation that WILL happen to you. You launch your app with a `users` table. A month later, you need to add a `subscription_tier` column. But your database already has 10,000 users in it. You can't just delete the table and recreate it — you'd lose all your data!\n\n**🧒 The Home Renovation Analogy:**\n\nImagine you live in a house (your database). You want to add a new room (a new column). You can't demolish the entire house and rebuild it — all your furniture (data) is inside! Instead, you hire a contractor who carefully adds the room WHILE you keep living there.\n\nThat contractor is **Alembic**. It manages database migrations — careful, reversible changes to your database structure without losing any data.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_alembic_renovation.png\" alt=\"Database Migration Home Renovation Analogy\" />\n\n**Setting Up Alembic:**\n```bash\npip install alembic\nalembic init alembic    # Creates the migration folder structure\n```\n\nThis creates:\n```\nmy_app/\n├── alembic/\n│   ├── versions/       # Each migration file lives here\n│   ├── env.py          # Configuration\n│   └── script.py.mako  # Template for new migrations\n├── alembic.ini         # Connection string goes here\n└── models.py           # Your SQLAlchemy models\n```\n\n**Creating Your First Migration:**\n```bash\n# Auto-detect changes in your models and create a migration\nalembic revision --autogenerate -m \"add subscription_tier to users\"\n\n# Apply the migration to your database\nalembic upgrade head\n\n# Oops, made a mistake? Roll back!\nalembic downgrade -1\n```\n\n**What a Migration File Looks Like:**\n```python\n# alembic/versions/001_add_subscription_tier.py\nfrom alembic import op\nimport sqlalchemy as sa\n\ndef upgrade():\n    op.add_column('users',\n        sa.Column('subscription_tier', sa.String(), default='free')\n    )\n\ndef downgrade():\n    op.drop_column('users', 'subscription_tier')\n```\n\n**🔑 The Key Rules:**\n1. **Never edit your database schema by hand** — Always use Alembic migrations\n2. **Always test migrations locally first** — Run `alembic upgrade head` on your dev database before touching production\n3. **Migrations are version-controlled** — They go into Git, so your entire team can reproduce the exact same database structure\n4. **Each migration has upgrade AND downgrade** — If something goes wrong, you can always roll back\n\nThink of migration files as a journal of your database's life story. Anyone can read through them and understand exactly how the database evolved over time."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Redis Caching for AI Applications",
            "explanation": "Let's talk about a problem that will eat your budget alive if you don't solve it. Every time a user asks \"What is machine learning?\", your app calls the OpenAI API. That costs money. But here's the thing — 100 different users might ask the EXACT same question in one day. Are you going to pay OpenAI 100 times for the same answer?\n\n**🧒 The Sticky Note on the Fridge Analogy:**\n\nImagine your family keeps asking you the same question: \"What's the WiFi password?\" The first time, you walk upstairs, dig through a drawer, find the router manual, and read the password. That takes 5 minutes.\n\nSo you write it on a sticky note and stick it on the fridge. Now when someone asks, you just point at the fridge. 2 seconds instead of 5 minutes.\n\nThat sticky note is your **cache**. And **Redis** is a super-fast, industrial-strength fridge for your AI application.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_redis_cache.png\" alt=\"Redis Caching Sticky Note Analogy\" />\n\n**Why Redis for AI Apps?**\n- 🚀 Responses in **microseconds** (vs. 2-5 seconds for an OpenAI API call)\n- 💰 Save money by not repeating identical API calls\n- 📈 Handle thousands of users without overwhelming your AI provider\n- ⏰ Built-in expiration — cache expires after 1 hour so answers stay fresh\n\n**Setting Up Redis Caching:**\n```python\nimport redis\nimport json\nimport hashlib\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI()\ncache = redis.Redis(host='localhost', port=6379, db=0)\nCACHE_TTL = 3600  # Cache for 1 hour\n\ndef get_cache_key(message: str, model: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Create a unique key for each unique prompt.\"\"\"\n    content = f\"{model}:{message}\"\n    return f\"ai_cache:{hashlib.md5(content.encode()).hexdigest()}\"\n\n@app.post('/chat')\nasync def chat(message: str, model: str = 'gpt-4o'):\n    cache_key = get_cache_key(message, model)\n\n    # Check cache first\n    cached = cache.get(cache_key)\n    if cached:\n        return {\n            'response': json.loads(cached),\n            'cached': True  # Let the frontend know this was cached\n        }\n\n    # Cache miss — call the AI\n    response = await call_openai(message, model)\n\n    # Store in cache for next time\n    cache.setex(cache_key, CACHE_TTL, json.dumps(response))\n\n    return {'response': response, 'cached': False}\n```\n\n**💡 When to Cache vs. When NOT to Cache:**\n- ✅ Cache: FAQ-style questions, summarization of static content, translations\n- ❌ Don't cache: Creative writing (users expect different outputs), real-time data questions, personalized responses\n\n**Redis can also be used for:**\n- **Rate limiting** — Track how many requests each user has made\n- **Session storage** — Store JWT tokens for fast validation\n- **Job queues** — Track background task progress"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Storing Embeddings: Introduction to Vector Databases",
            "explanation": "You've already learned about embeddings in earlier modules — turning text into arrays of numbers that capture meaning. But here's a question nobody asks at first: **Where do you STORE those embeddings?**\n\nIf you have 10,000 documents, each with a 1536-dimension embedding, that's 10,000 arrays of 1,536 numbers each. You can't just throw those in a regular PostgreSQL column and run a SQL query like `WHERE meaning SIMILAR TO 'machine learning'`. SQL doesn't understand meaning. It only understands exact matches.\n\n**🧒 The Music Store Analogy:**\n\nImagine you walk into a music store and say: \"I want something that SOUNDS like Bohemian Rhapsody.\" The store clerk can't look that up alphabetically. They need a special system that understands musical similarity — tempo, genre, mood, instrumentation.\n\nA vector database is that special system, but for text. Instead of sorting documents alphabetically, it organizes them by **meaning**. So when you ask \"What's our refund policy?\", it finds the document about returns — even if the word \"refund\" never appears in it.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_vector_db.png\" alt=\"Vector Database Music Store Analogy\" />\n\n**How Semantic Search Works (The Full Flow):**\n```\n1. Your documents → OpenAI Embeddings API → Arrays of numbers\n2. Store those arrays in a Vector Database\n3. User asks a question → Same Embeddings API → Array of numbers\n4. Vector DB finds the closest matching document vectors\n5. Send the matched documents + question to the LLM → Answer\n```\n\nThat's **RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation)** — and it's the backbone of every production AI app that needs to answer questions about YOUR specific data.\n\n**Popular Vector Database Options:**\n| Database | Best For | Pricing |\n|----------|----------|---------|\n| **pgvector** (PostgreSQL extension) | Teams already using PostgreSQL | Free |\n| **Pinecone** | Managed, zero-ops | Free tier, then paid |\n| **ChromaDB** | Local development, prototyping | Free |\n| **Qdrant** | High performance, self-hosted | Free |\n| **Weaviate** | Enterprise, multi-modal | Free tier, then paid |\n\n**For this course, we'll use pgvector** — because you're ALREADY using PostgreSQL. No extra database to manage, no extra bills to pay. Just add the extension and your PostgreSQL becomes a vector database."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Integrating pgvector with FastAPI",
            "explanation": "Let's turn your PostgreSQL database into a full-blown vector database. Remember the music store analogy? We're about to install that \"similarity search engine\" right inside your existing database.\n\n**Step 1: Enable pgvector in PostgreSQL:**\n```sql\n-- Run this once in your PostgreSQL database\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n```\n\n**Step 2: Define a Model with Embeddings:**\n```python\n# models.py\nfrom sqlalchemy import Column, Integer, String, Text, DateTime\nfrom pgvector.sqlalchemy import Vector\nfrom database import Base\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nclass Document(Base):\n    __tablename__ = 'documents'\n\n    id = Column(Integer, primary_key=True, index=True)\n    title = Column(String, nullable=False)\n    content = Column(Text, nullable=False)\n    embedding = Column(Vector(1536))  # OpenAI's embedding dimension\n    created_at = Column(DateTime, default=datetime.utcnow)\n```\n\n**Step 3: Build the Semantic Search Endpoint:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Depends\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\nfrom database import get_db\nfrom models import Document\n\napp = FastAPI()\nopenai_client = AsyncOpenAI()\n\nasync def get_embedding(text: str) -> list[float]:\n    response = await openai_client.embeddings.create(\n        model='text-embedding-3-small',\n        input=text\n    )\n    return response.data[0].embedding\n\n@app.post('/documents')\nasync def add_document(title: str, content: str, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    # Generate embedding for the document\n    embedding = await get_embedding(content)\n\n    doc = Document(title=title, content=content, embedding=embedding)\n    db.add(doc)\n    db.commit()\n    return {'message': f'Document \"{title}\" stored with embedding'}\n\n@app.post('/search')\nasync def semantic_search(query: str, top_k: int = 5, db: Session = Depends(get_db)):\n    # Generate embedding for the user's query\n    query_embedding = await get_embedding(query)\n\n    # Find the most similar documents using cosine distance\n    results = db.query(Document).order_by(\n        Document.embedding.cosine_distance(query_embedding)\n    ).limit(top_k).all()\n\n    return [{\n        'title': doc.title,\n        'content': doc.content[:200] + '...',\n        'relevance': 'high'\n    } for doc in results]\n```\n\n**🔍 What Just Happened?**\n1. When you add a document, we convert its text into a 1536-dimensional vector using OpenAI's embedding model\n2. That vector is stored alongside the text in PostgreSQL\n3. When a user searches, we convert their query into a vector too\n4. PostgreSQL finds the documents whose vectors are CLOSEST to the query vector\n5. \"Closest\" means \"most semantically similar\" — it understands meaning, not just keywords\n\n**This is the foundation of RAG.** Once you have this semantic search, you can pipe the results into an LLM for a complete \"chat with your documents\" experience."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Putting It All Together — A Complete AI Backend",
            "explanation": "You've learned all the individual pieces. Now let's see how they all fit together in ONE complete application. This is the moment where everything clicks.\n\n**Think of It Like Building a Car:**\n- Engine = FastAPI (powers everything)\n- Fuel system = Database (stores data)\n- Dashboard = Pydantic models (shows what's happening)\n- Locks = Authentication (keeps unauthorized people out)\n- Speed governor = Rate limiting (prevents abuse)\n- GPS = Middleware (tracks everything)\n- Streaming = The exhaust system... okay, the analogy breaks down here 😄\n\n**Our Complete App Has:**\n1. ✅ Health check endpoint\n2. ✅ Chat endpoint with Pydantic validation\n3. ✅ Streaming AI responses\n4. ✅ API key authentication\n5. ✅ Rate limiting middleware\n6. ✅ CORS for frontend access\n7. ✅ Request logging\n8. ✅ Error handling\n\n**The Complete Application:**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, HTTPException, Header, Request\nfrom fastapi.responses import StreamingResponse\nfrom fastapi.middleware.cors import CORSMiddleware\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom openai import AsyncOpenAI\nfrom typing import Optional\nimport time, logging\n\n# --- App Setup ---\napp = FastAPI(title='AI Chatbot API', version='1.0')\nclient = AsyncOpenAI()\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"ai_api\")\n\n# --- CORS ---\napp.add_middleware(\n    CORSMiddleware,\n    allow_origins=['http://localhost:3000'],\n    allow_credentials=True,\n    allow_methods=['*'],\n    allow_headers=['*'],\n)\n\n# --- Request Models ---\nclass ChatRequest(BaseModel):\n    message: str\n    model: str = 'gpt-4o'\n    temperature: float = 0.7\n    stream: bool = False\n\n# --- Logging Middleware ---\n@app.middleware('http')\nasync def log_requests(request: Request, call_next):\n    start = time.time()\n    response = await call_next(request)\n    duration = time.time() - start\n    logger.info(f\"{request.method} {request.url.path} → {response.status_code} ({duration:.2f}s)\")\n    return response\n\n# --- Auth Dependency ---\nVALID_KEYS = {'sk-test-key-123'}\n\nasync def verify_key(x_api_key: str = Header(..., alias='X-API-Key')):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(401, 'Invalid API key')\n    return x_api_key\n\n# --- Endpoints ---\n@app.get('/health')\ndef health():\n    return {'status': 'healthy'}\n\n@app.post('/chat')\nasync def chat(req: ChatRequest, api_key: str = Depends(verify_key)):\n    response = await client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=req.model,\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': req.message}],\n        temperature=req.temperature\n    )\n    return {'response': response.choices[0].message.content}\n```\n\n**🔑 Notice How Clean This Is:**\nEach piece we learned — CORS, middleware, auth, Pydantic, async — slots into its own place. The endpoint code is just 5 lines because all the plumbing is handled by middleware and dependencies. That's the power of FastAPI's architecture."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dependency Injection — Keeping Your Code Clean",
            "explanation": "As your app grows, you'll notice you're writing the same code over and over. Every protected endpoint checks the API key. Every database endpoint creates a session. Every AI endpoint initializes the client. That's a LOT of duplicated code.\n\n**🧒 The USB Port Analogy:**\n\nImagine if every device needed its own unique charger. Your phone, tablet, laptop, headphones — all different cables. Chaos! Instead, we standardized on USB-C. One port, any device. Plug it in and it works.\n\n`Depends()` is FastAPI's USB-C port. You define a reusable function once, and any endpoint can \"plug it in.\"\n\n**The Problem (Without Depends):**\n```python\n# Repeating auth check in EVERY endpoint 😫\n@app.get('/endpoint1')\ndef endpoint1(x_api_key: str = Header(...)):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(401, 'Unauthorized')\n    return {'data': 'secret1'}\n\n@app.get('/endpoint2')\ndef endpoint2(x_api_key: str = Header(...)):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(401, 'Unauthorized')\n    return {'data': 'secret2'}\n```\n\n**The Solution — Depends():**\n```python\nfrom fastapi import Depends\n\n# Define the dependency ONCE\ndef verify_api_key(x_api_key: str = Header(..., alias='X-API-Key')):\n    if x_api_key not in VALID_KEYS:\n        raise HTTPException(401, 'Invalid API key')\n    return x_api_key\n\n# Use it in any endpoint — just add Depends()\n@app.get('/endpoint1')\ndef endpoint1(key: str = Depends(verify_api_key)):\n    return {'data': 'secret1'}\n\n@app.get('/endpoint2')\ndef endpoint2(key: str = Depends(verify_api_key)):\n    return {'data': 'secret2'}\n```\n\n**Chaining Dependencies:**\n```python\n# A dependency that DEPENDS on another dependency\ndef get_current_user(\n    token: str = Depends(verify_token),\n    db: Session = Depends(get_db)\n):\n    user = db.query(User).filter(User.id == token['user_id']).first()\n    if not user:\n        raise HTTPException(404, 'User not found')\n    return user\n\n@app.get('/profile')\ndef my_profile(user: User = Depends(get_current_user)):\n    return {'email': user.email, 'role': user.role}\n```\n\n**🔍 What FastAPI Does Behind the Scenes:**\n1. Request comes in for `/profile`\n2. FastAPI sees `Depends(get_current_user)`\n3. `get_current_user` depends on `verify_token` → runs that first\n4. `get_current_user` depends on `get_db` → runs that too\n5. Everything passes → endpoint runs with the resolved dependencies\n6. If anything fails → HTTPException is raised automatically\n\nIt's like a dependency tree. FastAPI resolves the entire tree before running your endpoint. This pattern keeps your code DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) and makes testing incredibly easy — you can swap out dependencies for mock versions in tests."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Project Structure — Organizing a Real Application",
            "explanation": "When your API grows from 5 endpoints to 50 endpoints, having everything in one `main.py` file becomes a nightmare. You can't find anything, merge conflicts happen constantly, and new team members have no idea where to start.\n\n**🧒 The House vs. Apartment Building Analogy:**\n\nA single `main.py` is like living in a studio apartment — everything in one room. When it's just you, it's fine. But try fitting a family of five with all their stuff into a studio. Chaos.\n\nA proper project structure is like a house with rooms — kitchen, bedrooms, living room, bathroom. Everything has its place. You know exactly where to find the cooking utensils (service functions) vs. the towels (utility functions).\n\n**Small Project (1 file) — Fine for learning:**\n```\nmy_app/\n├── main.py          # Everything in one file\n└── requirements.txt\n```\n\n**Medium Project (modular) — Use this for real apps:**\n```\nmy_app/\n├── main.py           # App creation + startup\n├── config.py         # Environment variables (pydantic_settings)\n├── database.py       # Database connection + get_db\n├── routers/\n│   ├── chat.py       # /chat endpoints\n│   ├── users.py      # /users endpoints\n│   └── admin.py      # /admin endpoints\n├── models/\n│   ├── database.py   # SQLAlchemy models (tables)\n│   ├── requests.py   # Pydantic request models\n│   └── responses.py  # Pydantic response models\n├── services/\n│   ├── ai_service.py # OpenAI API calls\n│   ├── auth.py       # JWT + API key logic\n│   └── cache.py      # Redis caching\n├── middleware/\n│   ├── logging.py    # Request logging\n│   └── rate_limit.py # Rate limiting\n├── tests/\n│   ├── test_chat.py\n│   └── test_auth.py\n├── .env              # Secrets (NOT in Git!)\n├── .gitignore\n├── Dockerfile\n├── docker-compose.yml\n└── requirements.txt\n```\n\n**Using APIRouter to Split Endpoints:**\n```python\n# routers/chat.py\nfrom fastapi import APIRouter, Depends\n\nrouter = APIRouter(prefix='/chat', tags=['Chat'])\n\n@router.post('/')\nasync def send_message(message: str):\n    return {'response': 'Hello!'}\n\n@router.post('/stream')\nasync def stream_message(message: str):\n    return StreamingResponse(...)\n```\n\n```python\n# main.py\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom routers import chat, users, admin\n\napp = FastAPI(title='AI Backend', version='1.0')\n\napp.include_router(chat.router)\napp.include_router(users.router)\napp.include_router(admin.router)\n```\n\n**💡 The Rule of Thumb:**\n- **< 5 endpoints** → Single file is fine\n- **5-20 endpoints** → Split into routers\n- **20+ endpoints** → Full project structure with services, models, and middleware layers\n\nThis structure is used by every production FastAPI application. Get comfortable with it — it's how professional Python backends are built."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Deploying Your API — Going Live!",
            "explanation": "Right now, your API only works on your laptop (`localhost`). To let other people use it, you need to deploy it to a server in the cloud. This is where your AI goes from a personal project to a real product.\n\n**🧒 The Home Kitchen vs. Restaurant Analogy:**\n\nRight now, you're cooking in your home kitchen. Great food, but only your family can eat it. Deploying is like opening a restaurant — you need a commercial kitchen (cloud server), a storefront (domain name), and a way for customers to find you (DNS).\n\n**The Deployment Stack:**\n1. **Uvicorn** = The engine that runs your FastAPI app\n2. **Gunicorn** = A process manager that runs multiple Uvicorn workers (more workers = more concurrent users)\n3. **Docker** = Packages your app into a portable container (next lesson!)\n4. **Cloud Platform** = The computer in the sky\n\n**Step 1: Prepare for Production:**\n```python\n# main.py — Add these production settings\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\n\napp = FastAPI(\n    title=\"My AI API\",\n    version=\"1.0\",\n    docs_url=\"/docs\" if ENVIRONMENT == \"development\" else None,  # Hide docs in prod\n    redoc_url=None\n)\n```\n\n**Step 2: Create requirements.txt:**\n```\nfastapi==0.115.0\nuvicorn[standard]==0.30.0\nopenai==1.50.0\npydantic==2.9.0\npydantic-settings==2.5.0\npython-jose[cryptography]==3.3.0\nsqlalchemy==2.0.35\nredis==5.1.0\n```\n\n**Step 3: Run with Multiple Workers:**\n```bash\n# Development (1 worker, auto-reload)\nuvicorn main:app --reload\n\n# Production (4 workers, no reload)\ngunicorn main:app -w 4 -k uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker --bind 0.0.0.0:8000\n```\n\n**Popular Deployment Platforms for AI APIs:**\n| Platform | Best For | Pricing |\n|----------|----------|---------|\n| **Railway** | Quick deployments, hobby projects | Free tier, then $5/mo |\n| **Render** | Auto-deploy from GitHub | Free tier, then $7/mo |\n| **AWS EC2/ECS** | Full control, enterprise scale | Pay per use |\n| **Google Cloud Run** | Serverless, auto-scaling | Pay per request |\n| **Fly.io** | Edge deployment, global | Free tier, then $5/mo |\n\n**🔑 Pre-Deployment Checklist:**\n- ☐ All secrets are in environment variables (not hardcoded!)\n- ☐ CORS allows your production frontend URL\n- ☐ Rate limiting is configured\n- ☐ Error handling returns clean messages (no stack traces)\n- ☐ Health check endpoint exists at `/health`\n- ☐ Logging is set up (you'll need it for debugging)\n- ☐ Database migrations are up to date\n\nIn the next lessons, we'll cover Docker and Docker Compose, which make deployment reproducible and reliable across any platform."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Dockerizing Your FastAPI Application",
            "explanation": "You've heard the classic developer excuse: \"But it works on MY machine!\" Docker eliminates that excuse forever.\n\n**🧒 The Shipping Container Analogy:**\n\nBefore shipping containers were invented, loading a cargo ship was chaos. Every item was a different shape, needed different handling, and took forever to load. Then someone invented the standard shipping container — a uniform metal box. Now ANY crane at ANY port can load ANY container. The contents don't matter.\n\nDocker does the same thing for software. Your FastAPI app, with ALL its dependencies, Python version, and system libraries, gets packed into a standard \"container.\" This container runs identically on your laptop, your colleague's Mac, and an AWS server in Virginia.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_docker_container.png\" alt=\"Docker Shipping Container Analogy\" />\n\n**Writing Your Dockerfile:**\n```dockerfile\n# Use an official Python image\nFROM python:3.12-slim\n\n# Set the working directory inside the container\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Copy requirements first (for Docker layer caching)\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\n# Copy the rest of your app\nCOPY . .\n\n# Expose the port FastAPI will run on\nEXPOSE 8000\n\n# Start the server\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\n**Building and Running:**\n```bash\n# Build the container image\ndocker build -t my-ai-api .\n\n# Run it\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 --env-file .env my-ai-api\n\n# Your API is now running at http://localhost:8000 🎉\n```\n\n**💡 Why Copy requirements.txt FIRST?**\nDocker builds in layers. If your code changes but your dependencies don't, Docker reuses the cached layer where `pip install` ran. This makes rebuilds go from 2 minutes to 5 seconds. Always copy and install dependencies before copying your code.\n\n**The .dockerignore File:**\n```\n# .dockerignore — don't copy these into the container\n.env\n__pycache__\n.git\n.vscode\n*.pyc\nvenv/\n```\n\nDocker is not optional for production AI applications. Every serious company uses containers. It's the universal language of deployment."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Writing a docker-compose.yml File",
            "explanation": "Your API doesn't run alone. It needs PostgreSQL for data, Redis for caching, and maybe a frontend too. Running each one with separate `docker run` commands is tedious. That's where Docker Compose comes in.\n\n**🧒 The Orchestra Conductor Analogy:**\n\nImagine an orchestra. Each musician (container) plays a different instrument (service). Without a conductor, they'd all start at different times, play in different keys, and create chaos. The conductor's sheet music tells everyone when to start, what to play, and how to coordinate.\n\n`docker-compose.yml` is your conductor's sheet music. One file that starts ALL your services together, in the right order, connected to each other.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_docker_compose.png\" alt=\"Docker Compose Orchestra Analogy\" />\n\n**Your AI Application's docker-compose.yml:**\n```yaml\nversion: '3.8'\n\nservices:\n  # Your FastAPI application\n  api:\n    build: .\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n    env_file:\n      - .env\n    depends_on:\n      postgres:\n        condition: service_healthy\n      redis:\n        condition: service_started\n    restart: unless-stopped\n\n  # PostgreSQL database (with pgvector!)\n  postgres:\n    image: pgvector/pgvector:pg16\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: ai_user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: secure_password\n      POSTGRES_DB: ai_app\n    volumes:\n      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    ports:\n      - \"5432:5432\"\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U ai_user\"]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  # Redis for caching\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7-alpine\n    ports:\n      - \"6379:6379\"\n    volumes:\n      - redis_data:/data\n\nvolumes:\n  postgres_data:    # Persists database even when container stops\n  redis_data:       # Persists cache data\n```\n\n**Running Everything:**\n```bash\n# Start all services\ndocker-compose up -d\n\n# See what's running\ndocker-compose ps\n\n# View logs\ndocker-compose logs -f api\n\n# Stop everything\ndocker-compose down\n\n# Stop AND delete all data (nuclear option)\ndocker-compose down -v\n```\n\n**🔍 Key Things to Notice:**\n1. **depends_on** — The API won't start until PostgreSQL is healthy. No more \"database not ready\" crashes.\n2. **volumes** — Your data survives container restarts. Without volumes, `docker-compose down` would erase your entire database.\n3. **pgvector/pgvector:pg16** — We're using the pgvector image, so vector search works out of the box.\n4. **env_file** — Your secrets stay in `.env`, not in the compose file.\n\nOne command. Three services. All connected. That's the power of Docker Compose."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Writing API Tests with Pytest and TestClient",
            "explanation": "You've built an amazing AI backend. But how do you KNOW it works? \"I tested it manually in Postman\" doesn't count. What about the 47 edge cases you didn't think of? What about when your colleague changes the auth middleware and accidentally breaks the chat endpoint?\n\n**🧒 The Crash Test Dummy Analogy:**\n\nCar manufacturers don't test safety by having a person drive into a wall. They use crash test dummies — automated, repeatable tests that check every scenario. Your API tests are your crash test dummies. They slam into every endpoint hundreds of times and report exactly what broke.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_testing_crashdummy.png\" alt=\"API Testing Crash Test Analogy\" />\n\n**FastAPI's Built-in TestClient:**\n```python\n# test_api.py\nfrom fastapi.testclient import TestClient\nfrom main import app\n\nclient = TestClient(app)\n\n# Test 1: Health check works\ndef test_health_check():\n    response = client.get('/health')\n    assert response.status_code == 200\n    assert response.json() == {'status': 'healthy'}\n\n# Test 2: Chat endpoint returns a response\ndef test_chat_endpoint():\n    response = client.post('/chat', json={\n        'message': 'Hello, AI!',\n        'model': 'gpt-4o'\n    })\n    assert response.status_code == 200\n    assert 'response' in response.json()\n\n# Test 3: Missing message returns 422\ndef test_chat_missing_message():\n    response = client.post('/chat', json={})\n    assert response.status_code == 422\n\n# Test 4: Invalid API key returns 401\ndef test_unauthorized_access():\n    response = client.get(\n        '/protected-endpoint',\n        headers={'X-API-Key': 'invalid-key'}\n    )\n    assert response.status_code == 401\n\n# Test 5: Rate limiting kicks in\ndef test_rate_limiting():\n    for i in range(11):  # Limit is 10 per minute\n        response = client.get('/health')\n    assert response.status_code == 429  # Too Many Requests\n```\n\n**Running Tests:**\n```bash\n# Run all tests\npytest test_api.py -v\n\n# Run with coverage report\npytest --cov=. --cov-report=html\n\n# Run only tests that match a name\npytest -k \"test_chat\" -v\n```\n\n**💡 The Testing Pyramid for AI APIs:**\n1. **Unit tests (70%)** — Test individual functions (does the prompt formatter work?)\n2. **Integration tests (20%)** — Test endpoints end-to-end (does the chat endpoint return valid JSON?)\n3. **E2E tests (10%)** — Test the full user flow (can a user register, log in, and chat?)\n\n**Pro Tip: Use Fixtures for Reusable Setup:**\n```python\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef auth_headers():\n    return {'X-API-Key': 'test-key-123'}\n\ndef test_protected_endpoint(auth_headers):\n    response = client.get('/data', headers=auth_headers)\n    assert response.status_code == 200\n```\n\nNever deploy without tests. They are your safety net. Every bug you catch in tests is a bug your users never see."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Adding Structured Logging to Your API",
            "explanation": "When your API is running in production and something goes wrong at 3 AM, you won't be there to debug it. The only thing you'll have is your logs. And if your logs look like this:\n\n```\nERROR: something went wrong\nINFO: request processed\nERROR: failed\n```\n\n...you're in trouble. That tells you NOTHING. When did it fail? Which user? Which endpoint? What was the input?\n\n**🧒 The Flight Recorder (Black Box) Analogy:**\n\nEvery airplane has a black box that records EVERYTHING — altitude, speed, engine status, pilot conversations. When something goes wrong, investigators can replay the exact sequence of events. Your API needs the same thing.\n\nStructured logging is your black box. Instead of random print statements, every log entry is a structured JSON object with all the context you need.\n\n<img src=\"/images/module 14/m14_structured_logging.png\" alt=\"Structured Logging Black Box Analogy\" />\n\n**Before (Bad Logging):**\n```python\nprint(f\"Got a request\")\nprint(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n**After (Structured Logging):**\n```python\nimport logging\nimport json\nfrom datetime import datetime\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI, Request\nimport uuid\n\n# Configure structured JSON logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"ai_api\")\n\napp = FastAPI()\n\n@app.middleware(\"http\")\nasync def log_requests(request: Request, call_next):\n    request_id = str(uuid.uuid4())[:8]\n    start = datetime.utcnow()\n\n    # Add request_id so we can trace the entire request lifecycle\n    logger.info(json.dumps({\n        \"event\": \"request_started\",\n        \"request_id\": request_id,\n        \"method\": request.method,\n        \"path\": request.url.path,\n        \"client_ip\": request.client.host,\n        \"timestamp\": start.isoformat()\n    }))\n\n    response = await call_next(request)\n\n    duration = (datetime.utcnow() - start).total_seconds()\n\n    logger.info(json.dumps({\n        \"event\": \"request_completed\",\n        \"request_id\": request_id,\n        \"status_code\": response.status_code,\n        \"duration_seconds\": round(duration, 3),\n        \"timestamp\": datetime.utcnow().isoformat()\n    }))\n\n    response.headers[\"X-Request-ID\"] = request_id\n    return response\n```\n\n**What This Gives You:**\n```json\n{\"event\": \"request_started\", \"request_id\": \"a1b2c3d4\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"/chat\", \"client_ip\": \"192.168.1.1\", \"timestamp\": \"2025-01-15T14:30:00\"}\n{\"event\": \"ai_call_started\", \"request_id\": \"a1b2c3d4\", \"model\": \"gpt-4o\", \"tokens_estimated\": 150}\n{\"event\": \"ai_call_completed\", \"request_id\": \"a1b2c3d4\", \"tokens_used\": 142, \"duration_seconds\": 2.3}\n{\"event\": \"request_completed\", \"request_id\": \"a1b2c3d4\", \"status_code\": 200, \"duration_seconds\": 2.5}\n```\n\nNow when something goes wrong, you can filter by `request_id` and see the ENTIRE journey of that specific request — from arrival to AI call to response. That's the power of structured logging.\n\n**🔑 What to Log in AI Applications:**\n- Every incoming request (method, path, user ID)\n- Every AI API call (model, token count, latency)\n- Every error (full traceback + context)\n- Every database query that takes > 1 second\n- Every cache hit/miss"
          }
        },
