      "id": "M015-databases",
      "title": "Module 15: Databases",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Do We Even Need Databases?",
            "explanation": "Imagine you're building an AI chatbot. It's working perfectly on your laptop. But then you deploy it and suddenly:\n- 10,000 users are chatting with it simultaneously\n- Every user has 50 messages in their history\n- Your AI needs to remember what each user said last week\n\nWhere do you store all of this? You might think: \"I'll just save it in a file!\"\n\nHere's the problem with plain files:\n- If two users write to the same file at the same millisecond, one save OVERWRITES the other\n- Searching through a million text files for one user's history takes minutes\n- If your server crashes mid-write, the file gets corrupted\n\n**This is exactly why databases exist.**\n\nA database is a specialized program that:\n1. **Stores data safely** — even if 10,000 users write at the same time\n2. **Retrieves data instantly** — finds 1 user out of 10 million in milliseconds\n3. **Protects your data** — if the server crashes mid-write, it rolls back safely\n4. **Scales up** — works for 10 users or 10 billion users\n\n**As a Generative AI Engineer, you'll use databases for:**\n- Storing chat history and user sessions\n- Powering RAG (Retrieval Augmented Generation) with document search\n- Logging every LLM call (tokens used, latency, cost)\n- Storing user accounts, API keys, and billing info\n\nBy the end of this module, you'll understand every type of database used in production AI systems and know exactly which one to use for which job."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Bookshelf Analogy: Understanding Databases",
            "explanation": "Think of your data like books in a library.\n\n**Without a Database (Plain Files):**\nImagine a library with no shelves, no catalog, no librarian. Books are just stacked in random piles on the floor. To find the book you need, you pick up every single book until you find it. If two people try to grab a book from the same pile at the same time, chaos ensues.\n\n**With a Database:**\nNow imagine a perfectly organized library:\n- Every book has a unique ID number (like a database Primary Key)\n- Books are sorted alphabetically on shelves (like database indexes)\n- A catalog system lets you find any book in seconds (like database queries)\n- A librarian manages checkouts so two people can't take the same book simultaneously (like concurrency control)\n\n**The Four Superpowers of Every Database:**\n\n1. **Speed** — Database indexes are like a book's table of contents. Instead of reading the whole book to find Chapter 5, you jump straight to the page number. Databases jump straight to your data.\n\n2. **Concurrency** — 10,000 users can read and write simultaneously without stepping on each other's toes. The database acts as the traffic controller.\n\n3. **Durability** — When you save something, it's ACTUALLY saved. Even if the power cuts out mid-write, the database either completes the save fully or rolls it back completely. No half-written corrupted data.\n\n4. **Query Power** — Instead of reading through every record, you write a query: \"Give me all users who signed up last week and spent more than $50.\" The database handles the complex filtering for you."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "You're building an AI chatbot where 5,000 users might send messages at the exact same second. What core database feature ensures their messages don't overwrite each other?",
              "options": [
                "Concurrency control",
                "Vector embeddings",
                "Data compression",
                "SQL queries"
              ],
              "correct": 0,
              "explanation": "Concurrency control is the mechanism that allows multiple operations to happen simultaneously without conflicting. Databases use techniques like locking and transactions to ensure data integrity when thousands of users write at the same time.",
              "answer": 0
            },
            {
              "question": "Why is storing AI chat history in plain text files a bad idea in production?",
              "options": [
                "Text files can't store text",
                "Simultaneous writes can corrupt data and searching is extremely slow at scale",
                "AI models can't read text files",
                "Text files use too much CPU"
              ],
              "correct": 1,
              "explanation": "Plain text files have no concurrency control (simultaneous writes cause data loss) and no indexing (searching a million files takes minutes). Databases solve both problems.",
              "answer": 0
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Relational Databases — The Internet's Foundation",
            "explanation": "If you've ever used Gmail, Twitter, or Spotify, you've used a relational database.\n\nA relational database stores data in **tables** — just like a spreadsheet, but MUCH more powerful.\n\n**A Simple Example:**\nLet's say you're building an AI chat app. You might have a `users` table:\n\n```\n| id  | name    | email              | plan    | created_at          |\n|-----|---------|--------------------|---------|---------------------|\n| 1   | Alice   | alice@email.com    | pro     | 2024-01-15 09:00:00 |\n| 2   | Bob     | bob@email.com      | free    | 2024-01-16 14:23:00 |\n| 3   | Charlie | charlie@email.com  | pro     | 2024-01-17 11:45:00 |\n```\n\nAnd a separate `messages` table:\n\n```\n| id  | user_id | role      | content                    | timestamp           |\n|-----|---------|-----------|----------------------------|---------------------|\n| 1   | 1       | user      | How do I fine-tune GPT?    | 2024-01-20 10:00:00 |\n| 2   | 1       | assistant | Fine-tuning involves...    | 2024-01-20 10:00:01 |\n| 3   | 2       | user      | Explain RAG to me          | 2024-01-20 11:30:00 |\n```\n\nNotice `user_id` in the messages table? That's a **Foreign Key** — it LINKS the message to the user who sent it.\n\n**Why This Structure is Brilliant:**\n- You never duplicate data. Alice's email is stored ONCE in users, not repeated in every message\n- You can find all of Alice's messages instantly with a JOIN query\n- You can change Alice's email in one place and it automatically applies everywhere\n\n**Popular Relational Databases:**\n- **PostgreSQL** — The most powerful open-source option. Used by Instagram, Reddit, Discord\n- **MySQL** — The most widely deployed. Powers most WordPress sites\n- **SQLite** — A tiny database in a single file. Perfect for development and mobile apps\n- **Supabase** — PostgreSQL with a Firebase-like API. Very popular for AI startups"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "SQL: The Language of Relational Databases",
            "explanation": "SQL (Structured Query Language) is how you talk to a relational database. It reads almost like plain English, which makes it surprisingly easy to learn.\n\n**The 4 Core Operations (CRUD):**\n\n**1. SELECT — Read data**\n```sql\n-- Get all pro users\nSELECT id, name, email\nFROM users\nWHERE plan = 'pro';\n```\n\n**2. INSERT — Add new data**\n```sql\n-- Add a new user\nINSERT INTO users (name, email, plan)\nVALUES ('Diana', 'diana@email.com', 'free');\n```\n\n**3. UPDATE — Modify existing data**\n```sql\n-- Upgrade a user to pro\nUPDATE users\nSET plan = 'pro'\nWHERE email = 'diana@email.com';\n```\n\n**4. DELETE — Remove data**\n```sql\n-- Delete inactive users\nDELETE FROM users\nWHERE last_active < '2023-01-01';\n```\n\n**JOINs — Combining Tables:**\nThis is where the real power lives. You can combine tables to ask complex questions:\n\n```sql\n-- Get all messages sent by Alice\nSELECT u.name, m.role, m.content, m.timestamp\nFROM users u\nJOIN messages m ON u.id = m.user_id\nWHERE u.name = 'Alice'\nORDER BY m.timestamp;\n```\n\nThis query links two tables using the foreign key relationship and returns Alice's full chat history — a query that would take hours to write in pure Python code, done in 6 lines of SQL."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Basic SQL: Find Pro Users",
            "description": "Write an SQL query to select the `name` and `email` columns from the `users` table where the `plan` is 'pro'. Order the results by `name` alphabetically.",
            "startingCode": "-- Write your SQL query here\nSELECT \nFROM users\nWHERE \nORDER BY ;",
            "solution": "SELECT name, email FROM users WHERE plan = 'pro' ORDER BY name;",
            "language": "sql",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "validation": true,
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "ACID Properties: Why You Can Trust SQL Databases",
            "explanation": "Here's a scary scenario: a user is upgrading their subscription plan. Your code charges their credit card AND then updates their `plan` column from 'free' to 'pro'. But what if the server crashes between those two operations? The user got charged but their plan never upgraded. Disaster.\n\n**ACID properties guarantee this never happens.** Let's break them down:\n\n**A — Atomicity (All or Nothing)**\nA transaction is like a deal: either EVERYTHING happens, or NOTHING happens. If you charge the card and THEN the server crashes, the database rolls back the charge too. Half-completed operations are impossible.\n\n```sql\nBEGIN TRANSACTION;\n  UPDATE billing SET charged = true WHERE user_id = 1;\n  UPDATE users SET plan = 'pro' WHERE id = 1;\nCOMMIT;  -- Both happen, or neither does\n```\n\n**C — Consistency**\nThe database never allows data that breaks your rules (called constraints). For example, you can set a rule that `user_id` in `messages` MUST exist in the `users` table. Orphaned messages pointing to deleted users become impossible.\n\n**I — Isolation**\nTwo transactions happening at the same time don't interfere with each other. If Alice and Bob both try to book the last seat on a flight simultaneously, isolation ensures only ONE of them succeeds.\n\n**D — Durability**\nOnce a transaction COMMITS (saves), it's permanent. Even if the server loses power 1 millisecond after saving, the data survives. The database writes to disk before confirming success.\n\n**The AI Engineer's Takeaway:**\nUse SQL databases for anything where correctness is critical: user accounts, payments, API keys, and subscription plans. The ACID guarantee is worth every bit of structure it requires."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
