          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Database Indexes: The Secret to Instant Queries",
            "explanation": "Imagine you have a `messages` table with 100 million rows. A user loads your chat app and you run:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM messages WHERE user_id = 12345;\n```\n\n**Without an index:** The database reads ALL 100 million rows one by one checking if `user_id = 12345`. This could take 30 seconds.\n\n**With an index on `user_id`:** The database has a pre-sorted lookup structure. It jumps DIRECTLY to user 12345's messages in milliseconds.\n\n**Creating an Index:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_messages_user_id ON messages(user_id);\n```\n\nThat's it. One line and your query goes from 30 seconds to 5 milliseconds.\n\n**Common Indexes in AI Apps:**\n```sql\n-- Speed up user lookups by email (e.g., login)\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email);\n\n-- Speed up loading a user's chat sessions\nCREATE INDEX idx_sessions_user_id ON sessions(user_id);\n\n-- Speed up finding recent messages\nCREATE INDEX idx_messages_timestamp ON messages(timestamp DESC);\n```\n\n**The Trade-off:**\nIndexes make reads faster but writes slightly slower (because every INSERT must also update the index). The general rule: index every column you frequently search or filter on, especially foreign keys.\n\n**For AI Apps:** Always index `user_id`, `session_id`, and `created_at`. These are the columns you'll filter on 90% of the time."
          }
        },
        {
