          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "NoSQL Databases — When Structure Gets in the Way",
            "explanation": "SQL databases are incredible, but they have one fundamental limitation: **you must define your structure upfront**.\n\nBefore you can store anything, you have to declare every column:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id        INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n  user_id   INTEGER,\n  role      TEXT,\n  content   TEXT,\n  timestamp DATETIME\n);\n```\n\n**The Problem with AI Workloads:**\nLLMs return wildly different shaped outputs. An AI agent might call a weather tool (returning `{ temperature, humidity, wind_speed }`) or a stock tool (returning `{ price, volume, pe_ratio, dividends }`) or a web search (returning `{ url, title, snippet, published_date }`). These shapes are completely different. Creating a SQL table that accommodates ALL possible AI tool outputs would require dozens of nullable columns — a mess.\n\n**NoSQL databases solve this with a simple idea: just store the JSON.**\n\nInstead of rigid tables, you store flexible **documents**:\n```json\n// Document 1: weather tool result\n{\n  \"session_id\": \"abc123\",\n  \"tool\": \"weather\",\n  \"result\": { \"temperature\": 72, \"humidity\": 65, \"city\": \"NYC\" }\n}\n\n// Document 2: stock tool result (completely different shape!)\n{\n  \"session_id\": \"abc124\",\n  \"tool\": \"stock_lookup\",\n  \"result\": { \"ticker\": \"AAPL\", \"price\": 185.50, \"volume\": 62000000 }\n}\n```\n\nBoth documents live happily in the same collection with no schema conflicts.\n\n**When to Use NoSQL:**\n- Storing raw LLM outputs and tool call results (varying shapes)\n- Chat sessions with complex nested message objects\n- Real-time event streaming and logging\n- Rapid prototyping where your schema is still evolving\n\n**Popular NoSQL Databases:**\n- **MongoDB** — The most popular document database. JSON-native.\n- **Firestore** (Google Firebase) — Real-time sync, great for mobile AI apps\n- **DynamoDB** (AWS) — Serverless, scales to any load automatically\n- **Redis** — Lightning-fast in-memory store, perfect for caching LLM responses"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scenario Practice",
            "text": "You're building a multi-tool AI agent. Each time the agent calls a tool, it gets back data in a completely different shape: weather data has temperature and humidity, web search returns URLs and snippets, calculator returns a single number. You try creating a SQL table for this but end up with 30 columns where 25 are always NULL for each row. You switch to MongoDB, storing each tool result as a raw JSON document. Now each document contains exactly the fields it needs — nothing more, nothing less. Querying is simpler, storage is more efficient, and you can add new tools without touching the database schema."
          }
        },
