          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Vector Databases — How AI Searches by Meaning",
            "explanation": "This is the most important database concept for AI engineers. Everything so far — SQL, NoSQL — searches by EXACT matches. But humans don't think in exact matches.\n\n**The Problem with Exact Search:**\nIf a user searches for \"dog\" in a traditional database, it will NOT find documents that contain \"puppy\", \"canine\", or \"German Shepherd\". These are semantically the same concept, but different strings.\n\n**How Vector Databases Solve This:**\nInstead of storing text directly, we convert text into **embeddings** — arrays of numbers that represent meaning.\n\nWords with similar meanings get similar numbers:\n```\n\"dog\"     -> [0.82, 0.15, 0.91, 0.33, ...] (384 numbers)\n\"puppy\"   -> [0.81, 0.17, 0.89, 0.31, ...] (very similar!)\n\"canine\"  -> [0.80, 0.14, 0.92, 0.35, ...] (also similar!)\n\"airplane\" -> [0.12, 0.94, 0.05, 0.78, ...] (very different)\n```\n\nTo find documents similar to a search query:\n1. Convert the search query into an embedding vector\n2. Calculate the mathematical distance between that vector and ALL stored vectors\n3. Return the vectors (documents) that are mathematically closest\n\n\"Dog\" and \"puppy\" are close in vector space. \"Dog\" and \"airplane\" are far apart. This is how AI does semantic search!\n\n**This is the backbone of RAG (Retrieval Augmented Generation):**\n1. You upload 1,000 documents to a vector database\n2. User asks a question\n3. You convert the question to a vector\n4. Vector DB finds the 5 most semantically similar documents\n5. You send those 5 documents + the question to the LLM\n6. LLM answers based on YOUR specific documents\n\nThis is why ChatGPT can answer questions about YOUR company's internal documents — even though it was never trained on them!"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Embeddings: Teaching Computers to Understand Meaning",
            "explanation": "Let's go deeper on embeddings because this is the core concept that makes modern AI work.\n\n**What is an Embedding?**\nAn embedding is a mathematical representation of meaning. Every word, sentence, or document gets converted into a list of numbers (a vector). The numbers are chosen by a neural network trained on billions of text examples so that similar meanings produce similar numbers.\n\n**Generating Embeddings in Python:**\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef get_embedding(text: str) -> list[float]:\n    response = client.embeddings.create(\n        model='text-embedding-3-small',\n        input=text\n    )\n    return response.data[0].embedding\n\n# This returns a list of 1536 numbers\nembedding = get_embedding('How do I fine-tune a language model?')\nprint(f'Vector length: {len(embedding)}')  # 1536\n```\n\n**Measuring Similarity (Cosine Similarity):**\nThe most common way to measure how similar two vectors are:\n- **Score = 1.0** → Identical meaning\n- **Score = 0.8** → Very similar meaning\n- **Score = 0.3** → Somewhat related\n- **Score = 0.0** → Completely unrelated\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef cosine_similarity(vec_a: list, vec_b: list) -> float:\n    a = np.array(vec_a)\n    b = np.array(vec_b)\n    return np.dot(a, b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b))\n\nquery = get_embedding('dog training')\ndoc1  = get_embedding('how to teach your puppy tricks')  # ~0.87\ndoc2  = get_embedding('airplane engine maintenance')     # ~0.12\n\nprint(cosine_similarity(query, doc1))  # 0.87 — very similar!\nprint(cosine_similarity(query, doc2))  # 0.12 — completely different\n```\n\n**The key insight:** By doing this math across millions of stored embeddings, a vector database can find the semantically closest documents to any query in milliseconds — even without a single exact keyword match."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building a Simple RAG System from Scratch",
            "explanation": "Let's build a complete RAG pipeline step by step. This is the most important pattern you'll implement as an AI Engineer.\n\n**Step 1: Chunk and Embed Your Documents**\n```python\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nimport json\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef chunk_text(text: str, chunk_size: int = 500) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"Split text into chunks of ~500 characters\"\"\"\n    words = text.split()\n    chunks = []\n    current = []\n    for word in words:\n        current.append(word)\n        if len(' '.join(current)) > chunk_size:\n            chunks.append(' '.join(current))\n            current = []\n    if current:\n        chunks.append(' '.join(current))\n    return chunks\n\ndef embed(text: str) -> list[float]:\n    res = client.embeddings.create(\n        model='text-embedding-3-small', input=text\n    )\n    return res.data[0].embedding\n```\n\n**Step 2: Store Embeddings in a Vector Database (Chroma)**\n```python\nimport chromadb\n\nchroma = chromadb.Client()\ncollection = chroma.create_collection('my_docs')\n\n# Index your documents\nwith open('company_handbook.txt') as f:\n    full_text = f.read()\n\nchunks = chunk_text(full_text)\nfor i, chunk in enumerate(chunks):\n    collection.add(\n        documents=[chunk],\n        embeddings=[embed(chunk)],\n        ids=[f'chunk_{i}']\n    )\nprint(f'Indexed {len(chunks)} chunks')\n```\n\n**Step 3: Search and Generate Answers**\n```python\ndef rag_answer(user_question: str) -> str:\n    # 1. Embed the question\n    query_embedding = embed(user_question)\n\n    # 2. Find 3 most similar chunks\n    results = collection.query(\n        query_embeddings=[query_embedding],\n        n_results=3\n    )\n    context = '\\n\\n'.join(results['documents'][0])\n\n    # 3. Ask the LLM with the retrieved context\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model='gpt-4o',\n        messages=[\n            {'role': 'system', 'content': f'Use this context to answer: {context}'},\n            {'role': 'user', 'content': user_question}\n        ]\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\n# Use it!\nanswer = rag_answer('What is the vacation policy?')\nprint(answer)  # Answers from YOUR company handbook!\n```\n\nThis is the exact pattern used by enterprise AI systems — from GitHub Copilot to custom customer support bots."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scenario Practice",
            "text": "A law firm hires you to build an AI assistant that can answer questions about their 10,000-page case library. You can't fit 10,000 pages into an LLM's context window, and you can't fine-tune on 10,000 pages cheaply. Instead, you chunk all case documents into 500-word segments, generate embeddings for each chunk, and store them in a vector database. When a lawyer asks 'What precedents exist for software patent disputes?', the system embeds that question, finds the 5 most semantically similar case document chunks, and passes them to GPT-4. The lawyer gets a precise answer sourced from their own case library — even though the LLM was never trained on it."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "ANN Search: How Vector DBs Handle Millions of Vectors",
            "explanation": "Here's a practical challenge: if you have 10 million document embeddings and each is a vector of 1,536 numbers, comparing your query vector against ALL 10 million would require 15 billion multiplication operations. Even on a GPU, that could take seconds.\n\n**The Solution: Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) Search**\n\nInstead of checking every vector, ANN algorithms build smart index structures that let you find the nearest neighbors without searching everything.\n\n**HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World) — The Most Popular Algorithm:**\n\nThink of it like navigating a city:\n- **Top layer:** Major highways — coarse, fast navigation\n- **Middle layers:** Main roads — medium precision\n- **Bottom layer:** Every street — fine-grained search\n\nYou start at the top layer, quickly find which neighborhood your target is in, then zoom into that area with finer layers. You search maybe 0.1% of all vectors but find the correct answer 99% of the time.\n\n**The Speed vs Accuracy Trade-off:**\n```\nExact Search: 100% accuracy, but takes 10 seconds for 10M vectors\nHNSW Search:  99% accuracy, but takes 5 milliseconds for 10M vectors\n```\n\nFor real-world RAG systems, 99% accuracy at 5ms is far more valuable than 100% accuracy at 10 seconds.\n\n**Popular Vector Database Options:**\n\n| Database | Best For | Notes |\n|----|----|----|----|\n| **Chroma** | Local dev, prototyping | Open source, easy to start |\n| **Pinecone** | Production SaaS | Fully managed, no ops needed |\n| **Weaviate** | Production self-hosted | Rich filtering + vector search |\n| **Qdrant** | High performance | Rust-based, very fast |\n| **pgvector** | Already using PostgreSQL | No new DB needed |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Vector Similarity Search with ChromaDB",
            "description": "You have a ChromaDB collection called `docs` with company documents already indexed. Write Python code to: 1) Get an embedding for the query 'remote work policy', and 2) Query the collection to get the top 3 most similar documents.",
            "startingCode": "import chromadb\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nchroma = chromadb.Client()\ncollection = chroma.get_collection('docs')\n\n# Step 1: Embed the query\nquery = 'remote work policy'\nquery_embedding = # YOUR CODE HERE\n\n# Step 2: Search for similar documents\nresults = # YOUR CODE HERE\n\nprint(results['documents'][0])",
            "solution": "import chromadb\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nchroma = chromadb.Client()\ncollection = chroma.get_collection('docs')\n\nquery = 'remote work policy'\nquery_embedding = client.embeddings.create(model='text-embedding-3-small', input=query).data[0].embedding\n\nresults = collection.query(query_embeddings=[query_embedding], n_results=3)\n\nprint(results['documents'][0])",
            "language": "python",
