        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "pgvector — Vector Search Inside PostgreSQL",
            "explanation": "Running a separate vector database alongside your SQL database means managing two systems, two connection pools, two sets of backups, and two bills. For many AI applications, there's a simpler solution:\n\n**pgvector: the PostgreSQL extension that adds vector search to your existing database.**\n\nWith pgvector, you add an `embedding` column to any PostgreSQL table and query for semantic similarity using standard SQL:\n\n**Setup:**\n```sql\n-- Enable the pgvector extension (one time setup)\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n\n-- Create a table that stores both text AND its embedding\nCREATE TABLE documents (\n  id        SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  content   TEXT NOT NULL,\n  source    TEXT,\n  embedding vector(1536)  -- 1536-dimension OpenAI embedding\n);\n\n-- Create an index for fast similarity search\nCREATE INDEX ON documents\nUSING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n```\n\n**Inserting Documents with Embeddings:**\n```python\nimport psycopg2\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nconn = psycopg2.connect('postgresql://localhost/mydb')\ncur = conn.cursor()\n\ndef insert_document(content: str, source: str):\n    embedding = client.embeddings.create(\n        model='text-embedding-3-small',\n        input=content\n    ).data[0].embedding\n\n    cur.execute(\n        'INSERT INTO documents (content, source, embedding) VALUES (%s, %s, %s)',\n        (content, source, embedding)\n    )\n    conn.commit()\n```\n\n**Querying for Similar Documents:**\n```python\ndef semantic_search(query: str, limit: int = 5) -> list:\n    query_embedding = client.embeddings.create(\n        model='text-embedding-3-small',\n        input=query\n    ).data[0].embedding\n\n    cur.execute(\n        '''\n        SELECT content, source,\n               1 - (embedding <=> %s::vector) AS similarity\n        FROM documents\n        ORDER BY embedding <=> %s::vector\n        LIMIT %s\n        ''',\n        (query_embedding, query_embedding, limit)\n    )\n    return cur.fetchall()\n\n# Semantic search from plain SQL!\nresults = semantic_search('vacation and time off policy')\n```\n\n**The `<=>` operator is pgvector's cosine distance operator.**\n\nThe beauty here: you can combine vector search with regular SQL filters in ONE query:\n```sql\n-- Find similar documents BUT only from the HR department\nSELECT content FROM documents\nWHERE source = 'HR'\nORDER BY embedding <=> '[...]'::vector\nLIMIT 5;\n```\n\nThis is called **hybrid search** — semantic similarity + metadata filtering — and it's much harder to achieve with a pure vector database."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "pgvector: Semantic Search Query",
            "description": "You have a PostgreSQL `documents` table with an `embedding vector(1536)` column. Using pgvector's cosine distance operator `<=>`, write a SQL query that finds the 5 most similar documents to a given query vector `$1`. Return the `content` and `source` columns, along with a similarity score calculated as `1 - (embedding <=> $1)`. Order by most similar first.",
            "startingCode": "SELECT \n  content,\n  source,\n  -- Calculate similarity score\nFROM documents\nORDER BY \nLIMIT 5;",
            "solution": "SELECT\n  content,\n  source,\n  1 - (embedding <=> $1::vector) AS similarity\nFROM documents\nORDER BY embedding <=> $1::vector\nLIMIT 5;",
            "language": "sql",
            "difficulty": "Medium",
            "validation": true,
            "question": "Write the code here:",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Graph Databases and GraphRAG",
            "explanation": "Vector search is brilliant at finding similar content, but it struggles with complex multi-hop relationship questions.\n\n**Example question that breaks vector search:**\n\"What are the patents held by companies that were acquired by the CEO's former employer?\"\n\nThis requires following a chain of relationships: CEO -> Former Employer -> Companies They Acquired -> Patents those companies hold.\n\n**Graph databases are built exactly for this.**\n\nIn a graph database, data is stored as:\n- **Nodes** — Entities (Person, Company, Patent, Technology)\n- **Edges** — Relationships between entities (WORKS_AT, ACQUIRED, HOLDS_PATENT)\n\n```\nExample Graph:\n\n(Elon Musk) --[FOUNDED]--> (Tesla)\n(Elon Musk) --[FOUNDED]--> (SpaceX)\n(Tesla)     --[HOLDS]--> (Patent: Battery Technology)\n(Tesla)     --[HOLDS]--> (Patent: Autopilot System)\n(SpaceX)    --[HOLDS]--> (Patent: Rocket Reuse)\n```\n\n**Graph Query (Cypher language for Neo4j):**\n```cypher\n// Find all patents held by companies Elon Musk founded\nMATCH (person:Person {name: 'Elon Musk'})\n      -[:FOUNDED]->\n      (company:Company)\n      -[:HOLDS]->\n      (patent:Patent)\nRETURN company.name, patent.title\n```\n\nThis traverses TWO relationships in one query — something that would require multiple JOIN statements in SQL.\n\n**GraphRAG — Combining Graphs with LLMs:**\nMicrosoft Research released GraphRAG in 2024. The idea:\n1. Use an LLM to read your documents and extract entities and relationships\n2. Build a knowledge graph from those entities\n3. When answering questions, traverse the graph to pull relevant sub-graphs\n4. Feed the connected facts to the LLM for a comprehensive answer\n\n**When to Use Graph Databases:**\n- Recommendation systems (users who liked X also liked Y)\n- Fraud detection (tracing transaction networks)\n- Enterprise knowledge graphs (org charts, product relationships)\n- Biomedical research (gene-protein-disease networks)"
          }
        },
        {
