        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Choosing the Right Database for Your AI App",
            "explanation": "In a production AI application, you'll almost certainly use MULTIPLE database types together. The skill is knowing exactly which database handles which job.\n\n**The Complete AI Application Stack:**\n\n```\nYour AI Application\n       |\n       |--- PostgreSQL (SQL)\n       |    User accounts, subscriptions, billing\n       |    API keys, usage limits\n       |    Audit logs, structured reports\n       |\n       |--- pgvector / Pinecone (Vector)\n       |    RAG document chunks with embeddings\n       |    Semantic search across user content\n       |    Conversation memory retrieval\n       |\n       |--- Redis (Cache)\n       |    LLM response caching\n       |    Active session state\n       |    Rate limiting counters\n       |\n       |--- MongoDB (NoSQL) — optional\n       |    Raw LLM logs (flexible JSON)\n       |    Tool call results\n       |    A/B test data\n       |\n       |--- Neo4j (Graph) — for complex apps\n            Knowledge graphs\n            Recommendation engines\n```\n\n**Quick Decision Guide:**\n\n| Your Need | Use This |\n|----|----|----|\n| Store user accounts, payments | PostgreSQL (SQL) |\n| Find semantically similar docs | Vector DB / pgvector |\n| Cache expensive LLM calls | Redis |\n| Store dynamic JSON logs | MongoDB |\n| Query complex relationships | Neo4j (Graph DB) |\n| Real-time updates to clients | Firebase / Firestore |\n\n**For Most AI Startups, Start Here:**\nPostgreSQL + pgvector + Redis covers 90% of your needs in a single stack. PostgreSQL handles structured data, pgvector handles semantic search, and Redis handles caching and sessions. Add a dedicated vector database (Pinecone/Weaviate) only when your embeddings exceed tens of millions of vectors."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Scenario Practice",
            "text": "You're the lead engineer of an AI writing assistant with 50,000 users. Your system needs to: (1) Store user accounts and subscription plans, (2) Remember users' previous documents for personalized suggestions, (3) Allow users to search their own document library semantically, (4) Cache expensive GPT-4 calls to reduce costs by 60%, and (5) Log every LLM call for billing and debugging. Your architecture decision: PostgreSQL for user data and billing (ACID required), pgvector in the same PostgreSQL instance for document semantic search (avoids managing a separate vector DB), Redis for LLM response caching and session state, and a separate MongoDB collection for raw LLM call logs (flexible JSON schema, high write volume). This gives you a robust, cost-effective architecture using four complementary database types."
          }
