        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Production Database Patterns for AI Engineers",
            "explanation": "Now let's look at the patterns that separate hobby projects from production AI systems.\n\n**Pattern 1: Connection Pooling**\nOpening a new database connection for every request is expensive. Connection pools keep a set of connections open and reuse them:\n\n```python\nfrom sqlalchemy import create_engine\n\n# Bad: Opens a new connection every request\ndef bad_get_user(user_id):\n    conn = psycopg2.connect('...')  # Slow! ~100ms\n    # ...\n    conn.close()\n\n# Good: Reuses connections from a pool\nengine = create_engine(\n    'postgresql://localhost/mydb',\n    pool_size=10,        # Keep 10 connections open\n    max_overflow=20,     # Allow up to 20 extra when busy\n    pool_pre_ping=True   # Check connection is alive before use\n)\n```\n\n**Pattern 2: Database Migrations**\nAs your app grows, your database schema evolves. Migrations track these changes like Git tracks code:\n```bash\n# Using Alembic (the standard Python migration tool)\nalembic revision --autogenerate -m 'add embedding column to messages'\nalembic upgrade head  # Apply the migration\n```\n\n**Pattern 3: Read Replicas for AI Workloads**\nAI apps have a unique read/write pattern:\n- **Writes are rare:** User sends a message, you log it once\n- **Reads are constant:** Every RAG retrieval reads from the vector store\n\nSolution: Set up a read replica that handles all search queries while the primary handles writes. This doubles your read capacity.\n\n**Pattern 4: Async Database Access**\nLLM APIs are slow (1-5 seconds per call). While waiting for OpenAI to respond, your server can handle other requests if you use async database queries:\n```python\nimport asyncpg\n\nasync def get_user_history(user_id: int) -> list:\n    conn = await asyncpg.connect('postgresql://...')\n    # While this query runs, the server handles other requests\n    rows = await conn.fetch(\n        'SELECT * FROM messages WHERE user_id = $1 ORDER BY timestamp',\n        user_id\n    )\n    await conn.close()\n    return rows\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Designing the AI Chat History Schema",
            "explanation": "Let's design a production-grade schema for an AI chat application. This is the kind of system design question you'll face in real interviews.\n\n**The Requirements:**\n- Users can have multiple chat sessions\n- Each session has multiple messages\n- Messages need vector embeddings for semantic memory\n- We need to track token usage for billing\n- Sessions should expire after 30 days of inactivity\n\n**The Schema:**\n```sql\n-- Enable pgvector\nCREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;\n\n-- Users table\nCREATE TABLE users (\n  id           UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  email        TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,\n  plan         TEXT DEFAULT 'free',\n  created_at   TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- Sessions (one conversation thread)\nCREATE TABLE sessions (\n  id           UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  user_id      UUID REFERENCES users(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  title        TEXT,\n  created_at   TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n  last_active  TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- Messages with embeddings for semantic memory\nCREATE TABLE messages (\n  id           UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid(),\n  session_id   UUID REFERENCES sessions(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,\n  role         TEXT NOT NULL,  -- 'user' or 'assistant'\n  content      TEXT NOT NULL,\n  embedding    vector(1536),   -- For semantic memory search\n  tokens_used  INTEGER,\n  created_at   TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);\n\n-- Indexes for performance\nCREATE INDEX ON sessions(user_id);\nCREATE INDEX ON messages(session_id);\nCREATE INDEX ON messages USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);\n```\n\n**Semantic Memory — Remembering What Matters:**\n```python\nasync def recall_relevant_history(user_id: str, current_message: str) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Find past messages semantically related to current topic\"\"\"\n    embedding = get_embedding(current_message)\n\n    # Find similar messages from user's history across ALL sessions\n    results = await db.fetch(\n        '''\n        SELECT m.content, m.role, s.title\n        FROM messages m\n        JOIN sessions s ON m.session_id = s.id\n        WHERE s.user_id = $1\n        ORDER BY m.embedding <=> $2::vector\n        LIMIT 5\n        ''',\n        user_id, embedding\n    )\n    return results\n```\n\nThis gives your AI a long-term semantic memory across all past sessions — a feature that makes your chatbot feel genuinely intelligent."
          }
        },
