        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Module Recap: Your Complete Database Toolkit",
            "explanation": "You've just covered everything a production AI Engineer needs to know about databases. Let's recap the full picture:\n\n**Phase 1 — Foundations:**\nDatabases solve the three core problems with plain files: concurrency (many simultaneous users), speed (instant lookups with indexes), and durability (data survives crashes via ACID).\n\n**Phase 2 — SQL / Relational Databases:**\nTables, rows, columns, JOIN queries. PostgreSQL is your best friend. Use it for anything where data correctness is critical: users, billing, API keys, and structured logs.\n\n**Phase 3 — NoSQL Databases:**\nMongoDB for flexible JSON documents (dynamic LLM outputs). Redis for ultra-fast in-memory caching, session state, and rate limiting. These complement SQL, not replace it.\n\n**Phase 4 — Vector Databases:**\nThe AI engineer's secret weapon. Embeddings turn text into numbers that represent meaning. Vector search finds semantically similar content even without exact keyword matches. This is the backbone of every RAG system.\n\n**Phase 5 — pgvector:**\nGet vector search inside your existing PostgreSQL. One database instead of two. The `<=>` operator does cosine distance. Add an HNSW index for production performance.\n\n**Phase 6 — Graph Databases:**\nWhen your data is highly connected (social networks, knowledge graphs, fraud detection). GraphRAG combines LLMs with knowledge graphs for multi-hop reasoning.\n\n**Phase 7 — Choosing the Right Tool:**\nMost AI apps use PostgreSQL + pgvector + Redis as their core stack. Add MongoDB for dynamic logs, and a dedicated vector DB only at very large scale.\n\n**Phase 8 — Production Patterns:**\nConnection pooling, database migrations, read replicas, and async queries are what separate production-grade code from hobby projects.\n\n**You are now equipped to design the database architecture for any AI application — from a weekend hackathon to a billion-user platform.**"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Capstone Project: Multi-Database Analytics Dashboard",
            "explanation": "**Your Capstone Mission:**\n\nBuild a data analytics system that uses multiple database types (SQL, NoSQL, Vector DB) together, demonstrating when and why you would choose each one.\n\n**What You Will Build:**\nAn \"AI Content Analytics Platform\" that tracks articles, user interactions, and content recommendations. It uses PostgreSQL for structured data, MongoDB for flexible content storage, Redis for real-time metrics, and pgvector for content similarity search.\n\n**Project Requirements:**\n\n1. **PostgreSQL (Relational)**:\n   - Users table with authentication data\n   - Content metadata (title, author, publish date, category)\n   - Analytics events (views, likes, shares) with proper indexing\n   - Write complex queries: JOINs, aggregations, window functions\n\n2. **MongoDB (Document Store)**:\n   - Store full article content with flexible schema\n   - Nested comments and reactions\n   - Handle schema evolution (articles v1 vs v2)\n\n3. **Redis (Key-Value Cache)**:\n   - Cache trending articles (TTL-based)\n   - Real-time view counters\n   - Session management\n   - Leaderboard with sorted sets\n\n4. **pgvector (Vector Database)**:\n   - Store article embeddings\n   - Find similar articles using cosine similarity\n   - Build a \"Recommended for You\" feature\n\n5. **Data Pipeline**:\n   - Sync data between databases\n   - Handle consistency across stores\n   - Implement a simple ETL process\n\n**SQL Queries You Must Write:**\n```sql\n-- Top 10 most-read articles this week\n-- User engagement funnel (view -> like -> share)\n-- Author performance comparison\n-- Content category trends over time\n```\n\n**Architecture Diagram:**\n```\nUser Request → API Layer\n                ├── PostgreSQL (users, analytics, metadata)\n                ├── MongoDB (full content, comments)\n                ├── Redis (cache, real-time counters)\n                └── pgvector (similarity search, recommendations)\n```\n\n**Stretch Goals:**\n- Add a GraphQL API layer\n- Implement database replication\n- Build a real-time analytics dashboard with WebSockets\n\nThis project proves you understand when to use which database — the most important skill in database engineering!"
          }
        }
      ]
