          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Cost Optimization — Cutting LLM Costs by 80%",
            "explanation": "LLM API costs can spiral out of control. A production chatbot processing 100,000 queries/day can cost $10,000-$50,000/month. Here's how to slash that.\n\n**The Cost Equation:**\n```\nCost = (Input Tokens + Output Tokens) × Price per Token × Number of Requests\n```\n\n**Strategy 1: Model Routing (Save 60-80%)**\nNot every query needs GPT-4. Route simple questions to cheap models:\n\n```python\nasync def smart_route(query: str) -> str:\n    complexity = classify_complexity(query)  # Use a tiny classifier\n    \n    if complexity == 'simple':\n        # 'What time do you close?' -> $0.0001\n        return await call_model('gpt-4o-mini', query)\n    elif complexity == 'medium':\n        # 'Explain our refund policy' -> $0.002\n        return await call_model('gpt-4o', query)\n    else:\n        # 'Compare our enterprise plans...' -> $0.01\n        return await call_model('gpt-4', query)\n\n# Result: 70% of queries go to mini, saving massive costs!\n```\n\n**Strategy 2: Semantic Caching (Save 30-50%)**\n```python\n# Similar questions get cached responses\n# 'What is Python?' and 'Tell me about Python' -> same cache hit\nfrom redis import Redis\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef semantic_cache(query: str, threshold=0.95):\n    query_embedding = embed(query)\n    cached = redis.get_nearest(query_embedding)\n    if cached and cached.similarity > threshold:\n        return cached.response  # Free! No API call\n    return None  # Cache miss, call the LLM\n```\n\n**Strategy 3: Prompt Compression (Save 20-40%)**\n```python\n# Before: 2,000 tokens of context\ncontext = \"The company was founded in 2015 by John Smith...\"\n\n# After: 800 tokens (same information)\ncompressed = llm_lingua.compress(context, ratio=0.4)\n# 'Company founded 2015 by John Smith...'\n```\n\n**Strategy 4: Streaming + Early Termination**\nIf the user navigates away mid-response, stop generating:\n```python\nasync for chunk in stream:\n    if user_disconnected:\n        break  # Stop paying for tokens nobody will read!\n    yield chunk\n```\n\n**The Cost Optimization Stack:**\n\n| Strategy | Savings | Effort |\n|----------|---------|--------|\n| Model Routing | 60-80% | Medium |\n| Semantic Cache | 30-50% | Low |\n| Prompt Compression | 20-40% | Low |\n| Output Length Limits | 10-20% | Trivial |\n| Batch Processing | 20-30% | Medium |\n| Early Termination | 5-15% | Low |\n\nCombined, these strategies can reduce your LLM costs by 80-90%."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "LLM Serving Infrastructure — Self-Hosting vs API",
            "explanation": "Should you use OpenAI's API or run your own LLM? This is one of the most important infrastructure decisions.\n\n**Option 1: API-Based (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google)**\n\nPros:\n- Zero infrastructure to manage\n- Best models available (GPT-4, Claude, Gemini)\n- Scales automatically\n- Pay only for what you use\n\nCons:\n- Data leaves your network (privacy concerns)\n- Vendor lock-in and rate limits\n- Costs scale linearly with usage\n- No control over model updates\n\n**Option 2: Self-Hosted (vLLM, Ollama, TGI)**\n\nPros:\n- Complete data privacy (nothing leaves your servers)\n- Fixed cost regardless of usage volume\n- Full control over model version\n- Customize and fine-tune freely\n\nCons:\n- Expensive GPU hardware ($10K-$100K+)\n- You manage infrastructure, scaling, failover\n- Open-source models are generally weaker than GPT-4\n\n**GPU Memory Estimation:**\nThe #1 question when self-hosting: 'How much GPU memory do I need?'\n\n```python\ndef estimate_gpu_memory(params_billions: float, precision: str) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Estimate GPU memory needed for an LLM.\"\"\"\n    bytes_per_param = {\n        'fp32': 4,    # 32-bit floating point\n        'fp16': 2,    # 16-bit (half precision)\n        'int8': 1,    # 8-bit quantization\n        'int4': 0.5,  # 4-bit quantization (GPTQ, GGUF)\n    }\n    \n    model_size_gb = params_billions * bytes_per_param[precision]\n    overhead_gb = model_size_gb * 0.2  # ~20% overhead for KV cache\n    \n    return model_size_gb + overhead_gb\n\n# Examples:\nprint(estimate_gpu_memory(7, 'fp16'))   # Llama 7B: ~16.8 GB\nprint(estimate_gpu_memory(70, 'int4'))  # Llama 70B: ~42 GB\nprint(estimate_gpu_memory(7, 'int4'))   # Llama 7B quantized: ~4.2 GB\n```\n\n**vLLM — The Production Serving Engine:**\n```python\n# Start a vLLM server (OpenAI-compatible API!)\n# vllm serve meta-llama/Llama-3-8B-Instruct \\\n#   --gpu-memory-utilization 0.9 \\\n#   --max-model-len 4096\n\n# Then call it like OpenAI:\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nclient = OpenAI(base_url='http://localhost:8000/v1')\nresponse = client.chat.completions.create(\n    model='meta-llama/Llama-3-8B-Instruct',\n    messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Hello!'}]\n)\n```\n\nvLLM uses PagedAttention and continuous batching to serve 10-24x more requests per GPU than naive serving."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "LLM GPU Memory Estimator",
            "question": "Complete the function that estimates the GPU memory required to serve an LLM. It should take the number of parameters (in billions) and precision format, then return the estimated memory in GB (including 20% overhead for KV cache).",
            "starterCode": "def estimate_gpu_memory(params_billions: float, precision: str) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Estimate GPU VRAM needed to serve an LLM.\n    \n    Args:\n        params_billions: Model size in billions of parameters\n        precision: One of 'fp32', 'fp16', 'int8', 'int4'\n    \n    Returns:\n        Estimated GPU memory in GB\n    \"\"\"\n    bytes_per_param = {\n        'fp32': 4,\n        'fp16': 2,\n        'int8': 1,\n        'int4': 0.5,\n    }\n    \n    # Calculate model size and add 20% overhead\n    # Return total memory in GB",
            "solution": "def estimate_gpu_memory(params_billions: float, precision: str) -> float:\n    bytes_per_param = {\n        'fp32': 4,\n        'fp16': 2,\n        'int8': 1,\n        'int4': 0.5,\n    }\n    \n    model_size_gb = params_billions * bytes_per_param[precision]\n    overhead_gb = model_size_gb * 0.2\n    \n    return model_size_gb + overhead_gb",
            "language": "python",
            "hint": "Multiply params_billions by the bytes_per_param value to get model size in GB. Then add 20% overhead (model_size * 0.2)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Data Flywheel & Fine-Tuning Pipelines",
            "explanation": "The best AI companies don't just deploy models — they build data flywheels that make their models better over time.\n\n**The Data Flywheel:**\n```\nDeploy AI → Collect user interactions → Identify failures → \nCreate training data → Fine-tune model → Deploy improved AI → Repeat\n```\n\nEach cycle makes the model better, which attracts more users, which generates more data, which enables more improvement. This is why ChatGPT keeps getting better.\n\n**Fine-Tuning Methods (From Simplest to Most Complex):**\n\n**1. Few-Shot Prompting (No Training Required):**\n```python\nprompt = \"\"\"\nClassify customer support tickets.\n\nExamples:\n\"My order hasn't arrived\" -> shipping\n\"Can I get a refund?\" -> billing  \n\"The app crashes on login\" -> technical\n\nClassify: \"{user_ticket}\"\n\"\"\"\n```\n\n**2. Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT):**\nTrain the model on your specific task with labeled examples:\n```python\n# Training data format (JSONL)\n{\"messages\": [\n    {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are a medical AI assistant.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What are symptoms of diabetes?\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Common symptoms include...\"}\n]}\n```\n\n**3. LoRA (Low-Rank Adaptation) — The Game Changer:**\nFine-tune without modifying the base model weights:\n```python\nfrom peft import LoraConfig, get_peft_model\n\nconfig = LoraConfig(\n    r=16,              # Rank of adaptation matrices\n    lora_alpha=32,     # Scaling factor\n    target_modules=['q_proj', 'v_proj'],  # Which layers to adapt\n    lora_dropout=0.05,\n)\n\n# Only trains 0.1% of parameters!\n# Base model: 7 billion params (frozen)\n# LoRA adapter: 7 million params (trainable)\nmodel = get_peft_model(base_model, config)\n```\n\n**4. RLHF / DPO (Alignment):**\n- **RLHF**: Train a reward model on human preferences, then optimize the LLM to maximize rewards\n- **DPO**: Directly optimize preferences without a separate reward model (simpler!)\n\n**When to Fine-Tune vs Prompt Engineer:**\n- **Prompt Engineering**: Works for 80% of use cases. Always try this first.\n- **Fine-Tuning**: When you need consistent formatting, domain-specific knowledge, or prompting hits its limits.\n- **LoRA**: When you want fine-tuning benefits with minimal compute cost."
          }
        },
