      "id": "M018-production-system-design",
      "title": "Module 18: Production AI System Design",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What Does 'Production' Actually Mean?",
            "explanation": "You've built amazing AI projects throughout this course — chatbots, RAG systems, agents, and more. But there's a huge difference between a project that works on YOUR laptop and one that works for THOUSANDS of users 24/7.\n\n**Think of it like cooking:**\n- Building a prototype = cooking dinner for yourself. If you burn the garlic, no big deal.\n- Going to production = running a restaurant. Hundreds of customers expect consistent quality, the kitchen can't go down during rush hour, and health inspectors check your safety standards.\n\n**A production AI system must handle 3 things:**\n\n1. **Reliability** — It works 99.9% of the time. When the LLM API goes down at 3 AM, the system handles it gracefully instead of showing users a blank screen.\n\n2. **Scalability** — It handles 10 users today and 10,000 users tomorrow without rewriting everything. The architecture grows WITH your user base.\n\n3. **Security** — User data is protected. Prompt injection attacks are blocked. PII (Personally Identifiable Information) never leaks. Compliance requirements (GDPR, HIPAA) are met.\n\n**Why Prototypes Fail in Production:**\n\n• No error handling → one API timeout crashes everything\n• No caching → you pay $100/day in LLM costs for repeated queries\n• No rate limiting → one aggressive user takes down your service\n• No monitoring → you don't know something's broken until users complain\n• Hardcoded API keys → security breach waiting to happen\n\nThis module teaches you how to take everything you've built and make it production-ready. We'll start simple and progressively add enterprise-grade features."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What are the 3 key requirements for a production AI system?",
              "options": [
                "Speed, beauty, simplicity",
                "Reliability, scalability, security",
                "Cost, features, documentation",
                "Testing, deployment, monitoring"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "The 3 pillars of production systems are: Reliability (it works 99.9% of the time), Scalability (it handles growing traffic), and Security (user data is protected and attacks are blocked)."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why do most AI prototypes fail when put into production?",
              "options": [
                "The AI model is too slow",
                "They lack error handling, caching, rate limiting, and monitoring",
                "Python is not suitable for production",
                "LLM APIs don't work outside localhost"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Prototypes typically lack the infrastructure that production systems need: error handling for API failures, caching to reduce costs, rate limiting to prevent abuse, and monitoring to detect issues."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The 6-Layer Reference Architecture",
            "explanation": "Every production AI system follows a similar blueprint — a layered architecture where each layer has a specific job. Think of it like building a house: you need a foundation, walls, plumbing, electrical, roof, and security system. Each layer can be built and improved independently.\n\n**Here's the simple version:**\n\n**Layer 1 — Client Layer (the face):** What users see and interact with. A React/Next.js frontend, a mobile app, or a Slack bot. This layer sends requests and displays responses.\n\n**Layer 2 — API Gateway (the bouncer):** The single door into your system. It checks IDs (authentication), controls the crowd (rate limiting), and directs people to the right room (routing). Every request passes through here first.\n\n**Layer 3 — Application Layer (the brain):** Your FastAPI backend. This is where agent orchestration, prompt management, and business logic live. It receives validated requests from the gateway and coordinates everything.\n\n**Layer 4 — AI Layer (the engine):** The LLM providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.) and your RAG pipeline. This layer handles the actual AI work — generating responses, retrieving context, calling tools.\n\n**Layer 5 — Data Layer (the memory):** PostgreSQL for structured data, Redis for caching, and a Vector DB for embeddings. Everything your system needs to remember lives here.\n\n**Layer 6 — Infrastructure Layer (the foundation):** Kubernetes for orchestration, CI/CD for deployments, and monitoring tools (Prometheus, Grafana). This keeps everything running smoothly.\n\n**The Golden Rule:** Each layer is independently deployable. The frontend team ships UI updates without touching the backend. The AI team changes prompts without redeploying infrastructure. This separation is what makes large systems manageable.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Start Simple, Add Layers",
                    "body": "You don't build all 6 layers on day one. Start with Layers 3+4 combined (a simple FastAPI app calling OpenAI). Add layers as your needs grow. Most startups operate with just 3 layers for months before adding more."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Separation of Concerns",
                    "body": "Each layer has ONE job. The API Gateway doesn't run AI models. The AI Layer doesn't manage users. This separation means you can replace any layer without affecting others — swap PostgreSQL for MySQL, switch from OpenAI to Anthropic, or change your frontend from React to Vue."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Modular Monolith First",
                    "body": "Don't start with microservices! Begin with a single FastAPI app that has clear module boundaries (auth module, chat module, RAG module). Only split into separate services when a specific module needs independent scaling — like when your embedding service needs GPUs but your chat service doesn't."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "code-example",
                "type": "code",
                "content": "```python\nfrom fastapi import FastAPI\nfrom contextlib import asynccontextmanager\n\n@asynccontextmanager\nasync def lifespan(app: FastAPI):\n    # Startup: Initialize all services\n    app.state.llm_client = init_llm_provider()\n    app.state.vector_db = init_vector_store()\n    app.state.cache = init_redis_cache()\n    print(\"All AI services initialized\")\n    yield\n    # Shutdown: Clean up resources\n    print(\"Shutting down AI services\")\n\napp = FastAPI(\n    title=\"Production AI API\",\n    version=\"1.0.0\",\n    lifespan=lifespan\n)\n\n@app.get(\"/health\")\nasync def health_check():\n    return {\"status\": \"healthy\"}\n\n@app.post(\"/api/v1/chat\")\nasync def chat(request: ChatRequest):\n    # Layer 2 already handled auth + rate limiting\n    context = await retrieve_context(request.query)  # Layer 4: RAG\n    response = await generate_response(request, context)  # Layer 4: LLM\n    return {\"response\": response}\n```"
              }
            ]
          }
