        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Reliability: Designing for Failure",
            "explanation": "AI systems are inherently fragile. They depend on external APIs that go down, models that produce non-deterministic outputs, and networks that partition. The goal isn't zero failures — it's recovering in minutes, not hours.\n\n**The Core Reliability Patterns:**\n\n**1. Retry with Exponential Backoff**\nWhen an API fails, wait 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s before retrying. This gives the server time to recover. Always set max retries (3-5).\n\n**2. Circuit Breaker Pattern**\nLike an electrical circuit breaker. If a service fails 5 times in 60 seconds, stop calling it entirely. After a cooling period, try one test request.\n\nThree states:\n- **Closed** (normal): Requests flow, failures counted\n- **Open** (broken): All requests fail-fast\n- **Half-Open** (testing): One test request\n\n**3. Fallback Chains**\n- Primary: GPT-4o via OpenAI\n- Secondary: GPT-4o via Azure\n- Tertiary: Claude 3.5 Sonnet\n- Emergency: Cached response\n\n**4. Graceful Degradation**\n- Full mode: LLM + RAG + tools\n- Reduced: LLM only (if vector DB is down)\n- Minimal: Cached FAQ (if all LLMs are down)\n- Emergency: Static status page\n\nEach level provides less value but ensures users never see a blank screen."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Circuit Breaker State Machine",
            "description": "The circuit breaker prevents cascading failures by automatically stopping calls to a failing service.",
            "type": "flow",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "closed",
                "label": "CLOSED (Normal)",
                "description": "Requests flow through. Failures are counted. If threshold exceeded, circuit opens."
              },
              {
                "id": "open",
                "label": "OPEN (Broken)",
                "description": "All requests fail-fast immediately. No calls to failing service. After timeout, moves to half-open."
              },
              {
                "id": "half-open",
                "label": "HALF-OPEN (Testing)",
                "description": "One test request sent. Success closes circuit. Failure reopens it."
              },
              {
                "id": "success",
                "label": "Service Recovered",
                "description": "Failing service is back. Normal traffic resumes."
              },
              {
                "id": "failure",
                "label": "Still Failing",
                "description": "Service still down. Circuit reopens."
              }
            ],
            "edges": [
              {
                "from": "closed",
                "to": "open",
                "label": "5 failures in 60s"
              },
              {
                "from": "open",
                "to": "half-open",
                "label": "After 30s timeout"
              },
              {
                "from": "half-open",
                "to": "success",
                "label": "Test succeeds"
              },
              {
                "from": "success",
                "to": "closed",
                "label": "Resume"
              },
              {
                "from": "half-open",
                "to": "failure",
                "label": "Test fails"
              },
              {
                "from": "failure",
                "to": "open",
                "label": "Reopen"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Circuit Breaker Implementation",
            "difficulty": "Hard",
            "question": "Complete the circuit breaker's call method. Handle OPEN state (check timeout), try calling func, handle success (reset) and failure (increment).",
            "starterCode": "import time\nfrom enum import Enum\n\nclass State(Enum):\n    CLOSED = \"closed\"\n    OPEN = \"open\"\n    HALF_OPEN = \"half_open\"\n\nclass CircuitBreaker:\n    def __init__(self, threshold=5, timeout=30):\n        self.state = State.CLOSED\n        self.failures = 0\n        self.threshold = threshold\n        self.timeout = timeout\n        self.last_failure = 0\n    \n    def call(self, func, *args):\n        # TODO: Handle OPEN state\n        # TODO: Try calling func, handle success and failure\n        pass",
            "solution": "import time\nfrom enum import Enum\n\nclass State(Enum):\n    CLOSED = \"closed\"\n    OPEN = \"open\"\n    HALF_OPEN = \"half_open\"\n\nclass CircuitBreaker:\n    def __init__(self, threshold=5, timeout=30):\n        self.state = State.CLOSED\n        self.failures = 0\n        self.threshold = threshold\n        self.timeout = timeout\n        self.last_failure = 0\n    \n    def call(self, func, *args):\n        if self.state == State.OPEN:\n            if time.time() - self.last_failure > self.timeout:\n                self.state = State.HALF_OPEN\n            else:\n                raise Exception(\"Circuit is OPEN\")\n        \n        try:\n            result = func(*args)\n            self.failures = 0\n            self.state = State.CLOSED\n            return result\n        except Exception as e:\n            self.failures += 1\n            self.last_failure = time.time()\n            if self.failures >= self.threshold:\n                self.state = State.OPEN\n            raise",
            "hint": "Check if state is OPEN first. If timeout elapsed, move to HALF_OPEN. Then try func(). On success: reset. On failure: increment and check threshold.",
            "explanation": "The circuit breaker stops calling a failing service after repeated failures, preventing cascading failures across your system.",
            "concepts": [
              "Circuit Breaker",
              "Reliability",
              "Error Handling"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "State\\.OPEN.*State\\.HALF_OPEN"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What does exponential backoff mean for retries?",
              "options": [
                "Retry immediately",
                "Wait increasingly longer (1s, 2s, 4s, 8s...)",
                "Retry once and give up",
                "Send multiple retries simultaneously"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Exponential backoff doubles wait time between retries, giving the failing service time to recover."
            },
            {
              "question": "In the circuit breaker, what happens in HALF-OPEN state?",
              "options": [
                "All requests blocked",
                "50% of requests allowed",
                "One test request sent to check recovery",
                "Circuit is permanently broken"
              ],
              "answer": 2,
              "explanation": "HALF-OPEN allows one test request. Success closes the circuit, failure reopens it."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is graceful degradation?",
              "options": [
                "Slowly making the app worse",
                "Offering reduced functionality instead of complete failure",
                "Reducing model quality",
                "Degrading the UI for mobile"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Graceful degradation provides reduced functionality when components fail, ensuring users never see a blank screen."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Cost Engineering: Every Dollar Counts",
            "explanation": "At scale, LLM costs can dominate your entire engineering budget. Cost engineering ensures every dollar delivers maximum value.\n\n**The Cost Optimization Stack:**\n\n**1. Cost Visibility** — Track exact dollar cost per request, per user, per feature, per model. Display on a real-time dashboard.\n\n**2. Prompt Compression** — Reduce input tokens: concise system prompts, fewer few-shot examples. Tools like LLMLingua save 20-40% with <2% quality loss.\n\n**3. Batch Processing** — For non-realtime workloads: OpenAI Batch API gives 50% discount, results within 24 hours.\n\n**4. Token Budget Management** — Set hard limits: max input tokens, max output tokens, max conversation turns, max tool calls. Enforce server-side.\n\n**5. Unit Economics** — Calculate: cost per conversation, cost per customer per month, cost per revenue dollar. Below 3x ratio? Optimize aggressively."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "language": "python",
            "title": "Cost Tracker",
            "difficulty": "Easy",
            "question": "Complete the track() method. Calculate cost from token counts and pricing (per million tokens), add to monthly spending, warn if budget >90% consumed.",
            "starterCode": "from datetime import datetime\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nclass CostTracker:\n    PRICING = {\n        \"gpt-4o\": {\"input\": 2.50, \"output\": 10.00},\n        \"gpt-4o-mini\": {\"input\": 0.15, \"output\": 0.60},\n    }\n    \n    def __init__(self, monthly_budget=100.0):\n        self.budget = monthly_budget\n        self.spending = defaultdict(float)\n    \n    def track(self, model, input_tokens, output_tokens):\n        # TODO: Calculate cost, add to spending, check budget\n        pass\n\ntracker = CostTracker(monthly_budget=50.0)\ncost = tracker.track(\"gpt-4o\", 1500, 500)\nprint(f\"Cost: ${cost:.4f}\")",
            "solution": "from datetime import datetime\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nclass CostTracker:\n    PRICING = {\n        \"gpt-4o\": {\"input\": 2.50, \"output\": 10.00},\n        \"gpt-4o-mini\": {\"input\": 0.15, \"output\": 0.60},\n    }\n    \n    def __init__(self, monthly_budget=100.0):\n        self.budget = monthly_budget\n        self.spending = defaultdict(float)\n    \n    def track(self, model, input_tokens, output_tokens):\n        prices = self.PRICING.get(model, self.PRICING[\"gpt-4o-mini\"])\n        cost = (input_tokens * prices[\"input\"] + output_tokens * prices[\"output\"]) / 1_000_000\n        month = datetime.now().strftime(\"%Y-%m\")\n        self.spending[month] += cost\n        remaining = self.budget - self.spending[month]\n        if remaining < self.budget * 0.1:\n            print(f\"Budget alert: ${remaining:.2f} remaining\")\n        return cost\n\ntracker = CostTracker(monthly_budget=50.0)\ncost = tracker.track(\"gpt-4o\", 1500, 500)\nprint(f\"Cost: ${cost:.4f}\")",
            "hint": "Cost = (input_tokens * input_price + output_tokens * output_price) / 1_000_000",
            "explanation": "Tracks exact dollar cost per API call. In production, this data feeds dashboards and automated model downgrade decisions.",
            "concepts": [
              "Cost Tracking",
              "Token Pricing",
              "Budget Management"
            ],
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "input_tokens.*prices.*output_tokens.*prices.*1_000_000"
            }
          }
        },
