          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Capstone Project: Full-Stack AI Chat Application",
            "explanation": "**Your Capstone Mission:**\n\nBuild a full-stack AI chat application with a polished frontend, real-time streaming responses, conversation history, and multiple AI personas.\n\n**What You Will Build:**\nA \"ChatHub\" application where users can chat with different AI personas (coding tutor, creative writer, data analyst) with streaming responses, conversation management, and a beautiful modern UI.\n\n**Project Requirements:**\n\n1. **Frontend (React/Next.js)**:\n   - Modern chat UI with message bubbles\n   - Real-time streaming (Server-Sent Events or WebSockets)\n   - Persona selector with animated transitions\n   - Markdown rendering in AI responses (code blocks, tables, lists)\n   - Dark/light theme toggle\n   - Mobile responsive design\n\n2. **Backend (Node.js or Python)**:\n   - RESTful API for conversation CRUD\n   - Streaming endpoint for AI responses\n   - User authentication (email + OAuth)\n   - Rate limiting per user\n\n3. **AI Integration**:\n   - Multiple persona system prompts\n   - Context window management (smart truncation)\n   - Token counting and usage tracking\n   - Fallback handling when API is down\n\n4. **Data Persistence**:\n   - Store conversation history in database\n   - Export conversations as markdown\n   - Search across past conversations\n\n5. **Polish & UX**:\n   - Typing indicators while AI is thinking\n   - Copy code button for code blocks\n   - Keyboard shortcuts (Ctrl+Enter to send)\n   - Error states with retry buttons\n\n**Tech Stack:**\n```\nFrontend: Next.js + Tailwind CSS + Framer Motion\nBackend: FastAPI or Express.js\nDatabase: PostgreSQL + Redis (caching)\nAI: OpenAI API with streaming\nAuth: NextAuth.js or Clerk\n```\n\n**Stretch Goals:**\n- Add voice input/output (speech-to-text, text-to-speech)\n- Implement file upload and analysis\n- Add collaborative chat rooms (multiple users + AI)\n\nBuild a ChatGPT-quality experience from scratch!"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "M019-cloud-deployment",
      "title": "Module 19: Cloud & Deployment",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Why Deployment Matters: From Code to Users",
            "explanation": "You've built amazing AI applications in the previous modules — chatbots, RAG systems, agents, and full-stack apps. But right now, they only run on YOUR computer. Deployment is how you make your application available to the entire world.\n\n**What is Deployment?**\nDeployment is the process of taking your locally-running application and putting it on a server that anyone with internet access can use, 24/7. Think of it like the difference between cooking dinner at home vs. opening a restaurant — the food (code) is the same, but serving it to thousands of people requires a completely different setup.\n\n**The Deployment Journey:**\nWe'll go from absolute basics to production-grade infrastructure:\n\n1. **Environment Setup** — Managing configuration and secrets safely\n2. **Docker** — Packaging your app so it runs identically everywhere\n3. **Kubernetes** — Running and scaling your app automatically\n4. **Cloud Providers** — Where your app actually lives (AWS, Azure, GCP)\n5. **Local & Edge AI** — Running models without the cloud\n6. **GPU Infrastructure** — Choosing the right hardware for AI workloads\n7. **CI/CD** — Automating the entire deployment process\n8. **Monitoring** — Keeping your deployed app healthy and performant\n\n**Why Can't I Just Share My Code?**\nYour laptop has specific Python versions, specific libraries, specific environment variables, and specific files. Another machine might have different versions of everything, causing your app to crash. Deployment tools solve this 'works on my machine' problem.\n\n**Real-World Context:**\nEvery AI product you use — ChatGPT, Copilot, Midjourney — went through this same journey. Someone wrote the code, packaged it, deployed it to thousands of servers, and set up monitoring to keep it running. By the end of this module, you'll understand exactly how that pipeline works.",
            "tip": "Deployment is not a one-time task — it's a continuous process. Every time you update your code, you re-deploy. That's why automation (CI/CD) is so important."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Environment Variables & Configuration Management",
            "explanation": "Before we deploy anything, we need to understand how to manage secrets and configuration. This is the #1 mistake beginners make — hardcoding API keys directly in their code.\n\n**What Are Environment Variables?**\nEnvironment variables are key-value pairs stored OUTSIDE your code that your application reads at runtime. They keep sensitive information (API keys, database passwords) separate from your source code.\n\n**Why Not Hardcode?**\nIf you write `OPENAI_API_KEY = 'sk-abc123...'` directly in your Python file and push it to GitHub, anyone can see and steal your API key. Environment variables solve this:\n\n```python\nimport os\napi_key = os.getenv('OPENAI_API_KEY')  # Reads from environment\n```\n\n**The .env File:**\nDuring development, store your variables in a `.env` file (which is listed in `.gitignore` so it never gets committed):\n\n```\nOPENAI_API_KEY=sk-abc123...\nDATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@localhost/mydb\nENVIRONMENT=development\n```\n\nUse the `python-dotenv` library to load these automatically:\n\n```python\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\nload_dotenv()  # Loads .env into os.environ\n```\n\n**Configuration Hierarchy (from least to most important):**\n1. Default values in code\n2. Config files (.env, config.yaml)\n3. Environment variables (override config files)\n4. Command-line arguments (override everything)\n\n**In Production:**\nNever use .env files in production. Use cloud-native secret managers:\n• AWS Secrets Manager\n• Azure Key Vault  \n• GCP Secret Manager\n• Docker Secrets / Kubernetes Secrets\n\nThese encrypt your secrets at rest and in transit, provide access control, and enable automatic rotation.",
            "example": "import os\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\n\nload_dotenv()\n\n# Safe: reads from environment\napi_key = os.getenv('OPENAI_API_KEY')\ndb_url = os.getenv('DATABASE_URL', 'sqlite:///local.db')  # Default fallback\nenv = os.getenv('ENVIRONMENT', 'development')\n\nif not api_key:\n    raise ValueError('OPENAI_API_KEY not set!')\n\nprint(f'Running in {env} mode')",
            "warning": "NEVER commit .env files, API keys, or passwords to Git. Add `.env` to your `.gitignore` file immediately. If you accidentally push a secret, consider it compromised — rotate the key immediately."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Why should you NEVER hardcode API keys directly in your Python code?",
              "options": [
                "It makes the code run slower",
                "Anyone who sees your code (e.g., on GitHub) can steal your API key",
                "Python doesn't allow string constants",
                "Environment variables are faster to read"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Hardcoded secrets in source code are visible to anyone with access to the repository. If pushed to a public GitHub repo, bots can find and abuse your API key within minutes."
            },
            {
              "question": "What Python function reads an environment variable?",
              "options": [
                "os.read()",
                "os.getenv()",
                "env.get()",
                "sys.env()"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "`os.getenv('KEY_NAME')` reads the value of an environment variable. You can also provide a default: `os.getenv('KEY', 'default_value')`."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Docker? The Container Revolution",
            "explanation": "Docker is the single most important deployment tool you'll learn. It solves the oldest problem in software: 'It works on my machine!'\n\n**The Problem:**\nYour AI app needs Python 3.11, PyTorch 2.1, LangChain 0.2, specific CUDA drivers, and 15 other libraries at exact versions. Your colleague has Python 3.9 and different library versions. The cloud server has Python 3.10. Your app crashes everywhere except your laptop.\n\n**The Solution — Containers:**\nDocker lets you create a 'container' — think of it as a lightweight, self-contained mini-computer that has EVERYTHING your app needs pre-installed. This container runs identically on:\n• Your laptop\n• Your colleague's laptop\n• A staging server\n• A production cloud server\n• A GPU instance\n\n**Container vs Virtual Machine:**\nA VM (Virtual Machine) runs an entire operating system — it's like building a new house for every app. A container shares the host's OS kernel — it's like renting a furnished apartment. Containers start in seconds (VMs take minutes) and use far less memory.\n\n**Key Docker Concepts:**\n• **Image** — A blueprint/recipe for creating a container (like a class in OOP)\n• **Container** — A running instance of an image (like an object from a class)\n• **Dockerfile** — The recipe file that tells Docker how to build an image\n• **Docker Hub** — A public registry of pre-built images (like PyPI for containers)\n\n**Basic Docker Commands:**\n```bash\ndocker build -t my-ai-app .          # Build an image from Dockerfile\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 my-ai-app    # Run the container\ndocker ps                              # List running containers\ndocker stop <container_id>            # Stop a container\ndocker images                          # List all images\n```\n\n**Real-World Analogy:**\nThink of a Docker image as a shipping container. It doesn't matter if it's on a truck, a ship, or a train — the contents inside stay the same. Docker does the same for software.",
            "tip": "Install Docker Desktop on your machine and try running `docker run hello-world` — it pulls a tiny test image and runs it, confirming your installation works."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Docker Workflow: From Code to Container",
            "diagram": [
              "Your Python Code + requirements.txt",
              "Dockerfile (build recipe)",
              "docker build → Docker Image (blueprint)",
              "docker run → Running Container (live app)",
              "Users access your app on port 8000"
            ],
            "explanation": "The Docker workflow is simple: you write a Dockerfile that describes how to set up your environment. Docker builds an image from that recipe. You then run containers from that image. Multiple containers can run from the same image, just like multiple objects from one class."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Writing Your First Dockerfile",
            "explanation": "A Dockerfile is a simple text file with step-by-step instructions for building your container image. Think of it as a recipe — each line is one step in setting up your environment.\n\n**The Essential Dockerfile Instructions:**\n\n• `FROM` — Choose your base image (starting point)\n• `WORKDIR` — Set the working directory inside the container\n• `COPY` — Copy files from your computer into the container\n• `RUN` — Execute a command during the build process\n• `EXPOSE` — Document which port your app uses\n• `CMD` — The default command to run when the container starts\n\n**A Simple AI App Dockerfile:**\n```dockerfile\n# Step 1: Start with a Python base image\nFROM python:3.11-slim\n\n# Step 2: Set the working directory\nWORKDIR /app\n\n# Step 3: Copy and install dependencies FIRST (for caching)\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\n# Step 4: Copy the rest of your code\nCOPY . .\n\n# Step 5: Tell Docker which port the app uses\nEXPOSE 8000\n\n# Step 6: Define the startup command\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]\n```\n\n**Why Copy requirements.txt First?**\nThis is a critical optimization called 'layer caching'. Docker caches each instruction as a layer. By copying and installing requirements BEFORE copying your code:\n• If you only change your Python code → Docker reuses the cached pip install layer (saves 5+ minutes)\n• If you change requirements.txt → Docker re-installs packages\n\nWithout this trick, every tiny code change triggers a full pip install of all your heavy AI libraries (PyTorch, Transformers, etc.).\n\n**Why `--host 0.0.0.0`?**\nBy default, apps listen on `localhost` (127.0.0.1), which means only connections from inside the container work. Using `0.0.0.0` makes the app accessible from outside the container — which is the whole point of deploying it!",
            "example": "# Build and run your first Docker container:\n\n# 1. Build the image (run from the folder with your Dockerfile)\n# docker build -t my-ai-app .\n\n# 2. Run it\n# docker run -p 8000:8000 -e OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-xxx my-ai-app\n\n# 3. Test it\n# curl http://localhost:8000/health\n\n# The -p flag maps port 8000 on your machine to port 8000 in the container\n# The -e flag passes environment variables into the container",
            "warning": "Always use `-slim` base images (e.g., `python:3.11-slim` instead of `python:3.11`). The full image includes compilers and dev tools you don't need, adding 700MB+ of unnecessary bloat."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Write Your First Dockerfile",
            "language": "dockerfile",
            "question": "Complete the Dockerfile below for a FastAPI AI application.\n1. Use `python:3.11-slim` as the base image.\n2. Set the working directory to `/app`.\n3. Copy `requirements.txt` and install dependencies.\n4. Copy the rest of the application code.\n5. Set the startup command to run `uvicorn main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000`.",
            "starterCode": "# TODO: Choose the base image\n\n# TODO: Set the working directory\n\n# TODO: Copy requirements and install dependencies\n\n\n# TODO: Copy application code\n\n# TODO: Set the default startup command",
            "solution": "FROM python:3.11-slim\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\nCOPY . .\n\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]",
            "hint": "Start with `FROM python:3.11-slim`. Use `WORKDIR /app`. Copy requirements.txt first with `COPY requirements.txt .`, then `RUN pip install`. Finally `COPY . .` and `CMD` as a JSON array.",
            "explanation": "This Dockerfile follows the layer caching best practice: dependencies are installed before copying source code. This means code changes don't trigger expensive pip reinstalls.",
            "validation": {
              "type": "regex_match",
              "pattern": "FROM\\s+python:3\\.11-slim.*COPY\\s+requirements\\.txt.*RUN\\s+pip\\s+install.*COPY\\s+\\.\\s+\\..*CMD\\s*\\[\\s*\"uvicorn\""
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Dockerfile Command Knowledge",
            "question": "Fill in the correct Dockerfile instructions.",
            "template": [
              "____ python:3.11-slim",
              "",
              "____ /app",
              "",
              "COPY requirements.txt .",
              "____ pip install -r requirements.txt",
              "",
              "COPY . .",
              "",
              "____ [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\"]"
            ],
            "options": [
              "FROM",
              "WORKDIR",
              "RUN",
              "CMD",
              "COPY",
              "EXPOSE",
              "ARG",
              "ENV"
            ],
            "answers": {
              "1": "FROM",
              "2": "WORKDIR",
              "3": "RUN",
              "4": "CMD"
            },
            "explanation": "FROM sets the base image, WORKDIR sets the directory, RUN executes commands during build, and CMD defines the container's default startup command.",
            "hint": "The first instruction always starts with the base image. WORKDIR sets where commands run. RUN executes during build time. CMD runs when the container starts.",
            "solution": "# Solution",
            "starterCode": "# Write code here"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Docker Compose: Running Multi-Container AI Stacks",
            "explanation": "Real AI applications aren't just a single container. They're a collection of services working together: your FastAPI backend, a PostgreSQL database, a Redis cache, and maybe a Celery worker for async tasks. Docker Compose lets you define and run all these services with a single command.\n\n**What is Docker Compose?**\nDocker Compose is a tool for defining multi-container applications in a single YAML file (`docker-compose.yml`). Instead of running 4 separate `docker run` commands, you run:\n\n```bash\ndocker compose up    # Starts everything\ndocker compose down  # Stops everything\n```\n\n**A Typical AI Application Stack:**\n\n| Service | Purpose | Image |\n|---------|---------|-------|\n| api | FastAPI backend | Your custom image |\n| db | PostgreSQL + pgvector | pgvector/pgvector:pg16 |\n| redis | Caching & rate limiting | redis:7-alpine |\n| worker | Async task processing | Your custom image |\n\n**Key Docker Compose Concepts:**\n• `services` — Define each container (api, db, redis, worker)\n• `ports` — Map container ports to host ports\n• `depends_on` — Define startup order (db starts before api)\n• `volumes` — Persist data (database files survive container restarts)\n• `environment` — Set environment variables for each service\n\n**Networking:**\nDocker Compose automatically creates a network where all services can find each other by name. Your FastAPI app connects to the database using `postgresql://user:pass@db:5432/mydb` — notice the hostname is just `db` (the service name), not an IP address.",
            "example": "# docker-compose.yml for a complete AI application\nversion: \"3.8\"\n\nservices:\n  api:\n    build: .\n    ports:\n      - \"8000:8000\"\n    environment:\n      - DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:pass@db:5432/aidb\n      - REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379\n    depends_on:\n      - db\n      - redis\n\n  db:\n    image: pgvector/pgvector:pg16\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_DB: aidb\n      POSTGRES_USER: user\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: pass\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n\n  redis:\n    image: redis:7-alpine\n    ports:\n      - \"6379:6379\"\n\n  worker:\n    build: .\n    command: celery -A tasks worker --loglevel=info\n    depends_on:\n      - redis\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:",
            "tip": "Use `docker compose up -d` to run in detached mode (background). Use `docker compose logs -f api` to follow logs for a specific service."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Advanced Docker: Optimizing AI Container Images",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand Docker basics, let's tackle the challenges specific to AI applications. AI Docker images are fundamentally different from traditional web apps — they're massive (5-15GB due to PyTorch, Transformers, CUDA), require GPU access, and have complex dependency trees.\n\n**Problem: Naively containerizing an AI app results in:**\n• Images that take 20 minutes to build\n• Images that take 10 minutes to pull/deploy\n• 15GB+ of wasted disk space\n\n**Solution: Advanced Docker techniques that reduce build times from 20 to 2 minutes and sizes from 15GB to 3GB.**",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Multi-Stage Builds",
                    "body": "Use a heavy 'builder' stage with all compilation tools (gcc, CUDA toolkit, build-essential) to compile dependencies, then copy only the compiled artifacts to a minimal 'runtime' stage (python:3.11-slim). The runtime image doesn't include compilers, header files, or build tools — often reducing size by 60-70%."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Layer Caching Optimization",
                    "body": "Docker caches each instruction as a layer. Optimization:\n\n1. Copy requirements.txt first and run pip install BEFORE copying source code.\n2. Order layers from least-frequently-changed to most-frequently-changed.\n3. The cache is invalidated from the first changed layer onward, so put stable layers first."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "CUDA & GPU Configuration",
                    "body": "For GPU-accelerated inference, use NVIDIA's base images: `FROM nvidia/cuda:12.1-runtime-ubuntu22.04`. Install the nvidia-container-toolkit on the host. Run with `docker run --gpus all`. Use `runtime` (not `devel`) base images for inference — they're 3-4GB smaller."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": ".dockerignore",
                    "body": "The .dockerignore file prevents unnecessary files from being sent to Docker. For AI projects, ignore: .git/, __pycache__/, *.pyc, venv/, node_modules/, .env, model weights, datasets, and logs. A proper .dockerignore can reduce build context from 10GB to 100MB."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Model Weight Management",
                    "body": "Never bake model weights into the Docker image — they change frequently and bloat the image. Instead: download weights at container startup from S3/GCS/HF Hub, mount a host volume with pre-downloaded weights, or use a model registry (MLflow, W&B Artifacts)."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Container Security",
                    "body": "1. Run as a non-root user: `RUN adduser appuser` then `USER appuser`.\n2. Use --no-cache-dir with pip.\n3. Scan images with Trivy or Snyk for CVEs.\n4. Pin ALL dependency versions in production Dockerfiles."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Container Registries",
                    "body": "Store built images in a registry: Docker Hub (public), AWS ECR (private, integrated with ECS/EKS), Google Artifact Registry (integrated with GKE), or GitHub Container Registry (integrated with GitHub Actions). Enable vulnerability scanning and implement tagging conventions (v1.2.3, latest, sha-abc123)."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "# Multi-stage Dockerfile — Builder + Runtime\nFROM python:3.11-slim as builder\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n\nFROM python:3.11-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=builder /usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/ /usr/local/lib/python3.11/site-packages/\nCOPY . .\n\nRUN useradd -m appuser && chown -R appuser /app\nUSER appuser\n\nEXPOSE 8000\nCMD [\"uvicorn\", \"main:app\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"8000\"]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "Why should you copy requirements.txt and run pip install BEFORE copying your application code in a Dockerfile?",
              "options": [
                "It's a Python convention with no real benefit",
                "Docker caches layers — this prevents re-installing packages when only code changes",
                "It makes the container start faster",
                "Python requires dependencies to be installed in a specific directory"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Docker caches each instruction as a layer. If you copy code first, any code change invalidates the cache and forces a slow pip install. By installing dependencies first, code changes only trigger a quick COPY, not a 5+ minute pip install."
            },
            {
              "question": "What is the purpose of a multi-stage Docker build?",
              "options": [
                "To run multiple applications in one container",
                "To reduce the final image size by separating build tools from runtime",
                "To run the build on multiple machines simultaneously",
                "To support multiple programming languages"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "Multi-stage builds use a heavy 'builder' stage with compilers and build tools, then copy only the compiled artifacts to a slim 'runtime' stage. This can reduce image sizes by 60-70%."
            },
            {
              "question": "How do you give a Docker container access to the host's NVIDIA GPUs?",
              "options": [
