        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "What is Kubernetes? Container Orchestration Explained",
            "explanation": "Docker runs one container on one machine. But what happens when your AI chatbot gets popular and one server can't handle all the traffic? You need more containers, on more servers, with automatic load balancing. That's what Kubernetes does.\n\n**The Problem Docker Doesn't Solve:**\n• What if your container crashes at 3 AM? Someone needs to restart it.\n• What if 10,000 users hit your app at once? One container isn't enough.\n• What if you push a buggy update? You need to roll back instantly.\n• What if you need to update without any downtime?\n\n**Kubernetes (K8s) = Container Orchestrator:**\nKubernetes is an open-source system that manages containers across many machines. It automatically:\n• **Restarts** crashed containers (self-healing)\n• **Scales** up during traffic spikes, scales down when quiet (auto-scaling)\n• **Balances** traffic across multiple containers (load balancing)\n• **Updates** your app with zero downtime (rolling deployments)\n• **Rolls back** to the previous version if an update fails\n\n**Kubernetes Analogy:**\nIf Docker is like putting your restaurant's kitchen in a shipping container, Kubernetes is the logistics company that manages a fleet of those containers — deciding which port they go to, replacing damaged ones, and adding more containers when demand is high.\n\n**Core K8s Concepts (Simplified):**\n• **Pod** — The smallest unit. Usually one container. Like a single server instance.\n• **Deployment** — Manages multiple identical pods. You say 'I want 3 copies' and K8s maintains exactly 3.\n• **Service** — A stable address that routes traffic to your pods. Like a load balancer.\n• **Ingress** — Routes external traffic (from the internet) to your services.\n\n**You DON'T need to manage K8s yourself!**\nEvery major cloud provider offers 'Managed Kubernetes':\n• AWS → EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service)\n• Google → GKE (Google Kubernetes Engine)\n• Azure → AKS (Azure Kubernetes Service)\n\nThey handle the complex infrastructure; you just deploy your containers.",
            "tip": "You don't need to be a Kubernetes expert to deploy AI applications. Many teams use simpler alternatives like Google Cloud Run or AWS App Runner for straightforward deployments. Learn K8s when you need auto-scaling and complex multi-service architectures."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "visual",
          "content": {
            "title": "Kubernetes Architecture: How It All Connects",
            "diagram": [
              "Internet Traffic (Users)",
              "Ingress (Routes HTTP requests)",
              "Service (Load balances across pods)",
              "Pod 1 [FastAPI] | Pod 2 [FastAPI] | Pod 3 [FastAPI]",
              "Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (scales pods up/down)"
            ],
            "explanation": "Users hit your Ingress, which routes requests to a Service. The Service load-balances across multiple Pods (each running your FastAPI container). The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler monitors CPU/memory and automatically adds or removes pods based on demand. If a pod crashes, Kubernetes replaces it automatically."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Kubernetes Core Primitives",
            "explanation": "Now let's dive deeper into each Kubernetes building block. Understanding these primitives is essential for deploying and scaling AI applications in production.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Pods & Containers",
                    "body": "A Pod wraps one or more containers that share networking and storage. For AI apps, a typical pod runs your FastAPI container. For inference, a pod might contain your model server (vLLM) with a sidecar for metrics. Pods are ephemeral — they can be killed and recreated at any time. Never store state in a pod; use external databases."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Deployments & ReplicaSets",
                    "body": "A Deployment manages identical pod replicas. You specify `replicas: 3` and K8s ensures exactly 3 pods are always running. If a pod crashes, K8s automatically replaces it. Deployments enable rolling updates: K8s gradually replaces old pods with new ones. Rollback is instant if health checks fail."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Services",
                    "body": "A Service provides a stable network endpoint (IP + DNS) that load-balances across all pods. Types: ClusterIP (internal only), NodePort (exposed on node IP), LoadBalancer (creates a cloud load balancer). For AI APIs, use LoadBalancer type to get a public-facing endpoint."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Ingress",
                    "body": "Manages external HTTP/HTTPS access to Services. Features: SSL/TLS termination, path-based routing (/api → API Service, /dashboard → UI Service), host-based routing (api.example.com vs app.example.com). Popular controllers: Nginx, Traefik, AWS ALB Ingress Controller."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "GPU Resource Management",
                    "body": "Request GPU resources in pod specs: `resources: { limits: { nvidia.com/gpu: 1 } }`. K8s schedules pods only on nodes with available GPUs. Use node selectors or taints to prevent wasting expensive GPU machines on CPU workloads."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)",
                    "body": "Automatically scales pod replicas based on metrics. Basic: maintain 70% average CPU. Advanced: scale on custom metrics like GPU utilization, request queue depth, or WebSocket connections. Configure min/max replicas and cooldown periods to prevent thrashing."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "ConfigMaps & Secrets",
                    "body": "ConfigMaps store non-sensitive config (model names, feature flags). Secrets store sensitive data (API keys, passwords) in base64-encoded format. Both are injected into pods as environment variables or mounted files. For true encryption, use external secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager)."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "# Kubernetes Deployment YAML for an AI API\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: ai-api\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: ai-api\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      labels:\n        app: ai-api\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: ai-api\n        image: myregistry/ai-api:v1.2\n        ports:\n        - containerPort: 8000\n        resources:\n          requests:\n            memory: \"512Mi\"\n            cpu: \"250m\"\n          limits:\n            memory: \"1Gi\"\n            nvidia.com/gpu: 1\n        livenessProbe:\n          httpGet:\n            path: /health\n            port: 8000\n          initialDelaySeconds: 30\n          periodSeconds: 10"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "drag_drop",
          "instruction": "Match each Kubernetes concept to its purpose:",
          "items": [
            "Pod",
            "Deployment",
            "Service",
            "Ingress",
            "HPA"
          ],
          "correctOrder": [
            "Smallest deployable unit — wraps one or more containers",
            "Manages multiple identical pod replicas with rolling updates",
            "Stable network endpoint that load-balances across pods",
            "Routes external HTTP/HTTPS traffic to internal services",
            "Automatically scales pods up/down based on metrics"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "speed_quiz",
          "timePerQuestion": 10,
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What tool packages your app and ALL dependencies into a portable container?",
              "options": [
                "Kubernetes",
                "Docker",
                "Terraform"
              ],
              "answer": 1
            },
            {
              "question": "What Kubernetes component automatically scales pods based on demand?",
              "options": [
                "Deployment",
                "Service",
                "Horizontal Pod Autoscaler"
              ],
              "answer": 2
            },
            {
              "question": "What Docker instruction sets the container's default startup command?",
              "options": [
                "RUN",
                "COPY",
                "CMD"
              ],
              "answer": 2
            },
            {
              "question": "What happens if a Kubernetes pod crashes?",
              "options": [
                "The app goes down permanently",
                "K8s automatically restarts or replaces it",
                "You must manually restart it"
              ],
              "answer": 1
            },
            {
              "question": "What file defines a multi-container Docker application?",
              "options": [
                "Dockerfile",
                "docker-compose.yml",
                "package.json"
              ],
              "answer": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Cloud Providers: AWS, Azure & GCP for AI",
            "explanation": "Now that you understand Docker and Kubernetes, the next question is: WHERE do your containers run? The answer is cloud providers. The three major cloud providers each offer specialized services for AI deployment.\n\n**Why Cloud?**\nCloud providers give you on-demand access to powerful servers (including GPUs) that you pay for by the hour. No need to buy and maintain physical hardware. You can scale from 1 server to 1,000 in minutes.\n\n**The Big Three:**",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "AWS (Amazon Web Services)",
                    "body": "The largest cloud provider with the broadest service ecosystem.\n\n• Amazon Bedrock — Managed access to foundation models (Claude, Llama, Titan)\n• SageMaker — End-to-end ML platform for training and deploying\n• Lambda — Serverless functions for lightweight AI inference\n• S3 — Object storage for datasets and model artifacts\n• EKS — Managed Kubernetes\n\nBest for: Teams wanting the broadest service selection and Bedrock's multi-model access."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Azure (Microsoft Azure)",
                    "body": "Microsoft's cloud, with the strongest AI integration through OpenAI partnership.\n\n• Azure OpenAI Service — Enterprise-grade GPT-4 with data privacy guarantees\n• Azure AI Studio — Visual workspace for building AI applications\n• Azure Cognitive Services — Pre-built APIs for vision, speech, language\n• AKS — Managed Kubernetes\n\nBest for: Enterprise teams needing OpenAI access with data residency and compliance guarantees."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "GCP (Google Cloud Platform)",
                    "body": "Google's cloud with native Gemini integration and TPU access.\n\n• Vertex AI — Unified ML platform for building, training, deploying\n• Gemini API — Direct access to Google's most capable models\n• Cloud Run — Serverless container deployment (perfect for FastAPI + Docker)\n• GKE — Managed Kubernetes (considered the best managed K8s)\n• TPU access for training at scale\n\nBest for: Teams needing Gemini, TPUs, or tight BigQuery integration."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Choosing a Cloud Provider",
                    "body": "For beginners: Start with GCP Cloud Run (simplest deployment — just point it at your Dockerfile) or AWS Lambda (for simple functions). For enterprises: Azure if you need OpenAI with compliance, AWS for the broadest ecosystem, GCP for the best Kubernetes experience."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Serverless AI Deployment",
                    "body": "Serverless means you don't manage any servers — the cloud provider handles everything. You just upload your code/container and it runs automatically.\n\n• Google Cloud Run — Deploy a Docker container, it auto-scales to zero (free when idle)\n• AWS Lambda — Run functions triggered by events (max 15 min execution)\n• Azure Container Apps — Similar to Cloud Run with Azure integration\n\nServerless is perfect for AI apps with variable traffic — you pay nothing during quiet periods."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Infrastructure as Code (IaC)",
                    "body": "Define your entire cloud infrastructure (databases, K8s clusters, networking) in code using Terraform or Pulumi. Benefits: environments are reproducible, changes are tracked in Git, and identical staging/production setups are created with a single command."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Cost Management",
                    "body": "Cloud costs can spiral quickly, especially with GPU instances. Key strategies:\n\n1. Use spot/preemptible instances for non-critical workloads (60-80% cheaper)\n2. Set up billing alerts to catch unexpected charges\n3. Auto-scale down to zero when possible (serverless)\n4. Use reserved instances for predictable workloads (30-50% discount)\n5. Monitor and right-size your instances — most are over-provisioned"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "# Deploying to Google Cloud Run (simplest cloud deployment)\n# Step 1: Build and push Docker image\n# gcloud builds submit --tag gcr.io/my-project/ai-api\n\n# Step 2: Deploy to Cloud Run\n# gcloud run deploy ai-api \\\n#   --image gcr.io/my-project/ai-api \\\n#   --region us-central1 \\\n#   --allow-unauthenticated \\\n#   --set-env-vars OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-xxx\n\n# That's it! Cloud Run gives you a URL like:\n# https://ai-api-xyz-uc.a.run.app\n\n# Using AWS Bedrock to call Claude:\nimport boto3\nimport json\n\nbedrock = boto3.client('bedrock-runtime', region_name='us-east-1')\n\nresponse = bedrock.invoke_model(\n    modelId='anthropic.claude-3-sonnet-20240229-v1:0',\n    body=json.dumps({\n        'anthropic_version': 'bedrock-2023-05-31',\n        'max_tokens': 500,\n        'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Explain serverless computing'}]\n    })\n)\n\nresult = json.loads(response['body'].read())\nprint(result['content'][0]['text'])"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "content": {
            "title": "Cloud Deployment Decision",
            "description": "Your company needs to deploy a production AI chatbot. Requirements: GPT-4 access with enterprise data privacy, auto-scaling, and compliance with European data residency laws (GDPR). Which cloud setup is best?",
            "choices": [
              {
                "text": "Azure OpenAI Service (data stays in Azure, GDPR-compliant EU regions) + Azure Kubernetes Service for auto-scaling.",
