        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Local & Edge AI Inference",
            "explanation": "Cloud-based LLM APIs are powerful but have drawbacks: they cost money per request, require internet connectivity, and send your data to external servers. Local and Edge AI inference runs models directly on your hardware — enabling private, free, and offline AI.\n\n**When to Use Local Inference:**\n✅ Data privacy requirements (healthcare, finance, government)\n✅ Zero API costs for development and testing\n✅ Offline capabilities (field work, poor connectivity)\n✅ Low latency (no network round-trip)\n\n**When to Use Cloud APIs:**\n✅ Need the most powerful models (GPT-4, Claude Opus)\n✅ Don't want to manage GPU hardware\n✅ Variable/unpredictable traffic patterns\n✅ Quick prototyping and iteration",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Ollama: The Easiest Way to Run LLMs Locally",
                    "body": "Ollama is a CLI tool that downloads and runs open-weight models (Llama, Mistral, Qwen, Gemma) with a single command: `ollama run llama3`. It wraps the model in a local HTTP server with an OpenAI-compatible API, so your existing LangChain code works with zero changes — just point it to localhost:11434."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "llama.cpp: The Engine Under the Hood",
                    "body": "Written in pure C/C++, llama.cpp is the high-performance inference engine that Ollama builds on. It runs LLMs on standard CPUs, Apple Silicon (Metal API), and GPUs with optimal hardware utilization by skipping heavy Python dependencies like PyTorch."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "GGUF: The Local Model Format",
                    "body": "GGUF (GPT-Generated Unified Format) bundles model weights, metadata, and tokenizer into a single file. Key feature: GGUF allows weights to split dynamically between GPU VRAM and CPU RAM, so you can run models that are larger than your GPU memory."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Quantization: Making Models Fit",
                    "body": "Full-precision models are too large for most hardware. Quantization reduces precision (e.g., from 16-bit to 4-bit), shrinking models by 4x with minimal quality loss. A 7B model goes from 14GB to ~4GB, making it runnable on a laptop with 8GB RAM."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "ONNX Runtime: Edge AI Everywhere",
                    "body": "ONNX (Open Neural Network Exchange) enables running optimized models across diverse hardware: web browsers (via WebAssembly), iOS/Android, desktop apps, and IoT devices. Convert your PyTorch model to ONNX format for 2-10x faster inference on edge devices."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Hardware & Efficiency Trade-offs",
                    "body": "Edge inference requires balancing model size (1B to 8B parameters) against speed (tokens/sec). A 1B model runs at 50+ tokens/sec on a laptop CPU. A 7B model needs a decent GPU for real-time chat. Larger models (13B+) generally require 16GB+ VRAM."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Hybrid Cloud-Edge Routing",
                    "body": "The best architecture often combines both: use local models for simple tasks (classification, summarization, routing) and cloud APIs for complex reasoning. This gives you fast, cheap responses for 80% of requests while maintaining quality for the hard 20%."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "# Running LLMs locally with Ollama\nimport requests\n\ndef ollama_chat(prompt, model='llama3'):\n    response = requests.post(\n        'http://localhost:11434/api/generate',\n        json={\n            'model': model,\n            'prompt': prompt,\n            'stream': False\n        }\n    )\n    return response.json()['response']\n\n# Using Ollama with LangChain (zero code changes!)\nfrom langchain_community.llms import Ollama\n\nllm = Ollama(model='llama3')\nresponse = llm.invoke('Explain gradient descent in simple terms')\nprint(response)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "GPU Infrastructure & Hardware Selection",
            "explanation": "The GPU you choose determines what models you can run, how fast they respond, how many users you can support, and how much it costs. This is a practical guide to choosing the right GPU for your AI workload.",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Why GPUs for AI?",
                    "body": "GPUs have thousands of small cores designed for parallel computation. AI models perform millions of matrix multiplications — GPUs handle these in parallel, making them 10-100x faster than CPUs for AI workloads. The key metric is VRAM (GPU memory) — your model must fit in VRAM to run."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Entry Level: L4 & A10G (24GB VRAM)",
                    "body": "NVIDIA L4: 24GB VRAM, ~$0.70/hour on GCP. Best for serving quantized 7-13B models (Llama-3-8B, Mistral-7B), embedding models. Very power-efficient.\nA10G: 24GB VRAM, ~$1.00/hour on AWS (g5 instances). Similar to L4 with slightly more compute. The go-to for small-to-medium model inference."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Professional: A100 (40GB/80GB VRAM)",
                    "body": "The workhorse of modern AI. The 80GB version can serve Llama-3-70B in 4-bit quantization. Excellent for both high-throughput inference and fine-tuning. Available at ~$2-4/hour. The 40GB version is often insufficient for 70B models — always check VRAM requirements first."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "High-End: H100 (80GB HBM3 VRAM)",
                    "body": "80GB HBM3 with Transformer Engine (native FP8). 3x faster than A100. Available at ~$4-8/hour. Only justified for: production serving of 70B+ models at scale, fine-tuning 70B+ models, or latency-critical applications. For most teams, A100 is sufficient."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Consumer GPUs (RTX 4090, 3090)",
                    "body": "RTX 4090: 24GB VRAM, excellent for local development. Can run quantized 7-13B models for personal use. RTX 3090: 24GB, affordable second-hand. Important: consumer GPUs are prohibited by NVIDIA's EULA for commercial cloud hosting. Use for development only."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Multi-GPU Configurations",
                    "body": "When a model doesn't fit in one GPU:\n\n1. Tensor Parallelism — split layers across GPUs (needs NVLink)\n2. Pipeline Parallelism — assign different layers to different GPUs\n\nFor vLLM: `--tensor-parallel-size 2` uses 2 GPUs. For fine-tuning: use FSDP or DeepSpeed ZeRO for distributed training."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Cost Optimization",
                    "body": "1. Use spot/preemptible instances for fine-tuning (60-80% cheaper, can be interrupted)\n2. Right-size your GPU — don't rent an A100 for a model that fits on an L4\n3. Use quantization to reduce VRAM needs and enable cheaper GPUs\n4. Time-share GPUs — inference during the day, fine-tuning at night\n5. Compare providers — GPU pricing varies 2-3x between clouds"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "import torch\n\ndef gpu_status():\n    if not torch.cuda.is_available():\n        return 'No GPU available — running on CPU'\n    \n    info = []\n    for i in range(torch.cuda.device_count()):\n        props = torch.cuda.get_device_properties(i)\n        allocated = torch.cuda.memory_allocated(i) / 1e9\n        total = props.total_mem / 1e9\n        info.append({\n            'gpu': i,\n            'name': props.name,\n            'total_vram_gb': f'{total:.1f}',\n            'used_gb': f'{allocated:.1f}',\n            'free_gb': f'{total - allocated:.1f}',\n        })\n    return info\n\n# Run this to check your GPU status\nprint(gpu_status())"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "scenario",
          "title": "GPU Selection Decision",
          "description": "You need to deploy a quantized Llama-3-8B model for a customer-facing chatbot serving ~500 concurrent users. Your budget is moderate. Which GPU setup is best?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "text": "2x NVIDIA L4 GPUs (24GB each, ~$1.40/hour total) with Kubernetes autoscaling.",
              "outcome": "Great choice! L4 GPUs are optimized for inference and can serve quantized 8B models efficiently. With K8s autoscaling, you can handle traffic spikes while keeping costs low during quiet periods.",
              "score": 100
            },
            {
              "text": "1x H100 GPU (80GB, ~$6/hour).",
              "outcome": "Overkill! An H100 is far more powerful than needed for an 8B model. You'd be paying 4x more for GPU capacity you don't use. Right-size your GPU to your model.",
              "score": 20
            },
            {
              "text": "Run it on CPU instances without any GPU.",
              "outcome": "Too slow for production. CPU inference on an 8B model would give response times of 10-30 seconds per query — unacceptable for a customer-facing chatbot.",
              "score": 5
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "CI/CD for AI Systems: Automating Deployment",
            "explanation": "CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Deployment) automates the process of testing and deploying your code. Instead of manually building Docker images, running tests, and deploying — a CI/CD pipeline does it automatically every time you push code.\n\n**Why CI/CD for AI?**\nAI systems have MORE things that can go wrong than traditional software:\n• Code changes can break functionality\n• Prompt changes can degrade output quality\n• Model swaps can reduce accuracy\n• Data pipeline changes can corrupt embeddings\n\nCI/CD catches these issues BEFORE they reach your users.\n\n**The AI CI/CD Pipeline:**\n```\nPush Code → Lint & Type Check → Unit Tests → Build Docker Image →\nRun Eval Suite → Push to Registry → Deploy to Staging →\nIntegration Tests → Deploy to Production → Monitor\n```",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "GitHub Actions: Your CI/CD Platform",
                    "body": "GitHub Actions is the most popular CI platform for AI teams. A typical workflow:\n\n1. On push: lint (ruff/black), type check (mypy), unit tests (pytest)\n2. On PR merge: build Docker image, push to registry, run integration tests\n3. On release tag: deploy to production with automatic rollback\n\nWorkflows are defined in `.github/workflows/` as YAML files."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Automated Prompt Testing",
                    "body": "Treat prompts as code: version them in Git, review changes in PRs, test them in CI. Use evaluation frameworks (Promptfoo, DeepEval) to compare new prompt quality against the baseline. Block PRs if any quality metric degrades beyond a threshold."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "Model Evaluation Gates",
                    "body": "When swapping or fine-tuning models, the CI pipeline should:\n1. Run the new model through your evaluation benchmark\n2. Compare scores against the currently deployed model\n3. Auto-approve if scores match or exceed baseline\n4. Flag for human review if some metrics improve while others regress"
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "Blue-Green Deployments",
                    "body": "Maintain two identical production environments: Blue (serving traffic) and Green (idle). Deploy new versions to Green, run smoke tests. If everything passes, switch traffic to Green. If something breaks, switch back to Blue instantly. Zero downtime deployments."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Infrastructure as Code in CI",
                    "body": "Version your K8s configs, Terraform files, and database schemas in Git. Changes go through the same PR review process as code. This ensures reproducibility and auditability — every infrastructure change is tracked."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Data Pipeline Testing",
                    "body": "For RAG systems, test your data pipeline in CI:\n1. Does ingestion handle all file formats (PDF, DOCX, HTML)?\n2. Does chunking produce correct-sized chunks?\n3. Are embeddings the correct dimensionality?\n4. Does the vector DB return results for known test queries?"
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "Rollback Strategy",
                    "body": "Every deployment must have automated rollback:\n1. Health check failures → automatic rollback within 2 minutes\n2. Error rate > 5% → automatic rollback\n3. Manual rollback via single command/button\n4. Previous versions retained for 30+ days\n\nTest your rollback regularly — an untested rollback is worse than no rollback."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "# .github/workflows/deploy.yml — GitHub Actions CI/CD\nname: AI App CI/CD\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: '3.11'\n      \n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n      \n      - name: Lint code\n        run: ruff check .\n      \n      - name: Run unit tests\n        run: pytest tests/ -v\n      \n      - name: Run LLM evaluation suite\n        run: python eval/run_evals.py --threshold 0.8\n  \n  deploy:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'\n    steps:\n      - name: Deploy to Cloud Run\n        run: gcloud run deploy ai-api --source . --region us-central1"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "challenge": {
            "title": "Write a GitHub Actions Workflow",
            "language": "yaml",
            "question": "Complete the GitHub Actions workflow YAML that:\n1. Triggers on push to the `main` branch\n2. Sets up Python 3.11\n3. Installs dependencies from requirements.txt\n4. Runs pytest on the tests/ directory",
            "starterCode": "name: AI App Tests\n\n# TODO: Trigger on push to main branch\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [____]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: '____'\n      \n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: pip install -r ____________\n      \n      - name: Run tests\n        run: ______ tests/ -v",
            "solution": "name: AI App Tests\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: '3.11'\n      \n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: pip install -r requirements.txt\n      \n      - name: Run tests\n        run: pytest tests/ -v",
            "hint": "The branch is 'main'. Python version is '3.11'. Dependencies are in 'requirements.txt'. The test command is 'pytest'.",
            "explanation": "This GitHub Actions workflow runs automatically on every push to main. It sets up a clean Python environment, installs your project dependencies, and runs your test suite. If any test fails, the deployment is blocked."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Monitoring & Observability: Keeping Your AI Alive",
            "explanation": "Running AI services in production without monitoring is like driving blind at 100 mph. You need real-time visibility into your system's health, performance, and AI-specific metrics.\n\n**The Three Pillars of Observability:**\n1. **Metrics** — Numbers over time (CPU usage, request latency, error rates)\n2. **Logs** — Discrete events with context (errors, warnings, request details)\n3. **Traces** — Following a single request across multiple services\n\n**AI-Specific Metrics You Must Track:**\n• Token usage and cost per request\n• Model response latency (P50, P95, P99)\n• Hallucination detection rate\n• RAG retrieval relevance scores\n• GPU utilization and memory usage",
            "blocks": [
              {
                "type": "sevenPoint",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "number": 1,
                    "title": "Prometheus: Collecting Metrics",
                    "body": "Prometheus is an open-source monitoring system that scrapes metrics from your containers every 15 seconds. Instrument your FastAPI app with the prometheus_client library to expose metrics like request count, latency, and error rates. Prometheus stores these and lets you query them with PromQL."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 2,
                    "title": "Grafana: Visualizing Metrics",
                    "body": "Grafana connects to Prometheus to create real-time dashboards. Build panels for: request rate over time, P95 latency heatmap, GPU utilization gauge, token cost per hour, error rate by endpoint. Grafana also supports alerting — get Slack/PagerDuty notifications when metrics cross thresholds."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 3,
                    "title": "OpenTelemetry: Unified Instrumentation",
                    "body": "OpenTelemetry is the open standard for instrumenting code. It generates traces, metrics, and logs from a single SDK. Instrument once, export to any backend (Prometheus, Jaeger, Datadog, Grafana Cloud). It's the future-proof choice — vendor-neutral and widely adopted."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 4,
                    "title": "GPU Monitoring (DCGM)",
                    "body": "NVIDIA's DCGM exposes GPU-specific metrics: compute utilization, memory usage, temperature, power draw, and PCIe throughput. The dcgm-exporter runs alongside GPU workloads and feeds metrics to Prometheus. Essential for detecting underutilized GPUs (wasting money) or GPU memory leaks."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 5,
                    "title": "Log Aggregation",
                    "body": "Centralize logs from all containers: ELK Stack (Elasticsearch + Logstash + Kibana), Loki + Grafana (lightweight), or cloud-native solutions (CloudWatch, Cloud Logging). Use structured JSON logging with consistent fields: timestamp, service_name, request_id, log_level, message."
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 6,
                    "title": "Alerting Best Practices",
                    "body": "1. Alert on symptoms, not causes — 'error rate > 5%' not 'disk space > 80%'\n2. Every alert must be actionable — if you can't do anything, it's noise\n3. Severity levels: P1 (production down, page NOW), P2 (degraded, fix in 1 hour), P3 (minor, fix in 1 day)\n4. Review alerts monthly — delete those that never fire or always fire"
                  },
                  {
                    "number": 7,
                    "title": "SLOs and Error Budgets",
                    "body": "Define Service Level Objectives: 'Our API will have 99.9% availability (< 8.7 hours downtime/year) and P95 latency < 2 seconds.' Track on a dashboard. When within budget → ship fast. When burning budget → slow down, fix stability. SLOs bridge technical metrics and business goals."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "example": "from prometheus_client import Counter, Histogram, start_http_server\nimport time\n\n# Define metrics\nREQUEST_COUNT = Counter('ai_requests_total', 'Total AI requests', ['model', 'status'])\nLATENCY = Histogram('ai_latency_seconds', 'AI request latency', ['model'])\n\ndef tracked_llm_call(prompt, model='gpt-4o'):\n    start = time.time()\n    try:\n        # ... make LLM call ...\n        result = 'AI response here'\n        REQUEST_COUNT.labels(model=model, status='success').inc()\n        return result\n    except Exception:\n        REQUEST_COUNT.labels(model=model, status='error').inc()\n        raise\n    finally:\n        LATENCY.labels(model=model).observe(time.time() - start)\n\n# Start metrics server on port 9090\n# Access at http://localhost:9090/metrics\nstart_http_server(9090)"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "mcq",
          "questions": [
            {
              "question": "What are the three pillars of observability?",
              "options": [
                "CPU, Memory, Disk",
                "Metrics, Logs, Traces",
                "Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform",
                "Frontend, Backend, Database"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "The three pillars are: Metrics (numbers over time), Logs (discrete events with context), and Traces (following a request across services). Together they give complete visibility into system behavior."
            },
            {
              "question": "What does a Service Level Objective (SLO) define?",
              "options": [
                "The programming language to use",
                "The target reliability and performance level for a service",
                "The cloud provider to deploy on",
                "The number of developers needed"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "An SLO defines measurable targets like '99.9% availability' and 'P95 latency < 2 seconds'. It helps teams decide when to focus on features vs. stability."
            },
            {
              "question": "Why should alerts be based on symptoms rather than causes?",
              "options": [
                "Symptoms are easier to measure",
                "Because a cause (high disk usage) might not always be a problem, but a symptom (errors for users) always is",
                "Causes are not measurable",
                "Symptoms generate fewer alerts"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explanation": "High disk usage at 80% might be normal. But a 5% error rate means users are affected RIGHT NOW. Alert on what matters to users, not on internal metrics that might be fine."
            }
          ]
        },
