    {
      "id": "M021-git-github",
      "title": "Module 21: Git & GitHub — Version Control Mastery",
      "stages": [
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Welcome to Git & GitHub",
            "explanation": "Imagine you are writing a long essay. You make changes, then realize the previous version was better — but you already saved over it. Now imagine this happening with thousands of lines of code, with multiple people editing the same files at the same time. That is the problem **version control** solves.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m21_github_overview.png\" alt=\"Git & GitHub Overview\" />\n\n**What is Version Control?**\nVersion control is a system that records every change you make to your files over time. It lets you:\n- **Go back** to any previous version of your code\n- **See who changed what** and when\n- **Work with others** without overwriting each other's work\n- **Experiment safely** using branches without breaking the main project\n\nThink of it like Google Docs version history — but much more powerful and designed specifically for code.\n\n**Why Every Developer Must Learn Git:**\n- **95%+ of software companies** use Git for version control\n- It is a **required skill** in every developer job description\n- Open source projects on GitHub have **millions of contributors** using Git\n- Without Git, collaboration on code is nearly impossible\n\nBy the end of this module, you will be comfortable using Git and GitHub like a professional developer."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Git vs GitHub — What is the Difference?",
            "explanation": "This is one of the most common confusions for beginners. Let us clear it up once and for all.\n\n**Git** is a **tool** that runs on your computer. It tracks changes to your files locally. You do not need the internet to use Git — it works entirely on your machine.\n\n**GitHub** is a **website** (github.com) that stores your Git repositories online. It adds collaboration features like pull requests, issues, and project boards on top of Git.\n\n**Simple Analogy:**\n- **Git** = Your personal diary where you write entries every day\n- **GitHub** = A library where you publish your diary so others can read it, suggest edits, and contribute\n\n**Other Git Hosting Platforms:**\n| Platform | Best For |\n|---------|---------|\n| **GitHub** | Open source, most popular, largest community |\n| **GitLab** | Built-in CI/CD, self-hosting option |\n| **Bitbucket** | Atlassian ecosystem (Jira integration) |\n| **Azure DevOps** | Microsoft/enterprise projects |\n\nWe will focus on **GitHub** because it is the industry standard and where most open-source projects live.\n\n**Key Takeaway:**\nYou use **Git** (the tool) on your computer to track changes. You use **GitHub** (the website) to share your code, collaborate with others, and back up your work online."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Core Concepts — Repository, Commit, Branch",
            "explanation": "Before touching any commands, let us understand three words you will hear constantly.\n\n**1. Repository (Repo)**\nA repository is simply a **folder that Git is tracking**. When you tell Git to track a folder, it creates a hidden `.git` folder inside it that stores the entire history of changes.\n\nThink of a repo as a **project folder with superpowers** — it remembers every version of every file that ever existed inside it.\n\n**2. Commit**\nA commit is a **snapshot** of your project at a specific moment in time. Every time you make changes and want to save them, you create a commit.\n\nThink of commits as **save points in a video game**. You can always go back to any save point.\n\nEach commit has:\n- A **unique ID** (called a hash) — like `a1b2c3d`\n- A **message** describing what changed — like `\"Fix login bug\"`\n- A **timestamp** — when it was created\n- An **author** — who made the change\n\n**3. Branch**\nA branch is a **separate line of development**. The default branch is called `main` (or `master` in older repos).\n\nThink of branches as **parallel universes**. You can create a new branch to try an idea without affecting the main project. If the idea works, you merge it back. If it does not, you delete the branch — no harm done.\n\n**How They Work Together:**\n```\nRepository (your project folder)\n  └── main branch\n       ├── Commit 1: \"Initial project setup\"\n       ├── Commit 2: \"Add login page\"\n       └── Commit 3: \"Fix header styling\"\n  └── feature branch\n       ├── Commit 4: \"Add dark mode\"\n       └── Commit 5: \"Add theme toggle button\"\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Installing Git on Your Computer",
            "explanation": "Let us get Git installed on your machine. The process is slightly different depending on your operating system.\n\n**Windows:**\n1. Go to https://git-scm.com/download/win\n2. Download the installer and run it\n3. During installation, keep all the default options selected\n4. Make sure **\"Git Bash\"** is included (it will be by default)\n5. After installation, open **Command Prompt** or **Git Bash**\n\n**macOS:**\n1. Open the **Terminal** app\n2. Type `git --version` and press Enter\n3. If Git is not installed, macOS will prompt you to install it\n4. Alternatively, install via Homebrew: `brew install git`\n\n**Linux (Ubuntu/Debian):**\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install git\n```\n\n**Verify Installation:**\nOpen your terminal (Command Prompt, Git Bash, or Terminal) and type:\n```bash\ngit --version\n```\n\nYou should see something like:\n```\ngit version 2.43.0\n```\n\nIf you see a version number, Git is installed and ready to use!\n\n**First-Time Setup — Tell Git Who You Are:**\nBefore making any commits, you need to configure your name and email. This information is attached to every commit you make.\n\n```bash\ngit config --global user.name \"Your Name\"\ngit config --global user.email \"your.email@example.com\"\n```\n\n**Verify your configuration:**\n```bash\ngit config --list\n```\n\nThis is a one-time setup. You will not need to do this again unless you want to change your details."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Creating Your First Repository",
            "explanation": "Let us create your very first Git repository. We will do this step by step.\n\n**Step 1: Create a project folder**\n```bash\nmkdir my-first-repo\ncd my-first-repo\n```\n\n**Step 2: Initialize Git**\n```bash\ngit init\n```\n\nYou will see:\n```\nInitialized empty Git repository in /path/to/my-first-repo/.git/\n```\n\nThat is it! Git is now tracking this folder. A hidden `.git` folder has been created inside your project. **Never delete or modify the .git folder manually** — it contains your entire project history.\n\n**Step 3: Create a file**\n```bash\necho \"Hello, Git!\" > hello.txt\n```\n\n**Step 4: Check the status**\n```bash\ngit status\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\nOn branch main\nUntracked files:\n  (use \"git add <file>...\" to include in what will be committed)\n        hello.txt\n\nnothing added to commit but untracked files present\n```\n\nGit sees the new file but is not tracking it yet. The file is **untracked** — Git knows it exists but will not save it until you tell it to.\n\n**Step 5: Add the file to staging**\n```bash\ngit add hello.txt\n```\n\n**Step 6: Make your first commit**\n```bash\ngit commit -m \"Add hello.txt with greeting\"\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\n[main (root-commit) a1b2c3d] Add hello.txt with greeting\n 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\n create mode 100644 hello.txt\n```\n\n**Congratulations!** You just made your first Git commit. Your project now has its first save point in history."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The Three Stages of Git",
            "explanation": "Every file in a Git repository goes through three stages. Understanding this is the key to understanding Git.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m21_git_workflow.png\" alt=\"Git Workflow - Working Directory, Staging Area, Repository\" />\n\n**Stage 1: Working Directory**\nThis is your project folder — the files you see and edit on your computer. When you create, modify, or delete files, those changes happen in the working directory.\n\n**Stage 2: Staging Area (Index)**\nThe staging area is like a **preparation zone**. Before you commit, you choose which changes to include by adding them to the staging area. This lets you commit only specific changes, not everything at once.\n\n**Stage 3: Repository (.git folder)**\nWhen you commit, the staged changes are permanently saved in the repository's history. Each commit is a snapshot that you can return to anytime.\n\n**The Flow:**\n```\nEdit files → git add → git commit\n(Working)    (Staging)   (Repository)\n```\n\n**Why Have a Staging Area?**\nImagine you changed 5 files, but only 3 of those changes are ready. The staging area lets you add just those 3 files to the next commit, while keeping the other 2 changes for later.\n\n```bash\n# You changed files A, B, C, D, E\n# But only A, B, C are ready\n\ngit add A.txt B.txt C.txt    # Stage only the ready files\ngit commit -m \"Feature X\"     # Commit only staged files\n\n# D.txt and E.txt remain as uncommitted changes\n```\n\n**Common Status Labels:**\n| Status | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Untracked** | Git does not know about this file yet |\n| **Modified** | File changed since last commit |\n| **Staged** | File is ready to be committed |\n| **Committed** | File is safely stored in the repository |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Essential Git Commands — Your Daily Toolkit",
            "explanation": "These are the commands you will use every single day as a developer. Let us master each one.\n\n**git status — Check what is happening**\n```bash\ngit status\n```\nShows which files are modified, staged, or untracked. Run this frequently — it is your best friend.\n\n**git add — Stage changes**\n```bash\ngit add filename.txt          # Stage a specific file\ngit add .                     # Stage ALL changes in current directory\ngit add *.py                  # Stage all Python files\ngit add src/                  # Stage everything in src folder\n```\n\n**git commit — Save a snapshot**\n```bash\ngit commit -m \"Your message here\"\n```\n\n**Writing Good Commit Messages:**\n- ✅ `\"Add user login with email validation\"`\n- ✅ `\"Fix crash when uploading large images\"`\n- ✅ `\"Update README with installation instructions\"`\n- ❌ `\"Fixed stuff\"`\n- ❌ `\"asdfgh\"`\n- ❌ `\"changes\"`\n\n**git log — View commit history**\n```bash\ngit log                       # Full history with details\ngit log --oneline             # Compact one-line-per-commit view\ngit log --oneline -5          # Last 5 commits only\ngit log --graph --oneline     # Visual branch graph\n```\n\n**git diff — See what changed**\n```bash\ngit diff                      # Changes in working directory (not yet staged)\ngit diff --staged             # Changes that are staged (ready to commit)\ngit diff HEAD~1               # Compare with previous commit\n```\n\n**Practical Workflow:**\n```bash\n# 1. Make changes to your files\n# 2. Check what changed\ngit status\ngit diff\n\n# 3. Stage the changes\ngit add .\n\n# 4. Commit with a clear message\ngit commit -m \"Add search feature to homepage\"\n\n# 5. Verify\ngit log --oneline -3\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Undoing Changes — Your Safety Net",
            "explanation": "Everyone makes mistakes. Git gives you powerful ways to undo changes at every stage.\n\n**Undo Changes in Working Directory (before staging):**\n```bash\n# Discard changes to a specific file (revert to last commit)\ngit checkout -- filename.txt\n\n# Modern alternative (Git 2.23+)\ngit restore filename.txt\n```\n⚠️ **Warning:** This permanently discards your uncommitted changes. There is no undo for this undo.\n\n**Unstage a File (after git add, before commit):**\n```bash\n# Remove file from staging area (keep changes in working directory)\ngit reset HEAD filename.txt\n\n# Modern alternative (Git 2.23+)\ngit restore --staged filename.txt\n```\n\n**Undo the Last Commit (keep changes):**\n```bash\n# Undo commit but keep all changes staged\ngit reset --soft HEAD~1\n\n# Undo commit and unstage changes (keep files as they are)\ngit reset HEAD~1\n\n# Undo commit AND discard all changes (DANGEROUS!)\ngit reset --hard HEAD~1\n```\n\n**Amend the Last Commit (fix a typo in the message):**\n```bash\n# Change the last commit message\ngit commit --amend -m \"Corrected commit message\"\n\n# Add a forgotten file to the last commit\ngit add forgotten-file.txt\ngit commit --amend --no-edit\n```\n\n**Quick Reference:**\n| Situation | Command |\n|-----------|---------|\n| Undo file edit (not staged) | `git restore filename` |\n| Unstage a file | `git restore --staged filename` |\n| Undo last commit (keep changes) | `git reset --soft HEAD~1` |\n| Undo last commit (discard all) | `git reset --hard HEAD~1` |\n| Fix last commit message | `git commit --amend -m \"new msg\"` |\n\n**Golden Rule:** If you have already pushed a commit to GitHub, **do not use reset**. Use `git revert` instead (we will cover this in the advanced section)."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "The .gitignore File — Keeping Secrets Out",
            "explanation": "Not every file should be tracked by Git. Some files are private, some are generated automatically, and some are just too large.\n\n**What is .gitignore?**\nA `.gitignore` file tells Git which files and folders to **ignore** — to pretend they do not exist. Git will not track, stage, or commit any file listed in `.gitignore`.\n\n**Create a .gitignore file in your project root:**\n```bash\n# Create the file\ntouch .gitignore\n```\n\n**Common .gitignore patterns:**\n```gitignore\n# Environment variables (NEVER commit API keys!)\n.env\n.env.local\n.env.production\n\n# Dependencies (install fresh with npm install)\nnode_modules/\nvenv/\n__pycache__/\n\n# Build outputs\ndist/\nbuild/\n*.pyc\n\n# IDE settings\n.vscode/\n.idea/\n*.swp\n\n# OS files\n.DS_Store\nThumbs.db\n\n# Logs\n*.log\nlogs/\n\n# Large files\n*.zip\n*.tar.gz\n*.mp4\n```\n\n**Why This Matters:**\n1. **Security** — Never commit API keys, passwords, or `.env` files. If you accidentally push a secret to GitHub, consider it compromised — even if you delete it, it lives in Git history forever.\n2. **Clean Repos** — Nobody wants to download 500 MB of `node_modules` when they can just run `npm install`.\n3. **No Conflicts** — IDE settings and OS files are personal and cause merge conflicts.\n\n**Pro Tip — GitHub provides templates:**\nWhen creating a new repository on GitHub, it offers `.gitignore` templates for different languages (Python, Node, Java, etc.). Always pick one!\n\n**Already tracked a file you want to ignore?**\n```bash\n# Stop tracking a file that is already committed\ngit rm --cached filename.txt\n# Then add it to .gitignore and commit\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Branching — Parallel Universes for Your Code",
            "explanation": "Branching is one of Git's most powerful features. It lets you work on new features, bug fixes, or experiments without touching the stable version of your code.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m21_git_branching.png\" alt=\"Git Branching and Merging Visualization\" />\n\n**Why Use Branches?**\n- **Safety** — Your main code stays stable while you experiment\n- **Organization** — Each feature or bug fix gets its own branch\n- **Collaboration** — Multiple people work on different branches simultaneously\n- **Review** — Changes can be reviewed before merging into main\n\n**Creating and Switching Branches:**\n```bash\n# See all branches (* marks the current branch)\ngit branch\n\n# Create a new branch\ngit branch feature-login\n\n# Switch to the new branch\ngit checkout feature-login\n\n# Shortcut: Create AND switch in one command\ngit checkout -b feature-login\n\n# Modern alternative (Git 2.23+)\ngit switch -c feature-login\n```\n\n**What Happens When You Branch?**\nWhen you create a branch, Git makes a copy of the current state. Changes you make on the new branch do NOT affect the `main` branch.\n\n```\nmain:           A ── B ── C\n                          |\nfeature-login:            C ── D ── E\n```\n\nCommits D and E exist only on `feature-login`. The `main` branch is completely unaffected.\n\n**Switching Between Branches:**\n```bash\ngit switch main              # Go back to main\ngit switch feature-login     # Go to feature branch\n\n# See which branch you are on\ngit branch\n```\n\n⚠️ **Important:** Always commit or stash your changes before switching branches. Uncommitted changes will follow you to the new branch.\n\n**Branch Naming Conventions:**\n| Prefix | Usage | Example |\n|--------|-------|---------|\n| `feature/` | New features | `feature/dark-mode` |\n| `fix/` | Bug fixes | `fix/login-crash` |\n| `hotfix/` | Urgent production fixes | `hotfix/security-patch` |\n| `refactor/` | Code cleanup | `refactor/api-structure` |\n| `docs/` | Documentation | `docs/api-guide` |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Merging Branches Together",
            "explanation": "Once your feature is complete and tested, you merge it back into the main branch.\n\n**Basic Merge:**\n```bash\n# Step 1: Switch to the branch you want to merge INTO\ngit switch main\n\n# Step 2: Merge the feature branch\ngit merge feature-login\n```\n\n**Types of Merges:**\n\n**1. Fast-Forward Merge (Simple)**\nWhen main has no new commits since you branched, Git simply moves the main pointer forward.\n```\nBefore:  main: A ── B ── C\n         feature:        C ── D ── E\n\nAfter:   main: A ── B ── C ── D ── E   (fast-forwarded!)\n```\n\n**2. Three-Way Merge (Common)**\nWhen both branches have new commits, Git creates a special merge commit that combines both.\n```\nBefore:  main:    A ── B ── C ── F\n         feature:           C ── D ── E\n\nAfter:   main:    A ── B ── C ── F ── G  (merge commit)\n                             \\       /\n                              D ── E\n```\n\n**Delete the Branch After Merging:**\nOnce merged, the feature branch is no longer needed.\n```bash\n# Delete a merged branch\ngit branch -d feature-login\n\n# Force delete an unmerged branch (careful!)\ngit branch -D feature-login\n```\n\n**Merge Conflicts — When Git Needs Your Help:**\nSometimes two branches change the same line of code. Git cannot decide which version to keep, so it asks you to resolve it manually.\n\n```bash\n# After running git merge, if there is a conflict:\nAuto-merging app.py\nCONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in app.py\n```\n\nGit marks the conflict in the file:\n```\n<<<<<<< HEAD\nprint(\"Hello from main branch\")\n=======\nprint(\"Hello from feature branch\")\n>>>>>>> feature-login\n```\n\n**To resolve:**\n1. Open the file and choose which version to keep (or combine them)\n2. Remove the `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, and `>>>>>>>` markers\n3. Save the file\n4. Stage and commit:\n```bash\ngit add app.py\ngit commit -m \"Resolve merge conflict in app.py\"\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Setting Up GitHub — Your Online Code Home",
            "explanation": "Now it is time to put your code on the internet using GitHub.\n\n**Step 1: Create a GitHub Account**\n1. Go to https://github.com\n2. Click **Sign Up**\n3. Choose a username (this will be your developer identity!)\n4. Complete the setup\n\n**Step 2: Create a New Repository on GitHub**\n1. Click the **+** button in the top-right corner\n2. Select **New repository**\n3. Fill in the details:\n   - **Repository name:** my-first-repo\n   - **Description:** My first Git project\n   - **Public** or **Private** (public means anyone can see it)\n   - **Do NOT** check \"Add a README file\" (we already have files locally)\n4. Click **Create repository**\n\nGitHub will show you commands to connect your local repo. Let us do that now.\n\n**Step 3: Connect Local Repo to GitHub**\n```bash\n# Add GitHub as a remote (copy the URL from GitHub)\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/your-username/my-first-repo.git\n\n# Verify the remote\ngit remote -v\n```\n\n**What is a Remote?**\nA remote is a link to an online copy of your repository. The name `origin` is the default name for the primary remote. Think of it as a **bookmark** to your GitHub repo.\n\n**Step 4: Push Your Code to GitHub**\n```bash\n# Push your main branch to GitHub\ngit push -u origin main\n```\n\nThe `-u` flag sets up tracking, so next time you can just type `git push`.\n\n**Authentication:**\nGitHub requires authentication when pushing. You can use:\n- **HTTPS with Personal Access Token** — Generate at GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens\n- **SSH Keys** (recommended for daily use):\n```bash\n# Generate SSH key\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"your.email@example.com\"\n\n# Copy the public key\ncat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub\n\n# Add it to GitHub → Settings → SSH and GPG Keys → New SSH Key\n```\n\nAfter this, your code is live on GitHub! 🎉"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Push, Pull & Clone — Syncing with GitHub",
            "explanation": "Now that your repo is on GitHub, let us learn the three essential commands for syncing.\n\n**git push — Upload your commits to GitHub**\n```bash\n# Push current branch to GitHub\ngit push\n\n# Push a specific branch\ngit push origin feature-login\n\n# Push and set upstream (first time only)\ngit push -u origin main\n```\nThink of push as **uploading** your local save points to the cloud.\n\n**git pull — Download changes from GitHub**\n```bash\n# Pull latest changes from GitHub\ngit pull\n\n# Pull from a specific branch\ngit pull origin main\n```\nThink of pull as **downloading** the latest changes made by you (from another computer) or by your teammates.\n\n**git clone — Copy an entire repo from GitHub**\n```bash\n# Clone a repo (creates a folder with the project)\ngit clone https://github.com/username/repo-name.git\n\n# Clone into a specific folder name\ngit clone https://github.com/username/repo-name.git my-folder\n```\nThink of clone as **downloading a project for the first time**. You only clone once — after that, you use push and pull.\n\n**The Daily Workflow:**\n```\nMorning:  git pull              ← Get latest changes\n          ... work on code ...\n          git add .\n          git commit -m \"message\"\nEvening:  git push              ← Upload your changes\n```\n\n**Push vs Pull — Quick Comparison:**\n| Command | Direction | Purpose |\n|---------|-----------|---------|\n| `git push` | Local → GitHub | Upload your commits |\n| `git pull` | GitHub → Local | Download team's commits |\n| `git clone` | GitHub → Local | First-time full download |\n\n**Common Error: \"rejected — non-fast-forward\"**\nThis means GitHub has commits that you do not have locally. Solution:\n```bash\ngit pull --rebase     # Pull first, then push\ngit push\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Pull Requests — The Heart of Collaboration",
            "explanation": "A **Pull Request (PR)** is how developers propose changes on GitHub. Instead of pushing directly to the main branch, you create a PR that lets others review your code before it is merged.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m21_github_collaboration.png\" alt=\"GitHub Collaboration Workflow\" />\n\n**Why Pull Requests?**\n- **Code Review** — Team members can review your code, suggest improvements, and catch bugs\n- **Discussion** — Comment on specific lines of code\n- **Testing** — Automated tests can run before merging\n- **History** — A clear record of why changes were made\n\n**Creating a Pull Request — Step by Step:**\n\n**Step 1: Create a branch and make changes**\n```bash\ngit switch -c feature/add-search\n# ... make your changes ...\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Add search functionality\"\n```\n\n**Step 2: Push the branch to GitHub**\n```bash\ngit push -u origin feature/add-search\n```\n\n**Step 3: Create the PR on GitHub**\n1. Go to your repository on GitHub\n2. You will see a banner: **\"feature/add-search had recent pushes — Compare & pull request\"**\n3. Click **Compare & pull request**\n4. Fill in:\n   - **Title:** Clear, descriptive title (e.g., \"Add search bar to homepage\")\n   - **Description:** Explain what you changed and why\n   - **Reviewers:** Assign team members to review\n5. Click **Create pull request**\n\n**Step 4: Review Process**\nReviewers will:\n- Read your code changes (the \"diff\")\n- Leave comments and suggestions\n- Approve or request changes\n\n**Step 5: Merge the PR**\nOnce approved, click **Merge pull request** on GitHub. Then clean up:\n```bash\ngit switch main\ngit pull                      # Get the merged changes\ngit branch -d feature/add-search  # Delete the local branch\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Forking — Contributing to Other Projects",
            "explanation": "**What is a Fork?**\nA fork is your **personal copy** of someone else's repository on GitHub. It lives in your GitHub account, and you have full control over it.\n\n**Fork vs Clone vs Branch:**\n| Concept | What It Is | Where It Lives |\n|---------|-----------|---------------|\n| **Branch** | A separate line of work in YOUR repo | Same repo |\n| **Clone** | A local copy of a repo on your computer | Your computer |\n| **Fork** | A copy of SOMEONE ELSE'S repo in YOUR GitHub | Your GitHub account |\n\n**When to Fork:**\n- Contributing to open-source projects (you cannot create branches in repos you do not own)\n- Creating your own version of a project\n- Experimenting with someone else's code\n\n**The Fork Workflow (Contributing to Open Source):**\n\n**Step 1: Fork the repository**\nOn GitHub, click the **Fork** button on the project's page. This creates a copy in your account.\n\n**Step 2: Clone YOUR fork**\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/project-name.git\ncd project-name\n```\n\n**Step 3: Add the original repo as upstream**\n```bash\ngit remote add upstream https://github.com/ORIGINAL-OWNER/project-name.git\n```\n\nNow you have two remotes:\n- `origin` → Your fork (you can push here)\n- `upstream` → Original repo (read-only, pull updates)\n\n**Step 4: Create a branch, make changes, push**\n```bash\ngit switch -c fix/typo-in-readme\n# ... make changes ...\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Fix typo in README\"\ngit push origin fix/typo-in-readme\n```\n\n**Step 5: Create a Pull Request**\nOn GitHub, open a PR from YOUR fork's branch to the ORIGINAL repo's main branch.\n\n**Keeping Your Fork Updated:**\n```bash\ngit switch main\ngit pull upstream main        # Get latest from original\ngit push origin main          # Update your fork\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Code Reviews — Best Practices",
            "explanation": "Code review is one of the most important practices in professional software development. Let us learn how to do it well.\n\n**As an Author (Writing a PR):**\n1. **Keep PRs small** — 200-400 lines of changes is ideal. Giant PRs get rubber-stamped without proper review.\n2. **Write a clear description** — Explain the \"why\" not just the \"what\"\n3. **Self-review first** — Read your own diff before requesting reviews\n4. **Add screenshots** — For UI changes, include before/after screenshots\n5. **Reference issues** — Link related issues with \"Fixes #123\" in the description\n\n**Good PR Description Template:**\n```markdown\n## What\nBrief description of changes.\n\n## Why\nWhy is this change needed?\n\n## How\nHow does it work? Any important design decisions?\n\n## Testing\nHow did you test this? What should reviewers check?\n\n## Screenshots (if applicable)\nBefore → After\n```\n\n**As a Reviewer:**\n1. **Be kind** — Remember there is a human on the other side\n2. **Be specific** — Instead of \"This is wrong\", say \"Consider using a Map here for O(1) lookups instead of array.find()\"\n3. **Ask questions** — \"Why did you choose this approach?\" is better than \"This is the wrong approach\"\n4. **Approve when ready** — Do not block PRs over minor style preferences\n5. **Use suggestions** — GitHub lets you suggest exact code changes that the author can accept with one click\n\n**Review Comment Types:**\n```\n🔴 Blocker:   \"This will crash if user is null — needs a null check\"\n🟡 Suggestion: \"Consider extracting this into a helper function\"\n🟢 Praise:    \"Nice approach! Clean and readable\"\n💡 Nit:       \"Minor: typo in variable name (optinal → optional)\"\n```\n\n**Pro Tip:** Good code reviews are a two-way learning experience. Authors learn from feedback, and reviewers learn by reading different approaches to problems."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Git Rebase — Rewriting History for a Clean Timeline",
            "explanation": "Rebase is an alternative to merge. Both combine work from different branches, but they do it differently.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m21_merge_vs_rebase.png\" alt=\"Git Merge vs Rebase Comparison\" />\n\n**Merge vs Rebase — The Difference:**\n\n**Merge** creates a new merge commit that ties the branches together. The history shows the branch structure.\n\n**Rebase** moves your branch's commits to the tip of the target branch. The history looks like a straight line — as if the branch never existed.\n\n**How Rebase Works:**\n```bash\n# You are on feature branch, main has new commits\ngit switch feature-branch\ngit rebase main\n```\n\n```\nBefore rebase:\n  main:    A ── B ── C ── F ── G\n  feature:           C ── D ── E\n\nAfter rebase:\n  main:    A ── B ── C ── F ── G\n  feature:                      G ── D' ── E'\n  (D' and E' are new commits — same changes, new base)\n```\n\n**Interactive Rebase — Edit Your Commit History:**\n```bash\n# Rewrite the last 3 commits\ngit rebase -i HEAD~3\n```\n\nAn editor opens showing:\n```\npick a1b2c3d Add user model\npick e4f5g6h Add login endpoint\npick i7j8k9l Fix typo in login\n```\n\nYou can:\n- **pick** — Keep the commit as is\n- **reword** — Change the commit message\n- **squash** — Combine with the previous commit\n- **drop** — Delete the commit entirely\n\n**When to Use Rebase:**\n✅ Clean up messy local commits before pushing\n✅ Keep feature branch up-to-date with main\n✅ Squash \"WIP\" commits into meaningful ones\n\n**When NOT to Use Rebase:**\n❌ On shared branches that others are working on\n❌ On commits already pushed to GitHub\n\n**The Golden Rule of Rebase:**\n> **Never rebase commits that have been pushed to a shared remote.** It rewrites history and will cause problems for everyone."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Git Stash — Save Work for Later",
            "explanation": "Sometimes you are in the middle of work and need to switch branches urgently. But your changes are not ready to commit. That is where `git stash` comes in.\n\n<img src=\"/images/m21_git_stash_tags.png\" alt=\"Git Stash and Tags Concepts\" />\n\n**What is Stash?**\nStash temporarily saves your uncommitted changes and gives you a clean working directory. Think of it as putting your work-in-progress into a drawer.\n\n**Basic Stash Usage:**\n```bash\n# Save current changes to stash\ngit stash\n\n# Your working directory is now clean\ngit status   # Shows \"nothing to commit\"\n\n# Switch to another branch, fix a bug, etc.\ngit switch main\n# ... do urgent work ...\ngit switch feature-branch\n\n# Bring your changes back\ngit stash pop\n```\n\n**Stash with a Message:**\n```bash\ngit stash push -m \"WIP: halfway through login form\"\n```\n\n**List All Stashes:**\n```bash\ngit stash list\n# stash@{0}: On feature-branch: WIP: halfway through login form\n# stash@{1}: On main: Quick experiment\n```\n\n**Apply a Specific Stash:**\n```bash\ngit stash apply stash@{1}    # Apply but keep in stash list\ngit stash pop stash@{1}      # Apply and remove from stash list\n```\n\n**Delete Stashes:**\n```bash\ngit stash drop stash@{0}     # Delete a specific stash\ngit stash clear               # Delete ALL stashes\n```\n\n**Common Stash Scenarios:**\n| Situation | Solution |\n|-----------|---------|\n| Need to switch branches quickly | `git stash` → switch → work → switch back → `git stash pop` |\n| Want to test something on clean code | `git stash` → test → `git stash pop` |\n| Accidentally started work on wrong branch | `git stash` → `git switch correct-branch` → `git stash pop` |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Cherry-Pick, Tags & Git Revert",
            "explanation": "Let us cover three more powerful Git features that professionals use regularly.\n\n**Git Cherry-Pick — Copy a Single Commit**\nSometimes you want to apply just one commit from another branch without merging the whole branch.\n\n```bash\n# Find the commit hash you want\ngit log --oneline feature-branch\n# a1b2c3d Add email validation   ← I want this one!\n# e4f5g6h Add signup form\n\n# Cherry-pick it onto your current branch\ngit cherry-pick a1b2c3d\n```\n\n**When to Cherry-Pick:**\n- Applying a critical bug fix from a feature branch to main\n- Moving a commit that was made on the wrong branch\n- Backporting a fix to an older release branch\n\n**Git Tags — Marking Important Points**\nTags are permanent labels you attach to specific commits. They are mainly used to mark release versions.\n\n```bash\n# Create a lightweight tag\ngit tag v1.0.0\n\n# Create an annotated tag (recommended — includes message and author)\ngit tag -a v1.0.0 -m \"First stable release\"\n\n# Tag a specific commit (not the current one)\ngit tag -a v0.9.0 -m \"Beta release\" a1b2c3d\n\n# List all tags\ngit tag\n\n# Push tags to GitHub\ngit push origin v1.0.0        # Push one tag\ngit push origin --tags         # Push ALL tags\n\n# Delete a tag\ngit tag -d v1.0.0              # Delete locally\ngit push origin --delete v1.0.0  # Delete from GitHub\n```\n\n**Semantic Versioning (SemVer):**\n```\nv MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\nv 1.     2.    3\n\nMAJOR → Breaking changes (users must update their code)\nMINOR → New features (backward compatible)\nPATCH → Bug fixes (backward compatible)\n```\n\n**Git Revert — Safely Undo a Pushed Commit**\nUnlike `git reset` (which rewrites history), `git revert` creates a NEW commit that undoes a previous commit. This is safe to use on shared branches.\n\n```bash\n# Revert a specific commit\ngit revert a1b2c3d\n\n# This creates a new commit that undoes the changes\n# History is preserved — nothing is deleted\n```"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "GitHub Issues, Projects & Discussions",
            "explanation": "GitHub is much more than code storage. It has built-in project management tools that teams use daily.\n\n**GitHub Issues — Tracking Tasks & Bugs**\nIssues are like to-do items for your project. Anyone can create an issue to report a bug, request a feature, or ask a question.\n\n**Creating a Good Issue:**\n```markdown\n## Bug Report: Login button unresponsive on mobile\n\n**Describe the bug**\nThe login button does not respond to taps on iPhone Safari.\n\n**Steps to Reproduce**\n1. Open the app on iPhone\n2. Navigate to login page\n3. Tap the \"Login\" button\n4. Nothing happens\n\n**Expected Behavior**\nShould open the login modal.\n\n**Screenshots**\n[Attach screenshot here]\n\n**Environment**\n- Device: iPhone 14\n- OS: iOS 17.2\n- Browser: Safari\n```\n\n**Issue Labels:**\nLabels help categorize issues. Common ones:\n| Label | Color | Purpose |\n|-------|-------|---------|\n| `bug` | 🔴 Red | Something is broken |\n| `feature` | 🟢 Green | New functionality |\n| `documentation` | 🔵 Blue | Docs improvement |\n| `good first issue` | 🟣 Purple | Easy for newcomers |\n| `help wanted` | 🟡 Yellow | Community help needed |\n\n**Closing Issues with Commits:**\nYou can automatically close issues by referencing them in commit messages:\n```bash\ngit commit -m \"Fix mobile login button - Fixes #42\"\n```\nWhen this commit is merged into main, issue #42 is automatically closed.\n\n**GitHub Projects (Kanban Boards):**\nGitHub Projects lets you organize issues into visual boards:\n```\n| To Do          | In Progress    | Done           |\n|---------------|---------------|---------------|\n| Issue #12     | Issue #8      | Issue #5      |\n| Issue #15     | Issue #10     | Issue #7      |\n| Issue #18     |               | Issue #9      |\n```\n\n**GitHub Discussions:**\nA forum-like space for Q&A, announcements, and community conversations. Unlike issues, discussions are for general conversation that is not a specific task or bug."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "GitHub Actions — Automating Your Workflow",
            "explanation": "GitHub Actions lets you automate tasks every time something happens in your repository — like running tests when you push code, or deploying your app when you merge a PR.\n\n**What is CI/CD?**\n- **CI (Continuous Integration)** — Automatically test your code every time you push\n- **CD (Continuous Deployment)** — Automatically deploy your app after tests pass\n\n**Your First GitHub Action:**\nCreate a file at `.github/workflows/ci.yml`:\n\n```yaml\nname: Run Tests\n\n# When to run this workflow\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n  pull_request:\n    branches: [main]\n\n# What to do\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n\n    steps:\n      # Step 1: Get the code\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      # Step 2: Set up Python\n      - name: Set up Python\n        uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: '3.12'\n\n      # Step 3: Install dependencies\n      - name: Install dependencies\n        run: |\n          pip install -r requirements.txt\n\n      # Step 4: Run tests\n      - name: Run tests\n        run: |\n          python -m pytest tests/ -v\n```\n\n**What This Does:**\n1. Every time you push to `main` or open a PR targeting `main`\n2. GitHub spins up a fresh Ubuntu machine\n3. Installs Python and your dependencies\n4. Runs your tests\n5. Shows ✅ or ❌ on the PR\n\n**Common Workflow Triggers:**\n| Trigger | When It Runs |\n|---------|-------------|\n| `push` | When code is pushed |\n| `pull_request` | When a PR is opened or updated |\n| `schedule` | On a cron schedule (e.g., daily) |\n| `workflow_dispatch` | Manual trigger (button in GitHub UI) |\n\n**Badge in README:**\nShow your test status in your README:\n```markdown\n![Tests](https://github.com/username/repo/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)\n```\n\nThis shows a green \"passing\" or red \"failing\" badge — a sign of a professional project."
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Professional Git Workflows",
            "explanation": "Teams follow specific workflows to keep their codebase organized. Here are the most popular ones.\n\n**1. GitHub Flow (Simple — Recommended for Beginners)**\nThe most common workflow for teams using GitHub.\n\n```\nmain ──────────────────────────────────────────────→\n       \\                    /\n        feature-branch ────  (PR + review + merge)\n```\n\nRules:\n1. `main` is always deployable\n2. Create a branch for every change\n3. Open a PR for review\n4. Merge to main after approval\n5. Deploy from main\n\n**2. Git Flow (Structured — For Release-Based Projects)**\n```\nmain     ──────────────────────────────────→  (production)\n              \\           /\ndevelop   ────────────────────────────────→  (staging)\n                \\       /\n                feature-x                     (work)\n```\n\nUses dedicated branches:\n- `main` → Production releases only\n- `develop` → Integration branch\n- `feature/*` → New features\n- `release/*` → Release preparation\n- `hotfix/*` → Emergency production fixes\n\n**3. Trunk-Based Development (Fast — For Experienced Teams)**\nEveryone commits directly to main (or very short-lived branches). Requires strong testing and feature flags.\n\n**Which Workflow to Use?**\n| Team Size | Experience | Workflow |\n|-----------|-----------|----------|\n| 1-5 developers | Any | GitHub Flow |\n| 5-20 developers | Mixed | Git Flow |\n| 20+ developers | Senior | Trunk-Based |\n\n**Branch Protection Rules (GitHub Settings):**\nProtect your main branch by requiring:\n- ✅ Pull request reviews before merging\n- ✅ Status checks (tests) must pass\n- ✅ Branches must be up-to-date before merging\n- ✅ No force-pushes to main\n\nSet this up in: Repository → Settings → Branches → Branch protection rules"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Git Tips, Tricks & Troubleshooting",
            "explanation": "Let us cover the most useful tips and common problems you will encounter.\n\n**Useful Shortcuts:**\n```bash\n# Stage and commit in one step (only for tracked files)\ngit commit -am \"Quick fix\"\n\n# See a visual log with branches\ngit log --oneline --graph --all\n\n# See who changed each line of a file\ngit blame filename.py\n\n# Search through commit messages\ngit log --grep=\"login\"\n\n# Search through code changes\ngit log -S \"function_name\"\n\n# Show what changed in a specific commit\ngit show a1b2c3d\n```\n\n**Aliases — Create Custom Shortcuts:**\n```bash\ngit config --global alias.co checkout\ngit config --global alias.br branch\ngit config --global alias.ci commit\ngit config --global alias.st status\ngit config --global alias.lg \"log --oneline --graph --all\"\n```\nNow you can type `git co main` instead of `git checkout main`.\n\n**Common Problems & Solutions:**\n\n**Problem: \"I committed to the wrong branch\"**\n```bash\n# Move the last commit to a new branch\ngit branch correct-branch     # Create branch at current commit\ngit reset --hard HEAD~1       # Remove commit from wrong branch\ngit switch correct-branch     # Go to correct branch\n```\n\n**Problem: \"I need to update my PR with latest main\"**\n```bash\ngit switch feature-branch\ngit pull origin main          # Merge main into your branch\n# OR\ngit rebase origin/main        # Rebase on top of main\ngit push --force-with-lease   # Update the PR (if rebased)\n```\n\n**Problem: \"I accidentally deleted a branch\"**\n```bash\n# Git keeps deleted branch data temporarily\ngit reflog                    # Find the commit hash\ngit branch recovered-branch a1b2c3d  # Recreate the branch\n```\n\n**Problem: \"My repo is a mess and I want to start fresh\"**\n```bash\n# Nuclear option — discard ALL uncommitted changes\ngit reset --hard HEAD\ngit clean -fd                 # Remove untracked files too\n```\n\n**The Git Cheat Sheet:**\n| Action | Command |\n|--------|---------|\n| Initialize repo | `git init` |\n| Clone repo | `git clone URL` |\n| Check status | `git status` |\n| Stage files | `git add .` |\n| Commit | `git commit -m \"msg\"` |\n| Push | `git push` |\n| Pull | `git pull` |\n| Create branch | `git switch -c branch-name` |\n| Switch branch | `git switch branch-name` |\n| Merge | `git merge branch-name` |\n| View log | `git log --oneline` |\n| Stash | `git stash` / `git stash pop` |\n| Tag | `git tag -a v1.0 -m \"msg\"` |"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Building an Impressive GitHub Profile",
            "explanation": "Your GitHub profile is your **developer resume**. Recruiters and hiring managers look at it. Let us make it stand out.\n\n**1. Create a Profile README**\nCreate a repository with the SAME name as your username (e.g., `github.com/yourname/yourname`). The README of this repo appears on your profile page.\n\n```markdown\n# Hi there, I am [Your Name] 👋\n\n🔭 I am currently working on AI applications\n🌱 I am learning LangChain and cloud deployment\n💬 Ask me about Python, Machine Learning, and APIs\n📫 How to reach me: your.email@example.com\n\n## 🛠️ Tech Stack\n![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/-Python-3776AB?logo=python&logoColor=white)\n![JavaScript](https://img.shields.io/badge/-JavaScript-F7DF1E?logo=javascript&logoColor=black)\n![React](https://img.shields.io/badge/-React-61DAFB?logo=react&logoColor=black)\n\n## 📊 GitHub Stats\n![Your GitHub stats](https://github-readme-stats.vercel.app/api?username=yourusername&show_icons=true&theme=radical)\n```\n\n**2. Pin Your Best Repositories**\nOn your profile, pin 6 repositories that showcase your best work. Choose projects that are:\n- Well-documented (good README)\n- Have clean code\n- Show different skills\n\n**3. Write Great README Files**\nEvery project should have a README with:\n```markdown\n# Project Name 🚀\n\nBrief description of what this project does.\n\n## Features\n- Feature 1\n- Feature 2\n\n## Installation\n\\`\\`\\`bash\ngit clone https://github.com/you/project.git\ncd project\npip install -r requirements.txt\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Usage\n\\`\\`\\`bash\npython main.py\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Screenshots\n[Add screenshots here]\n\n## Tech Stack\n- Python, FastAPI, PostgreSQL\n\n## Contributing\nPRs welcome! Please read CONTRIBUTING.md first.\n\n## License\nMIT License\n```\n\n**4. Contribute to Open Source**\n- Start with repositories labeled **\"good first issue\"**\n- Fix documentation, typos, or small bugs\n- Explore https://goodfirstissue.dev for beginner-friendly projects\n\n**5. Stay Active**\nThe green contribution graph on your profile shows your activity. Regular commits (even small ones) show consistency and dedication.\n\n**Your GitHub profile is often the first thing recruiters check. Make it count!**"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "concept",
          "content": {
            "title": "Capstone Project: Build & Collaborate on a GitHub Repository",
            "explanation": "**Congratulations on completing the Git & GitHub module!**\n\nNow it is time to put everything together into a real project that demonstrates your Git mastery.\n\n**Your Mission:**\nCreate a professional, well-structured GitHub repository for a Python project and practice the full collaboration workflow.\n\n**Project Requirements:**\n\n**Part 1 — Repository Setup (Beginner Skills)**\n1. Create a new repository on GitHub with a README\n2. Clone it to your local machine\n3. Add a proper `.gitignore` file for Python\n4. Create an initial project structure and make your first commit\n5. Push to GitHub\n\n**Part 2 — Feature Development (Intermediate Skills)**\n1. Create a `feature/core-app` branch\n2. Build a simple Python application (CLI tool, API, or script)\n3. Make at least 5 meaningful commits with clear messages\n4. Push the branch and create a Pull Request\n5. Write a detailed PR description using the template from this module\n6. Merge the PR into main\n\n**Part 3 — Branching & Conflict Resolution (Intermediate Skills)**\n1. Create two branches from main that modify the same file\n2. Merge the first branch into main\n3. Try to merge the second branch — resolve the merge conflict\n4. Document what happened and how you resolved it\n\n**Part 4 — Advanced Features (Expert Skills)**\n1. Use `git stash` to save work-in-progress and switch branches\n2. Create a release tag (`v1.0.0`) with a descriptive message\n3. Use interactive rebase to squash 3 small commits into one clean commit\n4. Set up a GitHub Action that runs a simple test on every push\n5. Create a professional README with badges, screenshots, and clear documentation\n\n**Part 5 — Open Source Contribution (Expert Skills)**\n1. Find an open-source project on GitHub\n2. Fork it, clone your fork, and create a branch\n3. Make a meaningful contribution (fix a bug, improve docs, add a feature)\n4. Open a Pull Request to the original repository\n\n**Deliverables:**\n- ✅ A GitHub repository with clean commit history\n- ✅ At least one merged Pull Request\n- ✅ A resolved merge conflict in the history\n- ✅ A release tag (v1.0.0)\n- ✅ A GitHub Actions workflow\n- ✅ A professional README\n- ✅ A contribution (PR) to another open-source project\n\n**This capstone covers every skill from this module. Take your time, and remember — the best way to learn Git is by using Git every day!**"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}